window.__FLZ=[{id:"heathrobinson",n:"Heath Robinson Museum",a:"Pinner",pc:"HA5 1AE",lat:51.5968,lng:-0.387,c:"museum",s:"the Gadget King's contraptions",q:"Heath Robinson Museum London",w:"Nearly a thousand drawings by the man who gave us \"Heath Robinson contraption\" — those gloriously over-engineered machines of string, pulleys and teapots solving problems nobody had. Opened in October 2016 in a sleek cross-laminated timber building by ZMMA, tucked into Pinner Memorial Park beside the Georgian West House. Robinson (1872–1944) lived just up the road. The Gadget King, properly housed at last.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"headstonemanor",n:"Headstone Manor & Museum",a:"Harrow",pc:"HA2 6PX",lat:51.5903,lng:-0.3522,c:"medieval",s:"Middlesex's only filled moat",q:"Headstone Manor & Museum London",w:"The earliest surviving timber-framed building in Middlesex, its core dating to 1310, still sitting inside a moat that has never run dry — reputedly the only filled moat left in the old county. John de Stratford, Archbishop of Canterbury, held the land when the moat was dug in the 1340s. Grade I listed, a scheduled monument, and now Harrow's local museum. Water where you least expect it in suburbia.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stephenshouse",n:"Stephens House & Gardens",a:"Finchley",pc:"N3 3QE",lat:51.5945,lng:-0.1745,c:"museum",s:"the ink baron's estate",q:"Stephens House & Gardens London",w:"Home of Henry \"Inky\" Stephens (1841–1918), heir to the blue-black writing fluid his father concocted in 1832 and sold by the gallon to the Empire. He bought Avenue House and its ten acres in 1874, had Robert Marnock lay out the arboretum, and left the lot to Finchley on his death. In the grounds stands the Bothy, a castle-shaped folly and one of England's earliest non-Roman concrete buildings. Ink money, beautifully spent.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"salisburyhouse",n:"Salisbury House",a:"Edmonton",pc:"N9 9LF",lat:51.6228,lng:-0.0758,c:"history",s:"the last of a lost hamlet",q:"Salisbury House London",w:"A three-storey timber-framed survivor of the late 1500s, marooned where the old Bury Street hamlet of Edmonton once stood. Grade II* listed since 1954, it keeps a carved 1625 overmantel bearing the dry domestic motto \"He that is warme thynks all soe.\" It became Enfield's first arts centre in 1957 and still runs classes and a café. The last house standing from a vanished village.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"berniegrant",n:"Bernie Grant Arts Centre",a:"Tottenham",pc:"N15 4RX",lat:51.585,lng:-0.0718,c:"diaspora",s:"Adjaye behind the old baths",q:"Bernie Grant Arts Centre London",w:"David Adjaye's first major public building, opened in 2007, stitched together from the historic structures behind Tottenham Town Hall — a Victorian bath-house among them — into a timber-and-glass cultural village. It honours Bernie Grant (1944–2000), elected MP for Tottenham in 1987 as one of Britain's first Black members of Parliament. A theatre, a recording studio, an architect's calling card.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hitchcockmosaics",n:"Hitchcock Mosaics, Leytonstone Station",a:"Leytonstone",pc:"E11 1HE",lat:51.5683,lng:0.0083,c:"film",s:"17 mosaics where Hitch was born",q:"Hitchcock Mosaics, Leytonstone Station London",w:"Seventeen mosaics line the passage at Leytonstone station, scenes from Vertigo to The Birds laid in some eighty thousand tiles. Made by the Greenwich Mural Workshop and unveiled in 2001 for the centenary of Alfred Hitchcock, born just up the High Road at number 517 in 1899, above his father's greengrocer's shop. The Master of Suspense, commemorated where the trains run beneath him.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Leytonstone_Station.jpg/960px-Leytonstone_Station.jpg"},{id:"vestryhouse",n:"Vestry House Museum & the Bremer Car",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"E17 9NH",lat:51.5833,lng:-0.0125,c:"museum",s:"Britain's first petrol car",q:"Vestry House Museum & the Bremer Car London",w:"In a corner of this 1730 parish workhouse sits the Bremer Car, a claimant to the title of first British-built petrol motor car, knocked together by young Frederick Bremer in a Walthamstow backyard between 1892 and 1894. He drove it on local roads, then gave it to the museum in 1933. A homemade horseless carriage, parked for good in a building that once locked up the poor.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Courtyard_of_the_Vestry_House_Museum_-_geograph.org.uk_-_900047.jpg"},{id:"valentinesmansion",n:"Valentines Mansion & Gardens",a:"Ilford",pc:"IG1 4XA",lat:51.5718,lng:0.0758,c:"history",s:"the free 1690s mansion",q:"Valentines Mansion & Gardens London",w:"A red-brick mansion of around 1696, built for Elizabeth Tillotson, widow of an Archbishop of Canterbury, and these days free to walk into. Its garden once held a Black Hamburg vine, and it was a cutting from here that was planted at Hampton Court in 1768 — that monstrous Great Vine is Ilford's offspring. Handsomely restored and reopened in 2009. Grandeur without an admission charge.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tudorbarn",n:"Tudor Barn Eltham",a:"Eltham",pc:"SE9 6SZ",lat:51.4553,lng:0.0488,c:"pub",s:"London's only Tudor barn",q:"Tudor Barn Eltham London",w:"Built around 1525 by William Roper, the son-in-law and biographer of Sir Thomas More — Roper married Margaret More, who is said to have retrieved her father's head from London Bridge. Grade II* listed, it is the surviving fragment of the moated Well Hall estate, now ringed by the Pleasaunce gardens. The ground floor pours pints; the upper hall hangs art. A Tudor barn that has made its peace with the licensing laws.",city:"london"},{id:"arsenalpepperpot",n:"Royal Arsenal Pepper Pot Guard Houses",a:"Woolwich",pc:"SE18 6GH",lat:51.4918,lng:0.07,c:"history",s:"guardrooms of the Secret City",q:"Royal Arsenal Pepper Pot Guard Houses London",w:"Two squat domed guardrooms, the \"pepper pots,\" that once watched the gates of the Royal Arsenal — the walled, self-policed \"Secret City\" left blank on maps, where shells and gunpowder were made. At its WWI peak some 80,000 worked here, tens of thousands of them women, across 1,300 acres. Manufacturing ended in 1967, the military left in 1994. The little sentry boxes now sell flowers and coffee.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Woolwich_royal_arsenal_gatehouse_1.jpg/960px-Woolwich_royal_arsenal_gatehouse_1.jpg"},{id:"sydenhamfolly",n:"Sydenham Hill Wood Folly",a:"Sydenham Hill",pc:"SE26 6LS",lat:51.436,lng:-0.0738,c:"follies",s:"a fake ruined monastery",q:"Sydenham Hill Wood Folly London",w:"A sham ruined monastery mouldering among the trees, built in the early 1860s as a romantic garden ornament for Fairwood, a villa belonging to a future Lord Mayor of London. The crumbling \"stone\" is largely Pulhamite, the famous artificial rock of the Pulham firm, dressed up to look ancient. The wood itself is a surviving scrap of the old Great North Wood, now a London Wildlife Trust reserve. A fake ruin slowly becoming a real one.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rivoliballroom",n:"Rivoli Ballroom",a:"Crofton Park",pc:"SE4 2BY",lat:51.4543,lng:-0.0367,c:"music",s:"London's last 1950s ballroom",q:"Rivoli Ballroom London",w:"London's last intact 1950s dance hall, all red velvet, gilt and oversized Chinese lanterns — a time capsule with a sprung maple floor. It opened in 1913 as the Crofton Park Picture Palace, ran its last film in 1957, and reopened as a ballroom in 1959. Grade II listed since 2007, it has since stood in for countless films and music videos. The chandeliers are real; the decade is borrowed.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Rivoli_Ballroom%2C_Brockley%2C_SE4.jpg/960px-Rivoli_Ballroom%2C_Brockley%2C_SE4.jpg"},{id:"catfordcat",n:"The Catford Cat",a:"Catford",pc:"SE6 4RU",lat:51.4451,lng:-0.0203,c:"pop",s:"the giant grinning cat",q:"The Catford Cat London",w:"A giant grinning black cat, two storeys tall, swinging a paw over the entrance to the Catford Centre since 1974 — fibreglass, faintly menacing, beloved. It crowns the brutalist shopping precinct by Owen Luder and Rodney Gordon. Demolition threats in 2008 and again in 2017 brought out the placards; the cat, predictably, won. South-east London's most improbable landmark.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Catford_gyratory.jpg/960px-Catford_gyratory.jpg"},{id:"carewmanor",n:"Carew Manor & Dovecote",a:"Beddington",pc:"SM6 7NH",lat:51.3733,lng:-0.1398,c:"follies",s:"1,360 nests and a rotating ladder",q:"Carew Manor & Dovecote London",w:"An octagonal brick dovecote of around 1715, built for Sir Nicholas Carew with roughly 1,360 L-shaped nesting holes reached by a potence — a ladder that pivots on a central post so you can scoop squabs from every wall in turn. It is a scheduled monument; the adjoining manor keeps a hammerbeam-roofed Great Hall of about 1500. The Carews left in 1859 and the place is now a school. Industrial-scale pigeon farming, eighteenth-century style.",city:"london"},{id:"surreystmarket",n:"Surrey Street Market & Butcher Row",a:"Croydon",pc:"CR0 1RG",lat:51.3738,lng:-0.1008,c:"history",s:"Croydon's medieval shambles",q:"Surrey Street Market & Butcher Row London",w:"Croydon has hawked its wares on this slope since at least 1276, when the Archbishop of Canterbury was granted a market charter — the meat trade clustered in the lane once called the Shambles, the Flesh Market, before it became Surrey Street. Six days a week the fruit-and-veg stalls, some sixty-odd of them, still cry their prices. One of the longest-running street markets in the country, scarcely moved an inch.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Surrey_Street_Market_Croydon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_962324.jpg"},{id:"mordenhall",n:"Morden Hall Park",a:"Morden",pc:"SM4 5JD",lat:51.4039,lng:-0.1936,c:"green",s:"built on snuff money",q:"Morden Hall Park London",w:"A hundred and twenty-five acres of meadow and braided Wandle channels, paid for in snuff — the powdered tobacco ground at two waterwheel-driven mills here until 1922, when Gilliat Hatfeild shut them rather than concede a strike. He left the estate to the National Trust on his death in 1941, insisting the public always walk in free. A fortune sniffed up the nation's nostrils, now a riverside park.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mitchamgreen",n:"Mitcham Cricket Green",a:"Mitcham",pc:"CR4 4LB",lat:51.3997,lng:-0.162,c:"stadium",s:"reputedly the oldest cricket green",q:"Mitcham Cricket Green London",w:"Cricket has reputedly been played on this triangle of common since 1685, which would make it the oldest ground still in use anywhere. The 1904 pavilion sits across a public road from the pitch, so batsmen wait at the kerb of the A239 before walking out to the crease. Mitcham Cricket Club still bats here every summer. Mind the traffic, then mind the bowling.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bostonmanor",n:"Boston Manor House",a:"Brentford",pc:"TW8 9JX",lat:51.4945,lng:-0.3218,c:"history",s:"Jacobean plaster, painted windows",q:"Boston Manor House London",w:"A Jacobean house of 1623, built for Lady Mary Reade, a young widow whose initials sit in its glory — the plaster ceiling of the State Drawing Room, dense with allegories of the Five Senses, the Elements, and War and Peace. After a six-year, six-million-pound restoration it reopened in 2023. One of London's finest early-Stuart interiors, hiding by the M4. Look up.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hanwellbridges",n:"Three Bridges & Hanwell Locks",a:"Hanwell",pc:"UB2 4NJ",lat:51.4963,lng:-0.3418,c:"canals",s:"Brunel's last engineering work",q:"Three Bridges & Hanwell Locks London",w:"Three Bridges, where Brunel threaded a road over a canal aqueduct over a railway cutting — three levels of traffic stacked at one point, finished in 1859 and the great engineer's last major work before he died that September. Just along the towpath the Hanwell Flight drops the Grand Union through six locks, its water-saving side-ponds dug back in 1815. Brunel's parting trick, and it still works.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"popesgrotto",n:"Pope's Grotto",a:"Twickenham",pc:"TW1 4QG",lat:51.4419,lng:-0.329,c:"follies",s:"the poet's mineral tunnel",q:"Pope's Grotto London",w:"All that survives of Alexander Pope's Twickenham villa, demolished in 1808 — a tunnel he built around 1725 to link his garden to the house beneath the road, then encrusted with shells, crystals, ores and mirrors into a glittering subterranean cabinet. It now lies under Radnor House School, Grade II* listed and painstakingly restored by 2023. The poet's underworld, studded with rock. Worth the descent.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"jwgrave",n:"John Williams's Crossroads Grave",a:"Shadwell",pc:"E1",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.0567,c:"death",s:"staked at the crossroads",q:"John Williams's Crossroads Grave London",w:"They drove a stake through his heart and pitched him into a hole at the crossroads, the old folk-remedy against a restless murderer's ghost. John Williams, the prime suspect in the 1811 Ratcliffe Highway killings, hanged himself in his Coldbath Fields cell on 28 December before any trial; his body was then paraded past the murder houses and buried here, where Cannon Street Road meets Cable Street. Workmen dug up his bones a century on, laying a gas main. One of London's last crossroads burials.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"marrshop",n:"29 Ratcliffe Highway (Marr Murders)",a:"Wapping",pc:"E1W",lat:51.5106,lng:-0.0606,c:"death",s:"where the maul fell",q:"29 Ratcliffe Highway (Marr Murders) London",w:"The maul fell here on the night of 7 December 1811, behind the linen-draper's at number 29, and within an hour four people lay dead: Timothy Marr, his wife Celia, their infant son and the apprentice James Gowen. Twelve days later a second household was slaughtered nearby, and the Ratcliffe Highway murders gripped the nation, prompting De Quincey's essay on murder as a fine art. The Highway has been renamed and rebuilt; the terror left a longer mark than the bricks.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sepbell",n:"St Sepulchre & the Execution Bell",a:"Holborn",pc:"EC1A 2DQ",lat:51.5165,lng:-0.102,c:"death",s:"the handbell of the doomed",q:"St Sepulchre & the Execution Bell London",w:"In a glass case in the nave sits the handbell that tolled the doomed to their deaths. Robert Dowe, a Merchant Taylor, left £50 in 1605 so the parish sexton could ring it twelve times at midnight outside the condemned cells of nearby Newgate, reciting wholesome advice to men who would hang at dawn. The great bells of Old Bailey in the nursery rhyme are these. A more intimate, and crueller, alarm.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"giltwatch",n:"The Watch House, Giltspur Street",a:"Smithfield",pc:"EC1A",lat:51.5172,lng:-0.1018,c:"death",s:"guarding the freshly dead",q:"The Watch House, Giltspur Street London",w:"Built in 1791 against the north wall of St Sepulchre's, this little box sheltered men paid to watch over the freshly buried. Corpses fetched good money from the anatomy schools, and the resurrection men worked the churchyard with spades by night. Bombed in the war and rebuilt in 1962, it is the last surviving watch-house in the City of London. A sentry post for the dead, when the dead could not rest easy.",city:"london"},{id:"necrorail",n:"London Necropolis Railway Station",a:"Lambeth",pc:"SE1 7HR",lat:51.4998,lng:-0.1142,c:"death",s:"the dead-only train",q:"London Necropolis Railway Station London",w:"From 1854 a dead-only train left here for Brookwood, the vast Surrey cemetery built to relieve London's overflowing graveyards, carrying coffins and mourners the hour's run to the largest burial ground in the world. Carriages were divided by class and even by religion; some 200,000 of London's dead made the one-way trip. This 1902 frontage at 121 Westminster Bridge Road is all that survived the 1941 bombing that ended the service. First class, single.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"prinalice",n:"Princess Alice Disaster, Tripcock Point",a:"Thamesmead",pc:"SE28",lat:51.506,lng:0.089,c:"death",s:"lost in the sewage tide",q:"Princess Alice Disaster, Tripcock Point London",w:"On the evening of 3 September 1878 the pleasure steamer Princess Alice, rounding Tripcock Point into Gallions Reach, was cut in two by the collier Bywell Castle and sank in minutes. Around 650 day-trippers drowned, many in water fouled by 75 million gallons of raw sewage discharged into the Thames just an hour before. It remains the worst loss of life on any British inland waterway. The river itself helped finish them.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"newgatewall",n:"Newgate's Surviving Wall, Amen Court",a:"City of London",pc:"EC4M",lat:51.5151,lng:-0.1015,c:"death",s:"all that's left of Newgate",q:"Newgate's Surviving Wall, Amen Court London",w:"A blackened stretch of stone in a quiet clergy cul-de-sac is all that remains of Newgate, the prison that for seven centuries was a byword for horror. It closed in 1902 and was demolished to make way for the Old Bailey; this eastern wall, backing onto Amen Court, survived. Behind it once lay the press yard and the gallows that replaced Tyburn in 1783. The ghosts, by tradition, walk this wall still.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Newgate_Hollar.PNG"},{id:"soe64",n:"SOE HQ, 64 Baker Street",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1U 7DF",lat:51.5185,lng:-0.1575,c:"history",s:"set Europe ablaze",q:"SOE HQ, 64 Baker Street London",w:"From October 1940 the Special Operations Executive ran its war from these unmarked offices, the agency Churchill ordered to set Europe ablaze through sabotage and resistance behind enemy lines. Staff and locals alike called them the Baker Street Irregulars, after Conan Doyle's street urchins; agents trained, were briefed and vanished into occupied France from here until disbandment in 1946. No brass plate, of course. That was rather the point.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/64_Baker_Street_Closeup.jpg/960px-64_Baker_Street_Closeup.jpg"},{id:"noorkhan",n:"Noor Inayat Khan Statue",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1H 0PD",lat:51.5243,lng:-0.13,c:"history",s:"her last word, Liberte",q:"Noor Inayat Khan Statue London",w:"A bronze bust in the gardens she once walked honours Noor Inayat Khan, the SOE wireless operator who held out alone in occupied Paris before her betrayal and capture. She was shot at Dachau in September 1944; her last word, witnesses said, was Liberté. Unveiled in Gordon Square in 2012 by the Princess Royal, it was the first standalone memorial in Britain to a Muslim woman, and to an Asian woman. A princess by descent, a heroine by choice.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kgboratory",n:"Brompton Oratory KGB Dead-Drop",a:"South Kensington",pc:"SW7 2RP",lat:51.4983,lng:-0.169,c:"history",s:"behind the marble pillar",q:"Brompton Oratory KGB Dead-Drop London",w:"Among all this Italian marble, behind a pillar to the right of the front doors, the KGB ran one of its favoured Cold War dead-drops. Microfilm and notes were tucked away here for handlers to collect, the church chosen for its closeness to the Soviet embassy in Kensington Palace Gardens and to Harrods, whose many exits were perfect for shaking a tail. Confession booths and brush passes, under the same roof.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Brompton_Oratory-2.jpg/960px-Brompton_Oratory-2.jpg"},{id:"arcosraid",n:"The ARCOS Raid Site, 49 Moorgate",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2",lat:51.5175,lng:-0.089,c:"history",s:"the raid that broke the code",q:"The ARCOS Raid Site, 49 Moorgate London",w:"At twenty past four on 12 May 1927, police and Special Branch stormed the Soviet trade mission here, hunting a stolen Army signals manual rumoured to be photographed in the basement. They found little hard evidence, yet the raid broke off Anglo-Soviet diplomatic relations within weeks. Worse, ministers quoted decrypted Soviet cables in the Commons to justify it, warning Moscow that Britain read its traffic; the Soviets changed their ciphers, and a window went dark. A self-inflicted wound, expensively learnt.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"markovbridge",n:"Georgi Markov's Umbrella Murder",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5075,lng:-0.116,c:"history",s:"the poisoned umbrella jab",q:"Georgi Markov's Umbrella Murder London",w:"At the southern end of Waterloo Bridge, on 7 September 1978, the Bulgarian defector Georgi Markov felt a sharp jab in his thigh as a stranger fumbled an umbrella and muttered an apology. The brolly had fired a pinhead pellet drilled with ricin; Markov, a BBC World Service broadcaster, died four days later. The Bulgarian secret police, with Soviet help, are blamed, though no one has ever been charged. The most literary murder weapon of the Cold War.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"litvinenkobar",n:"Litvinenko & the Pine Bar",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1K 2HP",lat:51.5117,lng:-0.152,c:"history",s:"polonium in the teapot",q:"Litvinenko & the Pine Bar London",w:"In the Pine Bar here on 1 November 2006, Alexander Litvinenko drank green tea poured from a pot laced with polonium-210, a radioactive isotope so rare it could only have come from a state laboratory. The former FSB officer and Putin critic died three agonising weeks later, the first known victim of acute radiation poisoning by murder. The teapot, the cups, the bar itself glowed with contamination. A 2016 inquiry found the Kremlin probably approved it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bentinck5",n:"5 Bentinck Street (Cambridge Five Flat)",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1U",lat:51.518,lng:-0.1505,c:"history",s:"the spies' Blitz salon",q:"5 Bentinck Street (Cambridge Five Flat) London",w:"During the Blitz, Anthony Blunt and Guy Burgess shared a flat here, in a building owned by Victor Rothschild, while both quietly served Moscow. Their Soviet controllers were appalled that two of their agents lived openly together, breaking every rule of tradecraft, amid the bohemian, hard-drinking salon the address became famous for. Half the Cambridge ring passed through these rooms. A safe house that was anything but.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/JAMES_SMITHSON_-_9_Bentinck_Street_Marylebone_London_W1U_2EJ.jpg/960px-JAMES_SMITHSON_-_9_Bentinck_Street_Marylebone_London_W1U_2EJ.jpg"},{id:"wimpolemews",n:"17 Wimpole Mews (the Profumo Flat)",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1G",lat:51.521,lng:-0.1465,c:"history",s:"five shots and a scandal",q:"17 Wimpole Mews (the Profumo Flat) London",w:"Five shots fired into the door of Stephen Ward's flat here, on 14 December 1962, lit the fuse on the decade's great scandal. Johnny Edgecombe, a jilted lover of Christine Keeler, was after her; the arrest and trial dragged into the open her affairs with War Minister John Profumo and the Soviet naval attaché Yevgeny Ivanov. Profumo lied to the Commons, then resigned, and Macmillan's government never recovered. A jealous gunman who toppled a cabinet.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"leconfield",n:"Leconfield House (old MI5 HQ)",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1J",lat:51.507,lng:-0.149,c:"history",s:"where they grilled Philby",q:"Leconfield House (old MI5 HQ) London",w:"From 1945 to 1976 this Curzon Street block was the secret heart of MI5, its wartime roof once fitted with machine-gun turrets pointing up toward Hyde Park. It was here, in December 1951, that the Service first formally grilled Kim Philby over the flight of Burgess and Maclean, the interrogation finding him guilty in spirit but unprosecutable. He defected to Moscow twelve years later. The top floor held a staff bar, the Pig and Eye, where one could safely talk shop.",city:"london"},{id:"swanupping",n:"Swan Upping",a:"Sunbury-on-Thames",pc:"TW16",lat:51.405,lng:-0.417,c:"history",s:"counting the King's swans",q:"Swan Upping London",w:"Every third week of July, six scarlet-liveried skiffs of the King's Swan Marker and the Vintners' and Dyers' Companies row five days upstream from Sunbury to Abingdon, hoisting cygnets out of the water to count, weigh and ring them. The Crown has claimed every unmarked mute swan on open water since the twelfth century, when the birds were banquet food. A medieval property right, now a census in fancy dress.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"doggettrace",n:"Doggett's Coat & Badge Race",a:"London Bridge",pc:"EC4R",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.0877,c:"history",s:"the oldest race on Earth",q:"Doggett's Coat & Badge Race London",w:"The oldest continuously held rowing race on Earth, sculled every year since 1715 over four miles and five furlongs from London Bridge to Cadogan Pier, under eleven bridges. The comic actor Thomas Doggett founded it to mark the 1714 accession of George I, the prize an orange livery coat and a silver arm-badge representing Liberty. Up to six apprentice watermen still chase it. The coat, not the cash, is the point.",city:"london"},{id:"widowsbun",n:"The Widow's Son (the Bun House)",a:"Bromley-by-Bow",pc:"E3",lat:51.523,lng:-0.015,c:"pub",s:"the buns no one may eat",q:"The Widow's Son (the Bun House) London",w:"A blackened net of hot cross buns hangs over the bar, one added every Good Friday by a Royal Navy sailor, the rest left to petrify. The legend has a widow keeping a bun for a sailor son lost at sea who never came home, the cottage found stuffed with them after her death. The Grade II* listed pub, known as the Bun House, took the name; a 1980s fire charred the collection but the cinders stayed in the net. Buns no one may eat.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cartmark",n:"Cart Marking, Guildhall Yard",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2V 7HH",lat:51.5158,lng:-0.0917,c:"history",s:"branded by the Carmen",q:"Cart Marking, Guildhall Yard London",w:"On a July morning a red-hot iron is pressed to a wooden block bolted to each vehicle in Guildhall Yard, branding it before the Lord Mayor and assembled robes. The Worshipful Company of Carmen, chartered in 1517, has licensed the City's carts this way for some five centuries, one of the earliest vehicle-registration schemes anywhere. Today vintage lorries and steam wagons queue for the brand. The Glovers' Company, naturally, supplies the gloves.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sheepdrive",n:"London Sheep Drive",a:"Southwark",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.094,c:"history",s:"freemen drive the flock",q:"London Sheep Drive London",w:"On a Sunday each September around a thousand Freemen of the City exercise an ancient right, driving a flock of fifty sheep across a closed Southwark Bridge, a celebrity usually at the head of the crook. Medieval Freemen could bring livestock over the toll bridge to Smithfield free of charge, undercutting everyone else. Revived in 2013 by the Worshipful Company of Woolmen as a charity day. Eccentric, to shut a bridge in one of the planet's largest cities for some sheep.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"pearlyharvest",n:"Pearly Kings & Queens Harvest Festival",a:"Trafalgar Square",pc:"WC2N 4JJ",lat:51.5089,lng:-0.1265,c:"history",s:"the costermongers' kingdom",q:"Pearly Kings & Queens Harvest Festival London",w:"On the first Sunday of October the Pearly Kings and Queens process in suits sewn with thousands of mother-of-pearl buttons for their harvest festival service. The tradition descends from the costermongers, the street traders who lined caps and trousers with pearl buttons, and from Henry Croft (1861–1930), a Somers Town orphan and road sweeper who covered an entire suit to raise money for charity. Smoked-pearl royalty of the gutter, all for charity.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"beatbounds",n:"Beating the Bounds, All Hallows",a:"Tower Hill",pc:"EC3R 5BJ",lat:51.5095,lng:-0.0786,c:"history",s:"beating the river's edge",q:"Beating the Bounds, All Hallows London",w:"On Ascension Day a party of choristers thrashes the parish boundary marks with willow wands, and because the southern edge runs midstream the beaters take to a boat to strike the river itself. The Rogationtide custom of walking and beating the bounds is medieval, fixing borders in an age before maps. Every third year it stages a mock battle with the Yeomen Warders at the mark shared with the Tower, recalling a real 1698 riot. The Thames, duly beaten.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Southeastern_View_of_All_Hallows-by-the-Tower.jpg/960px-Southeastern_View_of_All_Hallows-by-the-Tower.jpg"},{id:"trialpyx",n:"Trial of the Pyx, Goldsmiths' Hall",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2V 6BN",lat:51.5151,lng:-0.0972,c:"history",s:"the coins on trial",q:"Trial of the Pyx, Goldsmiths' Hall London",w:"A jury of the Worshipful Company of Goldsmiths, robed in scarlet, weighs and assays a sample of the year's coinage drawn from sealed boxes, the pyx, to confirm the Royal Mint has not cheated on weight or metal. First held in 1282 under Edward I, it is among the oldest judicial proceedings still running, presided over by the King's Remembrancer. Isaac Newton, as Master of the Mint, once stood in the dock here. Money, on trial.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/EH1286469_Goldsmiths_Hall_02.jpg/960px-EH1286469_Goldsmiths_Hall_02.jpg"},{id:"lobbboot",n:"John Lobb Bootmaker",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1A 1EF",lat:51.505,lng:-0.139,c:"history",s:"boots with a gold-nugget heel",q:"John Lobb Bootmaker London",w:"Rejected by London's bootmakers, the Cornish farm boy John Lobb sailed to the Australian goldfields and prospered crafting boots with hollow heels in which diggers hid their gold nuggets, a 1862 Prize Medal following. He returned, won the Prince of Wales's warrant in 1863, and the firm has shod royalty by hand since. The current St James's Street shop dates from after a 1944 bombing. The heel shape still nods, cheekily, to the nuggets.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"paxtoncheese",n:"Paxton & Whitfield",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1Y 6JE",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.138,c:"history",s:"Stilton's first home",q:"Paxton & Whitfield London",w:"Britain's grandest cheesemonger, a partnership recorded in 1797 with roots in a 1742 Aldwych cheese stall, and the first shop in the land to sell Stilton. Harry Paxton and Charles Whitfield joined Sam Cullum to chase wealthy custom toward Jermyn Street, where the firm still stands at number 93, having moved up the road in 1894. Cheesemonger to Queen Victoria from 1850, and royal warrant holder ever since. Ripeness, by appointment.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"florisperfume",n:"Floris of London",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1Y 6JH",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.1379,c:"history",s:"perfume since 1730",q:"Floris of London London",w:"Juan Famenias Floris, a Menorcan who arrived in 1730, opened on Jermyn Street as a barber and comb-maker before homesickness for Mediterranean scent turned him to perfume. The shop is still at number 89, still family-run into the ninth generation, the handwritten formula book begun by the founder still inscribed with every commission. Its first royal warrant came in 1820 as comb-maker to George IV; nineteen more now hang on the wall. Comb-maker to the Crown, improbably.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Floris_of_London_logo.webp"},{id:"truefitt",n:"Truefitt & Hill",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1A 1PH",lat:51.5055,lng:-0.1392,c:"history",s:"the world's oldest barber",q:"Truefitt & Hill London",w:"Founded in 1805, the year of Trafalgar, by William Francis Truefitt, who styled himself hairdresser to the Court, and certified by Guinness in April 2000 as the oldest barbershop in the world. The firm has groomed the British monarchy through nine consecutive reigns and lathered Wellington, Gladstone, Dickens and Churchill in its time. A hot towel with provenance.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"jjfox",n:"James J. Fox, Cigar Merchant",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1A 1ES",lat:51.5052,lng:-0.1389,c:"history",s:"Churchill's cigar ledger",q:"James J. Fox, Cigar Merchant London",w:"London's oldest cigar shop, its lineage running back to Robert Lewis, who began trading tobacco on St James's Street in 1787 before the Dublin house of Fox absorbed the name in 1992. Downstairs is a free museum holding Churchill's chair, the leather ledgers recording Oscar Wilde's purchases between 1892 and 1893, and the oldest surviving box of Havanas, brought over for the 1851 Great Exhibition. Buy a Cohiba, then go and read Wilde's account.",city:"london"},{id:"jsmithumb",n:"James Smith & Sons Umbrellas",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1A 1BL",lat:51.5165,lng:-0.129,c:"history",s:"umbrellas since 1830",q:"James Smith & Sons Umbrellas London",w:"A perfectly preserved Victorian shopfront of mahogany and plate glass, all gilt lettering and brollies, trading umbrellas, walking sticks and parasols since James Smith opened in Foubert's Place in 1830. The firm has occupied Hazelwood House on New Oxford Street since 1857, the interior little altered since the 1860s, Gladstone and Lord Curzon among the names that bought sticks here. London's oldest umbrella shop, in a city that supplies the demand.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"drharris",n:"D.R. Harris, Chemist",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1A 1HB",lat:51.5053,lng:-0.1389,c:"history",s:"the chemist since 1790",q:"D.R. Harris, Chemist London",w:"London's oldest pharmacy, opened around 1790 at 11 St James's Street by cousins Henry and Daniel Rotely Harris, the latter a founder of the Royal Pharmaceutical Society. Now a few doors along at number 29, amid its original mahogany fittings, it still mixes the famous morning-after Pick-Me-Up alongside shaving soaps and English-flower colognes. Royal warrant holder, and chemist to King Charles III. Just what the doctor ordered, since before the Revolution in France.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"henrypoole",n:"Henry Poole & Co",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1S 3PJ",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.1413,c:"history",s:"inventor of the dinner jacket",q:"Henry Poole & Co London",w:"The founding father of Savile Row, established in 1806 as a military tailor and the first house to open its doors on the Row itself, in 1846. In the 1860s Poole cut a short smoking jacket for the Prince of Wales to wear informally at Sandringham, the garment that became the dinner jacket and, after one James Potter carried it home, the American tuxedo. Black tie was born here, off-duty.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Henry_Poole_%26_Co_%281%29.JPG/960px-Henry_Poole_%26_Co_%281%29.JPG"},{id:"cheapsideplane",n:"The Cheapside Plane (Wood Street Tree)",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2V 6AE",lat:51.5145,lng:-0.0935,c:"green",s:"the City's most protected tree",q:"The Cheapside Plane (Wood Street Tree) London",w:"A single London plane towers over two tiny one-storey shops at the corner of Wood Street, the only Great Tree of London inside the Square Mile and quite possibly the City's oldest living thing at well over two centuries. It rises from the lost churchyard of St Peter Cheap, gone since the Great Fire of 1666, and reputedly the shops' leases forbid building higher than the present roofline so nothing crowds its crown. Wordsworth wrote it into a poem. Roots beat developers, occasionally.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Cheapside_1909.jpg/960px-Cheapside_1909.jpg"},{id:"berkeleyplane",n:"The Berkeley Square Planes",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1J 6BR",lat:51.5095,lng:-0.1465,c:"green",s:"Britain's most valuable tree",q:"The Berkeley Square Planes London",w:"Some thirty London planes, all planted in 1789, ring the square in one of the city's earliest and stateliest plantings. In 2008 one of them was assessed under the amenity-tree valuation system at £750,000, making it the most valuable tree in Britain, the figure built from age, size, health and the sheer number of people it shades. Two-and-a-half centuries on, they still outlast the Bentley showrooms beneath them. A nightingale, allegedly, once sang here.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/46_Berkeley_Square%2C_Mayfair%2C_January_2022_02.jpg/960px-46_Berkeley_Square%2C_Mayfair%2C_January_2022_02.jpg"},{id:"totteridgeyew",n:"The Totteridge Yew",a:"Totteridge",pc:"N20 8PR",lat:51.631,lng:-0.188,c:"green",s:"possibly 2,000 years old",q:"The Totteridge Yew London",w:"In the churchyard of St Andrew's stands a yew with a girth of some eight metres and an age guessed at around 2,000 years, which would make it the oldest living thing in London by a margin of millennia. Sir John Cullum recorded its measurements in 1677, and even then its hollow trunk was already vast; the church beside it, parts 13th-century, is an infant by comparison. One of the original Great Trees of London. It was old when the Romans came.",city:"london"},{id:"greenwichchest",n:"Greenwich Park's Sweet Chestnuts",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10 8QY",lat:51.4769,lng:0.001,c:"green",s:"the Stuart-era avenue",q:"Greenwich Park's Sweet Chestnuts London",w:"A dozen veteran sweet chestnuts survive from the formal avenues laid out for Charles II in the early 1660s, when he commissioned André Le Nôtre, Louis XIV's gardener at Versailles, to plan the park. Some now approach 360 years old, their fissured trunks corkscrewing skyward, though ink-spot disease and the oriental gall wasp are thinning the ranks. The grandest survivors of a French king's gardener working for an English one. Bring a flask; sit beneath history.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Greenwich_Park%2C_London%2C_from_the_observatory.jpg/960px-Greenwich_Park%2C_London%2C_from_the_observatory.jpg"},{id:"qeoak",n:"Queen Elizabeth's Oak",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10 8QY",lat:51.4775,lng:0.002,c:"green",s:"the oak Elizabeth danced around",q:"Queen Elizabeth's Oak London",w:"A fallen colossus lies behind its railings, seeded in the 12th century and brought down by a storm in June 1991, left exactly where it dropped. Henry VIII is said to have danced around it with Anne Boleyn; their daughter Elizabeth I supposedly picnicked in its already-hollow trunk, which later served as a lock-up for poachers caught in the royal park. A young oak planted alongside by Prince Philip in 1992 keeps the name alive. Eight centuries, recumbent.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Queen_Elizabeths_Oak_in_Greenwich_Park_%28geograph_2337949%29.jpg/960px-Queen_Elizabeths_Oak_in_Greenwich_Park_%28geograph_2337949%29.jpg"},{id:"hithergreenpk",n:"Hither Green Parakeet Roost",a:"Hither Green",pc:"SE6 1TP",lat:51.449,lng:-0.001,c:"green",s:"thousands in the poplars",q:"Hither Green Parakeet Roost London",w:"At dusk, thousands of ring-necked parakeets pour into the poplars wedged between Hither Green Cemetery and Lewisham Crematorium, one of the largest winter roosts in the capital. They come in flights of a few birds or a few hundred, screeching emerald against the grey, descendants of the feral population that has colonised London since the 1990s. Stand at the railings around four on a winter afternoon. The noise is something to behold.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stjamespelican",n:"The Pelicans of St James's Park",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1A 2BJ",lat:51.5025,lng:-0.134,c:"green",s:"the Tsar's gift, fed at half-two",q:"The Pelicans of St James's Park London",w:"Pelicans have patrolled the lake here since 1664, when the Russian ambassador presented a pair of Dalmatian pelicans to Charles II, and more than forty have lived in the park since. They are fed fresh fish, roach and trout and the occasional herring, daily between half-past two and three, when keepers appear by the lake's east end. One was once filmed swallowing a pigeon whole. The royal absurdity has never lapsed.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/St_James%27s_Park_Lake_%E2%80%93_East_from_the_Blue_Bridge_-_2012-10-06.jpg/960px-St_James%27s_Park_Lake_%E2%80%93_East_from_the_Blue_Bridge_-_2012-10-06.jpg"},{id:"regentheron",n:"The Heronry, Regent's Park",a:"Regent's Park",pc:"NW1 4NR",lat:51.531,lng:-0.156,c:"green",s:"the herons of the boating lake",q:"The Heronry, Regent's Park London",w:"On an island in the boating lake nests Europe's most central heronry, barely a mile from Oxford Street, with around twenty pairs of grey herons raising young each spring. Herons first colonised the park in 1968, decamping to the island after the great storm of 1987 felled their original tree. They have grown so blasé about people that they squabble with the ducks for thrown bread by the water's edge. Prehistoric silhouettes, cadging crumbs.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Regent%27s_Park_bandstand.jpg/960px-Regent%27s_Park_bandstand.jpg"},{id:"chelseafig",n:"The Chelsea Bridge Fig",a:"Chelsea",pc:"SW1W",lat:51.484,lng:-0.149,c:"green",s:"the fig that seeded itself",q:"The Chelsea Bridge Fig London",w:"A fig tree clings to the wall where Chelsea Bridge meets the Embankment, a chance seedling unlike any cultivated variety, propagated and now sold to gardeners as 'The Chelsea Fig'. Each summer its heavy crop is plundered by parakeets and human scrumpers alike, a free fruit bowl on the Thames bank, and it stood in as a hideout in William Boyd's novel Ordinary Thunderstorms. One tree, endlessly cloned. Free figs, if the parakeets leave any.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Chelsea_Bridge%2C_London.jpg/960px-Chelsea_Bridge%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"mailrail",n:"Mail Rail, The Postal Museum",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"WC1X 0DL",lat:51.524,lng:-0.111,c:"history",s:"the post's secret railway",q:"Mail Rail, The Postal Museum London",w:"Six and a half miles of narrow-gauge tunnel beneath the city, the Post Office Railway carried letters from Paddington to Whitechapel from 1927 until 2003, the world's first driverless electric railway, ferrying up to four million items a day, 22 hours a day, with no driver aboard. Changing logistics killed it; it reopened in 2017 as a ride beneath the Postal Museum, on shrunken cars built for sacks, not passengers. Mind your knees.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Postal_Museum_%28London%29_Air_Mail_Post_Box.jpg/960px-Postal_Museum_%28London%29_Air_Mail_Post_Box.jpg"},{id:"britmusstn",n:"British Museum Disused Station",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC1V 6LR",lat:51.518,lng:-0.123,c:"history",s:"the ghost with a curse",q:"British Museum Disused Station London",w:"A Central line station that opened in 1900 and closed on 25 September 1933 when Holborn's new platforms made it redundant, its surface building long demolished. Legend gave it the ghost of an Egyptian princess, Amen-Ra, screaming in the tunnels near the museum's mummies, the tale so persistent a paper offered a reward to anyone who would spend the night. The 1935 film Bulldog Jack borrowed the curse. Trains rumble past a platform that isn't there.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/British_Museum_tube_station_2.jpg/960px-British_Museum_tube_station_2.jpg"},{id:"bromptonstn",n:"Brompton Road Disused Station",a:"Knightsbridge",pc:"SW7 1LX",lat:51.497,lng:-0.17,c:"history",s:"the Blitz command bunker",q:"Brompton Road Disused Station London",w:"A Piccadilly line station opened in 1906 and shut on 30 July 1934, killed by its closeness to a modernised Knightsbridge. On the eve of war the War Office bought the building and lift shafts, and through the Blitz it served as the Royal Artillery's anti-aircraft operations room for central London, plotting the guns from deep below Knightsbridge. The MoD sold it to a Ukrainian oligarch in 2014. From ticket hall to gun-control bunker to private lot.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Brompton_Road_Sept_2000.jpg"},{id:"skentishstn",n:"South Kentish Town Disused Station",a:"Kentish Town",pc:"NW5 2AA",lat:51.55,lng:-0.141,c:"history",s:"the station that never was",q:"South Kentish Town Disused Station London",w:"Opened in 1907 and abruptly closed in June 1924 during a power strike at Lots Road, never to reopen, this Northern line ghost lurks between Camden Town and Kentish Town. John Betjeman immortalised it in a 1951 radio piece imagining a passenger trapped on its platform after stepping off by mistake, spun from a real incident when a train did once halt and open its doors here, though nobody was actually stranded. The oxblood frontage survives above ground. Closed by accident, more or less.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Kentish_Town_stn_building.JPG/960px-Kentish_Town_stn_building.JPG"},{id:"belsizeshelter",n:"Belsize Park Deep-Level Shelter",a:"Belsize Park",pc:"NW3 2BX",lat:51.55,lng:-0.164,c:"history",s:"8,000 beneath the Northern line",q:"Belsize Park Deep-Level Shelter London",w:"Behind a squat concrete rotunda lie twin tunnels, each 1,200 feet long and split into two decks, bored beneath the Northern line to hold 8,000 people. One of eight deep-level shelters begun in 1940 and completed in 1942, it opened to the public in 1944 against the V-weapons. Its sister at Goodge Street housed Eisenhower's command staff. Built for a war that was nearly over by the time the doors opened.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Belsize_Park_stn_building.JPG/960px-Belsize_Park_stn_building.JPG"},{id:"stockwellshelter",n:"Stockwell Deep-Level Shelter",a:"Stockwell",pc:"SW9",lat:51.472,lng:-0.123,c:"history",s:"the painted war memorial",q:"Stockwell Deep-Level Shelter London",w:"The blunt concrete drum guarding one entrance to Stockwell's deep-level shelter wears a war-memorial mural painted between 1999 and 2001 by Brian Barnes and Myra Harris, with local schoolchildren's ideas worked in. Around its wall march Stockwell's own: the SOE agent Violette Szabo, Roger Moore, Van Gogh who lodged nearby in the 1870s, and the Empire Windrush. Beneath it, an 8,000-person shelter completed in 1942. A pillbox turned village memorial.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"towersubway",n:"Tower Subway",a:"Tower Hill",pc:"EC3N 4DT",lat:51.509,lng:-0.078,c:"history",s:"the world's first tube tunnel",q:"Tower Subway London",w:"Dug in 1869 and opened in 1870, this quarter-mile tube under the Thames from Tower Hill was the world's first, bored with the Barlow-Greathead shield that made every Underground line after it possible. A cable-hauled wooden carriage just seven feet wide ran for months until the system failed; reborn as a foot tunnel with a ha'penny toll, it drew 20,000 walkers a week before Tower Bridge stole them in 1894. It carries telecom cables now. The grandfather of the Tube, demoted to a duct.",city:"london"},{id:"cablestmural",n:"The Cable Street Mural",a:"Shadwell",pc:"E1 0BL",lat:51.5103,lng:-0.057,c:"streetart",s:"they shall not pass",q:"The Cable Street Mural London",w:"On the flank of the old St George's Town Hall, a riot in oils: police charging, barricades overturned, the East End refusing Mosley's Blackshirts on 4 October 1936. Begun in 1979 and finally unveiled in May 1983 by Dave Binnington, Ray Walker, Paul Butler and Desmond Rochfort, it has been firebombed and defaced and lovingly restored. They did not pass.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Cable_Street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_253565.jpg"},{id:"putneydebates",n:"The Putney Debates, St Mary's",a:"Putney",pc:"SW15 1SN",lat:51.4655,lng:-0.2155,c:"history",s:"the poorest he in England",q:"The Putney Debates, St Mary's London",w:"Inside this riverside church, from 28 October 1647, the New Model Army argued the future of England before a Cromwell who had just won a war. Here Colonel Thomas Rainsborough insisted the poorest he that is in England hath a life to live as the greatest he, pressing the Levellers' Agreement of the People. The franchise lost; the sentence never died.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/St_Mary%27s%2C_Putney_01.JPG/960px-St_Mary%27s%2C_Putney_01.JPG"},{id:"kenningtonchartist",n:"Kennington Park (Chartist Meeting)",a:"Kennington",pc:"SE11",lat:51.4855,lng:-0.106,c:"history",s:"the monster meeting",q:"Kennington Park (Chartist Meeting) London",w:"On 10 April 1848 perhaps fifty thousand Chartists massed on this common to carry their third great petition to Parliament, the year revolution swept Europe. Feargus O'Connor claimed nearly six million signatures; the Commons counted under two million, padded with obvious forgeries — ‘Victoria Rex’, the Duke of Wellington, even ‘Mr Punch’ and ‘No Cheese’. The procession was banned, the crowd dispersed, and Chartism quietly began to die.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"smithtyler",n:"Smithfield (where Wat Tyler fell)",a:"Smithfield",pc:"EC1A",lat:51.519,lng:-0.101,c:"history",s:"where Wat Tyler fell",q:"Smithfield (where Wat Tyler fell) London",w:"Here, on 15 June 1381, the Peasants' Revolt was decapitated in a single afternoon. Confronting the fourteen-year-old Richard II, Wat Tyler was stabbed by Lord Mayor William Walworth and finished off by a royal squire, then dragged from St Bartholomew's to be beheaded in the open ground. A livestock market for centuries; the bloodletting was not always bovine.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"southafricahouse",n:"South Africa House (Non-Stop Picket)",a:"Trafalgar Square",pc:"WC2N 5DP",lat:51.5078,lng:-0.128,c:"history",s:"the picket that never slept",q:"South Africa House (Non-Stop Picket) London",w:"For 1,408 days and nights, from 19 April 1986 until well after Mandela walked free in February 1990, the City of London Anti-Apartheid Group kept a picket going round the clock outside the South African embassy. Norma Kitson's idea, it never once stood down through four Trafalgar Square winters. The protest that refused to sleep.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"intlbrigades",n:"International Brigades Memorial",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1",lat:51.504,lng:-0.118,c:"history",s:"London's Spanish war dead",q:"International Brigades Memorial London",w:"Five bronze figures hoist a sixth, wounded and falling, on Ian Walters's four-and-a-half-metre monument unveiled by Michael Foot on 5 October 1985. It honours the 526 who died of the more than two thousand from Britain and Ireland who went to fight Franco. No pasaran, cast in metal above the river.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vauxhallgardens",n:"Vauxhall Pleasure Gardens",a:"Vauxhall",pc:"SE11 5HL",lat:51.486,lng:-0.123,c:"green",s:"London's first night out",q:"Vauxhall Pleasure Gardens London",w:"London's great pleasure ground for nearly two centuries, laid out as New Spring Gardens by 1661 and transformed from the 1720s by Jonathan Tyers into a paid wonderland of supper boxes, Handel concerts and thousands of oil lamps strung through the trees. Hogarth and Canaletto drank here; so did half of fashionable England. Bankrupt and shuttered in 1859 — the lamps long out, the name reclaimed by a small park.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cremornegates",n:"Cremorne Gardens & its Gates",a:"Chelsea",pc:"SW10 0QJ",lat:51.4798,lng:-0.1783,c:"green",s:"the maddest place after ten",q:"Cremorne Gardens & its Gates London",w:"Once the entrance to twelve riotous acres of dancing platforms, balloon ascents and fireworks that drew Whistler and scandalised the vestry, these wrought-iron gates are nearly all that survives. Cremorne ran from 1845 until its licence lapsed on 4 October 1877, killed by complaints of vice and rowdiness. The gates were restored and reset here in 2006 — a grand doorway to almost nothing.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Cremorne_The_Dancing_Platform_at_Cremorne_Gardens_by_Phoebus_Levin_1864.jpg/960px-Cremorne_The_Dancing_Platform_at_Cremorne_Gardens_by_Phoebus_Levin_1864.jpg"},{id:"ranelaghgardens",n:"Ranelagh Gardens",a:"Chelsea",pc:"SW3 4SR",lat:51.4855,lng:-0.159,c:"green",s:"the lost Rotunda's lawns",q:"Ranelagh Gardens London",w:"The lawns of the Royal Hospital's quietest garden once held a colossal wooden Rotunda, 150 feet across with a hundred supper boxes, where in 1764 an eight-year-old Mozart played for charity. Ranelagh out-glittered Vauxhall as the smart set's resort from 1742 until it closed in 1803; the Rotunda came down two years later. Today the Chelsea Flower Show pitches its tents over the ghost.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"egyptianhall",n:"Egyptian Hall Site, Piccadilly",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1J",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.1395,c:"history",s:"England's home of mystery",q:"Egyptian Hall Site, Piccadilly London",w:"Behind a pharaonic facade built for William Bullock in 1812 to house Cook's South Seas curiosities, this Piccadilly oddity became England's Home of Mystery. From 1873 the conjurors Maskelyne and Cooke ran a thirty-one-year season here, debunking fraudulent spiritualists between illusions. The last show was 5 January 1905; the temple fell that year for offices.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Egyptian_Hall%2C_Piccadilly_-_Shepherd%2C_Metropolitan_Improvements_%281828%29%2C_p295.jpg/960px-Egyptian_Hall%2C_Piccadilly_-_Shepherd%2C_Metropolitan_Improvements_%281828%29%2C_p295.jpg"},{id:"freemasonsfa",n:"Freemasons' Tavern (the FA's birthplace)",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2B 5DA",lat:51.5159,lng:-0.1224,c:"history",s:"where football was born",q:"Freemasons' Tavern (the FA's birthplace) London",w:"On the Monday evening of 26 October 1863, representatives of eleven clubs met at the Freemasons' Tavern on Great Queen Street and founded the Football Association, the solicitor Ebenezer Cobb Morley its first secretary. The split over hacking and handling soon birthed rugby as a separate code. The whole world's game, decided over drinks near Holborn.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"highburysq",n:"Highbury Square (Arsenal Stadium)",a:"Highbury",pc:"N5 1BU",lat:51.5575,lng:-0.103,c:"stadium",s:"the pitch is now a garden",q:"Highbury Square (Arsenal Stadium) London",w:"Arsenal played here from 1913 until 2006, and rather than flatten the ground they kept it: the listed Art Deco East and West Stands are now flats, some six hundred and fifty of them. The hallowed turf where Henry once ran is a landscaped communal garden, mown to the exact dimensions of the old pitch. You can buy a view of where the North Bank roared.",city:"london"},{id:"hernehillvelo",n:"Herne Hill Velodrome",a:"Herne Hill",pc:"SE24 9HE",lat:51.4548,lng:-0.0915,c:"stadium",s:"the last 1948 Olympic venue",q:"Herne Hill Velodrome London",w:"The last venue of the 1948 London Olympics still standing and still in use, this banked outdoor track opened in 1891 and hosted the Games' sprint, kilometre, tandem and pursuit. Bradley Wiggins learned to race on its boards as a boy. The spiritual home of British cycling, surviving where Empire Stadium did not.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Herne_Hill_Junction.jpg/960px-Herne_Hill_Junction.jpg"},{id:"lordsrealtennis",n:"Lord's Real Tennis Court",a:"St John's Wood",pc:"NW8 8QN",lat:51.5295,lng:-0.1727,c:"stadium",s:"the game before lawn tennis",q:"Lord's Real Tennis Court London",w:"Real tennis has been struck off these walls at Lord's since 1838, played with lopsided wooden racquets and a hard hand-sewn ball on a chamber laid out like a medieval courtyard. The MCC's 1875 Laws of Lawn Tennis, drafted with this court's dimensions in mind, gave Wimbledon its template. The original was razed in 1898 for the Mound Stand; this one dates from 1900.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Lords-Cricket-Ground-Pavilion-06-08-2017.jpg/960px-Lords-Cricket-Ground-Pavilion-06-08-2017.jpg"},{id:"ovalgasholder",n:"The Oval Gasholders",a:"Kennington",pc:"SE11 5SS",lat:51.484,lng:-0.115,c:"history",s:"cricket's iron backdrop",q:"The Oval Gasholders London",w:"Cricket's most televised piece of Victorian engineering looms over the gasworks end, where Gasholder No. 1 rose in 1877–79 as the largest in the world and an early triumph of wrought-iron framing. It was watching over the very first Test on English soil, England against Australia, in September 1880. Grade II listed in 2016 — the skyline England plays beneath.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/The_Kia_Oval_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4645561.jpg/960px-The_Kia_Oval_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4645561.jpg"},{id:"worpleroad",n:"All England Club Original Site, Worple Road",a:"Wimbledon",pc:"SW19",lat:51.418,lng:-0.208,c:"stadium",s:"Wimbledon before Wimbledon",q:"All England Club Original Site, Worple Road London",w:"The Championships were born not at Church Road but here, on a ground off Worple Road, where in July 1877 twenty-two gentlemen contested the first Wimbledon and Spencer Gore took the title. The club had added lawn tennis to its croquet in 1875 and never looked back; outgrowing the site, it decamped to its present home in 1922 before King George V and Queen Mary. The cradle, now a school's playing field.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"maryonpark",n:"Maryon Park (where Blow-Up was shot)",a:"Charlton",pc:"SE7 8DJ",lat:51.49,lng:0.044,c:"film",s:"where Blow-Up was shot",q:"Maryon Park (where Blow-Up was shot) London",w:"Antonioni shot Blow-Up here in 1966, having the council green the grass and black the paths to satisfy his eye, then planting the bushes that hid Thomas's photographed corpse. The tennis court where the mime troupe play an invisible match against an invisible ball survives, unchanged, halfway up the hill. A small, steep Charlton park standing in for the whole of swinging London.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Maryon_Park%2C_Charlton%2C_South_East_London.jpg/960px-Maryon_Park%2C_Charlton%2C_South_East_London.jpg"},{id:"economistplaza",n:"The Economist Plaza",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1A 1HG",lat:51.506,lng:-0.139,c:"brutalism",s:"the Smithsons' Sixties plaza",q:"The Economist Plaza London",w:"Alison and Peter Smithson's masterwork of 1959-64: three Portland-stone towers of five, eight and fifteen storeys rising from a raised pedestrian plaza reached by steps and a ramp. It broke the podium-and-slab orthodoxy of the post-war tall building and earned a Grade II* listing as Brutalism tempered by manners. Cleverest civic gesture in St James's.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"reptonbox",n:"Repton Boxing Club",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"E2 6EH",lat:51.524,lng:-0.066,c:"film",s:"the three-card-brag game",q:"Repton Boxing Club London",w:"The East End's most storied amateur gym, founded by Repton public school in 1884 to box the slum boys straight, and where the Kray twins trained as youths off nearby Vallance Road. Guy Ritchie staged the catastrophic three-card-brag game of Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels (1998) in these very rooms. The ring is real; the card sharps were not.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"royaloakcr",n:"The Royal Oak, Columbia Road",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"E2 7RG",lat:51.5295,lng:-0.07,c:"film",s:"Samoan Jo's and the Krays",q:"The Royal Oak, Columbia Road London",w:"A Grade II-listed corner pub on Columbia Road with a 1923 interior intact enough to time-travel through, which is precisely what Goodnight Sweetheart had it do. Both Kray films used it, and Guy Ritchie made it Samoan Joe's in Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels. Sunday roast where the Krays once drank.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Columbia_Road_Flower_Market_2022.jpg/960px-Columbia_Road_Flower_Market_2022.jpg"},{id:"parkstborough",n:"15 Park Street, Borough",a:"Borough",pc:"SE1 9AB",lat:51.5055,lng:-0.0905,c:"film",s:"the gang's Borough hideout",q:"15 Park Street, Borough London",w:"This unassuming address opposite Borough Market was Eddy's flat in Lock, Stock and Two Smoking Barrels (1998), with Dog's place next door at number 13 in the same frame. Guy Ritchie chose the street precisely because it had escaped development and still looked like old London. Now flanked by artisan coffee; the gang would not approve.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"boldcafe",n:"Bold Cafe (Fleabag's Cafe)",a:"Dartmouth Park",pc:"NW5 1SS",lat:51.564,lng:-0.143,c:"film",s:"the guinea-pig cafe",q:"Bold Cafe (Fleabag's Cafe) London",w:"The greasy spoon Phoebe Waller-Bridge made famous as Fleabag's failing, guinea-pig-themed café was the Village Café at 20 York Rise, dressed by the BBC for the part. The location filming ranged across Dartmouth Park and Kentish Town; this corner was its beating, rodent-haunted heart. Now serving Turkish food, the guinea pigs long evicted.",city:"london"},{id:"westminster28",n:"Westminster Bridge (the Empty City)",a:"Westminster",pc:"SW1A 2JH",lat:51.5008,lng:-0.1246,c:"film",s:"the empty city",q:"Westminster Bridge (the Empty City) London",w:"Cillian Murphy's Jim wanders an utterly deserted Westminster Bridge in the opening of Danny Boyle's 28 Days Later (2002), shot on Sunday dawns in windows as short as ten minutes, with police holding the traffic and six cameras rolling so he need only walk once. The most haunting image in British horror, achieved on an eight-million-dollar budget. Empty London, no CGI.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"benthamicon",n:"Jeremy Bentham's Auto-Icon",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1H 0AY",lat:51.5247,lng:-0.132,c:"museum",s:"present but not voting",q:"Jeremy Bentham's Auto-Icon London",w:"The utilitarian philosopher had his skeleton padded out, dressed in his own clothes and topped with a wax head, per his 1832 will, then sat upright in a cabinet for posterity. The macabre real head, mummified over sulphuric acid, is now stored safely away. Wheeled to UCL council twice and minuted, the legend runs, as present but not voting. Since 2020 he holds court in the Student Centre on Gordon Square.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"faradaymuseum",n:"The Faraday Museum, Royal Institution",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1S 4BS",lat:51.5089,lng:-0.1419,c:"museum",s:"where electricity was tamed",q:"The Faraday Museum, Royal Institution London",w:"In this dim Mayfair basement laboratory, kept little altered since the 1850s, Michael Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction in August 1831 and built the first transformer, work that underwrites every power station since. The original apparatus sits in the cases above, in the Royal Institution where he also founded the Christmas Lectures. Where electricity stopped being a parlour trick.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"grantmuseum",n:"The Grant Museum of Zoology",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1E 6DE",lat:51.5243,lng:-0.134,c:"museum",s:"the jar of moles",q:"The Grant Museum of Zoology London",w:"One of the last university zoology museums in London, founded in 1828 by Robert Grant, with 100,000 specimens crammed into one Bloomsbury room: a quagga skeleton, dodo bones, and the cult favourite, a single jar packed with eighteen whole preserved moles. The 2013 Micrarium backlights 2,300 microscope slides in a glowing cave. A Victorian cabinet of curiosities that never stopped being one.",city:"london",oh:"Tu-Fr 13:00-17:00; Sa 11:00-17:00; Su,Mo,PH off",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Grant_Museum_of_Zoology_%289201048849%29.jpg/960px-Grant_Museum_of_Zoology_%289201048849%29.jpg"},{id:"faradaymemorial",n:"The Michael Faraday Memorial",a:"Elephant & Castle",pc:"SE1",lat:51.4948,lng:-0.1,c:"oddity",s:"the steel box of power",q:"The Michael Faraday Memorial London",w:"Rodney Gordon's mirror-polished stainless-steel box of 1961 marks the spot near Faraday's Newington Butts birthplace, and houses, with poetic neatness, an Underground electrical substation feeding the Northern and Bakerloo lines. He wanted it glazed so you could watch the transformers; fear of vandals sealed it in steel instead. Grade II-listed, and almost nobody knows why it's there.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Faraday_Memorial_SE1_%2849052991483%29.jpg/960px-Faraday_Memorial_SE1_%2849052991483%29.jpg"},{id:"nightingalemus",n:"The Florence Nightingale Museum",a:"Lambeth",pc:"SE1 7EW",lat:51.4988,lng:-0.1186,c:"museum",s:"the lady and her lamp",q:"The Florence Nightingale Museum London",w:"Tucked into St Thomas' Hospital, where Florence Nightingale founded her pioneering nurses' training school in 1860, the museum holds her personal effects and, crucially, the lamp itself, a large Turkish fanoos lantern rather than the dainty oil lamp of legend. From Scutari's wards to lifelong sanitary reform, the real woman is sharper than the saint. The lady, and her surprisingly large lamp.",city:"london",oh:"Tu-Su 10:00-17:00; \"Last Th of month close at 20:00\"",wq:"a"},{id:"bowbells",n:"St Mary-le-Bow & the Bow Bells",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2V 6AU",lat:51.5138,lng:-0.0935,c:"music",s:"born within the sound",q:"St Mary-le-Bow & the Bow Bells London",w:"Wren's Cheapside masterpiece, rebuilt 1670-80 after the Great Fire and crowned by his tallest City spire, holds the twelve Bow Bells whose earshot, by ancient custom, defines a true Cockney. The bells called the curfew from 1469 and once carried for miles over a lower city. Be born within their sound, or be no Cockney at all.",city:"london",oh:"We 07:30-18:00; Th 07:30-18:30; Fr 07:30-16:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/St_Mary_le_Bow.jpg/960px-St_Mary_le_Bow.jpg"},{id:"singinglift",n:"The Singing Lift (Royal Festival Hall)",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1 8XX",lat:51.505,lng:-0.116,c:"oddity",s:"the elevator that sings",q:"The Singing Lift (Royal Festival Hall) London",w:"Martin Creed's Work No. 409 (2005) turns a lift in the Royal Festival Hall into an instrument: a choir's four-part voices climb the scale as you ascend, reaching A sharp at the top floor and falling to bottom E as you return to the ground. The 2001 Turner Prize winner's quietest, most charming gag. Conceptual art you can ride.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Royal_Festival_Hall%2C_Belvedere_Road_%281%29.jpg"},{id:"appearingrooms",n:"Appearing Rooms (Southbank)",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1 8XX",lat:51.506,lng:-0.115,c:"oddity",s:"the fountain that traps you",q:"Appearing Rooms (Southbank) London",w:"Jeppe Hein's summer waterwork, on the Queen Elizabeth Hall terrace since 2007, raises walls of water more than two metres high in a shifting square grid, then drops them at random, making and unmaking rooms around whoever's foolish enough to step in. Children, and the occasional drenched adult, gamble on the timing. A maze with no fixed walls, and no mercy.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wandlebell",n:"The Wandle Tide Bell",a:"Wandsworth",pc:"SW18",lat:51.467,lng:-0.193,c:"oddity",s:"rung by the tides",q:"The Wandle Tide Bell London",w:"Where the Wandle splits into a delta and joins the Thames at the Causeway, an old church bell hangs beneath a stone arch inscribed Salmon Swan Otter Eel Heron, and bears its own legend: I am rung by the tides. A turbine once let the flooding tide strike it, until river debris and a raised weir silenced the mechanism. The bell still hangs, the tide still comes; the two no longer speak.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bobola",n:"St Andrew Bobola Polish Church",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.5007,lng:-0.2283,c:"diaspora",s:"émigré canon",q:"St Andrew Bobola Polish Church London",w:"The garrison church of the post-war emigracja, five minutes from your door. Inside: a Katyń memorial chapel, squadron standards, and walls of plaques to soldiers who could never go home. Bought by Polish veterans in 1961 and made a shrine to the Second Republic in exile.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tvc",n:"BBC Television Centre",a:"White City",pc:"W12",lat:51.5099,lng:-0.2261,c:"pop",s:"local lore",q:"BBC Television Centre London",w:"The doughnut — the world's first purpose-built TV centre (1960), its circular plan allegedly sketched on the back of an envelope in a pub. Bowie did Top of the Pops here, Python ran riot, Blue Peter buried time capsules in the garden. The gilded Helios still holds the courtyard.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/At_London_2024_415.jpg/960px-At_London_2024_415.jpg"},{id:"limegrove",n:"Lime Grove Studios site",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.5036,lng:-0.2271,c:"pop",s:"on your doorstep",q:"Lime Grove Studios plaque Shepherd's Bush",w:"A film factory stood on this street from 1915 to 1993. Hitchcock shot The 39 Steps here for Gaumont-British; the BBC later taped the very first Doctor Who episode here in 1963. Demolished for housing — a plaque on the estate is all that remains of a street that invented British screen history.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Hammersmith_Public_Baths.jpg/960px-Hammersmith_Public_Baths.jpg"},{id:"whitecity",n:"White City Stadium site",a:"White City",pc:"W12",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.2244,c:"history",s:"Olympic deep cut",q:"White City Stadium 1908 marathon finish line",w:"The 1908 Olympics happened here — and fixed the marathon at 26.219 miles forever, stretched so the race could start under Windsor Castle's nursery windows and finish at the Royal Box. Dorando Pietri collapsed and was carried over the line. The finish line is marked in the pavement at White City Place.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/White_City_stn_entrance2.JPG/960px-White_City_stn_entrance2.JPG"},{id:"empire",n:"Shepherd's Bush Empire",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.5031,lng:-0.2243,c:"music",s:"venue lore",q:"O2 Shepherd's Bush Empire",w:"A 1903 Frank Matcham music hall that became one of London's great mid-size rooms. The Rolling Stones played a secret show here in '99; Bowie, Dylan and basically everyone else passed through. The dome on top once held a BBC transmitter.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Shepherd%27s_Bush_Empire.jpg/960px-Shepherd%27s_Bush_Empire.jpg"},{id:"scrubs",n:"Wormwood Scrubs meadow",a:"East Acton",pc:"W12",lat:51.5183,lng:-0.236,c:"green",s:"r/london deep cut",q:"Wormwood Scrubs Park",w:"A vast skylark meadow hiding behind the prison — one of inner London's last breeding grounds. MI5 actually worked out of the Scrubs in WWII, registry files and all. Eerie, enormous, and brilliant at dusk.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sindercombe",n:"Sindercombe's ambush site",a:"Goldhawk Road",pc:"W12",lat:51.503,lng:-0.229,c:"oddity",s:"Wikipedia rabbit hole",q:"Goldhawk Road Shepherd's Bush",w:"In 1657 Roundhead Miles Sindercombe rented a cottage on Goldhawk Road and built a frame of mounted muskets to assassinate Oliver Cromwell as his coach passed to Hampton Court. The plot was betrayed; Sindercombe poisoned himself in the Tower the night before execution. Your local high street, briefly the centre of a regicide plot.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mrfalafel",n:"Mr Falafel",a:"Shepherd's Bush Market",pc:"W12",lat:51.5054,lng:-0.2266,c:"food",s:"pilgrim queues",q:"Mr Falafel Shepherd's Bush Market",w:"A Palestinian counter inside the market that food writers keep calling the best falafel wrap in Britain. Everything made to order, lined with pickles and proper tahini. The queue is the lore — locals, cabbies and critics in one line.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"esarn",n:"Esarn Kheaw",a:"Uxbridge Road",pc:"W12",lat:51.5066,lng:-0.233,c:"food",s:"since 1992",q:"Esarn Kheaw Uxbridge Road",w:"A green-walled family time capsule serving north-eastern Isaan Thai since 1992 — som tam that actually scorches, sticky rice, grilled everything. One of the first kitchens in London to refuse to anglicise the heat. Critics' lists come and go; the room never changes.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 12:00-15:00, 18:00-22:30; Sa 18:00-22:30",wq:"a"},{id:"tatra",n:"Tatra",a:"Goldhawk Road",pc:"W12",lat:51.5037,lng:-0.2262,c:"diaspora",s:"the Green's Polish table",q:"Tatra Restaurant Goldhawk Road",w:"The Polish stalwart by the Green since 2008 — schabowy, pierogi, roast duck with red cabbage, and a serious vodka shelf. It carries the torch of the much-missed Patio a few doors down, where owner Ewa, a former opera singer, poured vodka on the house for two decades.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"t4",n:"T4 — Westfield",a:"Westfield London",pc:"W12",lat:51.5074,lng:-0.221,c:"boba",s:"home-zone fix",q:"T4 Tea For U Westfield London",w:"The reliable Taiwanese chain inside Westfield — proper brewed-tea base rather than syrup water. The closest legit boba to your flat when the Soho specialists are forty minutes away.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Westfield_London_shopping_area_in_London_Borough_of_Hammersmith_and_Fulham%2C_spring_2013_%2811%29.jpg/960px-Westfield_London_shopping_area_in_London_Borough_of_Hammersmith_and_Fulham%2C_spring_2013_%2811%29.jpg"},{id:"posk",n:"POSK — Polish Social & Cultural Centre",a:"Hammersmith",pc:"W6",lat:51.4928,lng:-0.2354,c:"diaspora",s:"émigré canon",q:"POSK Polish Social and Cultural Centre London",w:"The largest Polish cultural centre outside Poland, built with émigré donations and opened in 1974. Inside: a 250,000-volume Polish library, a theatre, a jazz club in the basement, and Łowiczanka upstairs for bigos and żurek. The whole building is the institutional memory of London's Polonia.",city:"london"},{id:"dove",n:"The Dove, Upper Mall",a:"Hammersmith",pc:"W6",lat:51.4904,lng:-0.2356,c:"food",s:"riverside canon",q:"The Dove pub Upper Mall Hammersmith",w:"A 1730s riverside pub holding the Guinness record for the smallest bar room in Britain. 'Rule, Britannia!' was reputedly composed upstairs by James Thomson; Graham Greene and Hemingway drank here. The terrace at high tide is the best seat on the Thames.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kelmscott",n:"Kelmscott House",a:"Hammersmith",pc:"W6",lat:51.4901,lng:-0.2366,c:"history",s:"Morris trail",q:"Kelmscott House Hammersmith",w:"William Morris's riverside home for the last 18 years of his life — the coach house hosted his Hammersmith Socialist League lectures, with Shaw and Yeats in the audience. An early electric telegraph was demonstrated across this garden in 1816, decades before Morse. Tiny museum, big history.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Kelmscott_House.JPG/960px-Kelmscott_House.JPG"},{id:"hbridge",n:"Hammersmith Bridge",a:"Hammersmith",pc:"W6",lat:51.4876,lng:-0.2298,c:"green",s:"golden hour",q:"Hammersmith Bridge",w:"Bazalgette's 1887 wedding-cake suspension bridge, now closed to cars and quietly returned to walkers. The IRA tried to blow it up three times; in 1939 a passer-by threw a smoking suitcase bomb into the river and got an OBE. Best free sunset platform in west London.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Hammersmith_Bridge_2008_06_19.jpg/960px-Hammersmith_Bridge_2008_06_19.jpg"},{id:"rivercafe",n:"The River Café",a:"Thames Wharf",pc:"W6",lat:51.4849,lng:-0.2218,c:"food",s:"chef factory",q:"The River Cafe London",w:"Ruth Rogers and Rose Gray rewired British-Italian cooking from a converted oil depot in 1987. Jamie Oliver was discovered here by a TV crew in '97; April Bloomfield, Hugh Fearnley-Whittingstall and half of modern London cooking passed through the kitchen. The wood oven that launched a thousand chefs.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cottage",n:"Craven Cottage & Johnny Haynes statue",a:"Fulham",pc:"SW6",lat:51.4749,lng:-0.2217,c:"pop",s:"your cathedral",q:"Craven Cottage Fulham",w:"The 1905 Cottage Pavilion and Archibald Leitch stand are listed — the oldest football ground in London still doing its job. The Haynes statue stands where the Maestro played 658 games; the ghost-site of Al Fayed's Michael Jackson statue remains the strangest chapter in club lore.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Craven_Cottage_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7559163.jpg"},{id:"bishops",n:"Bishop's Park & Fulham Palace",a:"Fulham",pc:"SW6",lat:51.4716,lng:-0.219,c:"green",s:"pre-match ritual",q:"Fulham Palace",w:"The riverside plane-tree avenue ends at the medieval palace of the Bishops of London — their country seat for over a thousand years. Free museum, Tudor courtyard, walled garden with ancient oaks. The moat was the longest in England until they filled it in.",city:"london"},{id:"fullers",n:"Griffin Brewery, Chiswick",a:"Chiswick",pc:"W4",lat:51.4906,lng:-0.2553,c:"history",s:"since 1654",q:"Fuller's Griffin Brewery Chiswick",w:"Beer has been brewed on this bend of the river since at least 1654 — the oldest brewing site in London, home of London Pride and ESB. The wisteria on the brewery wall, planted around 1816, is claimed as one of the oldest in Britain. Tours end in the sample room, obviously.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brompton",n:"Brompton Cemetery",a:"West Brompton",pc:"SW10",lat:51.4856,lng:-0.1903,c:"oddity",s:"Reddit favourite",q:"Brompton Cemetery",w:"Beatrix Potter lived nearby and lifted names straight off these headstones — Nutkins, McGregor, Jeremiah Fisher and Tod are all buried here. So are Emmeline Pankhurst and Long Wolf, a Lakota chief who died touring with Buffalo Bill. The colonnades double as a film set constantly.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Uk_london_Brompton_Cemetery.jpg/960px-Uk_london_Brompton_Cemetery.jpg"},{id:"peep",n:"Lil Peep Memorial Bench",a:"North Portobello",pc:"W10",lat:51.5202,lng:-0.2056,c:"music",s:"TikTok/Reddit canon",q:"Lil Peep Memorial Bench Portobello Road London",w:"The bench — by Faraday Road at the top of Portobello, facing the green near where Gus lived in 2017 while recording Come Over When You're Sober. Fan-tended ever since: flowers, lighters, tags, handwritten notes. The quiet centre of an internet-era pilgrimage.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"peepflat",n:"Peep's '4 Gold Chains' flat",a:"Ladbroke Grove",pc:"W10",lat:51.517,lng:-0.209,c:"music",s:"pilgrimage stop 2",q:"121 Ladbroke Grove London",w:"121 Ladbroke Grove — the flat from the 2017 London era that fans know from the 4 Gold Chains imagery. Exterior only; people live here. Pairs with the bench for the full loop down Portobello.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sarm",n:"Island Records / Sarm Studios",a:"Basing Street",pc:"W11",lat:51.5167,lng:-0.2011,c:"music",s:"one building, three eras",q:"Sarm Studios Basing Street Notting Hill",w:"A deconsecrated chapel with more history per square metre than Abbey Road: Led Zeppelin cut much of IV here, Bob Marley recorded at Island's HQ, and on 25 November 1984 Band Aid crammed in to record 'Do They Know It's Christmas' in a single day. Now flats, with a plaque.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"strummer",n:"Joe Strummer mural, Portobello Green",a:"Under the Westway",pc:"W10",lat:51.5208,lng:-0.2046,c:"music",s:"Clash country",q:"Joe Strummer mural Portobello",w:"The Clash formed, squatted and shot their debut album cover within a few hundred metres of this flyover. The Strummer mural under the Westway is the shrine; 'London Calling' is about exactly this stretch of road. The 101'ers were named after their squat at 101 Walterton Road.",city:"london"},{id:"mangrove",n:"The Mangrove site, 8 All Saints Rd",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"W11",lat:51.5174,lng:-0.2018,c:"history",s:"Black British history",q:"Mangrove restaurant All Saints Road plaque",w:"Frank Crichlow's Caribbean restaurant fed Hendrix, Marley and the local frontline — and was raided by police twelve times. The 1971 trial of the Mangrove Nine ended in the first judicial acknowledgement of racial hatred in the Met. Steve McQueen's Small Axe filmed the story; a plaque marks the spot.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/All_Saints_Notting_Hill_-_geograph.org.uk_-_331666.jpg"},{id:"golborne",n:"Golborne Road",a:"North Kensington",pc:"W10",lat:51.5213,lng:-0.2065,c:"food",s:"locals' Portobello",q:"Golborne Road London",w:"Portobello's scruffier, better northern end: Moroccan grills and mint tea, Portuguese custard tarts at Lisboa Patisserie since 1968, junk stalls on Fridays. The Trellick looms over everything. This was 'Little Morocco' decades before the rest of the city noticed.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kensal",n:"Kensal Green Cemetery",a:"Kensal Green",pc:"W10",lat:51.5295,lng:-0.2253,c:"history",s:"Magnificent Seven",q:"Kensal Green Cemetery",w:"The oldest of London's Magnificent Seven cemeteries (1833), modelled on Père Lachaise. Both Brunels are here, plus Thackeray, Trollope and a working hydraulic catafalque that still lowers coffins into the catacombs — which you can tour by guided candlelit visit.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Pub_on_Harrow_road%2C_Kensal_Green%2C_London.jpg/960px-Pub_on_Harrow_road%2C_Kensal_Green%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"meanwhile",n:"Meanwhile Gardens",a:"Westbourne Park",pc:"W10",lat:51.5222,lng:-0.2042,c:"green",s:"canal-side",q:"Meanwhile Gardens London",w:"Built 'meanwhile' on demolition wasteland in 1976 by a sculptor with a tiny grant — and never given back. Wildflower banks along the canal plus one of Europe's oldest concrete skate bowls, still ridden daily. The name became a whole category of urban planning.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stlukes",n:"St Luke's Mews",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"W11",lat:51.5167,lng:-0.2009,c:"pop",s:"screen pilgrimage",q:"St Luke's Mews London",w:"The pastel mews hiding the Love Actually cue-cards door at No. 27 — pink, much photographed, privately owned. The mews itself is the prize: former stables turned million-pound cottages, best at golden hour before the photoshoots arrive.",city:"london"},{id:"electric",n:"Electric Cinema",a:"Portobello",pc:"W11",lat:51.5158,lng:-0.2046,c:"pop",s:"institution",q:"Electric Cinema Portobello",w:"One of Britain's oldest working cinemas (1911) — leather armchairs, footstools, cashmere blankets and a bar inside the auditorium. It survived as a bingo hall before restoration; the serial killer John Christie of 10 Rillington Place worked here as a projectionist.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Electric_Cinema_Notting_Hill_2009.jpg/960px-Electric_Cinema_Notting_Hill_2009.jpg"},{id:"travelbookshop",n:"The Travel Bookshop site",a:"Blenheim Crescent",pc:"W11",lat:51.5159,lng:-0.2055,c:"pop",s:"the real one",q:"13 Blenheim Crescent Notting Hill bookshop",w:"13 Blenheim Crescent was the actual shop that inspired Hugh Grant's in Notting Hill — Richard Curtis lived around the corner. The original closed in 2011; the blue-fronted 'Notting Hill Bookshop' on Portobello trades on the fame. Come for the distinction, stay for the street.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"booksforcooks",n:"Books for Cooks",a:"Blenheim Crescent",pc:"W11",lat:51.516,lng:-0.2057,c:"food",s:"since 1983",q:"Books for Cooks Notting Hill",w:"A bookshop with a test kitchen in the back: most lunchtimes they cook from the cookbooks on the shelves and serve a short set menu for next to nothing. Opposite the old Travel Bookshop — this corner had the better story all along.",city:"london",oh:"Tu-Sa 10:00-18:00; \"3 weeks August\" off; \"10 days Christmas\" off; PH off",wq:"a"},{id:"hippodrome",n:"St John's & the lost racecourse",a:"Ladbroke Grove",pc:"W11",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.2068,c:"history",s:"why the streets curve",q:"St John's Church Ladbroke Grove",w:"All of Notting Hill was briefly a racecourse: the Hippodrome (1837–41) looped around this hill, with the church now crowning what was the grandstand mound. The clay was too heavy, locals kept a right of way through the middle, and it failed — but the crescents still trace the track.",city:"london"},{id:"leighton",n:"Leighton House",a:"Holland Park",pc:"W14",lat:51.4983,lng:-0.205,c:"history",s:"most sumptuous room in London",q:"Leighton House Museum",w:"Lord Leighton's private palace of art, built around the Arab Hall: a golden dome, a fountain, and walls of genuine Damascus tiles he shipped home from Syria. Upstairs the painting studio has its own discreet stair for models to slip in unseen. Nothing else in London looks like this.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Casa_de_Leighton%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2022-11-26%2C_DD_40.jpg/960px-Casa_de_Leighton%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2022-11-26%2C_DD_40.jpg"},{id:"towerhouse",n:"The Tower House",a:"Melbury Road",pc:"W14",lat:51.499,lng:-0.2071,c:"oddity",s:"rock-star gothic",q:"Tower House Melbury Road",w:"William Burges built himself a French-gothic fantasy here in the 1870s, every room themed and painted. Jimmy Page outbid David Bowie for it in 1972 and still owns it — he has spent decades fighting neighbours' basement excavations, including Robbie Williams', to protect the murals.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gardenlodge",n:"Garden Lodge wall",a:"Logan Place",pc:"W8",lat:51.4965,lng:-0.1968,c:"music",s:"Freddie's door",q:"Garden Lodge Logan Place Freddie Mercury",w:"Freddie Mercury's home until his death in 1991, left to Mary Austin. The green door and the graffiti-layered wall are the pilgrimage — fans have written on it continuously since 1991, and it keeps being repainted and rewritten. Quiet street; be gentle.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"debenham",n:"Debenham House (Peacock House)",a:"Addison Road",pc:"W14",lat:51.5023,lng:-0.2105,c:"oddity",s:"tiled fever dream",q:"Debenham House Addison Road",w:"A 1905 Arts and Crafts palazzo sheathed in peacock-blue and green Doulton tiles — built for the Debenhams department-store founder, with a domed hall like a tiny Byzantine cathedral. Film crews love it. Private, but the exterior alone is worth the detour.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Debenham_House_%2835482927182%29.jpg/960px-Debenham_House_%2835482927182%29.jpg"},{id:"roughtrade",n:"Rough Trade West",a:"Talbot Road",pc:"W11",lat:51.5174,lng:-0.2032,c:"music",s:"since 1976",q:"Rough Trade West Talbot Road",w:"The original 1976 shop that became a label, a movement and a global brand — punk and reggae sold over the same counter while the Clash rehearsed up the road. Still small, still opinionated; the staff-picks wall remains binding law.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"honestjons",n:"Honest Jon's",a:"Portobello Road",pc:"W10",lat:51.5198,lng:-0.2052,c:"music",s:"since 1974",q:"Honest Jon's Records Portobello",w:"Crate-digger's church since 1974: jazz, dub, broken beat, west African pressings. Damon Albarn co-runs the in-house label, which has put out everything from Mali sessions to lost calypso. The listening counter rewards patience.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thecow",n:"The Cow",a:"Westbourne Park Road",pc:"W2",lat:51.5181,lng:-0.1971,c:"food",s:"Guinness & oysters",q:"The Cow Westbourne Park Road",w:"Tom Conran's corner boozer that helped invent the gastropub in the 90s without ever looking like one — Formica, Irish landlord-chic, and the canonical order of a pint of Guinness with a plate of oysters. The dining room upstairs launched a generation of chefs.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"granger",n:"Granger & Co.",a:"Westbourne Grove",pc:"W11",lat:51.5155,lng:-0.1996,c:"brunch",s:"the hotcakes",q:"Granger and Co Westbourne Grove",w:"Bill Granger brought Sydney brunch culture to London in 2011, and the ricotta hotcakes with banana and honeycomb butter became a city-wide institution. Still a queue, still hit-and-miss on the eggs — order the famous things and you'll see why it endures.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sundaybk",n:"Sunday in Brooklyn",a:"Westbourne Grove",pc:"W2",lat:51.5152,lng:-0.1925,c:"brunch",s:"the pancakes",q:"Sunday in Brooklyn Westbourne Grove",w:"The New York original's only overseas outpost. The malted pancakes with hazelnut-maple praline and brown butter are the single most ordered brunch dish in west London — get them for the table and something savoury for yourself.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"beam",n:"Beam",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"W11",lat:51.516,lng:-0.2,c:"brunch",s:"2026 hot list",q:"Beam cafe Notting Hill",w:"The current local obsession: an all-day breakfast room with a Middle Eastern accent. The Beam Benedict — shawarma-spiced lamb patties, crispy poached eggs, harissa hollandaise — is the signature people cross town for.",city:"london"},{id:"eggbreak",n:"Egg Break",a:"Uxbridge Street",pc:"W8",lat:51.5083,lng:-0.1962,c:"brunch",s:"side-street find",q:"Egg Break Uxbridge Street",w:"A Soho House side project hiding on a quiet street off Notting Hill Gate — crab-cake benedicts, Mexican omelettes, Tabasco frittata sandos. Less queue than the Westbourne Grove circuit, more invention on the plate.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"permitroom",n:"Permit Room Portobello",a:"Portobello Road",pc:"W10",lat:51.518,lng:-0.2055,c:"brunch",s:"Dishoom's local",q:"Permit Room Portobello",w:"Dishoom's all-day Bombay café in an old Victorian pub — which means the legendary bacon naan roll without the Covent Garden queue. Irani-café breakfasts, chai on tap, and a drinks list built around Bombay 'permit room' culture.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"layla",n:"Layla Bakery",a:"North Kensington",pc:"W10",lat:51.52,lng:-0.2055,c:"brunch",s:"cult croissants",q:"Layla Bakery Notting Hill",w:"A tiny wood-fired bakery with a cult far bigger than the room — heritage-grain sourdough, and croissants that sell out by mid-morning on weekends. The move: go early, take it to Meanwhile Gardens, watch the skaters.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"farmgirl",n:"Farm Girl",a:"Portobello Road",pc:"W11",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.1998,c:"brunch",s:"the rose latte",q:"Farm Girl Cafe Portobello",w:"Aussie-run since 2015 in a courtyard off lower Portobello — the rose latte and acai bowls launched a thousand feeds, but the kitchen is better than its Instagram. Dog-friendly, sun-trap tables.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 08:30-16:30; Sa 09:00-17:00; Su 09:00-16:00",wq:"a"},{id:"goldensq",n:"Golden Square",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5106,lng:-0.1372,c:"history",s:"your office square",q:"Golden Square Soho",w:"Originally 'Gelding Close' — grazing land politely renamed by developers. Plague victims were buried nearby in 1665; by the 1700s it was ambassadors' row, and Dickens put Ralph Nickleby's house on the square. The statue in the middle is George II dressed as a Roman, and nobody is quite sure why.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"snow",n:"John Snow cholera pump",a:"Broadwick Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5133,lng:-0.1366,c:"history",s:"2 min from desk",q:"John Snow pump Broadwick Street",w:"Birthplace of epidemiology. In 1854 John Snow mapped cholera deaths around this pump, persuaded the parish to remove its handle, and proved the disease was waterborne while the establishment still blamed 'bad air'. Replica pump kerbside; the pub named after him serves a teetotaller's memory.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/John_Snow_pub.jpg/960px-John_Snow_pub.jpg"},{id:"ziggy",n:"Heddon Street — Ziggy plaque",a:"off Regent Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.511,lng:-0.1408,c:"music",s:"3 min from desk",q:"Ziggy Stardust plaque Heddon Street",w:"Where Bowie posed under the K. West sign on a wet January night in 1972 for the Ziggy Stardust cover. The phone box from the back cover is gone, but a plaque marks the doorway — one of very few in London honouring an album rather than a person.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 07:30-24:00; Fr 07:30-01:00; Sa 10:00-01:00; Su 10:00-23:00",wq:"a"},{id:"applecorps",n:"Apple Corps rooftop, 3 Savile Row",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.1411,c:"music",s:"the last concert",q:"3 Savile Row Beatles rooftop",w:"On 30 January 1969 the Beatles climbed onto this roof and played for 42 minutes — their final public performance, ended by the police. 'I hope we've passed the audition.' Four minutes' walk from your desk.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"trident",n:"Trident Studios, St Anne's Court",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5141,lng:-0.133,c:"music",s:"alley plaque",q:"Trident Studios St Anne's Court Soho",w:"An alley studio with an absurd discography: Hey Jude, Space Oddity, Ziggy Stardust, Transformer and Queen's first albums were all cut here, partly because Trident had London's best piano and its first 8-track. Blink and you miss the plaque in the passage.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/75/Saint_annes_ct.jpg"},{id:"marquee",n:"Marquee Club site",a:"90 Wardour Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5135,lng:-0.1338,c:"music",s:"plaque hunt",q:"Marquee Club plaque Wardour Street",w:"The Wardour Street years (1964–88) of the most important club in British rock: the Stones' early residency, The Who's Tuesday nights, Bowie's 1973 '1980 Floor Show' filmed here. The building is offices now; the plaque does heavy lifting.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f9/IMG_20240815_155054754.jpg/960px-IMG_20240815_155054754.jpg"},{id:"berwick",n:"Berwick Street",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5139,lng:-0.1352,c:"pop",s:"album cover",q:"Berwick Street Oasis cover",w:"The (What's the Story) Morning Glory? cover was shot here at dawn in 1995 — the man walking past was a passer-by, not a Gallagher. The market has traded since 1778 and claims the first sale of a tomato in Britain. Record shops cling on at the north end.",city:"london"},{id:"stannes",n:"St Anne's Soho",a:"Wardour Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.1318,c:"oddity",s:"a king in the crypt",q:"St Anne's Church Soho",w:"Buried here: Theodore von Neuhoff, the only ever King of Corsica, who died a debtor in Soho in 1756 — his epitaph, by Horace Walpole, notes that fate 'gave him a kingdom, and denied him bread'. Dorothy L. Sayers' ashes lie under the tower she helped save.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/West_Face_of_the_Church_of_Saint_Anne%2C_Soho_%2801%29.jpg/960px-West_Face_of_the_Church_of_Saint_Anne%2C_Soho_%2801%29.jpg"},{id:"quovadis",n:"Quo Vadis / Marx's garret",a:"28 Dean Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5138,lng:-0.1326,c:"food",s:"blue plaque upstairs",q:"Quo Vadis Dean Street",w:"Karl Marx lived in two squalid rooms above this restaurant from 1851–56, researching Das Kapital at the British Museum while three of his children died here. Below, Quo Vadis has fed Soho since 1926 — the smoked eel sandwich is the modern classic. The juxtaposition is the point.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-14:30, 17:00-22:00"},{id:"kettners",n:"Kettner's",a:"Romilly Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5133,lng:-0.1308,c:"food",s:"since 1867",q:"Kettner's Townhouse Soho",w:"Opened in 1867 by Napoleon III's chef — Oscar Wilde entertained here and it surfaces in his trial transcripts; Edward VII reputedly slipped in to meet Lillie Langtry. Now Soho House-run, but the champagne bar keeps the ghost lights on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"baritalia",n:"Bar Italia & the Baird plaque",a:"22 Frith Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5134,lng:-0.1316,c:"food",s:"two histories, one address",q:"Bar Italia Frith Street",w:"Downstairs: the 1949 espresso institution that never really closes, immortalised by Pulp. Upstairs: the room where John Logie Baird gave the world's first public demonstration of television in January 1926. One doorway, both revolutions caffeinated.",city:"london",oh:"24/7",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Bar_Italia_-_Soho_%284764432107%29.jpg/960px-Bar_Italia_-_Soho_%284764432107%29.jpg"},{id:"coachhorses",n:"The Coach & Horses (Norman's)",a:"Greek Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5131,lng:-0.1314,c:"history",s:"Soho bohemia HQ",q:"Coach and Horses Greek Street",w:"Norman Balon, self-styled 'London's rudest landlord', ran this for 63 years while Private Eye held its fortnightly lunches upstairs — they still do. Jeffrey Bernard drank himself into a column and a West End play here; 'Jeffrey Bernard is Unwell' is set entirely in this bar.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"trishas",n:"Trisha's (New Evaristo Club)",a:"57 Greek Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5136,lng:-0.1313,c:"food",s:"the green door",q:"Trisha's New Evaristo Club Greek Street",w:"An unmarked green door, a basement, and one of the last true Soho drinking dens — run by the same Italian family since the 1940s. Cash, dim lights, photos of dead regulars on the wall. The kind of place you're taken to once and then guard.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"frenchhouse",n:"The French House",a:"Dean Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.1322,c:"history",s:"Free French lore",q:"The French House Dean Street",w:"De Gaulle reputedly drafted his 'À tous les Français' appeal upstairs in 1940 when this was the Free French unofficial HQ. Beer comes in half-pints only by house law; Francis Bacon, Lucian Freud and Dylan Thomas — who left his Under Milk Wood manuscript under a chair — all drank here.",city:"london"},{id:"maisonbertaux",n:"Maison Bertaux",a:"Greek Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5134,lng:-0.131,c:"food",s:"since 1871",q:"Maison Bertaux Greek Street",w:"Soho's oldest pâtisserie, founded in 1871 by Communards fleeing Paris. Eccentric, theatrical, gloriously unrenovated — mismatched china, gallery shows upstairs, éclairs unchanged in living memory. Bastille Day is celebrated with full ceremony.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Maison_Bertaux%2C_Soho%2C_London_%283104693962%29.jpg/960px-Maison_Bertaux%2C_Soho%2C_London_%283104693962%29.jpg"},{id:"algerian",n:"Algerian Coffee Stores",a:"Old Compton Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5131,lng:-0.1317,c:"food",s:"since 1887",q:"Algerian Coffee Stores",w:"Roasting on this site since 1887 — over 80 coffees and 120 teas behind a shopfront that hasn't changed in a century. The takeaway espresso at the till remains one of the best and cheapest in the West End. The smell alone is worth the walk.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-We 10:00-18:00; Th-Sa 10:00-19:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Algerian_Coffee_Stores.jpg/960px-Algerian_Coffee_Stores.jpg"},{id:"linastores",n:"Lina Stores",a:"Brewer Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5119,lng:-0.1346,c:"food",s:"since 1944",q:"Lina Stores Brewer Street",w:"The pistachio-green Italian delicatessen that has fed Soho since 1944 — fresh pasta made in the window, panettone towers at Christmas. The restaurant offshoots queue round the block, but the original counter is the real artefact.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Tu 08:30-19:30; We-Fr 08:30-20:30; Sa 10:00-19:30; Su 11:00-17:00"},{id:"denmark",n:"Denmark Street",a:"St Giles",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5153,lng:-0.1292,c:"music",s:"Tin Pan Alley",q:"Denmark Street London",w:"Britain's Tin Pan Alley: the Stones recorded their first album at Regent Sounds at No. 4, Bowie slept in a van outside, Elton John was a tea boy, and the Sex Pistols lived at No. 6 — where their graffiti is now literally protected by Historic England listing.",city:"london"},{id:"cecilcourt",n:"Cecil Court",a:"off Charing Cross Road",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5108,lng:-0.1281,c:"bookshops",s:"booksellers' row",q:"Cecil Court London",w:"A gas-lamped Victorian alley of antiquarian book and print dealers, unchanged for a century. Mozart lodged here for four months in 1764, aged eight, while hustling London society. Persistent rumour holds it as an inspiration for Diagon Alley; the dealers neither confirm nor mind.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/London_Cecil_Court.jpg/960px-London_Cecil_Court.jpg"},{id:"goodwins",n:"Goodwin's Court",a:"off St Martin's Lane",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5108,lng:-0.1273,c:"oddity",s:"gaslit 1690s",q:"Goodwin's Court London",w:"A crack in the wall off St Martin's Lane opening onto a row of bow-fronted 1690s shopfronts, still gaslit nightly. Dickens knew it; Potter location-scouts clearly did too. At dusk, with the lamps on, it's the most convincingly 18th-century thirty metres in London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"nealsyard",n:"Neal's Yard",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5143,lng:-0.1267,c:"pop",s:"Python HQ",q:"Neal's Yard Covent Garden",w:"The rainbow courtyard was semi-derelict when Monty Python set up their offices and edit suite here in the 70s — Life of Brian was cut in this yard, and a plaque marks the spot. The wholefood revolution (Neal's Yard Dairy, Remedies) started in the same few doorways.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Neal%27s_Yard_20130408_034.jpg/960px-Neal%27s_Yard_20130408_034.jpg"},{id:"rules",n:"Rules",a:"Maiden Lane",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5101,lng:-0.1228,c:"food",s:"est. 1798",q:"Rules Restaurant Maiden Lane",w:"London's oldest restaurant, serving game and oysters since 1798. Dickens, Thackeray and Graham Greene were regulars; Edward VII kept a private door and a curtained room upstairs for dinners with Lillie Langtry. Still owns its own grouse moor.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-23:30; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Rules%2C_London%27s_oldest_restaurant._-_geograph.org.uk_-_510375.jpg"},{id:"gordons",n:"Gordon's Wine Bar",a:"Villiers Street",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5079,lng:-0.1238,c:"food",s:"since 1890",q:"Gordon's Wine Bar",w:"The oldest wine bar in London (1890), burrowed into cellars under a house where Kipling once lodged and Pepys before him. Candlelight only, sherry from the barrel, walls of yellowed front pages. Arrive early or stand — the caves don't take bookings.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 11:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Gordon%27s_Wine_Bar%2C_20130414_181.jpg/960px-Gordon%27s_Wine_Bar%2C_20130414_181.jpg"},{id:"savoy",n:"Savoy Court & Kaspar",a:"Strand",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5102,lng:-0.1208,c:"oddity",s:"pub-quiz gold",q:"Savoy Court London",w:"The only road in Britain where traffic must drive on the right, by special dispensation, so cabs can swing to the hotel door. Inside, since 1927, any table of 13 is joined by Kaspar — a metre-high black cat sculpture given his own place setting, after an 1898 dinner of 13 ended in a guest's death.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"watergate",n:"York House Watergate",a:"Embankment Gardens",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5078,lng:-0.1232,c:"follies",s:"Thames archaeology",q:"York House Watergate Embankment Gardens",w:"A 1626 palace river-gate now marooned 140 metres inland — the Thames used to lap its steps until Bazalgette built the Embankment in the 1860s. The single best physical proof of how much of central London is reclaimed river.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/York_Watergate_and_Adelphi_Buildings%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"pickering",n:"Pickering Place & Berry Bros",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1",lat:51.5057,lng:-0.1379,c:"oddity",s:"finance-adjacent",q:"Pickering Place St James's",w:"Britain's smallest public square, gaslit, reached down a panelled 1730s alley — reputedly the site of London's last duel. A plaque marks the legation of the Republic of Texas (1842–45). Berry Bros & Rudd at the entrance has sold wine since 1698 and weighed customers on giant coffee scales since Byron.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Berry_Bros._%26_Rudd.png"},{id:"lockco",n:"Lock & Co. Hatters",a:"St James's Street",pc:"SW1",lat:51.506,lng:-0.1382,c:"history",s:"since 1676",q:"Lock and Co Hatters St James's",w:"The oldest hat shop in the world (1676). They invented the bowler in 1849 — house name still 'the Coke' — and the ledger recording Admiral Nelson's bicorne order, with built-in eye shade, survives. Churchill and Chaplin have head-shapes on file in the conformateur records.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"chopinstj",n:"Chopin's last London address",a:"4 St James's Place",pc:"SW1",lat:51.5066,lng:-0.1397,c:"diaspora",s:"plaque",q:"Chopin plaque 4 St James's Place",w:"From this house in November 1848, a dying Chopin went out to give his final public performance — at Guildhall, in aid of Polish refugees. He could barely climb the stairs and had to be carried; a year later he was dead in Paris. The plaque is small; the story isn't.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mountst",n:"Mount Street Gardens",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1",lat:51.5097,lng:-0.1494,c:"green",s:"hidden microclimate",q:"Mount Street Gardens Mayfair",w:"A walled former burial ground turned garden, so sheltered that a Canary Island date palm fruits outdoors. Ringed by pink terracotta mansion blocks and almost silent despite sitting in the middle of Mayfair. The lunch-bench upgrade you've been ignoring.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Mount_Street_Gardens%2C_London_W1_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1536010.jpg"},{id:"burlington",n:"Burlington Arcade",a:"Piccadilly",pc:"W1",lat:51.5093,lng:-0.1399,c:"oddity",s:"no whistling",q:"Burlington Arcade",w:"Opened 1819 with its own private police force — the Beadles, ex-soldiers in top hats who still patrol, arguably the oldest police unit in Britain. Whistling, humming and running remain banned: whistles were once the signal code of pickpockets working the arcade.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Burlington_Arcade%2C_north_entrance.jpg/960px-Burlington_Arcade%2C_north_entrance.jpg"},{id:"tyburn",n:"Tyburn Tree marker",a:"Marble Arch",pc:"W2",lat:51.5133,lng:-0.1593,c:"oddity",s:"dark history",q:"Tyburn Tree plaque Marble Arch",w:"A brass triangle on a traffic island marks the site of the Tyburn gallows, where an estimated 50,000 people were hanged over six centuries — executions were public holidays with grandstand seating. The Tyburn Convent nearby keeps relics of the Catholic martyrs and shows its crypt on request.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Tyburn_gallows_1746.jpg"},{id:"andrewedmunds",n:"Andrew Edmunds",a:"Lexington Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.137,c:"food",s:"50 m from the office",q:"Andrew Edmunds restaurant Soho",w:"A candlelit 1740s townhouse with a handwritten daily menu and one of the best-value wine lists in London — the late owner was a print dealer who sold Georgian satire next door. Permanently voted Soho's most romantic room; book the ground floor.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 12:00-15:30, 17:30-22:45; Sa 12:30-15:30, 17:30-22:45; Su 13:00-16:00, 18:00-22:30",wq:"a"},{id:"zedel",n:"Brasserie Zédel",a:"Sherwood Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5103,lng:-0.1349,c:"food",s:"gilded basement",q:"Brasserie Zedel",w:"Descend from a Piccadilly side street into a vast gold-and-marble 1930s ballroom — the former Regent Palace Hotel's grill, restored as the most affordable grand brasserie in town. Steak haché and a carafe for less than the meal deal economics suggest is possible.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-We 12:00-22:30; Th-Sa 11:30-23:00; Su 11:30-22:00",wq:"a"},{id:"wiltons",n:"Wiltons",a:"Jermyn Street",pc:"SW1",lat:51.5077,lng:-0.1377,c:"food",s:"est. 1742",q:"Wiltons Jermyn Street",w:"Trading since 1742 — older than the USA — and the establishment's dining room of record: oysters, Dover sole, waitresses traditionally addressed as 'nanny'. In 1942 a bomb fell nearby mid-service; the owner sold the place on the spot to a customer for the price of his unpaid lunch bill.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dishoom",n:"Dishoom Carnaby",a:"Kingly Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.139,c:"brunch",s:"the bacon naan",q:"Dishoom Carnaby",w:"The Bombay-café revivalists whose bacon naan roll — smoked bacon, chilli tomato jam, cream cheese in fresh naan — became modern London's definitive breakfast. Bottomless chai while you wait. Weekday before 9am is the move.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 08:00-23:00; Fr 08:00-00:00; Sa 09:00-00:00; Su 09:00-23:00"},{id:"koya",n:"Koya Soho",a:"Frith Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5137,lng:-0.1315,c:"brunch",s:"udon for breakfast",q:"Koya Bar Soho",w:"A counter that serves breakfast udon: kama tama (hot noodles, raw egg, soy) or a full-English-inspired bowl with bacon and egg in dashi. Opens early, no bookings, the best contrarian breakfast in the West End.",city:"london",oh:"10:00-22:00",wq:"a"},{id:"imads",n:"Imad's Syrian Kitchen",a:"Kingly Court",pc:"W1",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.1387,c:"brunch",s:"a story on a plate",q:"Imad's Syrian Kitchen Kingly Court",w:"Imad Alarnab ran restaurants in Damascus, lost everything, cooked for fellow refugees on the route to Europe, and rebuilt here. Breakfast fatteh — fried flatbread, chickpeas, tahini, ghee — is rich, restorative and quietly moving. One of Soho's genuinely good stories.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"barbruno",n:"Bar Bruno",a:"Wardour Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5132,lng:-0.134,c:"brunch",s:"the last Soho caff",q:"Bar Bruno Wardour Street",w:"A proper Formica caff that has survived every wave of Soho gentrification — full English, bubble and squeak, builder's tea, theatre crews and old boys in the booths. Eat here while you can; places like this are down to single digits in W1.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wolseley",n:"The Wolseley",a:"Piccadilly",pc:"W1",lat:51.5074,lng:-0.1414,c:"brunch",s:"the grand café",q:"The Wolseley Piccadilly",w:"A 1920s car showroom turned Viennese grand café — the definitive power breakfast in London. Kippers, kedgeree, eggs Benedict under black-and-gold chinoiserie; Lucian Freud ate here so often the staff guarded his table. Book, wear the good coat.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:00-00:00; Sa 08:00-00:00; Su 08:00-23:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/1%E2%80%933_Arlington_Street_and_157%E2%80%93160_Piccadilly.jpg/960px-1%E2%80%933_Arlington_Street_and_157%E2%80%93160_Piccadilly.jpg"},{id:"xingfutang",n:"Xing Fu Tang",a:"Frith Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5136,lng:-0.1314,c:"boba",s:"the brown sugar",q:"Xing Fu Tang Soho",w:"Taipei's brown-sugar specialists — pearls hand-stirred and caramelised in a big metal pan right in the window, then striped up the cup. The panna-cotta bunny topper is pure theatre; the tapioca is the actual point.",city:"london"},{id:"heytea",n:"HEYTEA",a:"Shaftesbury Avenue",pc:"W1",lat:51.5114,lng:-0.1322,c:"boba",s:"cheese-tea originators",q:"HEYTEA London Soho",w:"The Chinese mega-brand that invented cheese tea — salted cream-cheese foam over fruit or jasmine tea — chose Soho for its European debut. Sounds wrong, works completely: the salt cuts the sweetness. Order the signature grape or mango cheezo.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"machimachi",n:"Machi Machi",a:"Wardour Street",pc:"W1",lat:51.5122,lng:-0.1335,c:"boba",s:"the crème brûlée",q:"Machi Machi Wardour Street",w:"The Taipei cult shop that got famous when Jay Chou kept posting it — crème brûlée milk teas torched to order, panna cotta bottles, photogenic to a fault. The strawberry crème brûlée latte is the order.",city:"london"},{id:"kissaten",n:"Kissaten",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.512,lng:-0.1342,c:"boba",s:"gachapon while you wait",q:"Kissaten bubble tea Soho",w:"Japanese-leaning tea room crammed with gachapon machines — Earl Grey milk tea with a floral edge and honey tapioca while you turn a capsule for a tiny mascot. The most fun queue in the category.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 12:00-21:00",wq:"a"},{id:"cuppacha",n:"Cuppacha",a:"Newport Court",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5117,lng:-0.1283,c:"boba",s:"fruit tea formula",q:"Cuppacha Newport Court Chinatown",w:"The bright-pink shop on Chinatown's unofficial bubble-tea strip. Fruit teas are the strength — mango green with popping lychee boba, refreshment without the sugar wall. Weekday visits dodge the queue.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"milktrain",n:"Milk Train",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5122,lng:-0.1228,c:"boba",s:"the candy-floss cloud",q:"Milk Train Covent Garden",w:"Soft serve crowned with a cloud of candy floss, plus solid milk teas underneath the spectacle. Engineered for the camera but the matcha soft serve genuinely delivers. A detour for when you've earned something ridiculous.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mithraeum",n:"London Mithraeum",a:"Walbrook",pc:"EC4",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.0907,c:"history",s:"free, book a slot",q:"London Mithraeum",w:"A Roman temple of Mithras, discovered in 1954 and rebuilt seven metres beneath Bloomberg's HQ on its original spot by the lost Walbrook river. The reconstruction uses haze, light and chanting; upstairs, a wall of Roman writing tablets includes the oldest financial document in Britain — an IOU.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/London_Mithraeum.jpg/960px-London_Mithraeum.jpg"},{id:"boe",n:"Bank of England Museum",a:"Bartholomew Lane",pc:"EC2",lat:51.5142,lng:-0.0876,c:"finance",s:"free entry",q:"Bank of England Museum",w:"Free, somehow always empty, and you get to lift a real 13 kg gold bar through a hole in a case. Original Soane architecture survives inside; exhibits run from the Old Lady's founding in 1694 to crisis-era stress charts. Kenneth Grahame of Wind in the Willows worked here — and was shot at by an intruder.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/London.bankofengland.arp.jpg/960px-London.bankofengland.arp.jpg"},{id:"coffeealleys",n:"Change Alley coffee houses",a:"Cornhill",pc:"EC3",lat:51.513,lng:-0.0858,c:"finance",s:"where markets began",q:"Jamaica Wine House St Michael's Alley",w:"London's first coffee house opened in St Michael's Alley in 1652 — the Jamaica Wine House marks the spot. Around the corner, Jonathan's Coffee House posted stock prices from 1698: the direct ancestor of the London Stock Exchange. Broking, insurance and the South Sea Bubble were all caffeinated into existence in these alleys.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"georgevulture",n:"The George & Vulture",a:"Castle Court",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.086,c:"history",s:"Pickwick HQ",q:"George and Vulture Castle Court",w:"A 1748 chop house down an alley maze, mentioned at least twenty times in The Pickwick Papers — Dickens drank here and his descendants still hold family gatherings in the dining room. City lunches as they were two hundred years ago.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/The_George_and_Vulture%2C_Castle_Court_-_City_Of_London._%284793266343%29.jpg/960px-The_George_and_Vulture%2C_Castle_Court_-_City_Of_London._%284793266343%29.jpg"},{id:"cloth",n:"Cloth Cornhill (ex-Simpson's Tavern)",a:"Ball Court",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5131,lng:-0.0862,c:"food",s:"reborn mid-2026",q:"Cloth Cornhill Ball Court",w:"London's oldest chophouse (1757) — Dickens and Thackeray's haunt — was evicted in 2022 and is reopening right about now as Cloth Cornhill, booths and panelling restored. During the works they found a 19th-century house cookbook and are reviving its 'luncheon beef sausage'. Check it's open, then go.",city:"london",oh:"Tu-Fr 08:30-15:30; Mo 11:30-15:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Barrels_outside_Simpson%27s_Tavern_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1771986.jpg"},{id:"sweetings",n:"Sweetings",a:"Queen Victoria Street",pc:"EC4",lat:51.5122,lng:-0.0937,c:"finance",s:"since 1889",q:"Sweetings restaurant City of London",w:"The City's fish ordinary since 1889: lunch only, weekdays only, no reservations, oysters and potted shrimps at mahogany counters, Black Velvet in silver tankards. The dining ritual of old finance, miraculously intact. Go once a quarter and feel the continuity.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Sweetings%2C_Queen_Victoria_Street.jpg/960px-Sweetings%2C_Queen_Victoria_Street.jpg"},{id:"stjohn",n:"St. JOHN Smithfield",a:"St John Street",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5202,lng:-0.1003,c:"food",s:"the 1994 revolution",q:"St John restaurant Smithfield",w:"Fergus Henderson's white room beside the meat market launched nose-to-tail eating in 1994 and changed British food's self-respect. The roast bone marrow with parsley salad has been on every 'eat before you die' list since. The Welsh rarebit and a glass at the bar is the cheap way in.",city:"london",oh:"24/7"},{id:"cheshire",n:"Ye Olde Cheshire Cheese",a:"Fleet Street",pc:"EC4",lat:51.5142,lng:-0.1067,c:"food",s:"rebuilt 1667",q:"Ye Olde Cheshire Cheese Fleet Street",w:"Rebuilt the year after the Great Fire, with cellars from a 13th-century monastery. Johnson, Dickens, Twain and Conan Doyle all drank in its lightless rooms. Its parrot Polly was so famous her death in 1926 was announced on the BBC and obituarised in two hundred newspapers.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 11:30-23:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Yeoldcheshirecheese.jpg"},{id:"blackfriar",n:"The Blackfriar",a:"Queen Victoria Street",pc:"EC4",lat:51.512,lng:-0.1035,c:"oddity",s:"art nouveau marvel",q:"The Blackfriar pub London",w:"A wedge-shaped 1905 fever dream of bronze friars, marble and mottoes ('Industry is All') built on a Dominican friary site — the only Art Nouveau pub in London. Saved from demolition in the 60s by a campaign led by John Betjeman. Every surface rewards staring.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Black-Friar-Pub-Londres-230819.jpg/960px-Black-Friar-Pub-Londres-230819.jpg"},{id:"postman",n:"Postman's Park",a:"St Martin's Le Grand",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5166,lng:-0.0986,c:"oddity",s:"quiet gut-punch",q:"Postman's Park Watts Memorial",w:"The Watts Memorial to Heroic Self-Sacrifice: hand-lettered ceramic tiles honouring ordinary people who died saving others — clerks, labourers, children, each story told in a sentence. Begun 1900, still occasionally added to. The quietest, heaviest five minutes in the City.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Postman%27s_Park_and_the_Wall_of_Heroes.JPG/960px-Postman%27s_Park_and_the_Wall_of_Heroes.JPG"},{id:"mice",n:"The Philpot Lane Mice",a:"Eastcheap",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.084,c:"oddity",s:"smallest sculpture",q:"Philpot Lane mice sculpture",w:"Two mice eating cheese, carved high on a cornice — London's smallest public sculpture. The story: two builders fought over a stolen sandwich and fell to their deaths; mice were the real thieves, and the masons left this memorial.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Philpot_Lane%2C_London.jpg/960px-Philpot_Lane%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"devils",n:"The Cornhill Devils",a:"Cornhill",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5133,lng:-0.0858,c:"oddity",s:"architect's revenge",q:"Cornhill Devils London",w:"Three terracotta devils glare down at St Peter upon Cornhill from the neighbouring Victorian block — installed by an architect after the rector forced him to redraw his plans over a boundary dispute. The lowest devil is said to wear the rector's face.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"londonstone",n:"London Stone",a:"Cannon Street",pc:"EC4",lat:51.5114,lng:-0.0904,c:"oddity",s:"mythic lump",q:"London Stone Cannon Street",w:"A limestone block of unknown origin, referenced since the 1100s — Roman milestone? Druid altar? Nobody knows. The proverb says London stands while the Stone is safe; rebel Jack Cade struck his sword on it in 1450 to claim the city. It now sits in its own little shrine in an office wall.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/The_London_Stone.jpg/960px-The_London_Stone.jpg"},{id:"gherkingirl",n:"The Gherkin's Roman girl",a:"St Mary Axe",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5145,lng:-0.0803,c:"oddity",s:"a 1,600-year tenancy",q:"Roman girl grave Gherkin",w:"Excavation for the Gherkin uncovered the grave of an unknown Roman girl. When the tower was finished she was reburied at its foot in 2007, with a funeral and a slate inscribed 'DIS MANIBVS' — to the spirits of the dead. She kept her spot; the skyscraper built around her.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/30_St_Mary_Axe%2C_%27Gherkin%27.JPG/960px-30_St_Mary_Axe%2C_%27Gherkin%27.JPG"},{id:"stolave",n:"St Olave's 'Ghastly Grim'",a:"Hart Street",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5106,lng:-0.0794,c:"oddity",s:"Dickens-named",q:"St Olave Hart Street skull gate",w:"The 1658 churchyard gate bristles with stone skulls and spikes — Dickens nicknamed it 'St Ghastly Grim' and claimed to visit at midnight by lightning. Inside, Samuel Pepys and his wife Elizabeth face each other across the nave; he built her monument so she could watch him in his pew forever.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"aldgatepump",n:"Aldgate Pump",a:"Aldgate",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5139,lng:-0.0769,c:"oddity",s:"dark hydrology",q:"Aldgate Pump",w:"The brass wolf's head marks where the last wolf in the City was supposedly shot. Darker: the prized mineral tang of its water turned out, in the 1870s, to be calcium from human remains — the spring filtered through cemeteries on its way into town. Hundreds sickened before it was switched to mains.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/1600_Aldgate.jpg"},{id:"monument",n:"The Monument",a:"Fish Street Hill",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5102,lng:-0.0859,c:"history",s:"secret laboratory",q:"The Monument London",w:"Wren and Hooke designed the Great Fire column as a covert scientific instrument: a fixed zenith telescope with a hinged lid, a central shaft for pendulum and barometer experiments, and an underground lab that still exists. Traffic vibrations ruined the astronomy; the 311 steps still work.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/The_Monument_to_the_Great_Fire_of_London.JPG/960px-The_Monument_to_the_Great_Fire_of_London.JPG"},{id:"leadenhall",n:"Leadenhall Market",a:"Gracechurch Street",pc:"EC3",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.0836,c:"pop",s:"Diagon Alley",q:"Leadenhall Market",w:"A maroon-and-cream Victorian arcade (1881) built directly over the Roman forum and basilica — the centre of Roman London is under the cheese shop. The blue door of an optician in Bull's Head Passage played the Leaky Cauldron entrance; the wizard tourists know exactly which one.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Leadenhall_Market_In_London_-_Feb_2006_rotated.jpg/960px-Leadenhall_Market_In_London_-_Feb_2006_rotated.jpg"},{id:"sikorski",n:"Sikorski Museum & Polish Institute",a:"South Kensington",pc:"SW7",lat:51.5009,lng:-0.1714,c:"diaspora",s:"check opening hours",q:"Polish Institute and Sikorski Museum London",w:"General Sikorski's archive in an Italianate townhouse: Enigma material, the banners of disbanded regiments, the sabre room, Monte Cassino relics. The reading room holds the records of the government-in-exile — the paper trail of a state that refused to die. Limited hours; plan the pilgrimage.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/IPMS.jpg/960px-IPMS.jpg"},{id:"ognisko",n:"Ognisko Polskie",a:"Exhibition Road",pc:"SW7",lat:51.4974,lng:-0.1742,c:"diaspora",s:"since 1940",q:"Ognisko Polskie Exhibition Road",w:"The Polish Hearth Club, opened in July 1940 by exiled officers and aristocrats with the Duke of Kent in attendance. Chandeliers, portraits, nalewki at the bar, and a terrace dining room that remains the émigré salon it always was. The blinis are the house ritual.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"daquise",n:"Daquise",a:"South Kensington",pc:"SW7",lat:51.4942,lng:-0.174,c:"diaspora",s:"since 1947",q:"Daquise restaurant South Kensington",w:"London's oldest Polish restaurant (1947), one room, lace curtains, unchanged. Christine Keeler met Soviet attaché Yevgeny Ivanov here during the Profumo affair — pick the corner table and order the duck with apples like nothing happened.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"katyn",n:"Katyń Memorial, Gunnersbury",a:"Gunnersbury Cemetery",pc:"W3",lat:51.4936,lng:-0.2855,c:"diaspora",s:"1976, against the FO's wishes",q:"Katyn Memorial Gunnersbury Cemetery",w:"The black granite obelisk the Foreign Office tried for years to block, fearing Soviet offence — the émigrés built it anyway in 1976, with the date 1940 that said everything Moscow denied. Serving British officers were discouraged from attending the unveiling in uniform. Heavy, and necessary.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"chopin",n:"Chopin monument, Southbank",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5058,lng:-0.1158,c:"diaspora",s:"gift from Poland",q:"Chopin statue Royal Festival Hall",w:"Poland's bronze Chopin beside the Festival Hall, a gift unveiled in the 1970s — flowers appear around his birthday every year without fail. Pairs with the St James's Place plaque across the river: the beginning and the end of his London story.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"conrad",n:"Joseph Conrad plaque, Pimlico",a:"Gillingham Street",pc:"SW1",lat:51.4934,lng:-0.1431,c:"diaspora",s:"blue plaque",q:"Joseph Conrad plaque Gillingham Street",w:"17 Gillingham Street — where Józef Korzeniowski, twenty years a sailor, sat down and finished Almayer's Folly, the first novel of the Pole who became English literature's great stylist in his third language. A Pimlico detour for the genealogy soul.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"northolt",n:"Polish War Memorial, Northolt",a:"RAF Northolt",pc:"UB5",lat:51.553,lng:-0.418,c:"diaspora",s:"worth the trek",q:"Polish War Memorial Northolt",w:"The 1948 memorial to the Polish Air Force — 303 Squadron flew from Northolt and claimed more kills in the Battle of Britain than any other Hurricane squadron. Originally listing 1,243 fallen airmen, extended in 1996 to carry every name. The A40 roars past; the eagle doesn't move.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bowiemural",n:"Bowie mural & shrine",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW9",lat:51.4623,lng:-0.1156,c:"music",s:"born round the corner",q:"David Bowie mural Brixton",w:"Jimmy C's 2013 Aladdin Sane mural opposite Brixton tube became the world's Bowie shrine overnight in January 2016 — flowers ten feet deep, now protected by perspex and formally listed. Bowie was born two streets away on Stansfield Road.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"leakest",n:"Leake Street Arches",a:"Waterloo",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5021,lng:-0.1136,c:"pop",s:"Banksy's tunnel",q:"Leake Street Arches",w:"Banksy launched the Cans Festival in this disused taxi tunnel under Waterloo in 2008; it's been London's legal graffiti wall ever since. Everything you see was painted in the last few days and will be gone by next week. Bring a camera, or a can.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/232_-_The_Tunnel_Authorised_Graffiti_Area.jpg/960px-232_-_The_Tunnel_Authorised_Graffiti_Area.jpg"},{id:"blankst",n:"Blank Street Coffee — Westfield",a:"White City",pc:"W12",lat:51.5075,lng:-0.2205,c:"coffee",s:"home-zone fix",q:"Blank Street Coffee Westfield London",w:"The Brooklyn-born chain that took over London in a blink — small-batch oat flat whites and the cult blueberry-matcha and pistachio lattes, ordered ahead on the app. The Westfield kiosk is your two-minutes-from-the-Green default before a Central line run.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"alleywc",n:"The Alley — White City",a:"Westfield London",pc:"W12",lat:51.5076,lng:-0.2208,c:"boba",s:"your top-tier boba",q:"The Alley White City Westfield London",w:"Taiwan's antler-logo boba royalty, and the brand most London obsessives rank number one. They boil their own brown-sugar 'deerioca' pearls fresh on site — the Brown Sugar Deerioca Crème Brûlée milk is the cup people cross town for. This branch is on your doorstep.",city:"london"},{id:"domsubs",n:"Dom's Subs (now at Rasputin's)",a:"Hackney",pc:"E8",lat:51.5345,lng:-0.0565,c:"food",s:"now at Rasputin's, E8",q:"Rasputin's 171 Mare Street Hackney",w:"The cult of the 'impeccable sandwich': house-baked semolina rolls stacked with mortadella, nduja mayo and chilli oil — order That Spicy D. After the standalone shops all shut in early 2026, the Dom's crew now slings the subs out of their Hackney bar, Rasputin's, so the sandwich lives on at 171 Mare Street.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"heisenberg",n:"Heisenberg Coffee Co",a:"Bedford Park",pc:"W4",lat:51.4928,lng:-0.2588,c:"coffee",s:"Breaking Bad themed",q:"Heisenberg Coffee Co Bedford Park Corner Chiswick",w:"A Breaking Bad-themed corner café on the edge of Bedford Park — chemistry equations across the ceiling, lids shaped like Heisenberg's pork-pie hat, and a 'Mr White' dirty chai dusted with raspberry and chocolate. The coffee is genuinely good; the gimmick is just the hook. A fine reason to stroll west to Turnham Green.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sova",n:"Sova — wine & vinyl bar",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"W11",lat:51.5158,lng:-0.2052,c:"food",s:"opened 2026",q:"Sova wine bar Blenheim Crescent Notting Hill",w:"From the team behind Soho's Zima, a 40-cover wine-and-vinyl bar that landed on Blenheim Crescent in 2026 — low-intervention bottles from Georgia, Armenia and Ukraine, beef tartare on Borodinsky toast, suluguni with truffle honey, and a record programme curated with Rough Trade. Central Europe meets W11; book ahead.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"chiswickhouse",n:"Chiswick House & Gardens",a:"Chiswick",pc:"W4",lat:51.4855,lng:-0.2585,c:"history",s:"free gardens",q:"Chiswick House and Gardens",w:"Lord Burlington's 1729 Palladian villa effectively invented the English landscape garden — the template for every park that followed and half the stately homes in America. The Beatles filmed the promo clips for 'Paperback Writer' and 'Rain' in these gardens in 1966; the conservatory's camellia show is among the oldest in the West.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hogarth",n:"Hogarth's House",a:"Chiswick",pc:"W4",lat:51.49,lng:-0.2553,c:"history",s:"free",q:"Hogarth's House Chiswick",w:"William Hogarth's 'little country box by the Thames', now marooned beside the roaring Hogarth Roundabout. The satirist of Gin Lane summered here from 1749; the ancient mulberry in the garden survived a WWII firebomb. Free, tiny, and gloriously out of step with the dual carriageway outside.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bedfordpark",n:"Bedford Park",a:"Chiswick",pc:"W4",lat:51.4945,lng:-0.2595,c:"oddity",s:"first garden suburb",q:"Bedford Park Chiswick conservation area",w:"Widely called the world's first garden suburb (1875) — sweet red-brick Queen Anne houses by Norman Shaw, laid out so barely a tree was felled. It set the blueprint for Hampstead Garden Suburb and a century of leafy English estates. Pair it with a Heisenberg coffee at the corner.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"fleming",n:"Alexander Fleming Laboratory Museum",a:"Paddington",pc:"W2",lat:51.517,lng:-0.1735,c:"history",s:"book ahead",q:"Alexander Fleming Laboratory Museum St Mary's Paddington",w:"The actual cramped lab where, in September 1928, Alexander Fleming came back from holiday to find mould killing the bacteria in a forgotten Petri dish — and discovered penicillin. Restored to its 1928 state, it's one of the great 'this small room changed the world' stops in London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"italgardens",n:"Italian Gardens, Kensington Gardens",a:"Bayswater",pc:"W2",lat:51.5108,lng:-0.1818,c:"green",s:"park gem",q:"Italian Gardens Kensington Gardens",w:"A formal Victorian water garden of four fountains and marble urns — by tradition a gift from Prince Albert to Victoria — fed by a pump house disguised as a Renaissance loggia. Steps away sit the Peter Pan statue and the stretch where J.M. Barrie walked the dog that inspired him. A serene eastward detour into the park.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"paddingtonbear",n:"Paddington Bear statue",a:"Paddington",pc:"W2",lat:51.5167,lng:-0.1769,c:"pop",s:"on the concourse",q:"Paddington Bear statue Paddington Station",w:"On the station concourse sits the bronze of a bear from darkest Peru, found here with a label reading 'please look after this bear'. Michael Bond bought the toy that sparked it in a shop near here on Christmas Eve 1956; a second statue of Bond and Paddington shares a bench nearby. Marmalade optional.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/20230918_ScheWo_London_Paddington_Railway_Station_Paddington_Bear_Bronze.jpg/960px-20230918_ScheWo_London_Paddington_Railway_Station_Paddington_Bear_Bronze.jpg"},{id:"severs",n:"Dennis Severs' House",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5203,lng:-0.0778,c:"oddity",s:"silent candlelit tour",q:"Dennis Severs House Folgate Street",w:"American artist Dennis Severs spent the 1970s onward turning this Georgian house into a 'still-life drama' — ten candlelit rooms staged as if a family of Huguenot silk-weavers had just stepped out, half-eaten food and all. You tour it in total silence, by firelight and the scent of cloves; the Silent Night visits are the ones to book.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/18_Folgate_Street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_819517.jpg"},{id:"beigel",n:"Beigel Bake",a:"Brick Lane",pc:"E1",lat:51.5246,lng:-0.0717,c:"food",s:"24h, since 1974",q:"Beigel Bake Brick Lane",w:"Brick Lane's 24-hour bagel institution since 1974, turning out around 7,000 beigels a day from a counter that never closes. The hot salt-beef beigel with mustard is the order; the 2am queue of clubbers, cabbies and the occasional pop star is the floor show. The last working trace of the Lane's Jewish East End.",city:"london",oh:"24/7"},{id:"jammemasjid",n:"Brick Lane Jamme Masjid",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5202,lng:-0.0721,c:"history",s:"four faiths, one roof",q:"Brick Lane Jamme Masjid mosque",w:"One building that holds the whole story of immigrant London: built as a Huguenot chapel in 1743, it became a Methodist chapel, then a synagogue for Jewish refugees, and since the 1970s a Bangladeshi mosque. The sundial high on the Fournier Street corner still reads 'Umbra Sumus' — 'we are but shadows'.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Brick_Lane_Mosque2.JPG/960px-Brick_Lane_Mosque2.JPG"},{id:"dustyknuckle",n:"The Dusty Knuckle",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.5466,lng:-0.0748,c:"brunch",s:"London's best, they say",q:"The Dusty Knuckle Dalston bakery",w:"Repeatedly crowned London's best bakery, born in a shipping container in a Dalston car park in 2014 and now a social enterprise training young people facing barriers to work. Famous for potato sourdough and doorstop focaccia sandwiches — the chilli, egg and cheese is the cult one. Worth the trek east.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 08:00-15:30"},{id:"toad",n:"Toad Bakery",a:"Camberwell",pc:"SE5",lat:51.4742,lng:-0.0808,c:"brunch",s:"the SE5 queue",q:"Toad Bakery Camberwell",w:"A tiny Camberwell shopfront with a daily-changing menu and an orderly queue down Peckham Road every weekend. Open-plan kitchen, exceptional pastries and sandwiches, and one of the spots that earned the area its 'coolest neighbourhood' billing. Get there early or miss out.",city:"london",oh:"Tu-Sa 08:00-15:00; Mo,Su off",wq:"a"},{id:"arome",n:"Arôme Bakery",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5135,lng:-0.1238,c:"brunch",s:"French-Asian, near work",q:"Arome Bakery Covent Garden London",w:"A French-Asian bakery near Covent Garden where Parisian technique meets Singapore and Tokyo — miso-bacon escargots, sausage-and-cheese croissants with Japanese barbecue sauce, pillowy shokupan. One of the most-garlanded bakeries in the city, and a short walk from the office.",city:"london",oh:"We-Sa 08:00-17:00; Su 09:00-16:00",wq:"a"},{id:"fortitude",n:"Fortitude Bakehouse",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1",lat:51.5238,lng:-0.1232,c:"brunch",s:"beignets, blue doors",q:"Fortitude Bakehouse Bloomsbury",w:"A blue-doored Bloomsbury gem with queues for its beignets and buttery savoury pastries. Tiny, beloved, and the ideal detour between Soho and the British Museum. Two benches outside — get there early.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:30-15:00; Sa-Su 08:30-14:30",wq:"a"},{id:"highgate",n:"Highgate Cemetery",a:"Highgate",pc:"N6",lat:51.5668,lng:-0.147,c:"history",s:"Marx, Adams, McLaren",q:"Highgate Cemetery",w:"The Gothic showpiece of the Magnificent Seven, 170,000 graves climbing the hillside under ivy and Egyptian-style avenues. Karl Marx glowers from his tomb; Douglas Adams gets pots of pens and the number 42; Patrick Caulfield's stone simply reads 'DEAD', and Malcolm McLaren's quotes 'better a spectacular failure than a benign success'. George Eliot, George Michael and Lucian Freud are here too.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Highgate_Cemetery_East.JPG/960px-Highgate_Cemetery_East.JPG"},{id:"frankscafe",n:"Frank's Cafe & Bold Tendencies",a:"Peckham",pc:"SE15",lat:51.471,lng:-0.0688,c:"pop",s:"summer rooftop, SE15",q:"Frank's Cafe Bold Tendencies Peckham",w:"Every summer since 2009, the top deck of a Peckham multi-storey car park becomes the city's best rooftop bar — Negronis, St Paul's on the skyline, and the millennial-pink staircase that launched a thousand photos. Part of the Bold Tendencies art project, with sculpture and concerts on the floors below. Open roughly May to September, no bookings.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"polishislington",n:"Polish Church, Devonia Road",a:"Islington",pc:"N1",lat:51.5338,lng:-0.1033,c:"diaspora",s:"Polish Mission HQ since 1930",q:"Our Lady of Czestochowa and St Casimir Devonia Road Islington",w:"Home of the Polish Catholic Mission in England since 1930, in a Gothic former Swedenborgian seminary near Angel. Its 1945 stained glass — by Adam Bunsch, a Polish soldier-artist — depicts the struggle for Polish sovereignty rather than the usual saints. The quiet north-London counterpart to Bobola in the west.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wiltonshall",n:"Wilton's Music Hall",a:"Wapping",pc:"E1",lat:51.5108,lng:-0.0668,c:"music",s:"oldest grand music hall",q:"Wilton's Music Hall Graces Alley",w:"The oldest surviving grand music hall in the world (1859), hidden down an East End alley behind a row of old houses. It served as a Methodist mission and a rag warehouse before being saved from demolition; today it stages theatre and gigs in gloriously unrestored, candlelit decay. The barley-twist gallery columns are originals.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 12:00-23:00; Sa 17:00-23:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/The_Front_Door_of_Wilton%27_s_Music_Hall_%282010%29.jpg"},{id:"prospectwhitby",n:"The Prospect of Whitby",a:"Wapping",pc:"E1W",lat:51.5083,lng:-0.0512,c:"food",s:"c.1520 riverside",q:"Prospect of Whitby Wapping Wall",w:"A riverside tavern with roots claimed to around 1520 — reputedly the oldest on the Thames. Smugglers and the 'Hanging Judge' Jeffreys drank here; Pepys, Turner and Whistler came later; a noose still hangs over the water as a nod to nearby Execution Dock. The pewter-topped bar is centuries old.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Wapping_prospect_of_whitby_1.jpg"},{id:"observatory",n:"Royal Observatory & Prime Meridian",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10",lat:51.4769,lng:-0.0005,c:"history",s:"0° longitude, GMT",q:"Royal Observatory Greenwich Prime Meridian",w:"Stand astride the Prime Meridian and you're in both hemispheres at once — 0° longitude, the line from which the whole world has set its clocks since 1884. Wren's observatory, Flamsteed's house, and the red Time Ball that still drops at 1pm daily. Greenwich Mean Time was effectively invented on this hill.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Royal_observatory_greenwich.jpg"},{id:"cpdinosaurs",n:"Crystal Palace Dinosaurs",a:"Crystal Palace",pc:"SE19",lat:51.4185,lng:-0.067,c:"oddity",s:"first dinosaurs, 1854",q:"Crystal Palace Dinosaurs",w:"The world's first dinosaur sculptures, unveiled in 1854 — some thirty concrete monsters lurking around a south London lake, gloriously wrong by modern science (the Iguanodon wears its thumb-spike on its nose). The sculptor held a famous dinner inside the Iguanodon mould on New Year's Eve 1853. Grade I listed, and pure Victorian swagger.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Mantellodon_in_Crystal_Palace_Park.jpg/960px-Mantellodon_in_Crystal_Palace_Park.jpg"},{id:"soane",n:"Sir John Soane's Museum",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5168,lng:-0.1175,c:"history",s:"free · Seti I sarcophagus",q:"Sir John Soane's Museum Lincoln's Inn Fields",w:"The architect of the Bank of England left his Lincoln's Inn Fields house exactly as he lived in it — crammed floor to ceiling with antiquities, including the alabaster sarcophagus of the pharaoh Seti I, which he bought after the British Museum balked at the price. Hogarth's entire 'Rake's Progress' hangs on folding walls. Free, labyrinthine, and unmissable on a candlelit late opening.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Sir_John_Soane_Museum_%2813952611347%29.jpg/960px-Sir_John_Soane_Museum_%2813952611347%29.jpg"},{id:"abney",n:"Abney Park Cemetery",a:"Stoke Newington",pc:"N16",lat:51.564,lng:-0.076,c:"history",s:"overgrown · William Booth",q:"Abney Park Cemetery Stoke Newington",w:"A Magnificent Seven cemetery turned wild — a deliberate Victorian arboretum gone gloriously feral, with a derelict Gothic chapel at its heart. William Booth, founder of the Salvation Army, is buried here among nonconformists and music-hall stars. Stoke Newington's overgrown green secret.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Abney_Park_Chapel_2.jpg/960px-Abney_Park_Chapel_2.jpg"},{id:"roundhouse",n:"The Roundhouse",a:"Chalk Farm",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5435,lng:-0.1528,c:"music",s:"the Doors' only UK gig",q:"The Roundhouse Chalk Farm",w:"A Victorian railway engine shed — built in 1847 to turn locomotives on a giant turntable — that became a temple of 1960s counterculture. The Doors played their only UK shows here; Pink Floyd, Hendrix and later the Ramones followed. Still a working venue under that vast iron-ribbed roof.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/The_Roundhouse%2C_Chalk_Farm_Road%2C_London_NW1_-_geograph.org.uk_-_399270.jpg"},{id:"dauntbooks",n:"Daunt Books Marylebone",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1",lat:51.5212,lng:-0.1505,c:"bookshops",s:"galleried travel bookshop",q:"Daunt Books Marylebone High Street",w:"An Edwardian bookshop built for travel — long oak galleries, a stained-glass window, and a skylit back room where books are shelved by country, so a guidebook sits beside that nation's novels and history. Widely called the most beautiful bookshop in London, and it knows it.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 09:00-19:30; Su 11:00-18:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Daunt_Books%27s_famous_gallery.jpg"},{id:"littlevenice",n:"Little Venice",a:"Maida Vale",pc:"W9",lat:51.5225,lng:-0.1828,c:"canals",s:"canal junction, W9",q:"Little Venice Browning's Pool London",w:"Where the Regent's and Grand Union canals meet in a willow-lined pool of houseboats — Robert Browning, who lived nearby, is credited with the name. A floating puppet-theatre barge, waterside cafés, and the towpath walk to Camden all start here. Genuinely calm, minutes from Paddington.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"godsjunkyard",n:"God's Own Junkyard",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"E17",lat:51.5858,lng:-0.0148,c:"oddity",s:"neon cathedral, weekends",q:"God's Own Junkyard Walthamstow",w:"A Walthamstow warehouse blazing with salvaged neon — the life's work of the late Chris Bracey, who lit up Soho's sex shops and made signs for Kubrick and the Batman films. Now a family-run gallery with the Rolling Scones café among the glow; open weekends. Sensory overload, in the best possible way.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"monmouth",n:"Monmouth Coffee",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5141,lng:-0.1268,c:"coffee",s:"since 1978",q:"Monmouth Coffee Covent Garden",w:"The shop that effectively started London's specialty-coffee story, roasting on Monmouth Street since 1978. A wood-panelled hatch, single-origin beans weighed out by people who clearly care, and a flat white that still sets the citywide benchmark. The Borough Market branch is the bigger sibling.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 08:00-18:30"},{id:"prufrock",n:"Prufrock Coffee",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5205,lng:-0.1088,c:"coffee",s:"world-champion roots",q:"Prufrock Coffee Leather Lane",w:"Opened on Leather Lane in 2011 by 2009 World Barista Champion Gwilym Davies and now run by Square Mile Roasters — the closest London has to a coffee chapel, with a brew bar you can watch and a training lab out back. Serious filters, excellent eggs Benedict, zero pretension.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:30-16:30; Sa 08:00-17:00; Su 09:00-17:00",wq:"a"},{id:"kaffeine",n:"Kaffeine",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"W1",lat:51.5188,lng:-0.1408,c:"coffee",s:"antipodean benchmark",q:"Kaffeine Great Titchfield Street",w:"An Australian-Kiwi café on Great Titchfield Street that's been a Fitzrovia institution since 2009 — precise coffee, deli-style breakfasts, and the kind of consistency the whole London scene quietly copies. Ten minutes from the office and worth the walk.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:30-17:00; Sa 08:30-17:00; Su 09:00-17:00",wq:"a"},{id:"carbonkopi",n:"Carbon Kopi",a:"Hammersmith",pc:"W6",lat:51.4925,lng:-0.2295,c:"coffee",s:"NZ transplant, local",q:"Carbon Kopi Hammersmith",w:"A New Zealand coffee operation that crossed the world to land in Hammersmith in 2019 and promptly got named one of the UK's best. Tight menu, immaculate flat whites, and the best serious coffee within easy reach of your flat.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rosslyn",n:"Rosslyn Coffee",a:"The City",pc:"EC4",lat:51.5132,lng:-0.0905,c:"coffee",s:"City favourite",q:"Rosslyn Coffee City of London",w:"Irish-Australian precision dropped into the Square Mile — a tiny standing-room counter that consistently ranks among the world's best coffee shops. The morning bun and a batch brew is the move before a day of meetings.",city:"london"},{id:"mountain",n:"Mountain",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5118,lng:-0.1388,c:"food",s:"Tomos Parry, wood-fire",q:"Mountain restaurant Beak Street Soho",w:"Tomos Parry's follow-up to Brat — a Beak Street room built around live fire and Catalan-Basque flavours, all wood-roasted seafood, aged beef and bread you'll think about later. One of the hardest Soho tables to get, a few minutes from your desk.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kiln",n:"Kiln",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.1355,c:"food",s:"counter Thai BBQ",q:"Kiln Brewer Street Soho",w:"Sit at the counter on Brewer Street and watch clay pots and wood embers turn out fierce northern-Thai cooking — the clay-pot baked glass noodles with Tamworth pork are a modern London classic. No bookings at the counter; go solo, go early.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-14:30, 17:00-22:30; Su 13:00-20:00",wq:"a"},{id:"baosoho",n:"BAO Soho",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.1373,c:"food",s:"the gua bao cult",q:"BAO Soho Lexington Street",w:"The pillowy Taiwanese steamed-bun phenomenon that started as a market stall — the classic braised-pork bao and the fried-chicken bao launched a thousand imitators. Tiny, yellow-fronted, perpetual queue on Lexington Street.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lyles",n:"Lyle's",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E1",lat:51.5235,lng:-0.0778,c:"food",s:"Michelin, daily-changing",q:"Lyle's Shoreditch",w:"James Lowe's stripped-back, Michelin-starred room in the old Tea Building — a short daily-changing menu of impeccable British produce, no fuss, no tablecloths. One of the quiet pillars of modern London cooking.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 08:00-23:00; Sa 12:00-23:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Lyle%27s_restaurant_exterior.jpg/960px-Lyle%27s_restaurant_exterior.jpg"},{id:"brat",n:"Brat",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E1",lat:51.5238,lng:-0.0758,c:"food",s:"whole-grilled turbot",q:"Brat restaurant Shoreditch",w:"Basque wood-fire cooking up a Redchurch Street staircase — the whole grilled turbot is the signature, blistered and basted over coals, and the reason the room is permanently full. Tomos Parry's first, and still essential.",city:"london"},{id:"qualitychop",n:"Quality Chop House",a:"Farringdon",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5232,lng:-0.1092,c:"food",s:"since 1869",q:"Quality Chop House Farringdon Road",w:"A working-class chophouse from 1869 with the Grade II-listed wooden booths to prove it — 'Progressive Working Class Caterer' still etched on the glass. The confit potatoes are legendary; the wine shop next door is the trap on the way out.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-15:00, 18:00-23:00; Su 12:00-16:00",wq:"a"},{id:"noblerot",n:"Noble Rot",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1",lat:51.5228,lng:-0.1188,c:"food",s:"wine-mag wine bar",q:"Noble Rot Lamb's Conduit Street",w:"The wine magazine made flesh: a dark, brilliant wine bar and dining room on Lamb's Conduit Street with a list that reads like a novel and food serious enough to match. Ex-site of the much-loved Vats; book the back room.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 12:00-23:00",wq:"a"},{id:"hunterian",n:"Hunterian Museum",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5152,lng:-0.1168,c:"oddity",s:"free · not for the squeamish",q:"Hunterian Museum Royal College of Surgeons Lincoln's Inn Fields",w:"Reopened in 2023 and free, this is the surgeon John Hunter's 18th-century collection — some 3,500 anatomical specimens in glass, alongside Joseph Lister's instruments and four wooden anatomy tables made for the diarist John Evelyn in 1646. Not for the squeamish, and one of London's great 'this room changed medicine' stops — two doors from the Soane Museum.",city:"london"},{id:"wellcome",n:"Wellcome Collection",a:"Euston",pc:"NW1",lat:51.526,lng:-0.1335,c:"history",s:"free · the Reading Room",q:"Wellcome Collection Euston Road",w:"A free museum of medicine and what it means to be human, on Euston Road. The spiralling Reading Room — part library, part gallery, all sofas and curiosities — is the real draw, alongside the 'Being Human' gallery of prosthetics, genetics and the body. Thoughtful, strange, and quiet.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/The_Wellcome_Building.jpg/960px-The_Wellcome_Building.jpg"},{id:"bellfoundry",n:"Whitechapel Bell Foundry",a:"Whitechapel",pc:"E1",lat:51.5168,lng:-0.0668,c:"history",s:"cast Big Ben & Liberty Bell",q:"Whitechapel Bell Foundry",w:"Britain's oldest manufacturing company stood here from 1570 until 2017 — the foundry that cast Big Ben in 1858 and the Liberty Bell in 1752. The Grade II frontage still faces Whitechapel Road, though it's controversially being turned into a hotel. Stand outside and consider how much of the world this little works made ring.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Whitechapel_Bell_Foundry.jpg/960px-Whitechapel_Bell_Foundry.jpg"},{id:"crossbones",n:"Crossbones Graveyard",a:"Southwark",pc:"SE1",lat:51.503,lng:-0.0925,c:"oddity",s:"the outcast dead · ribbon gates",q:"Crossbones Graveyard Redcross Way",w:"A medieval paupers' burial ground where an estimated 15,000 of London's 'outcast dead' lie — by tradition the 'Winchester Geese', the sex workers once licensed by the Bishop of Winchester. The red gates on Redcross Way are festooned with ribbons and trinkets; a vigil is held on the 23rd of every month. The garden opens limited hours, but you can always read the shrine through the railings.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"horniman",n:"Horniman Museum",a:"Forest Hill",pc:"SE23",lat:51.4413,lng:-0.0613,c:"oddity",s:"the overstuffed walrus",q:"Horniman Museum Forest Hill",w:"A free, art-nouveau museum built by a Victorian tea magnate — and home to the internet-famous overstuffed walrus, taxidermied from a dried skin by people who'd never seen a live one, so he hasn't a single wrinkle. Add an aquarium, the UK's largest musical-instrument gallery, and sixteen acres of gardens with a long view back to the city.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/An_aerial_view_of_the_Horniman_Museum_and_Gardens.png/960px-An_aerial_view_of_the_Horniman_Museum_and_Gardens.png"},{id:"dulwichpg",n:"Dulwich Picture Gallery",a:"Dulwich",pc:"SE21",lat:51.4456,lng:-0.0876,c:"history",s:"England's first public gallery",q:"Dulwich Picture Gallery",w:"The oldest purpose-built public art gallery in England (1817), designed by Sir John Soane — Rembrandt, Rubens, Poussin and Canaletto in top-lit rooms, with the founders entombed in a strange amber-lit mausoleum at the building's heart. A serene south-London pilgrimage for anyone who loved the Soane Museum.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Dulwich_Picture_Gallery%2C_main_entrance.JPG/960px-Dulwich_Picture_Gallery%2C_main_entrance.JPG"},{id:"kenwood",n:"Kenwood House",a:"Hampstead",pc:"NW3",lat:51.5715,lng:-0.168,c:"history",s:"free · Rembrandt & Vermeer",q:"Kenwood House Hampstead Heath",w:"A free neoclassical villa on the edge of Hampstead Heath with Robert Adam interiors and a staggering art collection — Rembrandt's late self-portrait, Vermeer's 'Guitar Player', Gainsborough and Turner, all left to the nation. The terrace view across the Heath to the City skyline is one of London's finest.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Kenwood_House_2.jpg/960px-Kenwood_House_2.jpg"},{id:"spaniards",n:"The Spaniards Inn",a:"Hampstead",pc:"NW3",lat:51.5718,lng:-0.1782,c:"food",s:"1585 · Turpin & Dickens",q:"The Spaniards Inn Hampstead",w:"A 1585 coaching inn on the edge of the Heath, dark and creaking and thick with legend — the highwayman Dick Turpin is said to have been born here, Dickens put it in 'Pickwick', Keats wrote nearby, and Bram Stoker has Dracula's pursuers stop in. The walled garden is the summer prize.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"freud",n:"Freud Museum",a:"Hampstead",pc:"NW3",lat:51.5497,lng:-0.1772,c:"history",s:"the couch is here",q:"Freud Museum Maresfield Gardens London",w:"Sigmund Freud's last home, where he fled in 1938 after the Nazis took Vienna — and where the actual psychoanalytic couch, draped in its Persian rug, still sits in his study among his antiquities. His daughter Anna, a pioneer in her own right, lived on here for decades. Quietly extraordinary.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Freud_Museum_London%2C_20_Maresfield_Gardens_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7605194.jpg/960px-Freud_Museum_London%2C_20_Maresfield_Gardens_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7605194.jpg"},{id:"willowroad",n:"2 Willow Road",a:"Hampstead",pc:"NW3",lat:51.556,lng:-0.1722,c:"oddity",s:"the Bond-villain house",q:"2 Willow Road National Trust Hampstead",w:"Ernő Goldfinger's 1939 modernist home, preserved by the National Trust with its ingenious folding rooms and a wall of modern art (Henry Moore, Max Ernst). Its odd claim to fame: neighbour Ian Fleming so loathed the architect that he named a Bond villain Goldfinger out of spite.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"keatshouse",n:"Keats House",a:"Hampstead",pc:"NW3",lat:51.5556,lng:-0.1672,c:"history",s:"where Keats wrote",q:"Keats House Hampstead",w:"The modest white villa where John Keats lodged from 1818 — he wrote 'Ode to a Nightingale' under a plum tree in the garden, fell in love with the girl next door, Fanny Brawne, then left for Rome to die at twenty-five. Romantic poetry's most poignant address.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Keats_House.jpg/960px-Keats_House.jpg"},{id:"bunhill",n:"Bunhill Fields",a:"Finsbury",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5232,lng:-0.0878,c:"green",s:"Blake, Defoe & Bunyan",q:"Bunhill Fields burial ground City Road",w:"A walled nonconformist burial ground just beyond the old City wall, its name a corruption of 'Bone Hill'. William Blake, Daniel Defoe and John Bunyan all lie here among the plane trees; Blake's stone still draws pilgrims and fresh flowers. A startlingly peaceful pause off the City Road.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Bunhill_Fields_2020.jpg/960px-Bunhill_Fields_2020.jpg"},{id:"barbicancons",n:"Barbican Conservatory",a:"Barbican",pc:"EC2",lat:51.52,lng:-0.0938,c:"green",s:"jungle in the concrete",q:"Barbican Conservatory",w:"Hidden inside the brutalist Barbican is a steamy tropical jungle — more than 1,500 species of plants and trees spilling over the concrete around fish ponds, the second-largest conservatory in London. It opens only on select days, so check ahead; palms against raw concrete is the entire point.",city:"london"},{id:"chelseaphysic",n:"Chelsea Physic Garden",a:"Chelsea",pc:"SW3",lat:51.4842,lng:-0.164,c:"green",s:"since 1673 · walled",q:"Chelsea Physic Garden",w:"London's oldest botanic garden, founded by the Society of Apothecaries in 1673 to grow medicinal plants by the Thames. A walled four acres of rare and poisonous specimens, the country's oldest rock garden, and a microclimate mild enough for grapefruit to ripen outdoors. Paid entry, and worth it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"abbeyroad",n:"Abbey Road Crossing",a:"St John's Wood",pc:"NW8",lat:51.532,lng:-0.1775,c:"music",s:"the Beatles crossing",q:"Abbey Road Studios zebra crossing",w:"The most photographed zebra crossing on earth, outside the studios where the Beatles recorded almost everything and shot that 1969 cover. Tourists still hold up St John's Wood traffic to recreate the stride, and the studio wall is a rolling autograph book. Mind the cars — it's a live road.",city:"london"},{id:"allypally",n:"Alexandra Palace",a:"Muswell Hill",pc:"N22",lat:51.5945,lng:-0.13,c:"pop",s:"birthplace of TV · big views",q:"Alexandra Palace",w:"The Victorian 'People's Palace' on a north-London hilltop — and the birthplace of television: the BBC made the world's first regular high-definition broadcasts from here in 1936. The transmitter mast still crowns the ridge, and the terrace view back over the whole city is enormous.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Stage_Machinery_at_Alexandra_Palace_Theatre.jpg/960px-Stage_Machinery_at_Alexandra_Palace_Theatre.jpg"},{id:"templechurch",n:"Temple Church",a:"Temple",pc:"EC4",lat:51.5135,lng:-0.1105,c:"history",s:"the Templars' round church",q:"Temple Church London",w:"A round church built by the Knights Templar and consecrated in 1185, buried in the lawyers' warren of the Inns of Court — stone effigies of medieval knights lie across the floor of the nave. Made famous again by 'The Da Vinci Code', genuinely atmospheric, and very easy to walk past.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Temple_Church%2C_Temple%2C_London_EC4_%282%29.jpeg"},{id:"greatstbarts",n:"St Bartholomew the Great",a:"Smithfield",pc:"EC1",lat:51.519,lng:-0.0998,c:"history",s:"Norman, since 1123",q:"St Bartholomew the Great Smithfield",w:"London's oldest surviving parish church, founded in 1123 beside Smithfield — a dim, massive Norman interior that feels centuries older than anything around it. You'll recognise it from 'Four Weddings', 'Shakespeare in Love' and a dozen films. Enter under the Tudor gatehouse off the old meat market.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/St_Bartholomew-the-Great%2C_2025-02-28_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-St_Bartholomew-the-Great%2C_2025-02-28_%28cropped%29.jpg"},{id:"yeoldemitre",n:"Ye Olde Mitre",a:"Holborn",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5187,lng:-0.1075,c:"food",s:"1546 · hidden up an alley",q:"Ye Olde Mitre Ely Court London",w:"A tavern founded in 1546 and so well hidden up Ely Court that even cabbies miss it — once legally part of Cambridgeshire, so City constables couldn't make arrests inside. A preserved cherry tree marks where Elizabeth I is said to have danced; the snug back rooms are all dark oak. Closed weekends.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Ye_Olde_Mitre_Tavern_-_geograph.org.uk_-_763877.jpg"},{id:"trinitybuoy",n:"Trinity Buoy Wharf",a:"Leamouth",pc:"E14",lat:51.5085,lng:0.0085,c:"oddity",s:"lighthouse · 1,000-year song",q:"Trinity Buoy Wharf lighthouse London",w:"London's only lighthouse stands where the Lea meets the Thames, on a spot where Michael Faraday once ran lighthouse experiments. Inside it plays 'Longplayer' — a piece of music composed to run a thousand years without repeating, begun on New Year's 2000. Around it sits a quirky village of stacked shipping containers and an American diner.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Bow_Creek_Lighthouse_dusk.JPG/960px-Bow_Creek_Lighthouse_dusk.JPG"},{id:"the100club",n:"The 100 Club",a:"Soho",pc:"W1",lat:51.5163,lng:-0.134,c:"music",s:"jazz to punk, since 1942",q:"100 Club Oxford Street London",w:"A scruffy Oxford Street basement that's been a music room since 1942 — wartime jazz, the Rolling Stones, and the cradle of British punk: the legendary two-night Punk Special in 1976 with the Sex Pistols, the Clash and the Buzzcocks. Still putting bands on under your feet while the shoppers stream past above.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Link_Quartet_live_at_100_Club_-_2011.jpg/960px-Link_Quartet_live_at_100_Club_-_2011.jpg"},{id:"marxlibrary",n:"Marx Memorial Library",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1",lat:51.5225,lng:-0.106,c:"finance",s:"where Lenin worked",q:"Marx Memorial Library Clerkenwell Green",w:"On Clerkenwell Green, in a 1738 former charity school, sits a library of radical and labour history — and the small back room where Lenin edited the revolutionary paper 'Iskra' in 1902–03 before smuggling it into Tsarist Russia. The Green has been London's protest pitch for centuries; visits are by tour.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Marx_Memorial_Library_37a_Clerkenwell_Green.jpg/960px-Marx_Memorial_Library_37a_Clerkenwell_Green.jpg"},{id:"woodygrill",n:"Woody Grill",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.5046,lng:-0.2224,c:"food",s:"charcoal grill, open late",q:"Woody Grill 1 Uxbridge Road Shepherd's Bush",w:"A Shepherd's Bush charcoal-grill stalwart right on Uxbridge Road — Turkish and wider Middle Eastern, everything cooked over fire in the open kitchen and portions built for two. Lamb shish, kofte and a pile of bread; cheap, generous, open late, two minutes off the Green.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"truthcafe",n:"Truth Cafe",a:"Hammersmith",pc:"W6",lat:51.4925,lng:-0.2235,c:"brunch",s:"global brunch, Hammersmith",q:"Truth Cafe Hammersmith London",w:"A bright, easygoing Hammersmith brunch room that globe-trots its breakfasts — Mexican chipotle eggs Benedict, sweet-potato waffles, towering American pancakes, with gluten-free and vegan well covered. Dog-friendly, a few tables outside, minutes from the Tube.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vintagecafe",n:"Vintage Café Shoreditch",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E2",lat:51.5236,lng:-0.0718,c:"brunch",s:"all-day breakfast, off Brick Lane",q:"Vintage Cafe Shoreditch Cheshire Street",w:"A cosy, nostalgically furnished café on Cheshire Street just off Brick Lane — all-day breakfasts, Eggs Royal, smashed avocado on sourdough and a properly good iced coffee, run warmly and independently. The kind of unhurried Sunday spot the East End does so well.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"burnt",n:"Burnt",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.506,lng:-0.239,c:"brunch",s:"brunch every day · Askew Rd",q:"Burnt 163 Askew Road London",w:"An Askew Road favourite that treats brunch as the main event, every day of the week — creative plates well beyond the avocado cliché, a cult fried-chicken sandwich, Climpson & Sons coffee and Dusty Knuckle pastries given a house twist. Light, plant-filled, with a back garden for the sun.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"orchardaskew",n:"Orchard",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.5052,lng:-0.2392,c:"food",s:"seasonal · Askew Rd",q:"Orchard 136 Askew Road London W12",w:"A quietly excellent neighbourhood café-restaurant at the top of Askew Road, with a seasonal menu that shifts to whatever's actually good — relaxed weekend brunch, honest midweek dinners, the kind of place locals get territorial about. Book ahead on a sunny Saturday.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wildthyme",n:"Wild Thyme",a:"Shepherd's Bush",pc:"W12",lat:51.5042,lng:-0.2268,c:"brunch",s:"local brunch favourite",q:"Wild Thyme cafe Shepherd's Bush London",w:"A long-running, much-recommended brunch spot tucked on a residential street off Uxbridge Road — French toast, the hearty Wild Thyme Breakfast and a roll-call of classics done properly. One of Shepherd's Bush's most reliable weekend tables.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"climpsons",n:"Climpson & Sons",a:"Broadway Market",pc:"E8",lat:51.5378,lng:-0.0608,c:"coffee",s:"the East London roaster",q:"Climpson and Sons Broadway Market",w:"A Broadway Market institution and one of London's defining roasters since 2005 — the espresso that fuels half of East London's cafés, poured at the source from a tiny, perpetually busy shopfront. Grab a cup and walk the market on a Saturday.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:00-17:00; Sa 08:30-17:00; Su 09:00-17:00",wq:"a"},{id:"ozone",n:"Ozone Coffee Roasters",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"EC2A",lat:51.5258,lng:-0.084,c:"coffee",s:"NZ roastery, Shoreditch",q:"Ozone Coffee Roasters Leonard Street London",w:"A New Zealand roastery-café on a Shoreditch corner — roasting downstairs, serving a big, buzzy all-day menu upstairs, and pouring some of the most consistent flat whites in the city. A cornerstone of London's antipodean coffee wave.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"allpress",n:"Allpress Espresso",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E2",lat:51.524,lng:-0.0742,c:"coffee",s:"Redchurch St espresso",q:"Allpress Espresso Redchurch Street London",w:"The Kiwi roaster's Redchurch Street espresso bar — small, focused and excellent, the sort of place you queue at on the way to somewhere else. Beans roasted nearby; a flat white the obvious order.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"origincoffee",n:"Origin Coffee",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"EC2A",lat:51.5258,lng:-0.0795,c:"coffee",s:"Cornish roaster, Shoreditch",q:"Origin Coffee Charlotte Road London",w:"Cornwall's pioneering roaster brought to a sleek bar on Charlotte Road — single-origin filters and a flat white made with real care, in a calm, pared-back room. Quietly one of the best cups in Shoreditch.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 08:00-17:00; Sa-Su 08:00-16:00",wq:"a"},{id:"watchhouse",n:"WatchHouse — Bermondsey",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"SE1",lat:51.4985,lng:-0.0815,c:"coffee",s:"the Bermondsey hut",q:"WatchHouse Bermondsey Street London",w:"It began in a tiny Victorian watchman's hut on Bermondsey Street and grew into London's slickest specialty-coffee name — house-roasted beans, considered milk and a calm, design-led room. The original hut is still the one to seek out.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:00-18:00; Sa-Su 08:00-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"coffeeplant",n:"Coffeeplant",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"W11",lat:51.5175,lng:-0.2058,c:"coffee",s:"Portobello roaster",q:"Coffee Plant Portobello Road London",w:"A Portobello Road veteran roasting organic, fair-trade beans since the 1990s — no-frills and properly strong, in the middle of the market throng. A west-London antidote to the polished chains, and a fine fuel stop on a Portobello wander.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"barbican-estate",n:"Barbican Estate",a:"City",pc:"EC2Y",lat:51.52,lng:-0.0937,c:"brutalism",s:"the concrete city-within-a-city",q:"Barbican Centre London",w:"The largest brutalist complex in Britain and a self-contained 'city within a city' on a 35-acre WWII bombsite — 2,000 flats stacked above an arts centre, a lake, schools and the famous conservatory, laced together by elevated highwalks. Chamberlin, Powell & Bon's concrete masterwork, finished in 1976; follow the yellow line and get pleasurably lost.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Barbican_towers.jpg/960px-Barbican_towers.jpg"},{id:"trellick",n:"Trellick Tower",a:"Kensal Town",pc:"W10",lat:51.5215,lng:-0.2055,c:"brutalism",s:"Goldfinger's concrete icon",q:"Trellick Tower Golborne Road London",w:"Ernő Goldfinger's 31-storey brutalist landmark over Kensal Town, finished in 1972, with its slender slab and that detached, slot-windowed service tower. Once dubbed the 'Tower of Terror', now Grade II*-listed and one of London's most beloved concrete silhouettes — and barely a ten-minute walk from home.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"balfron",n:"Balfron Tower",a:"Poplar",pc:"E14",lat:51.5155,lng:-0.0105,c:"brutalism",s:"Trellick's Poplar prototype",q:"Balfron Tower Poplar London",w:"Trellick's older East End sibling — Goldfinger's 26-storey 1967 brutalist prototype, same dramatic detached lift tower, same raw concrete conviction. Goldfinger briefly moved into the top floor to prove it was liveable; since regenerated into private flats, controversially so. Best admired from the street.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"nationaltheatre",n:"National Theatre",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1",lat:51.507,lng:-0.114,c:"history",s:"Lasdun's concrete strata",q:"National Theatre South Bank London",w:"Denys Lasdun's 1976 brutalist masterpiece on the South Bank — board-marked concrete terraces stacked like geological strata, which Prince Charles likened to a nuclear power station and almost everyone else now adores. Catch a play, or just a drink on the riverside terrace at dusk.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/The_National_Theatre%2C_South_Bank%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1861458.jpg/960px-The_National_Theatre%2C_South_Bank%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1861458.jpg"},{id:"southbank-bru",n:"Hayward Gallery & Southbank Centre",a:"South Bank",pc:"SE1",lat:51.506,lng:-0.116,c:"history",s:"Hayward & the concrete undercroft",q:"Hayward Gallery Southbank Centre London",w:"The Southbank Centre's brutalist heart — the Hayward Gallery, Queen Elizabeth Hall and the walkways around them, all raw concrete, ramps and pyramidal rooflights from the 1960s. Skateboarders own the undercroft; the Hayward's exhibitions are among London's best.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Southbank_Centre_aerial_photo.jpg/960px-Southbank_Centre_aerial_photo.jpg"},{id:"alexandraroad",n:"Alexandra Road Estate (Rowley Way)",a:"South Hampstead",pc:"NW8",lat:51.5405,lng:-0.177,c:"brutalism",s:"Rowley Way's concrete ziggurat",q:"Alexandra Road Estate Rowley Way London",w:"Better known as Rowley Way, Neave Brown's 1978 ziggurat of pale concrete terraces steps down along a Camden railway cutting — a utopian, Grade II*-listed answer to the tower block, and a cult film location. One of the most humane things brutalism ever built.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brunswick",n:"Brunswick Centre",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1N",lat:51.524,lng:-0.1235,c:"brutalism",s:"Bloomsbury's stepped megastructure",q:"Brunswick Centre Bloomsbury London",w:"Patrick Hodgkinson's 1972 Bloomsbury megastructure — a low, stepped 'A-frame' of cream concrete terraces wrapped around a sunken shopping street, equal parts housing and piazza. Brutalism at its most sociable; grab a coffee and watch it work.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Brunswick_Centre_%284136275375%29.jpg/960px-Brunswick_Centre_%284136275375%29.jpg"},{id:"spacehouse",n:"Space House (One Kemble Street)",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC2B",lat:51.5145,lng:-0.119,c:"brutalism",s:"Seifert's concrete drum",q:"Space House One Kemble Street London",w:"Richard Seifert's 1968 'Space House' — a startling circular tower of pre-cast concrete Y-frames near Holborn, recently scrubbed back to life. Seifert also gave London Centre Point; this is his most sci-fi moment, a concrete drum hiding in plain sight.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Space_House_London_Covent_Garden_Office_Building.jpg/960px-Space_House_London_Covent_Garden_Office_Building.jpg"},{id:"centrepoint",n:"Centre Point",a:"West End",pc:"WC1A",lat:51.5163,lng:-0.1295,c:"history",s:"the honeycomb tower",q:"Centre Point Tottenham Court Road London",w:"Seifert's 1966 tower at the top of Charing Cross Road — 34 storeys of pre-cast concrete honeycomb that stood notoriously empty for years and became a byword for property speculation. Now flats; still one of the West End's most distinctive brutalist silhouettes.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Centre_Point%2C_New_Oxford_Street_%281%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2659023.jpg"},{id:"vaeast",n:"V&A East Storehouse",a:"Stratford",pc:"E20",lat:51.5465,lng:-0.0205,c:"history",s:"the salvaged Robin Hood Gardens façade",q:"V&A East Storehouse Stratford London",w:"When the brutalist Robin Hood Gardens estate was demolished, the V&A saved a three-storey chunk of its concrete façade — and you can now stand beneath it inside the free V&A East Storehouse, a working collection store you wander through like backstage at a museum. Brutalism preserved as a relic, opened 2025.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"admiralduncan",n:"The Admiral Duncan",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.5135,lng:-0.1303,c:"history",s:"Soho's defiant LGBTQ+ landmark",q:"Admiral Duncan Old Compton Street London",w:"One of Soho's oldest gay pubs, trading on Old Compton Street since the 1830s and an LGBTQ+ landmark since the '80s — among the first London venues to host drag. In 1999 a neo-Nazi nail bomb killed three people here; the pub reopened in defiance, and its memorial still anchors the street. Glittery, narrow, nightly cabaret.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rvt",n:"Royal Vauxhall Tavern",a:"Vauxhall",pc:"SE11",lat:51.4855,lng:-0.1235,c:"history",s:"the listed queer institution",q:"Royal Vauxhall Tavern London",w:"A Grade II-listed gin-palace that's one of the world's oldest surviving LGBTQ+ venues, going since the 1860s and home to the gloriously camp, political Saturday institution that is Duckie — the first building in Britain listed specifically for its queer history.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Royal_Vauxhall_Tavern%2C_Vauxhall%2C_SE11_%282866697236%29.jpg/960px-Royal_Vauxhall_Tavern%2C_Vauxhall%2C_SE11_%282866697236%29.jpg"},{id:"heaven",n:"Heaven",a:"Charing Cross",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5076,lng:-0.1241,c:"music",s:"the gay superclub under the arches",q:"Heaven nightclub Villiers Street London",w:"London's great gay superclub, under the railway arches by Charing Cross since 1979 — cavernous, multi-room, home of G-A-Y and the official Pride after-party, a stage where everyone from Madonna to Gaga has played. The cathedral of queer clubbing.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Heaven-Nightclub-Entrance-2025.jpg/960px-Heaven-Nightclub-Entrance-2025.jpg"},{id:"comptons",n:"Comptons of Soho",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.5134,lng:-0.1307,c:"history",s:"Old Compton Street's grande dame",q:"Comptons of Soho London",w:"The grande dame of Old Compton Street — a handsome two-floor traditional boozer that's anchored Soho's gay scene for decades: warm, unpretentious and reliably busy. The pub you start the night in.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"shesoho",n:"She Soho",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.5133,lng:-0.13,c:"history",s:"central London's sapphic home",q:"She Soho Old Compton Street London",w:"London's best-known bar for queer women and non-binary people — an intimate basement on Old Compton Street with cabaret, DJs and a rare, fiercely-loved lesbian home in the centre of town. Small, and all the better for it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dalstonsuperstore",n:"Dalston Superstore",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.547,lng:-0.0755,c:"music",s:"East London's queer hub",q:"Dalston Superstore Kingsland High Street London",w:"East London's queer heartbeat on Kingsland High Street — club, café, gallery and cabaret stage all at once, from day-drinking to dawn-dancing. The hub of a Dalston–Haggerston scene that's edgier and more experimental than Soho.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gaystheword",n:"Gay's the Word",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1N",lat:51.5253,lng:-0.1255,c:"bookshops",s:"the UK's oldest LGBTQ+ bookshop",q:"Gay's the Word bookshop Marchmont Street London",w:"Britain's oldest LGBTQ+ bookshop, on Marchmont Street since 1979 and the real-life meeting place behind the film 'Pride' — it's where Lesbians and Gays Support the Miners was born. It survived a 1984 Customs raid and remains a tiny, vital community cornerstone.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 10:00-18:30;Su 14:00-18:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Gays_the_Word_Storefront.jpg"},{id:"oscarwilde",n:"A Conversation with Oscar Wilde",a:"Charing Cross",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5092,lng:-0.1255,c:"oddity",s:"Wilde rising from the granite",q:"A Conversation with Oscar Wilde Adelaide Street London",w:"Maggi Hambling's 1998 granite sarcophagus-bench near Charing Cross, the writer's head rising from it cigarette in hand, inviting you to sit and talk. A memorial to Britain's most famous queer martyr, hiding on a traffic island off the Strand.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_Conversation_with_Oscar_Wilde_%2848928819126%29.jpg/960px-A_Conversation_with_Oscar_Wilde_%2848928819126%29.jpg"},{id:"queerbritain",n:"Queer Britain",a:"King's Cross",pc:"N1C",lat:51.5352,lng:-0.1255,c:"history",s:"the UK's first LGBTQ+ museum",q:"Queer Britain museum Granary Square London",w:"The UK's first dedicated LGBTQ+ museum, opened free in King's Cross in 2022 — compact galleries tracing queer life, protest and joy across British history, from Wolfenden to the present. A long-overdue home for the community's own story.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Queer_Britain.jpg/960px-Queer_Britain.jpg"},{id:"eaglehmd",n:"Eagle London",a:"Vauxhall",pc:"SE11",lat:51.4845,lng:-0.123,c:"music",s:"home of Horse Meat Disco",q:"Eagle London Kennington Lane London",w:"A Vauxhall institution and the spiritual home of Horse Meat Disco, the legendary queer Sunday party where disco, leather and everyone in between collides till the small hours. Unfussy, sweaty and joyous — Vauxhall's circuit at its best.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kyotogarden",n:"Kyoto Garden",a:"Holland Park",pc:"W8",lat:51.5018,lng:-0.203,c:"green",s:"the Japanese garden with peacocks",q:"Kyoto Garden Holland Park London",w:"A pocket of Japan in the middle of Holland Park — a kaiyū-shiki stroll garden of tiered waterfalls, a koi pond and stone lanterns, gifted by Kyoto in 1991. Peacocks patrol the paths; in autumn the Japanese acers turn to flame. Five minutes from home and endlessly calming.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stdunstaneast",n:"St Dunstan-in-the-East",a:"City",pc:"EC3R",lat:51.5095,lng:-0.082,c:"green",s:"a ruined church reclaimed by nature",q:"St Dunstan in the East garden London",w:"A ruined church turned secret garden in the City — St Dunstan-in-the-East was gutted in the 1941 Blitz, its tower left standing, and the roofless shell now wears climbing vines, palms and a quiet fountain. The most cinematic green corner in London; go early and have the churchyard to yourself.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/St_Dunstan-in-the-East.jpg/960px-St_Dunstan-in-the-East.jpg"},{id:"phoenixgarden",n:"The Phoenix Garden",a:"Soho",pc:"WC2H",lat:51.5145,lng:-0.1285,c:"green",s:"Soho's wild community garden",q:"Phoenix Garden Stacey Street London",w:"A scrap of wild calm behind the theatres between Soho and Covent Garden — the last of the area's 1980s community gardens, run by volunteers and left deliberately untidy for frogs, bees and birds. Bring a sandwich and disappear from the West End.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Phoenix_Gardens%2C_London_Borough_of_Camden%2C_WC2_%283304748918%29.jpg/960px-Phoenix_Gardens%2C_London_Borough_of_Camden%2C_WC2_%283304748918%29.jpg"},{id:"stjohnslodge",n:"St John's Lodge Garden",a:"Regent's Park",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5275,lng:-0.153,c:"green",s:"Regent's Park's hidden garden",q:"St John's Lodge Garden Regent's Park London",w:"Regent's Park's secret garden — slip through an unmarked gate off the Inner Circle into a chain of hedged 'rooms' designed in 1889 to be 'fit for meditation', with a pergola, fountains and white wisteria. Almost nobody realises it's open to the public.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"camleystreet",n:"Camley Street Natural Park",a:"King's Cross",pc:"N1C",lat:51.536,lng:-0.128,c:"green",s:"a canalside wild garden",q:"Camley Street Natural Park London",w:"A two-acre wild garden and nature reserve hidden beside the canal at King's Cross — reedbeds, woodland and a floating platform that bobs on the water, all a minute from Coal Drops Yard. Proof the city can rewild itself.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Camley_Street_Natural_Park.jpg/960px-Camley_Street_Natural_Park.jpg"},{id:"easterncurve",n:"Dalston Eastern Curve Garden",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.546,lng:-0.0755,c:"green",s:"a garden on an old railway",q:"Dalston Eastern Curve Garden London",w:"A much-loved community garden built along a disused railway curve in Dalston — a timber 'pineapple house', wildflowers, cake and gentle events, carved out of the East End by locals. The friendliest green space in Hackney.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"redcrossgarden",n:"Red Cross Garden",a:"Southwark",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5025,lng:-0.0925,c:"green",s:"a Victorian open-air sitting room",q:"Red Cross Garden Redcross Way London",w:"Octavia Hill's 'open-air sitting room for the tired inhabitants of Southwark', laid out in 1887 beside her model cottages — a pretty walled garden with a pond and bandstand, lovingly restored. A Victorian act of kindness you can still sit in.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Redcross_Gardens_and_Cottages_%287327429482%29.jpg/960px-Redcross_Gardens_and_Cottages_%287327429482%29.jpg"},{id:"amphitheatre",n:"Guildhall Roman Amphitheatre",a:"Guildhall",pc:"EC2V",lat:51.5159,lng:-0.0917,c:"roman",s:"gladiators under the yard",q:"Guildhall Roman Amphitheatre London",w:"London's only Roman amphitheatre, first raised in timber around AD 70 and rebuilt in stone by AD 120, with room for perhaps 7,000 baying spectators. Lost for centuries, rediscovered in 1988 beneath Guildhall Yard; a dark ellipse in the paving above traces it, the ruined arena dramatically lit in the gallery below.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bloombergtablets",n:"Bloomberg Writing Tablets",a:"Walbrook",pc:"EC4N",lat:51.5123,lng:-0.0905,c:"roman",s:"the AD 57 IOU",q:"Bloomberg Roman writing tablets oldest document",w:"Displayed free inside Bloomberg's HQ, above the Mithraeum: the oldest dated handwritten document from Britain, an IOU of 8 January AD 57 in which one Tibullus admits owing Gratus 105 denarii. One of 405 wooden tablets pulled from the Walbrook mud — ordinary Londoners' words, two thousand years on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"allhallows",n:"All Hallows by the Tower",a:"Tower Hill",pc:"EC3R",lat:51.5097,lng:-0.0784,c:"medieval",s:"oldest church, Roman floor",q:"All Hallows by the Tower Roman pavement",w:"The oldest church in the City, traditionally founded by Barking Abbey in 675 — a Saxon arch and a 2nd-century Roman tessellated pavement survive in the crypt. William Penn was baptised here and John Quincy Adams married here; Pepys climbed its tower to watch the Great Fire spread.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Southeastern_View_of_All_Hallows-by-the-Tower.jpg/960px-Southeastern_View_of_All_Hallows-by-the-Tower.jpg"},{id:"jeweltower",n:"Jewel Tower",a:"Westminster",pc:"SW1P",lat:51.4985,lng:-0.1268,c:"medieval",s:"medieval Westminster survivor",q:"Jewel Tower Westminster English Heritage",w:"A moated stone tower of around 1365, one of only two fragments of the old Palace of Westminster to survive — built to guard Edward III's personal treasure. Later it weighed the nation's official measures for three centuries. Quietly overlooked by the crowds streaming to the Abbey opposite.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Jewel_Tower.jpg"},{id:"winchesterpalace",n:"Winchester Palace",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5063,lng:-0.0905,c:"medieval",s:"the rose window",q:"Winchester Palace ruins Bankside rose window",w:"A skeletal gable wall on Bankside, pierced by a magnificent 14th-century rose window — all that survives of the riverside townhouse of the Bishops of Winchester, who ran Southwark (and licensed its brothels and bear-pits) for five centuries. Hidden until a fire in 1814 laid it bare.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Winchester_Palace_in_October_2020.jpg/960px-Winchester_Palace_in_October_2020.jpg"},{id:"stjohnsgate",n:"St John's Gate",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1M",lat:51.5223,lng:-0.102,c:"medieval",s:"Knights Hospitaller gate",q:"St John's Gate Clerkenwell Order of St John",w:"A startling slab of Tudor gatehouse marooned in Clerkenwell, built in 1504 as the grand south entrance to the priory of the Knights Hospitaller. Later a coffee house run by Hogarth's father and the offices of the Gentleman's Magazine; now the Order of St John and its small free museum.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/St_John%27s_Gate_2007_5.jpg/960px-St_John%27s_Gate_2007_5.jpg"},{id:"billingsgatebaths",n:"Billingsgate Roman House & Baths",a:"Lower Thames Street",pc:"EC3R",lat:51.5092,lng:-0.083,c:"roman",s:"a Roman home survives",q:"Billingsgate Roman House and Baths Lower Thames Street",w:"Beneath an office at 101 Lower Thames Street lie the only Roman house-and-baths in the City you can still walk into — a 2nd-century riverside dwelling with its own private bath suite and hypocaust. Opened only on special days; a genuine Roman home surviving under the traffic.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/Billingsgate_Cartographer%3B_Blome%2C_RichardSurveyor%3B_Stow%2C_John_1720.jpg"},{id:"londonwall",n:"London Wall (Cooper's Row)",a:"Tower Hill",pc:"EC3N",lat:51.5108,lng:-0.077,c:"roman",s:"2,000 years of wall",q:"London Wall Cooper's Row Roman",w:"Hidden in a hotel courtyard off Cooper's Row stands one of the finest surviving stretches of the Roman city wall — Roman masonry below, medieval above, with the very slots that held a sentry's walkway. Two thousand years of London's edge, free to walk up to at any hour.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/London_Roman_Wall_-_surviving_section_by_Tower_Hill_gardens_full_section.jpg/960px-London_Roman_Wall_-_surviving_section_by_Tower_Hill_gardens_full_section.jpg"},{id:"noblestreet",n:"Noble Street Roman Fort",a:"Barbican",pc:"EC2V",lat:51.5165,lng:-0.0967,c:"roman",s:"the fort's lost corner",q:"Noble Street Roman fort remains Barbican",w:"Peer over the railings near the Barbican and you are looking down at the south-west corner of London's Roman fort of around AD 120, its wall later thickened into the city defences. Blitz bombing stripped the later buildings and revealed it; an unloved fragment most stride straight past.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stmarylebowcrypt",n:"Crypt of St Mary-le-Bow",a:"Cheapside",pc:"EC2V",lat:51.5138,lng:-0.094,c:"medieval",s:"Norman arches under Wren",q:"St Mary-le-Bow crypt Norman Cheapside",w:"Beneath the church of the Bow Bells — within whose sound a true Cockney must be born — survives a Norman undercroft of around 1080, its stone arches (the 'bows') giving the church its name. Wren rebuilt above; the crypt now hides a café among the eleventh-century pillars.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/St_Mary_le_Bow.jpg/960px-St_Mary_le_Bow.jpg"},{id:"nunhead",n:"Nunhead Cemetery",a:"Nunhead",pc:"SE15",lat:51.4631,lng:-0.0556,c:"death",s:"overgrown & ruined",q:"Nunhead Cemetery ruined chapel",w:"The least-known and most romantically overgrown of the Magnificent Seven, consecrated in 1840 — ivy-swallowed monuments, a roofless Gothic chapel and a sudden keyhole view of St Paul's framed through the trees. Half wild nature reserve, half ruin; the connoisseur's London cemetery.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"westnorwood",n:"West Norwood Cemetery",a:"West Norwood",pc:"SE27",lat:51.431,lng:-0.101,c:"death",s:"the Greek necropolis",q:"West Norwood Cemetery Henry Tate Mrs Beeton",w:"Opened in 1837, a hilly Victorian necropolis famous for its Greek enclosure — a colony of Hellenic mausolea raised by London's Greek merchant princes. The sugar magnate Henry Tate and Mrs Beeton of cookery fame both rest here, among Gothic tombs and dark cedars.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"towerhamlets",n:"Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park",a:"Mile End",pc:"E3",lat:51.521,lng:-0.033,c:"death",s:"Blitzed nature reserve",q:"Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park",w:"The last of the Magnificent Seven (1841), which took perhaps 350,000 of the East End's poor before it closed. Bombed in the Blitz and abandoned, it is now a wild woodland nature reserve where toppled headstones lie under ivy — a graveyard returned almost wholly to the trees.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hardytree",n:"The Hardy Tree",a:"St Pancras",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5346,lng:-0.1283,c:"death",s:"a beautiful myth",q:"Hardy Tree St Pancras Old Church",w:"An ash in Old St Pancras churchyard once ringed by a dense spiral of gravestones — said (probably wrongly) to have been arranged by a young Thomas Hardy when the railway sliced through the burial ground in the 1860s. The tree itself fell in December 2022; the stones, and the lovely myth, remain.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Hardy_Ash%2C_St_Pancras_Old_Church.jpg/960px-Hardy_Ash%2C_St_Pancras_Old_Church.jpg"},{id:"charterhouse",n:"Charterhouse Square Plague Pit",a:"Farringdon",pc:"EC1M",lat:51.5205,lng:-0.098,c:"death",s:"Black Death mass grave",q:"Charterhouse Square Black Death plague pit",w:"Beneath this quiet Farringdon square lies one of London's great Black Death emergency cemeteries, opened in 1348 — nearby Crossrail engineers pulled up skeletons still carrying the plague bacterium. A Carthusian monastery, then almshouses, grew beside the dead; you sit on grass above thousands of them.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Charterhouse_Square.jpg/960px-Charterhouse_Square.jpg"},{id:"sevennoses",n:"Seven Noses of Soho",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.5129,lng:-0.1322,c:"oddity",s:"anti-CCTV protest",q:"Seven Noses of Soho Rick Buckley",w:"In 1997 the artist Rick Buckley secretly glued around 35 plaster casts of his own nose to buildings across London — a guerrilla protest against the spread of CCTV. About ten survive; tradition says finding all seven 'noses of Soho' brings infinite wealth. A spotting game hiding at eye level.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"admiraltynose",n:"Admiralty Arch Nose",a:"Trafalgar Square",pc:"SW1A",lat:51.5072,lng:-0.1276,c:"oddity",s:"not Wellington's nose",q:"Admiralty Arch nose Seven Noses",w:"A small nose juts from the inner wall of Admiralty Arch, about seven feet up. The beloved tale that soldiers rubbed Napoleon's (or Wellington's) nose for luck is charming nonsense — it is one of Buckley's 1997 noses. The most-touched of the Seven; give it a rub as you pass.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"leinstergardens",n:"Leinster Gardens Fake Houses",a:"Bayswater",pc:"W2",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.1836,c:"oddity",s:"façade hiding the gap",q:"Leinster Gardens fake houses 23 24",w:"Numbers 23 and 24 look like every other grand Bayswater stucco terrace — until you notice the windows are painted on. They are a five-foot-thick façade screening the open Underground cutting behind, built so the Metropolitan Railway's steam wouldn't scar the street. Decades of prank pizza deliveries have followed.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/No_41_-_46_Leinster_Gardens_%28geograph_4166526%29.jpg/960px-No_41_-_46_Leinster_Gardens_%28geograph_4166526%29.jpg"},{id:"smallestpolicestation",n:"Trafalgar Square Police Box",a:"Trafalgar Square",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5072,lng:-0.1273,c:"oddity",s:"the 'smallest police station'",q:"Trafalgar Square smallest police station lamp post",w:"A hollowed-out granite lamp-post plinth at the square's south-east corner, fitted in the 1920s with a tiny observation window and a direct line to Scotland Yard — long billed, with some exaggeration, as 'Britain's smallest police station'. It now stores cleaning kit; the legend is half the fun.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cartinglane",n:"Carting Lane Sewer Lamp",a:"Strand",pc:"WC2R",lat:51.5093,lng:-0.1207,c:"oddity",s:"the Iron Lily",q:"Carting Lane sewer gas lamp Webb patent",w:"On the steep lane behind the Savoy — locals call it 'Farting Lane' — stands London's last working Webb Patent Sewer Gas Destructor Lamp, quietly burning off the methane that rises from the drains below. Victorian ingenuity still alight; lean close and you can hear the gas.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"yorkwatergate",n:"York Watergate",a:"Embankment",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5078,lng:-0.123,c:"oddity",s:"the vanished riverbank",q:"York Watergate Embankment Gardens",w:"A grand ornamental arch built in 1626 as the private river-stairs of the Duke of Buckingham — step through it and you would once have stepped straight into a boat. It now stands stranded 150 metres from the Thames, marooned in Embankment Gardens when the Victorians reclaimed the riverbank.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/The_original_Watergate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7834659.jpg/960px-The_original_Watergate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7834659.jpg"},{id:"cleopatrasneedle",n:"Cleopatra's Needle",a:"Victoria Embankment",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.12,c:"oddity",s:"scarred by the first Blitz",q:"Cleopatra's Needle Embankment WWI bomb",w:"A genuinely ancient Egyptian obelisk, older than Rome, raised on the Embankment in 1878 and guarded by two bronze sphinxes — which face the wrong way. Its plinth still bears shrapnel scars from a 1917 air raid, among the first 'Blitz' wounds London ever took. A time capsule lies buried beneath.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Western_Side_of_Cleopatra%27s_Needle_in_London.jpg/960px-Western_Side_of_Cleopatra%27s_Needle_in_London.jpg"},{id:"littlecomptonstreet",n:"Little Compton Street",a:"Soho",pc:"WC2H",lat:51.5135,lng:-0.1295,c:"oddity",s:"a buried Victorian street",q:"Little Compton Street grille Charing Cross Road",w:"Peer down a grille on the traffic island where Old Compton Street meets Charing Cross Road and you will see two old Victorian street signs reading 'Little Compton Street', fixed to the wall of a thoroughfare swallowed when the road was widened. A whole street, still down there in the dark.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brixtonwindmill",n:"Brixton Windmill",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW2",lat:51.4533,lng:-0.1228,c:"oddity",s:"London's last windmill",q:"Brixton Windmill Blenheim Gardens",w:"A black tower mill of 1816 — 'Ashby's Mill' — that once ground flour amid open Surrey fields and is now improbably ringed by Brixton. London's last working windmill: restored, sails turning, open on summer days, a scrap of countryside the city simply grew around.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Windmill%2C_Brixton_Hill%2C_SW2_%283151353450%29.jpg/960px-Windmill%2C_Brixton_Hill%2C_SW2_%283151353450%29.jpg"},{id:"bruneltunnel",n:"Brunel's Thames Tunnel & Museum",a:"Rotherhithe",pc:"SE16",lat:51.5012,lng:-0.0531,c:"maritime",s:"first tunnel under a river",q:"Brunel Museum Thames Tunnel Rotherhithe",w:"The world's first tunnel under a navigable river — Marc Brunel and his son Isambard drove it beneath the Thames between 1825 and 1843 with a revolutionary tunnelling shield, at the cost of floods and lives. Overground trains rumble through it now; the museum crowns the original engine house and grand shaft.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Brunel_museum_mural.jpg"},{id:"executiondock",n:"Execution Dock",a:"Wapping",pc:"E1W",lat:51.5073,lng:-0.0566,c:"maritime",s:"where pirates hanged",q:"Execution Dock Wapping Captain Kidd",w:"For over 400 years the Admiralty hanged pirates, smugglers and mutineers here at the Wapping low-water mark — Captain Kidd among them in 1701 — leaving the bodies for three tides to wash over as a warning. A replica gallows by the river marks the grim spot, disputed between the pubs.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"townoframsgate",n:"Town of Ramsgate",a:"Wapping",pc:"E1W",lat:51.5079,lng:-0.0567,c:"pub",s:"Old Stairs & Judge Jeffreys",q:"Town of Ramsgate pub Wapping Old Stairs",w:"A narrow, ancient pub beside Wapping Old Stairs — quite likely the true site of Execution Dock. Press gangs once held men in its cellars, and the hated 'hanging judge' Jeffreys was seized nearby trying to flee disguised as a sailor. Step out the back for the river stairs the condemned descended.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"captainkidd",n:"The Captain Kidd",a:"Wapping",pc:"E1W",lat:51.5061,lng:-0.0556,c:"pub",s:"named for the pirate",q:"Captain Kidd pub Wapping",w:"A riverside pub named for the pirate hanged just along the shore in 1701 — and, despite the antique air, only opened in the 1980s in a converted warehouse. No matter: the Thames-side terrace and the view down to where the gallows stood make it the perfect place to toast a dead buccaneer.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mayflower",n:"The Mayflower",a:"Rotherhithe",pc:"SE16",lat:51.5018,lng:-0.0536,c:"pub",s:"where the Mayflower sailed",q:"Mayflower pub Rotherhithe 1620",w:"A snug, creaking riverside inn at the very Rotherhithe mooring where the Mayflower set off in 1620 to carry the Pilgrims to America. Her captain, Christopher Jones, lies in the churchyard opposite; the pub is still licensed to sell commemorative stamps. A wooden jetty hangs out over the water.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:00",wq:"a"},{id:"stmaryrotherhithe",n:"St Mary's Rotherhithe",a:"Rotherhithe",pc:"SE16",lat:51.5012,lng:-0.053,c:"maritime",s:"the Mayflower captain's grave",q:"St Mary's Rotherhithe Christopher Jones",w:"A handsome Georgian church on a medieval site, raised by sailors — burial place of Christopher Jones, captain of the Mayflower, who died here in 1622. Inside, a communion table and bishop's chair are carved from the timbers of the Temeraire, the old warship of Turner's great painting.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/St_Marys_Church_Rotherhithe.JPG"},{id:"clink",n:"The Clink Prison",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5072,lng:-0.0913,c:"history",s:"the original 'clink'",q:"Clink Prison Museum Clink Street",w:"The lock-up of the Bishops of Winchester from the 12th century, whose name became English slang for any jail — 'in the clink'. It held debtors, heretics and the very brothel-workers the bishops licensed, until a mob burned it in 1780. A small, ghoulish museum now keeps its memory on Clink Street.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Clink_Prison_Museum%2C_Soho_Wharf%2C_Clink_Street%2C_London%2C_United_Kingdom.JPG/960px-Clink_Prison_Museum%2C_Soho_Wharf%2C_Clink_Street%2C_London%2C_United_Kingdom.JPG"},{id:"hawleyarms",n:"The Hawley Arms",a:"Camden",pc:"NW1",lat:51.541,lng:-0.1466,c:"pub",s:"Amy Winehouse's local",q:"Hawley Arms Camden Amy Winehouse",w:"The Camden boozer where Amy Winehouse pulled pints and held court — 'as long as my family and friends are there, I could be anywhere,' she said of it. Gutted by the 2008 Camden Lock fire and rebuilt by the locals; still the beating, beery heart of indie Camden.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Hawley_Arms%2C_Camden_Town%2C_NW1_%283620924045%29.jpg/960px-Hawley_Arms%2C_Camden_Town%2C_NW1_%283620924045%29.jpg"},{id:"amywinehousestatue",n:"Amy Winehouse Statue",a:"Camden",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5419,lng:-0.1487,c:"music",s:"pilgrimage in bronze",q:"Amy Winehouse statue Stables Market",w:"A life-size bronze of Camden's own, unveiled in Stables Market on what would have been her 31st birthday in 2014 — beehive, Star of David, defiant hip. Fans leave flowers, lipstick and scribbled notes daily; the most-visited shrine in the neighbourhood that made her and still mourns her.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Amy_Winehouse_Statue%2C_Camden_%2814946739033%29.jpg/960px-Amy_Winehouse_Statue%2C_Camden_%2814946739033%29.jpg"},{id:"dublincastle",n:"The Dublin Castle",a:"Camden",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5371,lng:-0.1437,c:"music",s:"where Madness began",q:"Dublin Castle pub Camden Madness",w:"A small pub at 94 Parkway where Madness blagged their first gigs in 1979 by pretending to be a jazz band — and where Camden's whole sound seemed to incubate. Blur, Travis and Amy all played the back room; the stage is still barely bigger than a kitchen table.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/The_Dublin_Castle%2C_Camden_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4778210.jpg/960px-The_Dublin_Castle%2C_Camden_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4778210.jpg"},{id:"koko",n:"KOKO",a:"Mornington Crescent",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5346,lng:-0.1382,c:"music",s:"theatre, fire, rebirth",q:"KOKO Camden Theatre Mornington Crescent",w:"Opened as the Camden Theatre on Boxing Day 1900, reborn as the Camden Palace where Madonna played her first UK show and the Clash and Prince later raged. A 2020 fire nearly finished it; it returned, gilded and domed, one of London's most theatrical venues.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Koko_London.jpg/960px-Koko_London.jpg"},{id:"cecilsharphouse",n:"Cecil Sharp House",a:"Primrose Hill",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5365,lng:-0.1488,c:"music",s:"home of English folk",q:"Cecil Sharp House folk Camden",w:"The 1930 home of English folk, headquarters of the English Folk Dance and Song Society and named for the collector who rescued a vanishing tradition. A glorious mural-wrapped hall hosts ceilidhs, gigs and Morris sides — the gentlest, unlikeliest music venue in the city.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stpancrasold",n:"St Pancras Old Church",a:"Somers Town",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5346,lng:-0.1283,c:"history",s:"Wollstonecraft & the K2",q:"St Pancras Old Church Mary Wollstonecraft Soane",w:"One of the oldest sites of Christian worship in England, on a knoll above the buried Fleet. Mary Wollstonecraft was laid to rest here (Shelley courted her daughter at the grave); Sir John Soane's extraordinary mausoleum stands in the yard — its canopy the model for the red telephone box.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/St_Pancras_Old_Church_-_geograph.org.uk_-_757784.jpg"},{id:"primrosehill",n:"Primrose Hill",a:"Primrose Hill",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5393,lng:-0.1606,c:"green",s:"the protected view",q:"Primrose Hill summit view London",w:"A 64-metre grassy summit with one of London's legally protected views — by statute no building may rise to block the panorama of the city spread below. Haunt of the Romantic poets, site of mock-druid revivals and a thousand films; come at dusk and the whole skyline glows.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Primrose_Hill_%287263648010%29.jpg/960px-Primrose_Hill_%287263648010%29.jpg"},{id:"camdencatacombs",n:"Camden Catacombs",a:"Camden",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5418,lng:-0.149,c:"oddity",s:"the horse tunnels",q:"Camden Catacombs horse tunnels Stables Market",w:"A hidden network of brick tunnels and cast-iron 'pony pots' beneath Stables Market and Camden Lock, built in the 19th century to stable the horses that hauled goods between railway and canal. Largely sealed off — but the grilles and arches above hint at the warren below. (No public access.)",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"christchurchspitalfields",n:"Christ Church Spitalfields",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5193,lng:-0.0747,c:"history",s:"Hawksmoor's masterpiece",q:"Christ Church Spitalfields Hawksmoor",w:"Nicholas Hawksmoor's towering English Baroque masterpiece (1714–29), built to plant Anglican authority among the Huguenot weavers and stamp out Dissent. Its great spire still dominates Spitalfields — strange, severe and thrilling, the most powerful of Hawksmoor's six London churches.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Coat_of_arms_of_Great_Britain_%281714%E2%80%931801%29.svg/960px-Coat_of_arms_of_Great_Britain_%281714%E2%80%931801%29.svg.png"},{id:"princelet19",n:"19 Princelet Street",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5197,lng:-0.073,c:"history",s:"the hidden synagogue",q:"19 Princelet Street hidden synagogue",w:"A Huguenot silk-weaver's house of 1719 with a complete Victorian synagogue built secretly over its garden — layer upon layer of immigrant London under one fragile roof. Now a museum of immigration so delicate it opens only a handful of days a year; a building that is itself the exhibit. (Rare openings.)",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tenbells",n:"The Ten Bells",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5195,lng:-0.0745,c:"pub",s:"the victims' local",q:"Ten Bells pub Commercial Street",w:"A Victorian-tiled corner pub beside Christ Church, forever bound to the 1888 Whitechapel murders — two of the Ripper's victims are said to have drunk or touted here. The magnificent tilework survives and the dark tourism is unavoidable, but the pub itself is a genuine East End survivor.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-We,Su 12:00-24:00; Th-Sa 12:00-01:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Outside_Ten_Bells_Public_House%2C_Spitalfields.jpg/960px-Outside_Ten_Bells_Public_House%2C_Spitalfields.jpg"},{id:"boundaryestate",n:"Boundary Estate & Arnold Circus",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E2",lat:51.526,lng:-0.074,c:"history",s:"the first council estate",q:"Boundary Estate Arnold Circus Old Nichol",w:"Opened in 1900 on the cleared rubble of the Old Nichol — London's most notorious slum — this is the world's first council housing estate: seven handsome brick streets radiating from a raised bandstand mound built from the slum's own debris. Social idealism you can walk straight through.",city:"london"},{id:"stleonards",n:"St Leonard's Shoreditch",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"EC2A",lat:51.5266,lng:-0.078,c:"history",s:"the actors' church",q:"St Leonard's Shoreditch actors church Burbage",w:"The 'actors' church', burial place of James Burbage and his son Richard — Shakespeare's leading man — and a clutch of players who built the Elizabethan stage nearby. 'When I grow rich, say the bells of Shoreditch': this is the church the nursery rhyme means, its bells ringing from a Georgian tower.",city:"london"},{id:"thetheatre",n:"Site of The Theatre (1576)",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"EC2A",lat:51.5247,lng:-0.079,c:"history",s:"England's first playhouse",q:"The Theatre 1576 Curtain Road Shoreditch",w:"On Curtain Road stood The Theatre, England's first purpose-built playhouse, opened by James Burbage in 1576. When the lease soured in 1598 the company dismantled it overnight and ferried the timbers across the Thames — to build the Globe. Excavated foundations are preserved on the spot.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Burbage%27s_Theatre%2C_Holywell%2C_London_-_site_plan.png/960px-Burbage%27s_Theatre%2C_Holywell%2C_London_-_site_plan.png"},{id:"trumanbrewery",n:"Old Truman Brewery",a:"Brick Lane",pc:"E1",lat:51.5211,lng:-0.0713,c:"history",s:"once the world's biggest",q:"Old Truman Brewery Brick Lane",w:"The Black Eagle Brewery, founded around 1666 on Brick Lane and by 1853 the largest brewery on earth, pouring 400,000 barrels a year. Its vast Georgian-and-Victorian sprawl now holds markets, studios and bars — beer-money architecture turned into the engine of Brick Lane cool.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"columbiaroad",n:"Columbia Road Flower Market",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"E2",lat:51.529,lng:-0.07,c:"green",s:"Sunday flower market",q:"Columbia Road Flower Market",w:"A single Victorian street that erupts every Sunday into a roaring wall of blooms and Cockney patter — 'two bunches for a fiver, lovely!' Quiet and pretty the other six days, with old shopfronts and yards; on Sundays, the most gloriously chaotic green space in London.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Columbia_Road_Flower_Market_2022.jpg/960px-Columbia_Road_Flower_Market_2022.jpg"},{id:"georgeinn",n:"The George Inn",a:"Southwark",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5042,lng:-0.0905,c:"pub",s:"last galleried coaching inn",q:"George Inn Borough High Street National Trust",w:"London's last surviving galleried coaching inn, rebuilt in 1677 after the Southwark fire and now owned by the National Trust — timber galleries where Dickens drank, and which he wrote into Little Dorrit. Shakespeare's audiences knew its predecessor; pull up a bench in the cobbled yard.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 11:00-23:00; Fr-Sa 11:00-00:00; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/The_George_Inn_1.jpg/960px-The_George_Inn_1.jpg"},{id:"goldenhinde",n:"Golden Hinde",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5071,lng:-0.0905,c:"maritime",s:"Drake's galleon reborn",q:"Golden Hinde Bankside replica",w:"A full-size, fully seaworthy reconstruction of the galleon in which Sir Francis Drake circumnavigated the globe (1577–80) — and which has now itself sailed further than the original. Moored in a Bankside dock, astonishingly small for the voyage; clamber aboard and marvel anyone survived it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"southwarkcathedral",n:"Southwark Cathedral",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5061,lng:-0.09,c:"history",s:"Shakespeare & Harvard",q:"Southwark Cathedral Shakespeare John Harvard",w:"London's oldest Gothic church building, beside Borough Market — Shakespeare's actor brother Edmund is buried here and a memorial honours the Bard himself. John Harvard, founder of the university, was baptised here in 1607; a quiet, ancient counterweight to the Shard looming above.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Northwest_View_of_Southwark_Cathedral_%2801%29.jpg/960px-Northwest_View_of_Southwark_Cathedral_%2801%29.jpg"},{id:"oldoperatingtheatre",n:"Old Operating Theatre",a:"London Bridge",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5039,lng:-0.0883,c:"history",s:"surgery in a church attic",q:"Old Operating Theatre Museum St Thomas Street",w:"The oldest surviving operating theatre in Europe, built in 1822 in the herb garret of old St Thomas's — a wooden amphitheatre where students crowded to watch surgery done at speed, before anaesthetic. Climb the spiral stair into a hair-raising museum of saws, leeches and pre-Victorian medicine.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Old-op-the-lond.jpg"},{id:"boroughmarket",n:"Borough Market",a:"Southwark",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5055,lng:-0.0909,c:"history",s:"a thousand years of trade",q:"Borough Market history Southwark",w:"A market has traded at the foot of London Bridge for roughly a thousand years; the present cathedral of glass and iron is Victorian. Smells of truffle, cheese and chorizo now fill the arches where medieval Londoners bought their grain — gloriously busy, endlessly delicious.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/London_2018_March_IMG_0663.jpg/960px-London_2018_March_IMG_0663.jpg"},{id:"ferrymansseat",n:"The Ferryman's Seat",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5079,lng:-0.0955,c:"oddity",s:"worn stone perch",q:"Ferryman's Seat Bankside",w:"A small, smooth-worn stone seat set into a building wall on Bankside, where for centuries the watermen who rowed Londoners across the Thames waited for a fare. Polished by countless backsides; an easily-missed relic of the river-taxi trade that died as the bridges multiplied.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Ferryman%27s_seat%2C_Bankside%2C_London.jpg/960px-Ferryman%27s_seat%2C_Bankside%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"globe",n:"Shakespeare's Globe & the Rose",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5081,lng:-0.0972,c:"history",s:"the wooden O reborn",q:"Shakespeare's Globe Rose theatre Park Street",w:"Sam Wanamaker's faithful reconstruction of Shakespeare's open-air playhouse — thatched, timber-framed and standing close to the original, opened in 1997 after his decades-long crusade. A few steps away, marked under glass, lie the foundations of the Rose, the first Bankside theatre of 1587.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Restaurante_The_Swan%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2014-08-11%2C_DD_113.jpg/960px-Restaurante_The_Swan%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2014-08-11%2C_DD_113.jpg"},{id:"kingswaytunnels",n:"Kingsway Exchange Tunnels",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5176,lng:-0.111,c:"history",s:"the secret deep tunnels",q:"Kingsway Exchange Tunnels Chancery Lane",w:"A mile of tunnels dug 40 metres below High Holborn in the early 1940s as a bomb shelter, later home to a Cold War telephone exchange, a government 'citadel' and the rumoured Q-Whitehall hotline. Long sealed and mythologised; plans to open them to visitors are slowly advancing. (No public access yet.)",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Entrance_to_Kingsway_Telephone_Exchange_39_Furnival_Street_%28geograph_3983441%29.jpg/960px-Entrance_to_Kingsway_Telephone_Exchange_39_Furnival_Street_%28geograph_3983441%29.jpg"},{id:"churchillwarrooms",n:"Churchill War Rooms",a:"Westminster",pc:"SW1A",lat:51.5021,lng:-0.129,c:"history",s:"the disguised hotline",q:"Churchill War Rooms transatlantic phone",w:"The underground command bunker from which Churchill ran the Second World War, left almost exactly as it was on VE Day and now part of the Imperial War Museum. The Map Room's pin-stuck walls; the secret transatlantic hotline to Roosevelt hidden behind a door marked as a toilet — history frozen mid-crisis.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/MapRoomCabinetWarRoomsTrim.jpg/960px-MapRoomCabinetWarRoomsTrim.jpg"},{id:"downstreet",n:"Down Street Station",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1J",lat:51.5036,lng:-0.15,c:"history",s:"Churchill's bunker tube",q:"Down Street disused tube station Mayfair",w:"A Piccadilly line station that opened in 1907 in too-rich Mayfair to ever pay, and closed in 1932 — then secretly became Churchill's wartime refuge before the War Rooms were ready, and the Railway Executive's HQ. The oxblood-tiled façade still stands on Down Street; trains roar past the bricked-up platforms.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"claphamsouth",n:"Clapham South Deep Shelter",a:"Clapham",pc:"SW12",lat:51.4528,lng:-0.148,c:"history",s:"where Windrush slept",q:"Clapham South deep-level shelter Windrush",w:"A vast deep-level air-raid shelter (1940–42) with bunks for 8,000 people far below the Northern line. In 1948 it housed the first arrivals from the Empire Windrush, sleeping underground while they sought work and lodging in Brixton nearby — the birth of multicultural London, beneath it.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Clapham_South_entrance_2020.jpg/960px-Clapham_South_entrance_2020.jpg"},{id:"lowerrobertst",n:"Lower Robert Street",a:"Strand",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.1225,c:"oddity",s:"the cabbies' 'Bat Cave'",q:"Lower Robert Street Adelphi tunnel",w:"A dark, twisting one-way tunnel-street curving down beneath the Adelphi off the Strand — a survivor of the Adam brothers' 18th-century riverside scheme, which cabbies nickname the 'Bat Cave'. Damp, sodium-lit and faintly sinister; one of central London's last genuinely hidden streets.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"barnardsinn",n:"Barnard's Inn Hall",a:"Holborn",pc:"EC1N",lat:51.5181,lng:-0.109,c:"medieval",s:"free lectures under old rafters",q:"Barnard's Inn Hall Gresham College Holborn",w:"A rare medieval hall tucked behind shopfronts off Holborn — a 14th-century survivor with a 16th-century timber roof, once an Inn of Chancery and a setting in Dickens's Great Expectations. Now home to Gresham College, which has given free public lectures since 1597; slip in and sit under the rafters.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Barnards_inn.jpg/960px-Barnards_inn.jpg"},{id:"cafeinthecrypt",n:"Café in the Crypt",a:"Trafalgar Square",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.5089,lng:-0.1262,c:"oddity",s:"lunch among the dead",q:"Cafe in the Crypt St Martin in the Fields",w:"Lunch among the dead: a vaulted café in the 18th-century crypt of St Martin-in-the-Fields on Trafalgar Square, where you eat over the worn ledger-stones of the buried. Brick arches, candle-glow and a brass-rubbing studio next door; an atmospheric everyday detour beneath the church steps.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hillpergola",n:"The Hill Garden & Pergola",a:"Hampstead",pc:"NW3",lat:51.5605,lng:-0.181,c:"green",s:"the half-ruined pergola",q:"Hill Garden and Pergola Hampstead Heath",w:"An Edwardian fantasy on the edge of Hampstead Heath — a raised, vine-draped pergola walk built for the soap magnate Lord Leverhulme's garden parties, now romantically half-ruined and free to wander. Wisteria, stone columns and long views; the most quietly cinematic garden in north London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"twomice",n:"The Two Mice of Philpot Lane",a:"City",pc:"EC3M",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.0835,c:"oddity",s:"London's smallest sculpture",q:"Two Mice Philpot Lane Monument cheese",w:"London's smallest public sculpture: two tiny mice nibbling cheese, carved high on a building corner at Philpot Lane near the Monument. Legend says they remember two workmen who fell to their deaths brawling over a stolen lunch while building the Monument itself. Easy to walk past forever.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Philpot_Lane%2C_London.jpg/960px-Philpot_Lane%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"bovrilbrixton",n:"Bovril Ghost Sign",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW2",lat:51.4577,lng:-0.109,c:"ghostsign",s:"the great faded Bovril",q:"Bovril ghost sign Effra Road Brixton",w:"A vast faded advertisement for Bovril, painted on a flank wall above Effra Road by Windrush Square and dating from perhaps the 1920s — one of London's best-loved ghost signs, slowly dissolving into the brick. Look up; it's easy to walk under and never notice.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hoviscatford",n:"Hovis Ghost Sign",a:"Catford",pc:"SE6",lat:51.4465,lng:-0.015,c:"ghostsign",s:"a giant fading loaf",q:"Hovis ghost sign Inchmery Road Catford",w:"An enormous hand-painted Hovis advertisement on the flank of a building off Inchmery Road in Catford — a rare ghost sign for a brand that still exists. The 'wall dogs' who painted these mixed their pigment with linseed oil to make it last; a century on, it just barely has.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"criterionkilburn",n:"Criterion & Gillette Ghost Signs",a:"Kilburn",pc:"NW6",lat:51.5405,lng:-0.1958,c:"ghostsign",s:"two adverts, stacked",q:"223 Kilburn High Road ghost sign Criterion Gillette",w:"At 223 Kilburn High Road two ghost adverts bleed through one another — Criterion Matches beneath, Gillette Razors above — a palimpsest of early-20th-century brands sharing one weathered wall. The clearest example in London of signs painted over signs.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"nestlenewcross",n:"Nestlé Milk Ghost Sign",a:"New Cross",pc:"SE14",lat:51.4762,lng:-0.0402,c:"ghostsign",s:"three layers deep",q:"104 New Cross Road ghost sign Nestle",w:"On 104 New Cross Road three different painted adverts overlap, the most legible a Nestlé Milk sign — layers of a vanished high street's commerce, one brushed over the next as brands and decades changed. A whole archaeology of advertising on a single gable.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"playershackney",n:"Player's Please Ghost Sign",a:"Hackney",pc:"E9",lat:51.5495,lng:-0.0445,c:"ghostsign",s:"the tobacco slogan",q:"Player's Please ghost sign Kenworthy Road Hackney",w:"'Player's Please' — the old slogan for Player's Navy Cut cigarettes — still faintly legible on a wall on Kenworthy Road in Hackney. A snapshot of an era when tobacco was painted ten feet tall on the side of an ordinary street, long before any health warning.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"snashallwardrobe",n:"Snashall & Son Ghost Sign",a:"St Paul's",pc:"EC4V",lat:51.5132,lng:-0.0988,c:"ghostsign",s:"a printer in a hidden court",q:"Snashall Son ghost sign Wardrobe Place",w:"Tucked in Wardrobe Place — a tiny cobbled courtyard off Carter Lane in the shadow of St Paul's — a faded sign for 'Snashall & Son, Printers, Stationers & Account Book Manufacturers' clings to the brick. The quietest, most central ghost sign in the City.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"carrerasblackcat",n:"Carreras Black Cat Factory",a:"Mornington Crescent",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5335,lng:-0.1398,c:"artdeco",s:"Art Deco Egyptian cats",q:"Carreras Cigarette Factory Greater London House Mornington Crescent",w:"A startling 1928 Art Deco temple on Mornington Crescent, built for Carreras (makers of Black Cat cigarettes) in full Egyptian Revival — once flanked by two giant black bronze cats and a solar disc. Now offices as 'Greater London House'; the restored cats still guard the door.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/The_biggest_cats_in_London%5E_-_geograph.org.uk_-_670712.jpg"},{id:"elthampalace",n:"Eltham Palace",a:"Eltham",pc:"SE9",lat:51.4496,lng:0.0525,c:"history",s:"Art Deco on a Tudor hall",q:"Eltham Palace English Heritage Art Deco",w:"Henry VIII's boyhood palace — then, in the 1930s, the millionaire Courtaulds grafted a dazzling Art Deco mansion straight onto its surviving medieval Great Hall. Gold-leaf bathroom, a glass-domed entrance hall, and centrally-heated quarters built for Mah-Jongg, their pet ring-tailed lemur.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/St_Barnabas_church%2C_Eltham_%28geograph_2650441%29.jpg/960px-St_Barnabas_church%2C_Eltham_%28geograph_2650441%29.jpg"},{id:"neasdentemple",n:"BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir",a:"Neasden",pc:"NW10",lat:51.553,lng:-0.2495,c:"history",s:"the Neasden temple",q:"BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir Neasden",w:"A breathtaking traditional Hindu mandir rising out of suburban Neasden — carved from thousands of tonnes of Italian and Bulgarian limestone and Indian marble, shaped by hand in India and assembled here without a single beam of structural steel. One of the largest of its kind outside India.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"strawberryhill",n:"Strawberry Hill House",a:"Twickenham",pc:"TW1",lat:51.436,lng:-0.336,c:"oddity",s:"the Gothic Revival is born",q:"Strawberry Hill House Horace Walpole Twickenham",w:"Horace Walpole's 'little Gothic castle' of 1749 — a snow-white confection of battlements, pinnacles and papier-mâché vaulting that all but invented the Gothic Revival and inspired the first Gothic novel. Theatrical, asymmetrical and gloriously strange on the Twickenham riverside.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Chapel_in_the_Wood%2C_Strawberry_Hill_01.jpg/960px-Chapel_in_the_Wood%2C_Strawberry_Hill_01.jpg"},{id:"qehuntinglodge",n:"Queen Elizabeth's Hunting Lodge",a:"Chingford",pc:"E4",lat:51.633,lng:0.0015,c:"history",s:"a Tudor grandstand in the forest",q:"Queen Elizabeth's Hunting Lodge Chingford Epping Forest",w:"A three-storey Tudor timber grandstand of 1543 on the edge of Epping Forest, built so Henry VIII's court could watch — and shoot — the deer driven below. Despite the name, Elizabeth I only inherited it; an immaculate, surreal survivor among the ancient hornbeams.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"shirleywindmill",n:"Shirley Windmill",a:"Croydon",pc:"CR0",lat:51.364,lng:-0.056,c:"oddity",s:"a windmill marooned in suburbia",q:"Shirley Windmill Croydon",w:"A towering brick tower mill of 1854, one of the last in Greater London — and improbably marooned amid a quiet Croydon housing estate. Round a dull suburban corner and a giant Victorian windmill rears up; restored and occasionally open, a quintessential flâneur double-take.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"pophams-islington",n:"Pophams",a:"Islington",pc:"N1",lat:51.538,lng:-0.103,c:"bakery",s:"The bacon-maple morning bun",q:"Pophams bakery Islington cardamom bun",w:"Flaky, inventive Danishes and the famous bacon-maple morning bun — Pophams grew from a tiny Prince of Wales Road room in 2017 into Islington and Hackney all-day bakery-diners. Come for cardamom buns and a flat white; the laminated pastry is the draw.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"e5-bakehouse",n:"E5 Bakehouse",a:"London Fields",pc:"E8",lat:51.541,lng:-0.061,c:"bakery",s:"Railway-arch sourdough pioneer",q:"E5 Bakehouse London Fields",w:"Under a London Fields railway arch since 2010, E5 Bakehouse helped spark East London's sourdough obsession — milling its own grain, baking the much-imitated Hackney Wild loaf and roasting coffee in the same warm, floury room.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"st-john-bakery",n:"St. John Bakery",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"SE16",lat:51.498,lng:-0.066,c:"bakery",s:"The famous custard doughnut",q:"St John Bakery Druid Street doughnut",w:"The bakery arm of Fergus Henderson's nose-to-tail St John, in a Druid Street railway arch — home of the legendary custard doughnut that launched a thousand imitators. Eccles cakes, fennel sausage rolls and bread baked through the night.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"toklas-bakery",n:"Toklas Bakery",a:"Temple",pc:"WC2R",lat:51.511,lng:-0.117,c:"bakery",s:"Art-world viennoiserie",q:"Toklas bakery Temple London",w:"Opened by the founders of Frieze magazine and named for Alice B. Toklas, Gertrude Stein's partner — an art-world bakery near the Strand turning out impeccable viennoiserie and Mediterranean plates in a serene courtyard space.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"jolene-newington-green",n:"Jolene",a:"Newington Green",pc:"N16",lat:51.553,lng:-0.085,c:"bakery",s:"Regenerative-grain bakery-diner",q:"Jolene Newington Green bakery",w:"Jolene mills much of its own flour and buys from regenerative farmers, blurring bakery and neighbourhood diner. A Newington Green favourite for blistered pastries, natural wine and seasonal plates from a tiny open kitchen.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"buns-from-home",n:"Buns from Home",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"W11",lat:51.511,lng:-0.205,c:"bakery",s:"Pandemic-born bun empire",q:"Buns from Home Notting Hill",w:"Started as a lockdown side-project and grew into one of West London's most dependable bun fixes — gooey cinnamon and cardamom knots, pillowy and gloriously over-proofed. A reliable Notting Hill sugar hit.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"how-matcha-marylebone",n:"How Matcha",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1U",lat:51.519,lng:-0.153,c:"matcha",s:"Feng-shui matcha temple",q:"How Matcha Marylebone",w:"A community-minded, feng-shui-calm matcha bar sourcing ceremonial grade direct from Kagoshima farms — and rationing carefully through the 2024–25 matcha shortage. Whisked usucha, matcha lattes and a quiet Marylebone room to drink them in.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kova-soho",n:"Kova Patisserie",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.513,lng:-0.131,c:"matcha",s:"The matcha mille-crêpe cake",q:"Kova Patisserie Soho matcha crepe cake",w:"Kova built a cult around its matcha mille-crêpe cake — dozens of paper-thin crêpes layered with green-tea cream. A Soho counter where the Japanese-French sweets are as photogenic as they are good.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tokkia-covent-garden",n:"Tokkia",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2H",lat:51.513,lng:-0.126,c:"matcha",s:"Korean matcha monastery",q:"Tokkia matcha Covent Garden",w:"A tiny Covent Garden café with a strict 'only orderers inside' rule that keeps it monastically calm — Korean-inflected matcha, carefully whisked, in a room built for slowing down. Cult following, very few seats.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"frothee-kings-cross",n:"frothee",a:"King's Cross",pc:"N1C",lat:51.535,lng:-0.124,c:"matcha",s:"Weighed, whisked, properly frothy",q:"frothee matcha King's Cross Caledonian Road",w:"A minimalist, matcha-first café off Caledonian Road where the powder is weighed to the gram and whisked fresh to order. No frills, properly frothy — for people who take their usucha seriously.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wa-cafe-marylebone",n:"WA Café",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1U",lat:51.518,lng:-0.152,c:"matcha",s:"Tokyo patisserie, no WiFi",q:"WA Cafe matcha Japanese patisserie London",w:"A Tokyo-style patisserie (born, improbably, in Ealing) with a no-WiFi, sit-and-savour ethos — delicate Japanese cakes, matcha and pour-overs in a hushed Marylebone room that wants your attention, not your laptop.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 09:00-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"nagare-marylebone",n:"Nagare Coffee",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1U",lat:51.517,lng:-0.154,c:"matcha",s:"'Be water' Bruce Lee matcha",q:"Nagare Coffee Marylebone matcha",w:"Named for the Japanese word for 'flowing,' and Bruce Lee's 'be water' — a Marylebone coffee-and-matcha bar with a serene, design-led fit-out and seriously sourced green tea. Flow in, slow down.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"katsute100-islington",n:"Katsute 100",a:"Islington",pc:"N1",lat:51.537,lng:-0.103,c:"matcha",s:"Antique-shop tea house",q:"Katsute 100 Islington matcha tea house",w:"A jewel-box Islington tea house where matcha feels genuinely ceremonial — served among antique ceramics, dried flowers and wagashi sweets in a hidden room above an antique shop. Tea as quiet theatre.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"p-franco-clapton",n:"P. Franco / 107",a:"Clapton",pc:"E5",lat:51.561,lng:-0.055,c:"wine",s:"Hot-plate natural wine legend",q:"P Franco 107 Lower Clapton natural wine",w:"A former Lower Clapton grocer's that became the spiritual home of London natural wine — bottles on the shelf, a single hot-plate kitchen turning out brilliant small plates, elbow-to-elbow with strangers. Now trading as '107'; the legend stands.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"top-cuvee-highbury",n:"Top Cuvée",a:"Highbury",pc:"N5",lat:51.552,lng:-0.098,c:"wine",s:"Playful 80s-logo wine den",q:"Top Cuvee Highbury natural wine",w:"Small but perfectly formed, with a cheeky 1980s-style logo masking a serious low-intervention list — Top Cuvée pours adventurous glasses and plates ambitious food in a snug Highbury room, and ships bottles nationwide too.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sager-wilde-hackney",n:"Sager + Wilde",a:"Hackney",pc:"E2",lat:51.53,lng:-0.068,c:"wine",s:"The bar that changed London wine",q:"Sager and Wilde Hackney Road wine bar",w:"When it opened in a former East End boozer in 2013, the green-tiled Sager + Wilde helped rewrite how London drinks wine — a long marble bar, fair mark-ups and grilled-cheese to match. A genuine scene-shifter on Hackney Road.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bar-crispin-soho",n:"Bar Crispin",a:"Soho",pc:"W1F",lat:51.513,lng:-0.135,c:"wine",s:"Anti-aesthetic natural wine",q:"Bar Crispin Soho natural wine",w:"Bar Crispin shrugs off the usual wine-bar polish — interiors somewhere between grown-up kebab shop and Mediterranean canteen — and pours a sharp, low-intervention list beside excellent small plates. Anti-aesthetic, seriously good.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"diogenes-the-dog",n:"Diogenes the Dog",a:"Elephant & Castle",pc:"SE17",lat:51.488,lng:-0.095,c:"wine",s:"Cynic-philosopher wine bar",q:"Diogenes the Dog Elephant Castle wine",w:"Named for the Greek Cynic who lived in a barrel, this neighbourhood wine bar near Elephant & Castle pours a thoughtful, characterful list with warm service — proof the good stuff doesn't only live in the centre.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bambuni-nunhead",n:"Bambuni",a:"Nunhead",pc:"SE15",lat:51.466,lng:-0.056,c:"wine",s:"Deli-by-day, wine-by-night",q:"Bambuni Nunhead wine deli",w:"A pocket-sized Nunhead deli and wine shop that sells continental provisions by day and turns into a buzzing little wine bar by night — cheese, charcuterie and low-intervention bottles among the shelves. Neighbourhood gold.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sons-daughters-kx",n:"Sons + Daughters",a:"King's Cross",pc:"N1C",lat:51.536,lng:-0.125,c:"food",s:"Posh-nostalgia sandwich art",q:"Sons and Daughters sandwich Coal Drops Yard",w:"From James Ramsden and Sam Herlihy of Pidgin, a Coal Drops Yard counter that treats the sandwich as high craft — posh-nostalgia fillings on properly good bread, made to order. King's Cross lunch as art.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"doms-subs-hackney",n:"Dom's Subs (at Rasputin's)",a:"Hackney",pc:"E8",lat:51.54,lng:-0.056,c:"food",s:"Time Out's 'king' of subs",q:"Dom's Subs Hackney Mare Street Rasputin's",w:"Dom's brought New Jersey-style Italian-American subs to Hackney and Time Out crowned them London's best — towering, properly dressed, gloriously messy. The standalone shops closed in 2026; the subs now fly out of its Mare Street bar, Rasputin's.",city:"london",oh:"Tu-Fr 12:00-23:00; Sa-Su 12:00-23:30",wq:"a"},{id:"maxs-sandwich-shop",n:"Max's Sandwich Shop",a:"Crouch Hill",pc:"N4",lat:51.571,lng:-0.118,c:"food",s:"Pints and dictionary-thick sarnies",q:"Max's Sandwich Shop Crouch Hill",w:"Max Halley has been elevating the humble sandwich in Crouch Hill since 2014 — dictionary-thick, gleefully overloaded creations washed down with pints. So iconic that fashion houses have come knocking; still defiantly unpretentious.",city:"london",oh:"We 18:00-23:00; Th, Fr 18:00-23:00, 11:00-03:00; Sa 17:00-23:00, 11:00-03:30; Su 11:00-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"sandwich-sandwich-city",n:"Sandwich Sandwich",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2V",lat:51.515,lng:-0.093,c:"food",s:"City office-lunch superstar",q:"Sandwich Sandwich City London",w:"A Bristol export that went viral and now packs out the City at lunch — fronted by an evangelical owner and built on fat, fresh, no-nonsense fillings. Office-lunch theatre, with the queue to prove it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"paul-rothe-marylebone",n:"Paul Rothe & Son",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1U",lat:51.518,lng:-0.152,c:"food",s:"Family deli since 1900",q:"Paul Rothe and Son Marylebone deli",w:"A Marylebone family deli running since 1900, all wooden shelves and old jars, where sandwiches are built to order from whatever's good that day. A genuine time capsule among the boutiques — soup, a sarnie and a chat.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:30-20:00; Sa,Su 08:00-20:00",wq:"a"},{id:"beigel-shop-brick-lane",n:"Beigel Shop ('the yellow one')",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5238,lng:-0.0712,c:"bakery",s:"'Britain's first' — disputed",q:"Beigel Shop Brick Lane 155 oldest",w:"The yellow-signed 'original' at 155 Brick Lane claims a beigel trade here since 1855 — a date most historians treat as contested marketing (the present shop dates to the early 1960s). It briefly closed in 2024, then reopened; the rivalry with the white one next door is half the fun.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"panzers-deli",n:"Panzer's Deli",a:"St John's Wood",pc:"NW8",lat:51.534,lng:-0.173,c:"bakery",s:"North London's grand deli",q:"Panzer's Deli St John's Wood bagel",w:"A St John's Wood institution since the 1940s — a groaning Jewish deli of smoked salmon, rye, pickles and bagels, with fruit and provisions spilling onto the pavement. North London's grand old-school grocer.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"m-manze-tower-bridge",n:"M. Manze",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"SE1",lat:51.497,lng:-0.081,c:"pieandmash",s:"Oldest pie & mash, 1902",q:"M Manze Tower Bridge Road pie mash",w:"Generally said to be London's oldest surviving pie-and-mash shop, at 87 Tower Bridge Road since 1902 — marble counters, eel-and-liquor and green parsley sauce ladled with ritual. A working monument to Cockney eating.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 10:30-17:45; Fr 10:00-18:45; Sa 10:00-19:45; Su 11:00-14:45",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/M.Manze_Bermondsey_-_87_Tower_Bridge_Road_London_SE1_4TW.jpg/960px-M.Manze_Bermondsey_-_87_Tower_Bridge_Road_London_SE1_4TW.jpg"},{id:"f-cooke-hoxton",n:"F. Cooke",a:"Hoxton",pc:"N1",lat:51.53,lng:-0.078,c:"pieandmash",s:"Cooke dynasty since 1862",q:"F Cooke Hoxton Street pie mash",w:"The Cooke family have served pie and mash since Fred Cooke first opened in 1862 — the Hoxton Street shop a glorious survivor of tiled walls, eels and liquor. Eel-and-pie London, still trading against the odds.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"goddards-greenwich",n:"Goddards at Greenwich",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10",lat:51.481,lng:-0.009,c:"pieandmash",s:"Pie-makers since 1890",q:"Goddards Greenwich pie mash",w:"A family pie business going since 1890 and a Greenwich fixture a short walk from the Cutty Sark — pie, mash and liquor served to tourists and locals alike. Comfort food with a 130-year pedigree.",city:"london",oh:"Su-Th 10:00-19:30; Fr-Sa 10:00-20:00",wq:"a"},{id:"arments-walworth",n:"Arment's Pie & Mash",a:"Walworth",pc:"SE17",lat:51.487,lng:-0.093,c:"pieandmash",s:"Walworth's last survivor, 1914",q:"Arment's pie mash Walworth",w:"Arment's opened its first Walworth shop in 1914; the Westmoreland Road branch is the area's last survivor — eel, pie, mash and liquor in a neighbourhood that once had dozens. A vanishing South London ritual.",city:"london"},{id:"e-pellicci-bethnal-green",n:"E. Pellicci",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"E2",lat:51.527,lng:-0.056,c:"caff",s:"Grade II Art Deco, since 1900",q:"E Pellicci Bethnal Green cafe",w:"A Bethnal Green institution since 1900, its Grade II-listed 1946 marquetry interior carved by a regular — the Pellicci family still working the counter, the Krays once at the tables. The platonic ideal of the Italian-London caff.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 08:00-15:30",wq:"a"},{id:"regency-cafe-westminster",n:"Regency Café",a:"Westminster",pc:"SW1P",lat:51.494,lng:-0.134,c:"caff",s:"1946 Art Deco film star",q:"Regency Cafe Westminster",w:"Opened in 1946, this black-tiled Westminster classic is a film-location darling you'll recognise from a dozen movies — fry-ups barked across the room, formica, and a queue of cabbies and civil servants. Greasy-spoon perfection.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 07:00-14:30, 16:00-19:15; Sa 07:00-12:00"},{id:"terrys-cafe-borough",n:"Terry's Café",a:"Borough",pc:"SE1",lat:51.502,lng:-0.098,c:"caff",s:"Old-school British, since 1982",q:"Terry's Cafe Great Suffolk Street Borough",w:"Set up by Terry in 1982 — son of a pastry cook and an Army chef — and now run by his son, a proudly old-school British caff off Great Suffolk Street. Bubble and squeak, doorstep toast and Union Jacks; comfort with a wink.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gelupo-soho",n:"Gelupo",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.513,lng:-0.132,c:"bakery",s:"Bocca di Lupo's gelato sibling",q:"Gelupo Soho gelato",w:"The gelateria across the road from chef Jacob Kenedy's Bocca di Lupo, raiding the Italian repertoire for ricotta-and-sour-cherry, fresh-mint stracciatella and warm bombolini. Soho's best scoop, and a cup of proper hot chocolate in winter.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 11:00-23:00; Fr-Sa 11:00-24:00; Su 12:00-23:00",wq:"a"},{id:"oddonos-south-ken",n:"Oddono's",a:"South Kensington",pc:"SW7",lat:51.494,lng:-0.174,c:"bakery",s:"South Ken gelato since 2004",q:"Oddono's South Kensington gelato",w:"Christian Oddono drew on a family with a century of Italian gelato-making to open this South Kensington favourite in 2004 — dense, intense, natural-ingredient gelato that regularly wins national awards. The pistachio is the test.",city:"london",oh:"Su-Th 09:30-23:00, Sa 09:30-24:00; Fr 09:30-24:00"},{id:"ruby-violet-kings-cross",n:"Ruby Violet",a:"King's Cross",pc:"N1C",lat:51.536,lng:-0.126,c:"bakery",s:"Small-batch, wildly inventive",q:"Ruby Violet King's Cross ice cream",w:"One of London's few true scoop-shops making everything in-house, in the Midland Goods Shed at King's Cross — wildly inventive flavours, torched-meringue sundaes and a menu that doesn't take itself too seriously.",city:"london",oh:"Mo 10:00-19:00; Tu-Su 10:00-22:00",wq:"a"},{id:"la-grotta-ices",n:"La Grotta Ices",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"SE16",lat:51.498,lng:-0.067,c:"bakery",s:"Seasonal, small-batch cult",q:"La Grotta Ices Bermondsey",w:"A small-batch, fiercely seasonal gelato maker beloved by chefs, working out of a Bermondsey arch — flavours that shift with the market (blood orange, fennel, fig-leaf). Industry-insider ice cream you queue for at markets.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"three-uncles-brixton",n:"Three Uncles",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW9",lat:51.4621,lng:-0.1135,c:"dumpling",s:"Cantonese roast-meat siu mei",q:"Three Uncles Brixton Cantonese roast",w:"Founded in 2019 by three Hong Kong-raised childhood friends — the 'three uncles' — serving glistening Cantonese roast meats (char siu, crispy pork) over rice and proper siu mei. Born in Brixton Village; the real deal.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"xian-impression-highbury",n:"Xi'an Impression",a:"Highbury",pc:"N7",lat:51.5556,lng:-0.1086,c:"dumpling",s:"Hand-pulled noodles by the Emirates",q:"Xi'an Impression Highbury Arsenal biang biang",w:"A shoebox Shaanxi restaurant on Benwell Road, directly opposite Arsenal's Emirates — hand-pulled biang-biang noodles, cumin-lamb 'burgers' and chilli-slicked dumplings. Tiny, cash-friendly and worshipped on match days.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dumplings-legend-chinatown",n:"Dumplings' Legend",a:"Chinatown",pc:"W1D",lat:51.511,lng:-0.131,c:"dumpling",s:"Glass-walled xiao long bao",q:"Dumplings' Legend Chinatown soup dumplings",w:"A Chinatown stalwart where chefs hand-fold Shanghai-style xiao long bao behind a glass screen — soup dumplings, dim sum and late hours in the heart of the West End. Watch the pleating, then burn your mouth happily.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 11:30-03:00, Su 11:30-23:00",wq:"a"},{id:"prince-charles-cinema",n:"Prince Charles Cinema",a:"Soho",pc:"WC2H",lat:51.511,lng:-0.13,c:"cinema",s:"Cult sing-alongs since 1962",q:"Prince Charles Cinema Leicester Square",w:"London's temple of cult cinema since 1962, tucked behind Leicester Square — all-night marathons, sing-along screenings, 35mm rarities and a gleefully film-obsessed crowd. The anti-multiplex, and proud of it.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Prince_Charles_Cinema.jpg/960px-Prince_Charles_Cinema.jpg"},{id:"phoenix-east-finchley",n:"Phoenix Cinema",a:"East Finchley",pc:"N2",lat:51.587,lng:-0.165,c:"cinema",s:"One of Britain's oldest, 1912",q:"Phoenix Cinema East Finchley history",w:"Built in 1910 and showing films since 1912, the Phoenix is one of Britain's oldest continuously-running cinemas — a barrel-vaulted East Finchley gem with original plasterwork. A picture house that has simply never stopped.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"regent-street-cinema",n:"Regent Street Cinema",a:"Marylebone",pc:"W1B",lat:51.517,lng:-0.143,c:"cinema",s:"Birthplace of British cinema, 1896",q:"Regent Street Cinema Lumiere 1896",w:"Often called the birthplace of British cinema: in 1896 the Lumière brothers showed Britain its first moving pictures here, beneath what's now the University of Westminster. Beautifully restored, it screens arthouse and classics where the medium began.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/University_of_Westminster.jpg/960px-University_of_Westminster.jpg"},{id:"rio-dalston",n:"Rio Cinema",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.548,lng:-0.075,c:"cinema",s:"Art Deco single-screen survivor",q:"Rio Cinema Dalston Art Deco",w:"A Kingsland High Street picture palace with roots to 1909 and a glorious 1930s Art Deco auditorium — independent, community-owned and a Dalston landmark. Late films, festivals and a balcony worth the ticket alone.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"castle-cinema-homerton",n:"The Castle Cinema",a:"Homerton",pc:"E9",lat:51.546,lng:-0.045,c:"cinema",s:"Crowdfunded 1913 revival",q:"Castle Cinema Homerton",w:"A 1913 cinema that sat derelict for decades until a community crowdfunder brought it roaring back in 2017 — now a beloved Homerton two-screener with a bar and a fierce local following. Resurrection, one pledge at a time.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"the-lexi-kensal-rise",n:"The Lexi Cinema",a:"Kensal Rise",pc:"NW10",lat:51.535,lng:-0.22,c:"cinema",s:"UK's first social-enterprise cinema",q:"The Lexi Cinema Kensal Rise charity",w:"The UK's first social-enterprise cinema — charity-run and volunteer-staffed in Kensal Rise, donating its profits to a South African township project. A single screen with a conscience, and a properly good programme.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"garden-cinema-covent-garden",n:"The Garden Cinema",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2B",lat:51.516,lng:-0.121,c:"cinema",s:"Plush new arthouse, 2022",q:"The Garden Cinema Covent Garden",w:"An independent arthouse that opened in 2022 on Parker Street — all velvet, warm lighting and thoughtful repertory programming. A plush, grown-up antidote to the multiplex, hidden in plain sight near Holborn.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rough-trade-east",n:"Rough Trade East",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.52,lng:-0.072,c:"vinyl",s:"Brick Lane's gig-and-vinyl hub",q:"Rough Trade East Brick Lane",w:"The biggest of the Rough Trade shops, inside the Old Truman Brewery off Brick Lane — vast racks, in-store gigs, coffee and a wall of new vinyl. The flagship of the label that gave punk and post-punk a home.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 10:00-19:00, Su 11:00-17:00",wq:"a"},{id:"sounds-of-the-universe",n:"Sounds of the Universe",a:"Soho",pc:"W1F",lat:51.514,lng:-0.134,c:"vinyl",s:"Soul Jazz label HQ",q:"Sounds of the Universe Soho records",w:"Born as a Camden market stall, now a Broadwick Street cornerstone and home of the brilliant Soul Jazz label — reggae, funk, house, library music and reissues for serious diggers. Soho's deepest record room.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 10:00-18:30; Su 11:00-17:30",wq:"a"},{id:"reckless-records-soho",n:"Reckless Records",a:"Soho",pc:"W1F",lat:51.514,lng:-0.137,c:"vinyl",s:"On the Oasis album cover",q:"Reckless Records Berwick Street Soho",w:"A second-hand institution on Berwick Street since 1984 — and a slice of Britpop history, its shopfront immortalised on the cover of Oasis's Morning Glory. Browse the racks where the photo was shot.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 10:00-19:00",wq:"a"},{id:"flashback-islington",n:"Flashback Records",a:"Islington",pc:"N1",lat:51.538,lng:-0.098,c:"vinyl",s:"Essex Road crate-digging",q:"Flashback Records Islington Essex Road",w:"A North London institution since 1997, the Essex Road flagship hides a basement trove beneath a well-curated ground floor — secondhand and new across every genre, generously priced. A proper afternoon's dig.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 10:00-19:00",wq:"a"},{id:"casbah-greenwich",n:"Casbah Records",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10",lat:51.481,lng:-0.009,c:"vinyl",s:"Greenwich crate-digging cosy",q:"Casbah Records Greenwich",w:"A cosy Greenwich independent specialising in 60s and 70s pop, rock, soul, funk and cult soundtracks — the kind of small, knowledgeable shop where you go in for one record and leave with five.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 10:30-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"princess-louise-holborn",n:"The Princess Louise",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC1V",lat:51.517,lng:-0.12,c:"pub",s:"Victorian gin-palace, snob screens",q:"Princess Louise Holborn Victorian interior",w:"Built in 1872 with a sumptuous 1891 interior, this Grade II*-listed High Holborn pub keeps its rare Victorian 'snob screens' and etched-glass snugs intact — drinking in a gin-palace that time forgot, at Sam Smith's prices.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cittie-of-yorke-holborn",n:"Cittie of Yorke",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC1V",lat:51.518,lng:-0.112,c:"pub",s:"Cathedral-like long bar",q:"Cittie of Yorke Holborn pub",w:"A High Holborn pub with one of the longest bars in Britain, soaring triangular roof trusses and a row of cosy wooden cubicles — a vast, cathedral-like hall on a site that's served drinkers since the 15th century.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Cittie_of_Yorke_pub%2C_London_-_2023-01-27.jpg/960px-Cittie_of_Yorke_pub%2C_London_-_2023-01-27.jpg"},{id:"the-grenadier-belgravia",n:"The Grenadier",a:"Belgravia",pc:"SW1X",lat:51.501,lng:-0.153,c:"pub",s:"Haunted duel pub, notes on the ceiling",q:"The Grenadier Belgravia ghost Wilton Row",w:"Hidden down a Belgravia mews on Wilton Row, this 1818 pub began as an officers' mess — sentry box still outside, ceiling papered with banknotes, and a famous (if unprovable) ghost. A duel-haunted bolt-hole behind the grand houses.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:00",wq:"a"},{id:"seven-stars-carey-street",n:"The Seven Stars",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC2A",lat:51.515,lng:-0.113,c:"pub",s:"1602 pub behind the law courts",q:"Seven Stars Carey Street Holborn",w:"Established in 1602 — fourteen years before Shakespeare died — this tiny pub behind the Royal Courts of Justice survived the Great Fire and still pulls in barristers and a famously well-dressed pub cat. Four centuries of legal gossip.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 11:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Seven_Stars1.jpg/960px-Seven_Stars1.jpg"},{id:"sky-garden",n:"Sky Garden",a:"City of London",pc:"EC3M",lat:51.511,lng:-0.083,c:"view",s:"Free 'Walkie-Talkie' garden",q:"Sky Garden Fenchurch Street free booking",w:"The free public garden atop the 'Walkie-Talkie' at 20 Fenchurch Street — three storeys of palms and lookout terraces on the 35th floor, with sweeping Thames views. Book a (free) slot ahead; arrive for sunset if you can.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 10:00-18:00; Sa-Su,PH 11:00-21:00",wq:"a"},{id:"horizon-22",n:"Horizon 22",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2N",lat:51.515,lng:-0.082,c:"view",s:"Europe's highest free viewpoint",q:"Horizon 22 Bishopsgate free viewing",w:"Opened in 2023 on the 58th floor of 22 Bishopsgate, Horizon 22 is Europe's highest free public viewing gallery — 254 metres up, looking down on the Shard and the whole City sprawl. Free, but book ahead.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"garden-at-120",n:"The Garden at 120",a:"City of London",pc:"EC3M",lat:51.512,lng:-0.082,c:"view",s:"Free rooftop garden, no booking",q:"Garden at 120 Fen Court rooftop",w:"The City's largest public roof garden, on the 15th floor of Fen Court — wisteria walkways, a fountain and open-air views of the surrounding towers, with no booking and no charge. The easy, uncrowded rooftop.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"one-new-change-roof",n:"One New Change Roof Terrace",a:"City of London",pc:"EC4M",lat:51.514,lng:-0.095,c:"view",s:"Framed St Paul's close-up",q:"One New Change roof terrace St Paul's",w:"A free sixth-floor roof terrace atop the One New Change centre, delivering an unusually intimate, eye-level view of St Paul's dome — close enough to count the stonework. The best free St Paul's photo in London.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/One_New_Change.jpg/960px-One_New_Change.jpg"},{id:"parliament-hill",n:"Parliament Hill",a:"Hampstead Heath",pc:"NW5",lat:51.559,lng:-0.156,c:"view",s:"The classic free Heath panorama",q:"Parliament Hill view Hampstead Heath",w:"A protected viewpoint on Hampstead Heath whose sightline to St Paul's is shielded by law — kite-fliers, swimmers and the whole city laid out below. The classic, free, no-queue London panorama; bring a flask.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenwich-wolfe-statue",n:"General Wolfe Statue Viewpoint",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10",lat:51.477,lng:-0.001,c:"view",s:"Maritime Greenwich to Canary Wharf",q:"General Wolfe statue Greenwich Park view",w:"The terrace by the General Wolfe statue at the top of Greenwich Park frames the Old Royal Naval College, the Thames and Canary Wharf in one sweep — Wren's baroque maritime London with the towers rising behind. Unforgettable at dusk.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Statue_of_General_Wolfe%2C_Greenwich_Royal_Observatory_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3624175.jpg"},{id:"severndroog-castle",n:"Severndroog Castle",a:"Oxleas Wood",pc:"SE18",lat:51.464,lng:0.064,c:"follies",s:"Gothic folly above the woods",q:"Severndroog Castle Oxleas Wood viewpoint",w:"A Gothic triangular folly built in 1784 in Oxleas Wood on Shooter's Hill, commemorating a colonial siege — and, on clear days, offering views over several counties from its tower. A genuinely secret South-East London lookout in the trees.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tooting-bec-lido",n:"Tooting Bec Lido",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW16",lat:51.429,lng:-0.159,c:"lido",s:"UK's largest freshwater pool, 1906",q:"Tooting Bec Lido history",w:"Opened in 1906, this is the UK's largest freshwater pool by surface area — a vast, cold, gloriously unheated expanse ringed by candy-coloured changing cubicles. South London's great democratic swim, beloved by hardy year-round bathers.",city:"london"},{id:"parliament-hill-lido",n:"Parliament Hill Lido",a:"Gospel Oak",pc:"NW5",lat:51.555,lng:-0.154,c:"lido",s:"Art Deco unheated steel, 1938",q:"Parliament Hill Lido Gospel Oak",w:"A Grade II-listed 1938 lido on the edge of Hampstead Heath, famous for its gleaming stainless-steel pool — bracingly unheated and open year-round. Art Deco municipal swimming at its most photogenic.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brockwell-lido",n:"Brockwell Lido",a:"Herne Hill",pc:"SE24",lat:51.453,lng:-0.105,c:"lido",s:"'Brixton Beach' Art Deco icon",q:"Brockwell Lido Herne Hill",w:"A 1937 Art Deco lido nicknamed 'Brixton Beach,' ringed by Grade II-listed redbrick — a sun-trap of a summer institution between Herne Hill and Brixton. (Its operator's status has wobbled lately; check before you go.)",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 06:30-20:00;Fr 06:30-19:00;Sa-Su 07:00-18:00;PH 06:30-19:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Brockwell_Lido.jpg/960px-Brockwell_Lido.jpg"},{id:"hampstead-mixed-pond",n:"Hampstead Heath Mixed Bathing Pond",a:"Hampstead Heath",pc:"NW3",lat:51.564,lng:-0.161,c:"lido",s:"Wild swimming, three ponds",q:"Hampstead Heath bathing ponds swimming",w:"The Heath's spring-fed bathing ponds — Men's, Ladies' and Mixed — offer genuine wild swimming a few miles from the West End, among ducks and willow. Cold, clear and quietly addictive; lifeguarded in season.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"london-fields-lido",n:"London Fields Lido",a:"London Fields",pc:"E8",lat:51.541,lng:-0.062,c:"lido",s:"Heated Olympic-size, floodlit",q:"London Fields Lido Hackney",w:"A 50-metre heated outdoor pool kept around 25°C year-round and floodlit at dawn — Hackney's beloved Olympic-size lido, busy in summer, blissfully steaming in winter. The rare London lido you can happily swim in come January.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 06:30-21:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/St_Michael_and_All_Angels%2C_London_Fields_-_Exterior_view.jpg/960px-St_Michael_and_All_Angels%2C_London_Fields_-_Exterior_view.jpg"},{id:"serpentine-lido",n:"The Serpentine Lido",a:"Hyde Park",pc:"W2",lat:51.505,lng:-0.165,c:"lido",s:"Hyde Park's Peter Pan Cup",q:"Serpentine Lido Hyde Park swimming",w:"A roped-off swimming stretch of the Serpentine in Hyde Park, home to the Serpentine Swimming Club, who race for the Peter Pan Cup on Christmas morning. Open-water swimming in the very middle of the city.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brydges-place",n:"Brydges Place",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2N",lat:51.51,lng:-0.128,c:"alley",s:"London's narrowest alley",q:"Brydges Place narrowest alley London",w:"Running beside the Coliseum off St Martin's Lane, Brydges Place is claimed to be London's narrowest alley — barely shoulder-wide for much of its length, opening to a single hidden bar at the far end. Turn sideways and squeeze through.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"crown-passage",n:"Crown Passage",a:"St James's",pc:"SW1Y",lat:51.506,lng:-0.138,c:"alley",s:"Time-warp St James's lane",q:"Crown Passage St James's",w:"A narrow pedestrian lane between Pall Mall and King Street, lined with old-school shops, a sandwich bar and one of London's oldest pubs — a genuine time-warp slice of St James's the tourists walk straight past.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ely-place",n:"Ely Place & Ely Court",a:"Holborn",pc:"EC1N",lat:51.519,lng:-0.108,c:"alley",s:"Private street, hidden chapel",q:"Ely Place Holborn St Etheldreda's",w:"A gated private street that was the London estate of the Bishops of Ely and was long treated as a legal piece of Cambridgeshire — hiding St Etheldreda's, a rare 13th-century chapel, and (via Ely Court) the snug Ye Olde Mitre. A curiosity inside a curiosity.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Gatehouse_Ely_Place.jpg"},{id:"lovat-lane",n:"Lovat Lane",a:"City of London",pc:"EC3R",lat:51.509,lng:-0.084,c:"alley",s:"Cobbled medieval City lane",q:"Lovat Lane City of London cobbles",w:"A steep, cobbled City lane with a central gutter — a rare survival of the medieval street pattern, curving between Eastcheap and the river past the spire of St Mary-at-Hill. Two minutes from the glass towers, six centuries away.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"christchurch-gardens",n:"Christchurch Gardens",a:"Westminster",pc:"SW1E",lat:51.498,lng:-0.137,c:"death",s:"1665 plague burials under a park",q:"Christchurch Gardens Victoria Street plague pit",w:"A pocket of green between Victoria Street and the buses, pressed into service as a plague burying ground in 1665 and later a churchyard — now lawns and benches where office workers eat lunch above the dead. A Suffragette memorial stands at its edge; the burials lie quietly beneath.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bedlam-burial-ground",n:"Bedlam Burial Ground",a:"Liverpool Street",pc:"EC2M",lat:51.5178,lng:-0.0815,c:"death",s:"~25,000 dead beneath the Elizabeth line",q:"Bedlam burial ground Liverpool Street New Churchyard Crossrail",w:"The 'New Churchyard' beside Bethlem hospital took some 25,000 Londoners from the 1560s — plague dead, paupers and the odd executed radical — until Crossrail's diggers reached them in the 2010s. A small commemorative space near Liverpool Street now marks one of the city's great forgotten cemeteries.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"east-smithfield-cemetery",n:"East Smithfield Black Death Cemetery",a:"Tower Hill",pc:"E1W",lat:51.508,lng:-0.071,c:"death",s:"Emergency Black Death pit, 1348",q:"East Smithfield Black Death cemetery Royal Mint 1348",w:"When the Black Death overwhelmed London in 1348 the city opened an emergency cemetery here by the Tower — neat rows of plague victims later excavated beneath the old Royal Mint, their bones yielding the DNA that confirmed Yersinia pestis. Walk the pavement above one of the pandemic's best-studied graves.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/The_Royal_Mint%2C_London_%28378803870%29.jpg/960px-The_Royal_Mint%2C_London_%28378803870%29.jpg"},{id:"isleworth-all-saints",n:"All Saints Isleworth Plague Pit",a:"Isleworth",pc:"TW7",lat:51.4745,lng:-0.3215,c:"death",s:"Thames-side pit, marked by a yew",q:"All Saints Isleworth plague pit yew tree",w:"In the riverside churchyard of All Saints, a dark old yew is said to mark a pit where Isleworth buried its plague dead. A quiet Thames-path detour where the village's losses lie under the grass beside the water.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"clerkenwell-detention",n:"Clerkenwell House of Detention",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1R",lat:51.524,lng:-0.1078,c:"history",s:"Victorian prison vaults under a school",q:"Clerkenwell House of Detention catacombs",w:"Beneath a Clerkenwell school survive the brick vaults of a Victorian remand prison that once held nearly 300 at a time — damp, echoing tunnels locals call the Clerkenwell Catacombs. Rarely open, and a favourite film set, but you walk right above it on the green.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"morpeth-arms",n:"The Morpeth Arms",a:"Millbank",pc:"SW1P",lat:51.4905,lng:-0.1278,c:"pub",s:"Pub above Millbank prison cells",q:"Morpeth Arms Millbank prison cells ghost",w:"A handsome 1845 riverside pub built for the warders of the vast Millbank Penitentiary — whose holding cells still lurk in the cellar, complete with a resident ghost the staff swear by. Drink a pint by the Thames where convicts once waited for transportation to Australia.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Morpeth_Arms%2C_Pimlico%2C_SW1_%283106288271%29.jpg/960px-Morpeth_Arms%2C_Pimlico%2C_SW1_%283106288271%29.jpg"},{id:"viaduct-tavern",n:"The Viaduct Tavern",a:"Holborn",pc:"EC1A",lat:51.5163,lng:-0.1018,c:"pub",s:"Gin palace over Newgate cells",q:"Viaduct Tavern Newgate prison cells gin palace",w:"London's last great Victorian gin palace, all etched glass and beaten copper, sitting opposite the Old Bailey — and, the landlords will tell you, atop cells salvaged from Newgate Prison in its cellar. Ask nicely and you might see them; either way, admire one of the City's most ornate interiors.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 11:00-23:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/The_Viaduct_Public_House%2C_City_of_London_%2801%29.jpg/960px-The_Viaduct_Public_House%2C_City_of_London_%2801%29.jpg"},{id:"st-brides-charnel",n:"St Bride's Church & Charnel House",a:"Fleet Street",pc:"EC4Y",lat:51.5143,lng:-0.1053,c:"death",s:"Wren's printing church, bones below",q:"St Bride's Fleet Street crypt charnel house bones",w:"Wren's tiered 'wedding-cake' spire (said to have inspired the cake itself) crowns the journalists' church off Fleet Street — but the Blitz that gutted it also opened a crypt packed with centuries of bones, now a quietly macabre charnel house and museum. Printing history above, the dead below.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/St_Bride%27s_Church%2C_London_-_Diliff.jpg/960px-St_Bride%27s_Church%2C_London_-_Diliff.jpg"},{id:"order-of-st-john-crypt",n:"Museum of the Order of St John Crypt",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1M",lat:51.5228,lng:-0.1018,c:"medieval",s:"12th-century Hospitaller crypt",q:"Museum Order of St John Clerkenwell crypt St John's Gate",w:"Behind St John's Gate, a rare survival: the 12th-century crypt of the Knights Hospitaller's English priory, thick-walled and solemn, holding medieval effigies and an alabaster Spanish knight. Crusader history hidden under quiet Clerkenwell, and free to visit.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/St_John%27s_Gate%2C_Clerkenwell_-_London..jpg/960px-St_John%27s_Gate%2C_Clerkenwell_-_London..jpg"},{id:"garden-museum-crypt",n:"Garden Museum Crypt",a:"Lambeth",pc:"SE1",lat:51.4945,lng:-0.1218,c:"death",s:"Tombs beside Lambeth Palace",q:"Garden Museum St Mary-at-Lambeth crypt Tradescant Bligh tomb",w:"The old church of St Mary-at-Lambeth, saved from demolition as the Garden Museum, hides a glass-floored crypt and a churchyard of remarkable tombs — the plant-hunter Tradescants and Captain Bligh of the Bounty among them. Horticulture, history and the dead, in the shadow of Lambeth Palace.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Captain_bligh_tomb_garden_museum.jpg/960px-Captain_bligh_tomb_garden_museum.jpg"},{id:"whitefriars-crypt",n:"Whitefriars Crypt",a:"Fleet Street",pc:"EC4Y",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.1075,c:"medieval",s:"Carmelite vault under an office",q:"Whitefriars crypt Carmelite monastery Fleet Street Magpie Alley",w:"All that remains of a great Carmelite friary that once sprawled between Fleet Street and the river: a single medieval stone undercroft, now improbably preserved in the basement of a modern office off Magpie Alley. A 700-year-old secret beneath the glass.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenwich-foot-tunnel",n:"Greenwich Foot Tunnel",a:"Cutty Sark Gardens",pc:"SE10",lat:51.4828,lng:-0.0104,c:"oddity",s:"Subterranean Victorian walk",q:"Greenwich Foot Tunnel under Thames",w:"Opened in 1902 to replace the unreliable ferry for dock workers, this 370-metre cast-iron tunnel lets you walk directly beneath the Thames. Lined with 200,000 glazed white tiles, it is an echoing, surreal passage that feels completely detached from the city above — emerge on the Isle of Dogs for the classic Wren-framed view back at Greenwich.",city:"london"},{id:"tulip-stairs",n:"The Tulip Stairs",a:"Queen's House, Greenwich",pc:"SE10",lat:51.4811,lng:-0.0037,c:"history",s:"Britain's first self-supporting spiral",q:"Tulip Stairs Queen's House Greenwich",w:"Tucked inside Inigo Jones's Queen's House is the first geometric, self-supporting spiral staircase in Britain, built in the 1630s — the wrought-iron flowers in the balustrade give it its name. An architectural marvel, and famously the site of the 1966 'Rev. R. W. Hardy' ghost photograph that still draws the curious. Free to visit.",city:"london"},{id:"asylum-chapel",n:"Asylum Chapel",a:"Caroline Gardens, Peckham",pc:"SE15",lat:51.4785,lng:-0.0531,c:"history",s:"A cinematic ruin",q:"Asylum Chapel Caroline Gardens Peckham",w:"A Grade II listed chapel built in 1826 for retired pub landlords, bombed in the war and never fully restored. Today it stands as a beautifully weathered ruin — peeling paint, fractured stained glass, raw stone open to the light — and one of London's most photographed event spaces. Check listings; it opens for exhibitions and the odd ceremony.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"abbey-mills",n:"Abbey Mills Pumping Station",a:"Abbey Lane, Stratford",pc:"E15",lat:51.5312,lng:0.0003,c:"oddity",s:"The Cathedral of Sewage",q:"Abbey Mills Pumping Station Bazalgette",w:"Bazalgette's 1868 'Cathedral of Sewage' — a Byzantine-style temple of public health built to lift London's effluent into the great Northern Outfall sewer. Absurdly grand: cruciform plan, jewel-bright ironwork, Moorish detailing on a building for pumping waste. The exterior alone, glimpsed from the Greenway, is one of the city's strangest sights.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Abbey_Mills_Pumping_Station_main_building_%281%29_-_2022-04-03.jpg/960px-Abbey_Mills_Pumping_Station_main_building_%281%29_-_2022-04-03.jpg"},{id:"aldwych-station",n:"Aldwych Underground Station",a:"Strand",pc:"WC2R",lat:51.5115,lng:-0.1165,c:"history",s:"London's famous ghost station",q:"Aldwych ghost station Strand disused Underground",w:"Closed in 1994, this 'ghost station' sheltered Londoners during the Blitz and hid the British Museum's Elgin Marbles from the bombs. Now preserved in amber and a favourite film set (Atonement, Sherlock). Interior access needs a rare Transport Museum tour, but the original oxblood-tiled façade on the Strand is a monument to London legend in its own right.",city:"london"},{id:"handel-hendrix",n:"Handel & Hendrix",a:"Brook Street, Mayfair",pc:"W1K",lat:51.5129,lng:-0.1462,c:"plaque",s:"Neighbours, 200 years apart",q:"Handel Hendrix House Brook Street",w:"Jimi Hendrix lived at No. 23 (1968-69) right next door to where Handel composed Messiah at No. 25 — two English Heritage blue plaques side by side, now a joint museum. Hendrix cheerfully admitted he'd 'never heard much of the fella's stuff.'",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Handel_Hendrix_House_May_2023_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Handel_Hendrix_House_May_2023_%28cropped%29.jpg"},{id:"freddie-feltham",n:"Freddie Mercury's Family Home",a:"Gladstone Avenue, Feltham",pc:"TW13",lat:51.4459,lng:-0.4163,c:"plaque",s:"Where Zanzibar met Queen",q:"Freddie Mercury house 22 Gladstone Avenue Feltham",w:"The unassuming terrace where the Mercury family settled after fleeing Zanzibar in 1964; Freddie was living here when he met Brian May and Roger Taylor. The plaque was unveiled in 2016 by May and Freddie's sister — a true Queen-fan pilgrimage out west.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"chaplin-brixton",n:"Charlie Chaplin's Flat",a:"Glenshaw Mansions, Brixton",pc:"SW9",lat:51.472,lng:-0.113,c:"plaque",s:"A cherished haven",q:"Charlie Chaplin plaque Glenshaw Mansions Brixton Road",w:"The flat Chaplin shared with his brother Sydney (1908-10) at the very start of his career — a 'cherished haven' after one of the most brutal childhoods in Victorian London. Plaque unveiled by Paul Merton in 2017.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"agatha-christie-home",n:"Agatha Christie's House",a:"Sheffield Terrace, Holland Park",pc:"W8",lat:51.5076,lng:-0.1969,c:"plaque",s:"Murder she wrote here",q:"Agatha Christie 58 Sheffield Terrace plaque",w:"Where Christie wrote Murder on the Orient Express, Death on the Nile and The ABC Murders — three of the most-adapted mysteries ever written, all from one quiet house five minutes from Notting Hill Gate.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bacon-studio",n:"Francis Bacon's Studio",a:"Reece Mews, South Kensington",pc:"SW7",lat:51.4936,lng:-0.174,c:"plaque",s:"Chaos as a studio",q:"Francis Bacon studio 7 Reece Mews",w:"The cramped mews where Bacon lived and painted for over 30 years until 1992, in a legendary chaos of slashed canvases and paint-smeared walls — the room that produced Three Studies for a Crucifixion (the studio itself was later moved, brick by brick, to Dublin).",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mary-seacole-soho",n:"Mary Seacole's Home",a:"Soho Square",pc:"W1D",lat:51.5155,lng:-0.132,c:"plaque",s:"The other Crimean nurse",q:"Mary Seacole 14 Soho Square plaque",w:"The Jamaican-British heroine of the Crimean War — who funded her own passage to nurse soldiers when the War Office turned her away — lived here in the 1850s. Her only known surviving London home.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"turing-maida-vale",n:"Alan Turing's Birthplace",a:"Warrington Crescent, Maida Vale",pc:"W9",lat:51.5239,lng:-0.186,c:"plaque",s:"Born here, broke Enigma",q:"Alan Turing 2 Warrington Crescent plaque",w:"The birthplace of the codebreaker and father of computer science, whose Bletchley Park work is credited with shortening the war by years — and who was then hounded to his death by the state he saved.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"napoleon-iii-plaque",n:"The Napoleon III Plaque",a:"King Street, St James's",pc:"SW1Y",lat:51.507,lng:-0.137,c:"plaque",s:"The oldest plaque, 1867",q:"Napoleon III blue plaque King Street St James's",w:"The oldest surviving official London blue plaque, and the only one ever installed in its subject's own lifetime — it bears the imperial eagle and marks the exiled emperor's London lodgings.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"douglas-adams-grave",n:"Douglas Adams' Grave",a:"Highgate Cemetery East",pc:"N6",lat:51.5666,lng:-0.1455,c:"death",s:"Leave a pen",q:"Douglas Adams grave Highgate Cemetery East",w:"Fans leave pens and pencils in a pot before the plain dark headstone — a quiet running joke for the author of The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy. One of the cemetery's most-visited modern graves.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"caulfield-dead",n:"Patrick Caulfield's 'DEAD' Grave",a:"Highgate Cemetery East",pc:"N6",lat:51.5665,lng:-0.145,c:"death",s:"Says it like it is",q:"Patrick Caulfield DEAD gravestone Highgate",w:"The Pop artist secretly designed his own headstone: the single laser-cut word 'DEAD' pierced clean through the stone in his own Art Deco lettering. Asked what his epitaph should read, he reputedly replied: 'Dead, of course.'",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mclaren-grave",n:"Malcolm McLaren's Grave",a:"Highgate Cemetery East",pc:"N6",lat:51.5663,lng:-0.1442,c:"death",s:"A spectacular failure",q:"Malcolm McLaren grave Highgate Cemetery",w:"The Sex Pistols' manager lies under a bronze death mask, about 100 yards from Karl Marx, beneath the epitaph 'Better a spectacular failure, than a benign success' — the words of his old art-school teacher.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"george-michael-grave",n:"George Michael's Grave",a:"Highgate Cemetery West",pc:"N6",lat:51.5674,lng:-0.1489,c:"death",s:"Beloved son and brother",q:"George Michael grave Highgate Cemetery West",w:"In the family plot beside his mother and sister, the monument bears his birth name, Georgios Kyriacos Panayiotou. The West side is ticketed and tributes are gently discouraged — visit quietly and with respect.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"faraday-grave",n:"Michael Faraday's Grave",a:"Highgate Cemetery West",pc:"N6",lat:51.567,lng:-0.148,c:"death",s:"The modest genius",q:"Michael Faraday grave Highgate Cemetery West",w:"A deliberately plain Sandemanian headstone for the self-taught giant of electromagnetism — who turned down a knighthood and burial in Westminster Abbey for exactly this kind of modesty.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wombwell-lion",n:"George Wombwell's Lion Tomb",a:"Highgate Cemetery West",pc:"N6",lat:51.5668,lng:-0.1478,c:"death",s:"Nero sleeps on top",q:"George Wombwell lion tomb Highgate Cemetery",w:"The Victorian travelling-menagerie owner is buried beneath a stone sculpture of his star attraction — Nero, the lion so gentle he famously refused to fight the baiting dogs set on him.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bram-stoker-urn",n:"Bram Stoker's Urn",a:"Golders Green Crematorium",pc:"NW11",lat:51.579,lng:-0.198,c:"death",s:"Dracula's ashes",q:"Bram Stoker ashes Golders Green Crematorium columbarium",w:"The author of Dracula rests as ashes in an urn in the columbarium — viewable only by staff escort, a small final touch of gothic inaccessibility for the man who gave the world the Count.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"notting-hill-door",n:"The Notting Hill Blue Door",a:"Westbourne Park Road",pc:"W11",lat:51.518,lng:-0.201,c:"film",s:"Whoops-a-daisy",q:"Notting Hill blue door 280 Westbourne Park Road",w:"The (now replica) blue door of William Thacker's flat from Notting Hill — the original was auctioned for charity after the film. Pure rom-com pilgrimage; mind the queue for a photo.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Tabernacle_1.jpg/960px-Tabernacle_1.jpg"},{id:"mr-gruber-portobello",n:"Mr Gruber's Shop",a:"Portobello Road",pc:"W11",lat:51.516,lng:-0.205,c:"film",s:"Paddington's antique stop",q:"Alice's Antiques 86 Portobello Road Paddington",w:"The cluttered antiques shop that plays Mr Gruber's in the Paddington films — and it's a genuine Portobello institution, every bit as eccentric off screen as on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brown-house-chalcot",n:"The Brown Family House",a:"Chalcot Crescent, Primrose Hill",pc:"NW1",lat:51.539,lng:-0.154,c:"film",s:"Paddington's front door",q:"Chalcot Crescent Paddington Brown family house",w:"A pastel-fronted house on a perfect curve of Primrose Hill stuccoes plays the Browns' home in the Paddington films. The whole crescent is an Instagram set-piece in its own right.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"eaton-place-165",n:"165 Eaton Place",a:"Belgravia",pc:"SW1X",lat:51.4965,lng:-0.153,c:"film",s:"Upstairs, downstairs",q:"165 Eaton Place Upstairs Downstairs Belgravia",w:"The real Belgravia townhouse used for the exterior shots of the classic series Upstairs, Downstairs — the front door behind which a whole world of Edwardian servants and masters was imagined.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Pavement_in_Eaton_Place_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2122476.jpg"},{id:"clash-steps-camden",n:"The Clash Steps",a:"Stables Market, Camden",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5418,lng:-0.147,c:"music",s:"Punk's photo op",q:"The Clash debut album steps Stables Market Camden",w:"The trolley ramp outside the old Rehearsal Rehearsals where The Clash were shot for their 1977 debut album cover. Punk pilgrims still line up to recreate the pose in the railway arches.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"battersea-animals",n:"Battersea Power Station",a:"Battersea",pc:"SW11",lat:51.482,lng:-0.144,c:"music",s:"Mind the flying pig",q:"Battersea Power Station Pink Floyd Animals pig",w:"The building on Pink Floyd's Animals sleeve — during the 1976 shoot the inflatable pig broke its moorings and drifted off over Kent, grounding flights at Heathrow. Now a vast redeveloped destination in its own right.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Battersea_Power_Station_from_the_river.jpg/960px-Battersea_Power_Station_from_the_river.jpg"},{id:"word-on-the-water",n:"Word on the Water",a:"Regent's Canal, King's Cross",pc:"N1C",lat:51.5343,lng:-0.1257,c:"bookshops",s:"The floating bookshop",q:"Word on the Water canal bookshop King's Cross",w:"A 1920s Dutch barge moored on the canal, selling new and second-hand books from its decks with live jazz and poetry on the roof. One of the most photographed shops in London, and far lovelier than the photos.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 12:00-19:00",wq:"a"},{id:"hatchards",n:"Hatchards",a:"Piccadilly",pc:"W1J",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.139,c:"bookshops",s:"London's oldest bookshop, 1797",q:"Hatchards Piccadilly bookshop",w:"Founded in 1797 and at 187 Piccadilly since 1810 — royal-warranted, arranged like a private townhouse over five creaking floors, and still the most civilised place in London to lose an afternoon.",city:"london",oh:"Su-Fr 09:30-20:00; Sa 12:00-18:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Hatchards%2C_London.png/960px-Hatchards%2C_London.png"},{id:"persephone-books",n:"Persephone Books",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1N",lat:51.524,lng:-0.123,c:"literary",s:"Lost women writers",q:"Persephone Books Bloomsbury grey covers",w:"A publisher and shop reviving neglected (mostly women) writers of the 20th century, each reissued in the same elegant dove-grey cover with a patterned endpaper. A quietly radical act of literary rescue.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brick-lane-bookshop",n:"Brick Lane Bookshop",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.522,lng:-0.072,c:"literary",s:"The East End's own",q:"Brick Lane Bookshop Spitalfields",w:"A fiercely independent shop specialising in London and East End writing, running its own short story prize. Small, opinionated, and exactly the kind of place algorithms can't replace.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Brick_Lane_street_signs.JPG/960px-Brick_Lane_street_signs.JPG"},{id:"libreria-shop",n:"Libreria",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.52,lng:-0.072,c:"literary",s:"No phones, themed shelves",q:"Libreria bookshop Hanbury Street no phones",w:"A jewel-box shop by architects SelgasCano where books are shelved not by genre but by mood — 'Wanderlust', 'Enchantment for the Disenchanted' — under a strict no-phones policy designed to make you actually read.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stanfords",n:"Stanfords",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2H",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.126,c:"literary",s:"Maps since 1853",q:"Stanfords map travel bookshop Covent Garden Mercer Walk",w:"The legendary travel and map shop, founded 1853 and name-checked in The Hound of the Baskervilles — where explorers from Scott to Livingstone bought their charts. Now on Mercer Walk, floors of maps and guides intact.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/12-14_Long_Acre_-_Stanfords_Bookshop_%28geograph_2682247%29.jpg/960px-12-14_Long_Acre_-_Stanfords_Bookshop_%28geograph_2682247%29.jpg"},{id:"new-beacon-books",n:"New Beacon Books",a:"Finsbury Park",pc:"N4",lat:51.564,lng:-0.106,c:"literary",s:"UK's first Black publisher",q:"New Beacon Books Finsbury Park",w:"Founded in 1966 by poet John La Rose as Britain's first Black publisher and specialist bookshop — a cornerstone of Black British intellectual life and the Caribbean Artists Movement.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"terroni-clerkenwell",n:"Terroni of Clerkenwell",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1R",lat:51.5226,lng:-0.1075,c:"diaspora",s:"England's oldest deli, 1878",q:"Terroni of Clerkenwell Italian deli",w:"When Luigi Terroni opened here in 1878 it was, reputedly, the first delicatessen in all of England — set in old 'Little Italy' beside St Peter's Italian Church, and still slicing salami a century and a half on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"st-peters-italian",n:"St Peter's Italian Church",a:"Clerkenwell Road",pc:"EC1R",lat:51.523,lng:-0.108,c:"diaspora",s:"Little Italy's basilica",q:"St Peter's Italian Church Clerkenwell",w:"The first Italian church built outside Italy (1863), modelled on a basilica in Rome's Trastevere — still the heart of Little Italy, with its huge July Procession of Our Lady of Mount Carmel through the streets of Clerkenwell.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/St_Peter%27s_church_2020.jpg/960px-St_Peter%27s_church_2020.jpg"},{id:"sandys-row-synagogue",n:"Sandys Row Synagogue",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.518,lng:-0.077,c:"diaspora",s:"Oldest Ashkenazi shul",q:"Sandys Row Synagogue Spitalfields",w:"London's oldest surviving Ashkenazi synagogue, founded in 1854 by fifty poor Dutch-Jewish families inside a 1766 ex-Huguenot chapel — a building that has sheltered two waves of immigrants under one roof.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Sandys_Row_Synagogue_2008.jpg"},{id:"bevis-marks",n:"Bevis Marks Synagogue",a:"City of London",pc:"EC3A",lat:51.515,lng:-0.079,c:"diaspora",s:"Britain's oldest synagogue, 1701",q:"Bevis Marks Synagogue City of London",w:"Britain's oldest synagogue in continuous use, consecrated 1701 by London's Sephardi community — its candlelit interior, lit by hanging brass chandeliers, has barely changed in over three centuries.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Bevis_Marks.jpg/960px-Bevis_Marks.jpg"},{id:"windrush-square",n:"Windrush Square",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW2",lat:51.4625,lng:-0.113,c:"diaspora",s:"Where Windrush is remembered",q:"Windrush Square Brixton Black Cultural Archives",w:"Renamed in 1998 for the 50th anniversary of the Empire Windrush, the square holds the UK's first African and Caribbean War Memorial and the Black Cultural Archives — the civic heart of Black Brixton.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"fournier-street",n:"Fournier Street",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.519,lng:-0.073,c:"diaspora",s:"Look up for weavers' lofts",q:"Fournier Street Spitalfields Huguenot houses",w:"The best-preserved early-Georgian street in Britain, built in the 1720s for Huguenot master silk-weavers — look up for the broad attic 'weavers' lights' that lit their looms, and for the lace-cuff hand door-knockers.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/23_Fournier_Street%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"jamme-masjid",n:"London Jamme Masjid",a:"Brick Lane corner",pc:"E1",lat:51.5197,lng:-0.0718,c:"diaspora",s:"Chapel, synagogue, mosque",q:"Brick Lane Jamme Masjid Fournier Street",w:"One building that has been, in turn, a 1743 Huguenot chapel, a Methodist mission, a synagogue and now a mosque — the whole story of immigrant Spitalfields in one set of walls, beneath a sundial reading 'Umbra sumus' (We are shadows).",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mangal-2-dalston",n:"Mangal 2",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.547,lng:-0.075,c:"food",s:"Gilbert & George's canteen",q:"Mangal 2 Dalston ocakbasi",w:"The Dalston grill where artists Gilbert & George have famously dined almost nightly for decades — a charcoal ocakbaşı as known for its surreal social-media presence as for its lamb.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"song-que-pho",n:"Sông Quê Café",a:"Kingsland Road, Shoreditch",pc:"E2",lat:51.531,lng:-0.077,c:"food",s:"The Pho Mile's green corner",q:"Song Que Cafe Kingsland Road pho",w:"The bright-green flagship of Kingsland Road's 'Pho Mile', drawing queues out the door since 2002. The bowl that taught a generation of Londoners what pho actually tastes like.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lisboa-patisserie",n:"Lisboa Patisserie",a:"South Lambeth",pc:"SW8",lat:51.479,lng:-0.123,c:"bakery",s:"Pastéis in Little Portugal",q:"Lisboa Patisserie South Lambeth Road custard tart",w:"The custard-tart heart of London's Portuguese community on South Lambeth Road — strong bica, warm pastéis de nata, and a counter that has anchored Little Portugal for decades.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"fish-wings-tings",n:"Fish, Wings & Tings",a:"Brixton Village",pc:"SW9",lat:51.4615,lng:-0.115,c:"food",s:"Trinidadian institution",q:"Fish Wings and Tings Brixton Village",w:"A tiny, much-loved Caribbean kitchen in Brixton Village from a Trinidadian chef — tamarind-glazed jerk wings and curried goat that helped make Brixton's covered market a dining destination.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"roa-crane",n:"ROA's Crane Mural",a:"Hanbury Street, Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5205,lng:-0.072,c:"streetart",s:"Brick Lane's giant bird",q:"ROA crane mural Hanbury Street Brick Lane",w:"A roughly 40-foot bird painted in 2010 by the Belgian artist ROA — originally a heron, changed to a crane at the request of local Bengali residents, and now one of Brick Lane's most enduring works.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"banksy-guard-dog",n:"Banksy's 'Guard Dog'",a:"Rivington Street, Shoreditch",pc:"EC2A",lat:51.526,lng:-0.08,c:"streetart",s:"Banksy under perspex",q:"Banksy Guard Dog Rivington Street Shoreditch",w:"A 2003 Banksy stencil of a security guard and his poodle, surviving behind perspex in the old Cargo courtyard alongside the HMV dog. Worth checking the courtyard is open before you make the trip.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"banksy-royals",n:"Banksy's Royal Family Mural",a:"Stoke Newington",pc:"N16",lat:51.562,lng:-0.078,c:"streetart",s:"Waving royals since 2001",q:"Banksy royal family mural Stoke Newington Church Street",w:"One of Banksy's best-preserved early works — a mock royal family that has waved cheerfully from a Stoke Newington wall since 2001, long before the auction-house millions.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hackney-peace-mural",n:"Hackney Peace Carnival Mural",a:"Dalston Lane",pc:"E8",lat:51.546,lng:-0.074,c:"streetart",s:"1983's march, painted",q:"Hackney Peace Carnival Mural Dalston Lane Ray Walker",w:"Ray Walker's vivid 1985 mural of a multicultural anti-nuclear march down Dalston Lane — finished by his collaborators after his sudden death, and lovingly restored for its 40th anniversary.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"velazquez-banksy",n:"The Velázquez Banksy",a:"Portobello",pc:"W10",lat:51.521,lng:-0.206,c:"streetart",s:"Old master meets stencil",q:"Banksy Velazquez Portobello Acklam Road",w:"A 2008 Banksy riff on a Spanish old master, restored to view on the corner of Portobello and Acklam Roads — a sly bit of art-history graffiti hiding in plain sight on the market's edge.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenwich-wolfe-view",n:"General Wolfe Viewpoint",a:"Greenwich Park",pc:"SE10",lat:51.4769,lng:-0.001,c:"view",s:"Protected by law",q:"General Wolfe statue Greenwich Park view",w:"From beside the Wolfe statue at the top of Greenwich Park, the sweep over the Old Royal Naval College to Canary Wharf and the City is so prized that the sightline is legally protected from obstruction.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"viktor-wynd",n:"Viktor Wynd Museum of Curiosities",a:"Hackney",pc:"E2",lat:51.535,lng:-0.061,c:"museum",s:"Two-headed lambs & oddments",q:"Viktor Wynd Museum of Curiosities Hackney",w:"A subterranean Wunderkabinett beneath The Last Tuesday Society bar — taxidermy, occult relics, dodo bones and infamous celebrity oddments, best explored with a cocktail in hand. London's strangest collection by some distance.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"novelty-automation",n:"Novelty Automation",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC1N",lat:51.521,lng:-0.118,c:"museum",s:"A satirical penny arcade",q:"Novelty Automation Holborn arcade Tim Hunkin",w:"Tim Hunkin's hand-built arcade of satirical coin-op machines — test your nerve against a nuclear reactor, take a 'instant holiday', or have your money laundered, literally. Joyful, anarchic and unlike anywhere else.",city:"london",oh:"Tu,We,Fr,Sa 11:00-18:00; Th 12:00-20:00; Su 12:00-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"vagina-museum",n:"Vagina Museum",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"E2",lat:51.527,lng:-0.056,c:"museum",s:"The world's first",q:"Vagina Museum Bethnal Green",w:"The world's first bricks-and-mortar museum dedicated to the gynaecological — crowdfunded, pun-loving, trans-inclusive and quietly radical, with rotating exhibitions that are far more educational than the name suggests.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"magic-circle-museum",n:"The Magic Circle Museum",a:"Euston",pc:"NW1",lat:51.529,lng:-0.134,c:"museum",s:"Houdini's handcuffs",q:"Magic Circle Museum Euston Houdini",w:"The headquarters collection of the world's most famous society of magicians — including Houdini's own handcuffs — opened only for events and pre-booked tours behind a famously discreet door.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"the-grapes-limehouse",n:"The Grapes",a:"Limehouse",pc:"E14",lat:51.5095,lng:-0.0335,c:"pub",s:"Dickens's narrow tavern",q:"The Grapes Limehouse pub Narrow Street",w:"A sliver of an 18th-century riverside pub on Narrow Street — now co-owned by Ian McKellen — that Dickens put into Our Mutual Friend as the Six Jolly Fellowship Porters. Step out the back for the Thames at your feet.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Grapes%2C_Limehouse%2C_E14_%283679094059%29.jpg/960px-Grapes%2C_Limehouse%2C_E14_%283679094059%29.jpg"},{id:"hodge-the-cat",n:"Hodge the Cat",a:"Gough Square",pc:"EC4A",lat:51.5152,lng:-0.108,c:"oddity",s:"A very fine cat indeed",q:"Hodge the Cat statue Gough Square Dr Johnson",w:"A bronze of Dr Johnson's cat, perched on a dictionary with two oyster shells at his paws, outside the great lexicographer's house — 'a very fine cat indeed.' One of the city's 'Talking Statues.'",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sherlock-statue",n:"Sherlock Holmes Statue",a:"Baker Street",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5225,lng:-0.157,c:"oddity",s:"Deerstalker on",q:"Sherlock Holmes statue Baker Street station",w:"The deerstalkered detective in bronze outside Baker Street station, pipe in hand — and the most-listened-to of London's 'Talking Statues', if you call the number on the plaque.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dc/Statue_Of_Sherlock_Holmes-Marylebone_Road.jpg"},{id:"wollstonecraft-sculpture",n:"The Wollstonecraft Sculpture",a:"Newington Green",pc:"N16",lat:51.553,lng:-0.083,c:"oddity",s:"The statue that sparked a row",q:"Mary Wollstonecraft sculpture Newington Green Maggi Hambling",w:"Maggi Hambling's 2020 silvered sculpture — a swirl of female forms topped by a tiny nude figure — on the green where the mother of feminism ran her school. Controversial from the moment it was unveiled, and still arguing its case.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vine-street-wall",n:"City Wall at Vine Street",a:"Aldgate",pc:"EC3N",lat:51.5135,lng:-0.076,c:"roman",s:"Roman wall, indoors, free",q:"City Wall at Vine Street Roman bastion exhibition",w:"A ten-metre stretch of London's Roman and medieval wall, complete with a bastion, preserved in a basement gallery and opened to the public — free, climate-controlled, and astonishingly intact (book ahead).",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"loftus-road",n:"Loftus Road",a:"Shepherd's Bush, W12",pc:"W12",lat:51.5093,lng:-0.2321,c:"stadium",s:"QPR's tight, roaring home ground",q:"Loftus Road Stadium W12",w:"Queens Park Rangers' home since 1917 — one of the smallest, steepest, loudest grounds in London football, hemmed in by terraced streets a few minutes from your front door. Officially the Kiyan Prince Foundation Stadium since 2019, renamed for a 15-year-old academy player killed outside his school in 2006, to keep his name alive.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Loftus_Road_Stadium_25_April_2025.jpg/960px-Loftus_Road_Stadium_25_April_2025.jpg"},{id:"stamford-bridge",n:"Stamford Bridge",a:"Fulham Road, SW6",pc:"SW6",lat:51.4816,lng:-0.191,c:"stadium",s:"Chelsea's home since 1905",q:"Stamford Bridge Chelsea SW6",w:"Chelsea have played here since the club was founded in 1905 — the ground actually predates the team, built first with a club invented to fill it. Once an athletics stadium with a vast banked terrace; the Shed End behind the goal gave the club its noisiest support.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/London_Stamford_Bridge.jpg/960px-London_Stamford_Bridge.jpg"},{id:"emirates-stadium",n:"Emirates Stadium",a:"Holloway, N7",pc:"N7",lat:51.5549,lng:-0.1084,c:"stadium",s:"Arsenal's modern bowl",q:"Emirates Stadium Arsenal N7",w:"Arsenal's home since 2006, when they left nearby Highbury and its beloved Art Deco stands. A 60,000-seat bowl ringed with 'Arsenalisation' murals and the cannon crest; statues of Thierry Henry, Tony Adams and Dennis Bergkamp stand outside.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/London_Emirates_Stadium_arsenal.jpg/960px-London_Emirates_Stadium_arsenal.jpg"},{id:"spurs-stadium",n:"Tottenham Hotspur Stadium",a:"Tottenham, N17",pc:"N17",lat:51.6043,lng:-0.0664,c:"stadium",s:"Spurs' high-tech arena",q:"Tottenham Hotspur Stadium N17",w:"Opened in 2019 on the site of White Hart Lane, with a single-tier South Stand — the largest in the country — built for a sheer wall of noise. A retractable pitch slides away to reveal an artificial NFL surface beneath, and there's a microbrewery under the stands.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/London_Tottenham_Hotspur_Stadium.jpg/960px-London_Tottenham_Hotspur_Stadium.jpg"},{id:"london-stadium",n:"London Stadium",a:"Stratford, E20",pc:"E20",lat:51.5386,lng:-0.0166,c:"stadium",s:"West Ham's Olympic home",q:"London Stadium West Ham E20",w:"Built as the centrepiece of the 2012 Olympics — where Mo Farah and Jessica Ennis-Hill won gold — and now home to West Ham. The athletics track still rings the pitch, a sore point for fans who left the tight, fierce Upton Park in 2016.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 09:30-17:00; Sa 09:00-17:00; Su 11:00-17:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/London_Olympic_Stadium_West_Ham.jpg/960px-London_Olympic_Stadium_West_Ham.jpg"},{id:"gtech-stadium",n:"Gtech Community Stadium",a:"Brentford, TW8",pc:"TW8",lat:51.4906,lng:-0.2889,c:"stadium",s:"Brentford's compact new ground",q:"Gtech Community Stadium Brentford",w:"Brentford's home since 2020, wedged beside the Kew Bridge railway and the Thames, replacing beloved Griffin Park — famous as the only English ground with a pub on all four corners. The Bees' climb from League One to the Premier League was one of football's best recent stories.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Brentford_Community_Stadium_2020.jpg/960px-Brentford_Community_Stadium_2020.jpg"},{id:"wembley-stadium",n:"Wembley Stadium",a:"Wembley, HA9",pc:"HA9",lat:51.556,lng:-0.2796,c:"stadium",s:"the national stadium under the arch",q:"Wembley Stadium HA9",w:"England's home, crowned by a 134-metre steel arch visible right across the city. The 2007 rebuild replaced the old Twin Towers where England won the 1966 World Cup; today it hosts cup finals, internationals and the biggest concerts London can stage.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"selhurst-park",n:"Selhurst Park",a:"South Norwood, SE25",pc:"SE25",lat:51.3983,lng:-0.0855,c:"stadium",s:"Crystal Palace's raucous ground",q:"Selhurst Park Crystal Palace SE25",w:"Crystal Palace's home since 1924 and one of the most atmospheric grounds in the league — the Holmesdale Road end runs an ultras-style drum-and-banner show. Recently famous worldwide as AFC Richmond's 'dog pound' in Ted Lasso.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/2023_09_09_arne_mueseler_17_18_07_00743-Verbessert-RR_%2853283239217%29.jpg/960px-2023_09_09_arne_mueseler_17_18_07_00743-Verbessert-RR_%2853283239217%29.jpg"},{id:"the-den",n:"The Den",a:"Bermondsey, SE16",pc:"SE16",lat:51.4859,lng:-0.0507,c:"stadium",s:"Millwall's famously fierce home",q:"The Den Millwall SE16",w:"Home of Millwall, whose supporters wear their 'No one likes us, we don't care' chant as a badge of honour. The current Den opened in 1993 just up from the old one — a hard, proud South London club with deep dockland roots.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/The_New_Den_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1143517.jpg"},{id:"north-gower-sherlock",n:"Speedy's & North Gower Street",a:"Euston, NW1",pc:"NW1",lat:51.5266,lng:-0.1352,c:"film",s:"Sherlock's '221B Baker Street'",q:"Speedy's Cafe North Gower Street NW1",w:"The BBC's Sherlock barely touched Baker Street — 221B's front door is 187 North Gower Street, with Speedy's Sandwich Bar next door playing itself. The cafe still does a roaring trade in fans ordering the 'Sherlock wrap'.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Corner_of_Gower_Street_and_Euston_Road.jpg"},{id:"platform-nine-three-quarters",n:"Platform 9 3/4",a:"King's Cross Station, N1C",pc:"N1C",lat:51.5319,lng:-0.124,c:"film",s:"the Harry Potter trolley in the wall",q:"Platform 9 3/4 King's Cross Station",w:"A luggage trolley vanishing into the brickwork, marking where Harry caught the Hogwarts Express. The photo-op and its shop sit in the King's Cross concourse — queue for a scarf and a wand-flick, mid-leap, for the camera.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sis-building-mi6",n:"The SIS Building (MI6)",a:"Vauxhall, SE11",pc:"SE11",lat:51.4873,lng:-0.1247,c:"film",s:"Bond's HQ on the Thames",q:"SIS Building Vauxhall Cross London",w:"The real headquarters of the Secret Intelligence Service — the cream-and-green ziggurat on the river that Bond films love to blow up, most memorably in Skyfall and Spectre. Best seen from Vauxhall Bridge or the far bank.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/SIS_Building%2C_Vauxhall_Cross_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4541871.jpg/960px-SIS_Building%2C_Vauxhall_Cross_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4541871.jpg"},{id:"rangers-house-bridgerton",n:"Ranger's House",a:"Greenwich, SE10",pc:"SE10",lat:51.4683,lng:0.001,c:"film",s:"the Bridgerton family home",q:"Ranger's House Greenwich SE10",w:"This red-brick Georgian villa on the edge of Greenwich Park is the exterior of Bridgerton House — though the wisteria-draped gates are pure TV magic. Inside is the Wernher Collection of medieval and Renaissance treasures; the park beyond stood in for Mayfair.",city:"london"},{id:"princes-head-ted-lasso",n:"The Prince's Head",a:"Richmond Green, TW9",pc:"TW9",lat:51.4615,lng:-0.3062,c:"film",s:"Ted Lasso's 'Crown & Anchor'",q:"The Prince's Head Richmond Green",w:"The 300-year-old pub on Richmond Green that plays the Crown & Anchor in Ted Lasso (the interior is a studio rebuild). Step out and down Paved Court to find Ted's front door at number 11a — '9 3/4' on screen.",city:"london"},{id:"globe-tavern-bridget",n:"The Globe Tavern",a:"Borough Market, SE1",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5055,lng:-0.0905,c:"film",s:"Bridget Jones's flat, above the pub",q:"The Globe Tavern Bedale Street Borough",w:"On the edge of Borough Market, this is the pub over which Bridget Jones supposedly kept her flat — the camera looks down on the market from her window. The opening 'All By Myself' singalong made the spot a fan favourite.",city:"london"},{id:"earls-court-tardis",n:"Earl's Court Police Box",a:"Earl's Court, SW5",pc:"SW5",lat:51.4915,lng:-0.1935,c:"film",s:"a real-life TARDIS on the street",q:"Earl's Court police box TARDIS",w:"A genuine 1996 Metropolitan Police call box stands outside Earl's Court Tube — the same blue Mark 2 design the BBC turned into Doctor Who's TARDIS. Whovians treat the unassuming corner as a quiet shrine.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Earl%27s_Court_stn_eastern_entrance.JPG/960px-Earl%27s_Court_stn_eastern_entrance.JPG"},{id:"isabellaplant",n:"Isabella Plantation",a:"Richmond Park",pc:"TW10",lat:51.4334,lng:-0.2774,c:"green",s:"azaleas · hidden woodland garden",q:"Isabella Plantation Richmond Park",w:"Deep in Richmond Park’s deer-cropped acres hides a 40-acre woodland garden that detonates into azalea fire every late April. Lord Sidmouth fenced it off two centuries ago to keep the deer out; now its streams and ponds glow with rhododendron and Kurume azaleas brought from Japan in the 1920s. Few bother to seek it — wander the paths and let the colour do its quiet violence.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Isabella_Plantation%2C_Richmond_Park%2C_London_-_April_2011.jpg/960px-Isabella_Plantation%2C_Richmond_Park%2C_London_-_April_2011.jpg"},{id:"fryingpanalley",n:"Frying Pan Alley",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"E1",lat:51.5175,lng:-0.0775,c:"alley",s:"ironmongers’ ghost · a whispering passage",q:"Frying Pan Alley Spitalfields London",w:"A narrow gullet off the old Petticoat Lane, named for the frying pans that ironmongers once hung above their doors as trade signs. Step in and the centuries press close — East End grit gone quiet. The sort of passage you crave on an afternoon when you want alleys that whisper instead of shout.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bowcreek",n:"Bow Creek Ecology Park",a:"Canning Town",pc:"E16",lat:51.5121,lng:0.0034,c:"green",s:"ironworks turned wild · a Lea peninsula",q:"Bow Creek Ecology Park",w:"On a peninsula where the River Lea coils toward the Thames, an 1846 ironworks and coal wharf has been reclaimed by reeds, ponds and birdsong. The DLR rattles overhead; tidal water laps below. A Green Flag wildlife haven hiding a thousand feet from Canning Town station — proof the city still grows wild at its edges.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Bow_Creek_Ecology_Park.jpg/960px-Bow_Creek_Ecology_Park.jpg"},{id:"wickwoodland",n:"Wick Woodland",a:"Hackney",pc:"E9",lat:51.5475,lng:-0.0295,c:"green",s:"saved from concrete · 30,000 trees",q:"Wick Woodland Hackney Marshes",w:"A triangular plot at the southern tip of Hackney Marshes, planted with 30,000 trees in 1996 when locals fought off the developers. Silver birches reach up with a faintly ghostly hush; plane trees lean in from the towpath. The busy road runs close, yet the wood swallows the noise. London still has wild edges worth defending.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"littlebritain",n:"Little Britain",a:"Smithfield",pc:"EC1A",lat:51.5172,lng:-0.0985,c:"alley",s:"medieval echoes · by St Bart’s",q:"Little Britain street London Smithfield",w:"A crooked City street curling past St Bartholomew’s, named for the Dukes of Brittany who once kept houses here. The roar of the Square Mile fades to a whisper between the old walls; duck through toward Smithfield and half-expect a Dickens character to round the corner. Medieval London, still legible if you slow down.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/P10250LtlBrtn.JPG/960px-P10250LtlBrtn.JPG"},{id:"waterlanerich",n:"Water Lane",a:"Richmond",pc:"TW9",lat:51.4612,lng:-0.3068,c:"alley",s:"cobbles down to the Thames",q:"Water Lane Richmond riverside",w:"A short cobbled descent from Richmond’s bustle down to the river, lined with the backs of old buildings. At the bottom the Thames opens out — boats, willows, evening light. An atmospheric little throat of a lane, the kind you take slowly with nowhere in particular to be.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"suttonhouse",n:"Sutton House",a:"Hackney",pc:"E9",lat:51.5483,lng:-0.0503,c:"history",s:"Tudor survivor · a punk-squat past",q:"Sutton House Hackney National Trust",w:"A 1535 Tudor manor of warm red brick, marooned among modern Hackney — manor, then school, then in the 1980s a squatted punk den whose graffiti the National Trust chose to keep. Wander the panelled rooms and quiet courtyard and the centuries fold together: oak linenfold, Jacobean painted shields, and a rare survivor’s stubborn calm. A time machine off Homerton High Street.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Sutton_House_in_Hackney_Exterior.jpg/960px-Sutton_House_in_Hackney_Exterior.jpg"},{id:"carlyleshouse",n:"Carlyle’s House",a:"Chelsea",pc:"SW3",lat:51.4843,lng:-0.17,c:"literary",s:"a Victorian writer’s home · walled garden",q:"Carlyle’s House Cheyne Row Chelsea",w:"On a quiet Chelsea row built in 1708, the home of Thomas and Jane Carlyle survives almost unchanged — dim hallways, the decoupage screen Jane made in 1849, the small walled garden where the great Victorian wrote and fretted. Dickens, Tennyson and Ruskin all climbed these stairs. A modest house, thick with the texture of a literary life.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"alburystreet",n:"Albury Street",a:"Deptford",pc:"SE8",lat:51.4806,lng:-0.0244,c:"history",s:"early-Georgian doorcases · 1706",q:"Albury Street Deptford Georgian",w:"A cobbled Deptford side street, laid out in 1706 for dockyard officers and shipwrights, that has somehow kept its early-Georgian terrace intact. Look up at the doorways — carved wooden canopies, each bracket a different design, cut by ship’s carpenters. Look down at the cobbles. At the far end, the spire of St Paul’s. London as it once imagined itself: orderly, durable, slow.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Northern_Side_of_Albury_Street%2C_Deptford_%2803%29.jpg/960px-Northern_Side_of_Albury_Street%2C_Deptford_%2803%29.jpg"},{id:"ronniescotts",n:"Ronnie Scott’s",a:"Soho",pc:"W1D",lat:51.5137,lng:-0.1314,c:"music",s:"Soho jazz since 1959",q:"Ronnie Scott’s Jazz Club Frith Street",w:"Down a Frith Street doorway glows the red sign of Britain’s most storied jazz club, open since 1959. Miles, Monk and Hendrix all played here — the last gig Hendrix ever performed was on this stage. Velvet dark, low-lit and reverent: the kind of room where you order a drink, stop talking, and let the music have the floor.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Ronnie_Scott%27s-5469148_e540293d.jpg"},{id:"hackneyempire",n:"Hackney Empire",a:"Hackney",pc:"E8",lat:51.5453,lng:-0.0556,c:"music",s:"Edwardian music hall · 1901",q:"Hackney Empire theatre Mare Street",w:"Frank Matcham’s 1901 music hall on Mare Street, all gilt and terracotta swagger, where Marie Lloyd and Charlie Chaplin once worked the variety bill and stand-ups still do. The ornate auditorium still crackles with the ghosts of a thousand turns — a temple to performance that somehow dodged the wrecking ball. Magnificent, lit up at night.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Frank_Matcham_Hackney_Empiire_291_Mare_Street_291_London_E8_1EJ_3.jpg/960px-Frank_Matcham_Hackney_Empiire_291_Mare_Street_291_London_E8_1EJ_3.jpg"},{id:"ridleyroad",n:"Ridley Road Market",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.5476,lng:-0.0743,c:"food",s:"Dalston’s Afro-Caribbean market",q:"Ridley Road Market Dalston",w:"A century-old Dalston street market where the city’s appetites collide — yams and plantain, Turkish bread, Caribbean fish counters, bolts of fabric, gospel and the shouts of traders. Loud, cheap, gloriously alive and stubbornly itself amid the creeping cafes. Come hungry, wander slowly, and eat where the queue is longest.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 09:00-15:00; PH off",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Ridley_road_market_dalston_1.jpg"},{id:"ealingpolish",n:"Our Lady Mother of the Church Polish Church",a:"Ealing",pc:"W5",lat:51.5123,lng:-0.3018,c:"diaspora",s:"émigré canon",q:"Polish Church Ealing Our Lady Mother of the Church",w:"Former Ealing Broadway Methodist church (1868 Gothic Revival with tall spire) acquired by the Polish community in 1986. Part of the quiet westward drift of Polonia from Acton through Ealing Common. Now home to Polish Roman Catholic parish life — services, memory, community in a building that outlived its original congregation. A pilgrimage site for West London Poles.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rubens",n:"Rubens at the Palace (Polish Government-in-Exile HQ)",a:"Victoria",pc:"SW1",lat:51.4982,lng:-0.1421,c:"diaspora",s:"government in exile",q:"Rubens Hotel Buckingham Palace Road Polish exile",w:"During the war this hotel at 39 Buckingham Palace Road housed the Polish Government-in-Exile’s military headquarters. From here Sikorski and others coordinated the fight, intelligence and diplomacy while a state that refused to die operated from London. Plaques and memory remain — the paper trail of exile in plain sight near the palace gardens.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stclementdanespolish",n:"Polish Air Force Memorial, St Clement Danes",a:"Strand",pc:"WC2",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.117,c:"diaspora",s:"RAF Polish airmen",q:"Polish Air Force Memorial St Clement Danes London",w:"St Clement Danes, rebuilt after the Blitz as the Central Church of the Royal Air Force. The floor is inlaid with over a thousand hand-carved Welsh slate badges of RAF squadrons and units. Specific memorials honour the Polish Air Force — the men who flew with the RAF from British soil during the war, many never to return home. Ties directly into the wider story of Polish exile, contribution and memory.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thealbanydeptford",n:"The Albany",a:"Deptford",pc:"SE8",lat:51.478,lng:-0.026,c:"music",s:"radical community venue",q:"The Albany Deptford London",w:"A radical, community-run arts centre and theatre in Deptford with deep roots in 1970s–80s alternative culture, anti-racism work and local organising. Still going strong with a programme that mixes experimental performance, music and community projects. A living piece of South London’s grassroots cultural history in a former church hall.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Albany_Theatre_in_Deptford.jpg/960px-Albany_Theatre_in_Deptford.jpg"},{id:"hopeandanchor",n:"Hope & Anchor",a:"Islington",pc:"N1",lat:51.538,lng:-0.103,c:"music",s:"punk & new wave pub",q:"Hope & Anchor Islington London",w:"Legendary Islington pub that became one of the key venues of the late-1970s punk and new wave explosion. The Stranglers, The Damned, Joy Division and countless others played the tiny back room. Still a proper music pub with history you can feel in the walls. One of the last places where that raw, pre-corporate music scene feels tangible.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ealingstudios",n:"Ealing Studios",a:"Ealing",pc:"W5",lat:51.51,lng:-0.308,c:"film",s:"British comedy home",q:"Ealing Studios London",w:"The oldest continuously working film studio in the world and the spiritual home of the great Ealing Comedies (Kind Hearts and Coronets, The Lavender Hill Mob, Passport to Pimlico). The lot, stages and offices where Alec Guinness, Stanley Holloway and so many others created a very particular, very British kind of cinema. Still active, still full of ghosts.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tradescanttomb",n:"Tradescant Tomb",a:"Lambeth",pc:"SE1",lat:51.495,lng:-0.12,c:"oddity",s:"plant hunters’ grave",q:"Tradescant Tomb Lambeth London",w:"The extraordinary 17th-century tomb of John Tradescant the Elder and Younger — royal gardeners, plant hunters and collectors of curiosities — in the churchyard of St Mary-at-Lambeth. Carved with exotic plants, animals and the family crest. The men who brought the pineapple, the horse chestnut and countless other species to Britain. A strange, beautiful monument to curiosity and empire.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"theflask",n:"The Flask",a:"Highgate",pc:"N6",lat:51.571,lng:-0.148,c:"pub",s:"village pub with ghosts",q:"The Flask Highgate London",w:"A proper old Highgate village pub with 17th-century origins and a long list of famous (and infamous) drinkers — from highwaymen to writers and actors. Low ceilings, wood panelling, and the feeling that the village that once stood here is still just outside the door. One of the last places where old Highgate survives.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rosetheatre",n:"The Rose Theatre remains",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.507,lng:-0.093,c:"history",s:"Shakespeare’s rival",q:"Rose Theatre Bankside London",w:"The excavated remains of the Rose — the first purpose-built Elizabethan playhouse on Bankside and the main rival to the later Globe. Visible through a glass floor in the basement of a modern office building. The stage where Marlowe’s plays were first performed and where the very first Shakespeare productions happened. A genuine archaeological ghost of Elizabethan theatre.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rinkoffbakery",n:"Rinkoff Bakery",a:"Stepney",pc:"E1",lat:51.515,lng:-0.052,c:"bakery",s:"family bakery since 1911",q:"Rinkoff Bakery Commercial Road London",w:"Family bakery started in 1911 by Ukrainian refugee Hyman Rinkoff and his wife in Stepney. Passed through generations, still baking breads, cakes, pastries and legendary jam doughnuts from Eastern European recipes. A living piece of East End immigrant food history that has fed the community for over a century.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"regencycafe",n:"The Regency Cafe",a:"Pimlico",pc:"SW1",lat:51.492,lng:-0.138,c:"caff",s:"art deco working man’s caff",q:"Regency Cafe Pimlico London",w:"Classic art deco working man’s café with Formica tables, chrome and proper strong tea. Big full English breakfasts and a no-nonsense atmosphere. Has appeared in films but remains a genuine local caff. One of the best-preserved mid-century working-class eating places in London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"fcookepieandmash",n:"F. Cooke Pie & Mash",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.546,lng:-0.075,c:"pieandmash",s:"traditional East London pie shop",q:"F Cooke pie and mash Dalston London",w:"A proper traditional pie and mash shop in Dalston that has been feeding generations of East Londoners. Stewed eels, liquor, mash and the classic tiled interior. A living working-class food institution that feels increasingly rare. Strong tea and no fuss — exactly as it should be.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rainbowbakery",n:"Rainbow Bakery",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8",lat:51.546,lng:-0.075,c:"bakery",s:"UK’s only St Lucian bakery",q:"Rainbow Bakery Dalston London",w:"The UK’s only St Lucian bakery in Dalston — family-run and carrying Caribbean recipes and traditions into East London. Fresh bakes, community warmth and the feeling of a neighbourhood institution that belongs to the people who live here. A beautiful, specific piece of London’s immigrant food story.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"raabsbakers",n:"Raabs The Bakers",a:"Islington",pc:"N1",lat:51.536,lng:-0.103,c:"bakery",s:"est. 1948 old school bakery",q:"Raabs The Bakers Essex Road Islington",w:"Proper old-school London bakery on Essex Road since 1948. Fresh bread, crusty rolls, sweet and savoury bakes made the traditional way. The kind of place where quality has never been compromised and locals still queue for the real thing. A living piece of mid-century Islington food culture.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ivyscafe",n:"Ivy’s Cafe",a:"Poplar",pc:"E14",lat:51.511,lng:-0.015,c:"caff",s:"Chrisp Street caff, medieval roots",q:"Ivy's Cafe Chrisp Street Poplar London",w:"A proper East End market café on Chrisp Street that still serves pease pudding — a dish with medieval roots once common in working-class London eateries. Family-run, no fuss, and one of the last places keeping this specific piece of London food heritage alive.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hoopandgrapes",n:"Hoop and Grapes",a:"Aldgate",pc:"EC3",lat:51.514,lng:-0.077,c:"pub",s:"survived the Great Fire",q:"Hoop and Grapes Aldgate London",w:"One of the very few buildings in the City that survived the Great Fire of 1666. A timber-framed pub with centuries of history. The interior still carries the feeling of pre-Fire London. A quiet survivor in the shadow of the modern financial district.",city:"london",oh:"Su-Fr 12:00-23:30; Sa 11:00-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"thelamb",n:"The Lamb",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1",lat:51.522,lng:-0.124,c:"pub",s:"literary Bloomsbury pub",q:"The Lamb Bloomsbury London",w:"A beautiful tiled Victorian pub in the heart of Bloomsbury with a long association with writers, academics and the Bloomsbury Group. Etched glass snob screens and a peaceful back room. Still a proper local with deep cultural roots.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"oldbelltavern",n:"The Old Bell Tavern",a:"Fleet Street",pc:"EC4",lat:51.514,lng:-0.106,c:"pub",s:"Wren’s builders’ pub",q:"Old Bell Tavern Fleet Street London",w:"Built for the workmen who constructed St Bride’s Church after the Great Fire. A proper old Fleet Street boozer with a long association with journalists and printers. Quiet, slightly hidden and full of the layered history of the street.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Old_Bell%2C_Fleet_Street%2C_EC4_%282493490610%29.jpg"},{id:"thealbioncafe",n:"The Albion Cafe",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E1",lat:51.525,lng:-0.078,c:"caff",s:"neighbourhood survivor",q:"Albion Cafe Shoreditch London",w:"A proper Shoreditch caff that has held its corner through every wave of reinvention around it — strong tea, an unfussy plate and the easy regulars'-table feel that keeps a changing street human.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"workmanscafe",n:"Workman's Cafe",a:"Archway",pc:"N19",lat:51.565,lng:-0.135,c:"caff",s:"old-school greasy spoon",q:"Workman's Cafe Archway London",w:"A genuine Archway working man's caff — pork sausage and bacon, bubble and squeak, mugs of strong tea and the unhurried local conversation that has kept places like this going while the chains came and went.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"electriccafe",n:"The Electric Cafe",a:"West Norwood",pc:"SE27",lat:51.432,lng:-0.098,c:"caff",s:"beloved greasy spoon",q:"Electric Cafe West Norwood London",w:"A much-loved old greasy spoon between Tulse Hill and West Norwood — big breakfasts, proper tea and the unhurried calm of a real local that has quietly fed the area for years.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mappscafe",n:"Mapps Cafe",a:"Hackney Wick",pc:"E9",lat:51.542,lng:-0.025,c:"caff",s:"Hackney Wick anchor",q:"Mapps Cafe Hackney Wick London",w:"A friendly Hackney Wick local doing lentil soup, sandwiches and reliable home-style cooking — one of those small, steady anchors that holds a fast-changing neighbourhood together.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"yeastbakery",n:"Yeast Bakery",a:"Hackney",pc:"E8",lat:51.546,lng:-0.055,c:"bakery",s:"family-run bakery",q:"Yeast Bakery Hackney London",w:"A family-run Hackney bakery turning out some of the best croissants and pastries in the area — daily, early and made with real care. The kind of unflashy local a street organises its mornings around.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"electricavenue",n:"Electric Avenue",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW9",lat:51.4628,lng:-0.1148,c:"history",s:"first electric street",q:"Electric Avenue Brixton",w:"Built in 1888, this was the first market street in London lit by electricity — cast-iron canopies and incandescent bulbs that let traders work after dark while the rest of the city still squinted by gaslight. It gave Eddy Grant his 1983 anthem, written in the wake of the 1981 Brixton uprising, and still carries the African and Caribbean produce stalls at the heart of Brixton Market.",city:"london"},{id:"windmillpub",n:"The Windmill, Brixton",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW2",lat:51.454,lng:-0.1246,c:"music",s:"post-punk crucible",q:"The Windmill Brixton music venue",w:"A scruffy 1970s estate pub beside Windmill Gardens that became one of the most important small venues in the country. Through the late 2010s it incubated the South London guitar scene — Black Country, New Road, Squid, Fat White Family and black midi all cut their teeth on its tiny stage. Not to be confused with the heritage windmill a hundred metres away; this Windmill grinds something louder.",city:"london",oh:"24/7",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Windmill%2C_Brixton_Hill%2C_SW2_%283151353450%29.jpg/960px-Windmill%2C_Brixton_Hill%2C_SW2_%283151353450%29.jpg"},{id:"stnicholasdeptford",n:"St Nicholas Church, Deptford",a:"Deptford",pc:"SE8",lat:51.4824,lng:-0.0263,c:"literary",s:"Shakespeare’s rival",q:"St Nicholas Church Deptford Marlowe",w:"Christopher Marlowe — Shakespeare’s exact contemporary, rival, and probable government spy — was buried in an unmarked grave here on 1 June 1593, days after being stabbed through the eye in a Deptford lodging house, in a killing that still smells of the Walsingham intelligence network. The medieval tower survives; the rest was rebuilt in 1697. The carved skull-and-crossbones on the churchyard gateposts are said to have given the world the pirate’s Jolly Roger.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sayescourt",n:"Sayes Court Park (John Evelyn’s garden)",a:"Deptford",pc:"SE8",lat:51.4869,lng:-0.0283,c:"green",s:"tsar in the hedge",q:"Sayes Court Deptford John Evelyn",w:"The site of the most celebrated private garden in 17th-century England, laid out by the diarist John Evelyn from 1652 — French parterres and a legendary holly hedge. In 1698 Evelyn lent the house to Tsar Peter the Great, then in Deptford learning shipbuilding; the Tsar and his men wrecked the place, reportedly by barrowing one another through the hedge, and the Treasury paid out over £350 in damages. A quiet park and an ancient mulberry mark the spot — and it was very nearly the National Trust’s first-ever acquisition.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bonningtonsquare",n:"Bonnington Square Pleasure Garden",a:"Vauxhall",pc:"SW8",lat:51.4843,lng:-0.1233,c:"green",s:"squatters’ Eden",q:"Bonnington Square garden Vauxhall",w:"A lush pocket garden created by residents in 1990 on a WWII bomb site, named for the grand Vauxhall Pleasure Gardens that once lay just to the north. The 1870s railway-workers’ square was saved from demolition by 1980s squatters, who turned it into a housing co-op with a vegetarian café and a wholefood shop; designer Dan Pearson planted the garden, threading English borders through tropical exotics around a salvaged industrial waterwheel.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"littleportugal",n:"Little Portugal, South Lambeth Road",a:"Stockwell",pc:"SW8",lat:51.4783,lng:-0.1228,c:"diaspora",s:"pastéis and saudade",q:"Little Portugal South Lambeth Road Stockwell",w:"The stretch of South Lambeth Road between Vauxhall and Stockwell is the heart of the UK’s largest Portuguese-speaking community — over nine thousand in Lambeth alone — many with roots in Madeira and arriving from the 1960s on. Red-and-green pastelarias like Lisboa and Estrela serve pastéis de nata, salt cod and bica strong enough to walk you home. London’s quietest, most delicious bit of saudade.",city:"london"},{id:"vauxhallcityfarm",n:"Vauxhall City Farm",a:"Vauxhall",pc:"SE11",lat:51.4866,lng:-0.1199,c:"oddity",s:"alpacas by MI6",q:"Vauxhall City Farm",w:"Founded in 1977 on demolition-scarred land, this is one of London’s oldest and most central city farms — pigs, sheep, goats and alpacas grazing barely a stone’s throw from the MI6 building on the river. On a sliver of inner-city acreage it runs youth and education programmes and a Riding for the Disabled centre. Espionage on one bank, alpacas on the other.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Vauxhall_City_Farm_Entrance.jpg/960px-Vauxhall_City_Farm_Entrance.jpg"},{id:"southlondongallery",n:"South London Gallery",a:"Camberwell",pc:"SE5",lat:51.4743,lng:-0.0806,c:"museum",s:"art for the people",q:"South London Gallery Camberwell",w:"A free contemporary-art gallery in a purpose-built hall that opened in 1891, founded by William Rossiter to bring art to the people of South London — with Frederic Leighton, Burne-Jones and Walter Crane among its early backers and Gladstone as first president. In 2018 it spread across the road into a Grade II-listed former fire station. Ambitious, unstuffy and still free, more than a century on.",city:"london",oh:"Tu,Th-Su 11:00-18:00; We 11:00-21:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Passmore_Edwards_South_London_Art_Gallery%2C_Peckham_Road_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1441923.jpg"},{id:"limehousebasin",n:"Limehouse Basin",a:"Limehouse",pc:"E14",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.0395,c:"canals",s:"where canal meets river",q:"Limehouse Basin London",w:"The old Regent’s Canal Dock, opened in 1820 as the link between London’s canal network and the Thames — once a coal-and-timber port thick with barges and lighters, now a glassy yacht marina ringed by Docklands towers. From here the Limehouse Cut (1770), London’s oldest canal, strikes north to the Lea. Stand on the lock and you’re at the hinge between the inland canals and the tidal river.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"threemills",n:"Three Mills (House Mill)",a:"Bromley-by-Bow",pc:"E3",lat:51.529,lng:-0.0103,c:"canals",s:"the great tidal mill",q:"Three Mills House Mill Bromley-by-Bow",w:"On an island in the River Lea stands the House Mill of 1776 — one of the largest tidal mills left in the world, Grade I listed. It worked the rise and fall of the Lea to grind grain for an adjacent gin distillery, the tide doing for free what steam later charged for. A startlingly rural survivor in the shadow of the Olympic Park.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"trinitygreen",n:"Trinity Green Almshouses",a:"Mile End",pc:"E1",lat:51.5223,lng:-0.0524,c:"almshouses",s:"mariners’ refuge",q:"Trinity Green Almshouses Mile End",w:"Built in 1695 by Trinity House for ‘28 decay’d Masters & Commanders of Ships or ye Widows of such’, on land given by a Ratcliffe sea-captain. Two rows of cottages face a quiet green, model warships perched on the gable ends; C.R. Ashbee saved them from demolition in 1895. A pocket of the maritime 17th century stranded on the Mile End Road.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mordencollege",n:"Morden College",a:"Blackheath",pc:"SE3",lat:51.4695,lng:0.0195,c:"almshouses",s:"the ruined merchants’ haven",q:"Morden College Blackheath",w:"Founded around 1695 by the Levant Company merchant Sir John Morden for fellow traders ruined ‘by perils of the seas’ — a serene Baroque red-brick quadrangle long credited to Wren, though actually raised by his master mason Edward Strong. Still a working retirement community on the edge of Blackheath. Charity built on the risk of the sea, three centuries before bankruptcy law.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Morden_College.jpg/960px-Morden_College.jpg"},{id:"hoptonsalmshouses",n:"Hopton’s Almshouses",a:"Bankside",pc:"SE1",lat:51.5067,lng:-0.1015,c:"almshouses",s:"Georgian calm behind Tate Modern",q:"Hopton's Almshouses Bankside",w:"A trim Georgian courtyard of 1746–52, built under the will of the fishmonger Charles Hopton to house ‘poor decayed men’ of the parish — and now hidden directly behind the roar of Tate Modern. Twenty-six little brick houses around a lawn, almost absurdly peaceful given the Turbine Hall is a minute’s walk away.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Hopton%27s_Almshouses_and_Neo_Bankside.jpg/960px-Hopton%27s_Almshouses_and_Neo_Bankside.jpg"},{id:"museumofthehome",n:"Museum of the Home (Geffrye Almshouses)",a:"Hoxton",pc:"E2",lat:51.5331,lng:-0.0769,c:"almshouses",s:"ironmongers’ charity",q:"Museum of the Home Geffrye Hoxton",w:"Fourteen almshouses of 1714, built by the Ironmongers’ Company from the bequest of Sir Robert Geffrye — a merchant whose fortune was partly bound up in the transatlantic slave trade, a fact the museum now confronts head-on. Saved by the London County Council in 1911 and opened as a museum in 1914, it walks you through four centuries of the English living room, with a restored almshouse interior on select days.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Cmglee_London_Geffrye_Museum_garden.jpg/960px-Cmglee_London_Geffrye_Museum_garden.jpg"},{id:"trinityhospitalgreenwich",n:"Trinity Hospital, Greenwich",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10",lat:51.4853,lng:-0.002,c:"almshouses",s:"riverside Gothic almshouse",q:"Trinity Hospital Greenwich",w:"Founded in 1613 by Henry Howard, Earl of Northampton, for twenty poor men, and dressed up in toy-Gothic in 1812 — a tiny crenellated almshouse dwarfed by the old power station beside it on the Thames Path. It sits almost exactly on the Greenwich Meridian, run to this day by the Mercers’ Company. Easy to miss, impossible to forget.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/360SFEC_LONDON-20070917.JPG/960px-360SFEC_LONDON-20070917.JPG"},{id:"crystalpalacesubway",n:"Crystal Palace Subway",a:"Crystal Palace",pc:"SE19",lat:51.4178,lng:-0.0723,c:"follies",s:"the cathedral underground",q:"Crystal Palace Subway",w:"A breathtaking vaulted brick subway of 1865 by Charles Barry Jr., built to carry first-class passengers beneath the road to the High Level station serving the relocated Crystal Palace — red and cream brick fanning overhead like a Byzantine crypt. The palace burned in 1936; this jewel of a passage survived underground, opened now only on occasional days.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Crystal_Palace_railway_station_from_above.jpg/960px-Crystal_Palace_railway_station_from_above.jpg"},{id:"cabmensshelter",n:"Cabmen’s Shelter, Russell Square",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1B",lat:51.523,lng:-0.1255,c:"follies",s:"the little green hut",q:"Cabmen's Shelter Russell Square",w:"One of just thirteen surviving green-painted Victorian cabmen’s shelters — of sixty-one built between 1875 and 1950 — each by law no larger than a horse and cart so as not to block the road. Inside, a tiny kitchen still serves tea and bacon to black-cab drivers, a members’ club the size of a garden shed. All thirteen are now listed.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Russell_Square_station.jpg/960px-Russell_Square_station.jpg"},{id:"londonreviewbookshop",n:"London Review Bookshop",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1A",lat:51.5183,lng:-0.1245,c:"bookshops",s:"the critics’ bookshop",q:"London Review Bookshop Bloomsbury",w:"The bookshop of the ‘London Review of Books’, opened in 2003 a step from the British Museum — densely, intelligently stocked, with an adjoining cake shop that regulars rate almost as highly as the shelves. The natural habitat of the Bloomsbury reader, and one of the best events programmes in the city.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"housmans",n:"Housmans",a:"King’s Cross",pc:"N1",lat:51.5316,lng:-0.1206,c:"bookshops",s:"the radical bookshop",q:"Housmans Bookshop Kings Cross",w:"A radical and peace-movement bookshop founded in 1945 and named for the pacifist poet Laurence Housman, on the Caledonian Road near King’s Cross. Shelves of dissent, small-press politics and second-hand bargains in the basement; it has outlasted nearly every other bookshop of its kind in Britain.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Housmans_Bookshop_5_Caledonian_Road_Kings_Cross_London_N1_9DY.jpg/960px-Housmans_Bookshop_5_Caledonian_Road_Kings_Cross_London_N1_9DY.jpg"},{id:"watermenshall",n:"Watermen’s Hall",a:"City of London",pc:"EC3R",lat:51.5095,lng:-0.0833,c:"livery",s:"the only Georgian hall",q:"Watermen's Hall City of London",w:"The only original Georgian livery hall left in the City, built 1778–80 by William Blackburn for the Company of Watermen and Lightermen — the Thames boatmen who were London’s original cabbies. Uniquely it is a guild ‘without livery’, created and governed by Act of Parliament in 1555. A jewel-box of a hall down a lane near the river; interior by tour only.",city:"london"},{id:"apothecarieshall",n:"Apothecaries’ Hall",a:"Blackfriars",pc:"EC4V",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.1037,c:"livery",s:"the physic-garden founders",q:"Apothecaries' Hall Blackfriars",w:"The oldest surviving livery hall in the City, rebuilt by 1672 after the Great Fire on the site of a Dominican priory. The Society of Apothecaries founded the Chelsea Physic Garden in 1673 and made and sold drugs here into the 20th century; a young John Keats qualified to practise medicine within these walls in 1816. Glimpse the courtyard from Black Friars Lane.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Apothecaries_Hall_entrance%2C_Black_Friars_Lane_EC4_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1271897.jpg"},{id:"goldsmithshall",n:"Goldsmiths’ Hall",a:"City of London",pc:"EC2V",lat:51.5151,lng:-0.0966,c:"livery",s:"where ‘hallmark’ was born",q:"Goldsmiths' Hall City of London",w:"The opulent 1835 hall of one of the City’s ‘Great Twelve’ companies, by Philip Hardwick — and the literal origin of the word ‘hallmark’, for here silver and gold have been assayed and marked since the 14th century. The annual Trial of the Pyx still tests the nation’s coinage under its roof.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/EH1286469_Goldsmiths_Hall_02.jpg/960px-EH1286469_Goldsmiths_Hall_02.jpg"},{id:"mansionhouse",n:"Mansion House",a:"City of London",pc:"EC4N",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.0894,c:"livery",s:"the Lord Mayor’s palace",q:"Mansion House City of London",w:"The Palladian official residence of the Lord Mayor of London, built 1739–52 by George Dance the Elder — home to the barrel-vaulted Egyptian Hall and a world-class hoard of Dutch Golden Age paintings. The grandest civic house in the Square Mile, facing the Bank and the old Royal Exchange across the busiest junction in the City.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mansion_House%2C_City_of_London_%2840478193673%29.jpg/960px-Mansion_House%2C_City_of_London_%2840478193673%29.jpg"},{id:"florincourt",n:"Florin Court",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1M",lat:51.5205,lng:-0.0975,c:"artdeco",s:"Poirot’s Art Deco home",q:"Florin Court Charterhouse Square",w:"A curved Streamline-Moderne block of 1936 on Charterhouse Square, with a roof garden and basement pool — and, to a generation of television viewers, Hercule Poirot’s ‘Whitehaven Mansions’. Its sleek sun-trap balconies wrap the corner like the prow of a liner run aground in Clerkenwell.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Florin_Court_%282%29.jpg/960px-Florin_Court_%282%29.jpg"},{id:"daimlerhiregarage",n:"Daimler Hire Garage",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1N",lat:51.5246,lng:-0.1255,c:"artdeco",s:"the spiral motor garage",q:"Daimler Hire Garage Herbrand Street",w:"A rare 1931 Art Deco multi-storey car garage on Herbrand Street, by Wallis, Gilbert & Partners — the same hand as the Hoover Building — its great curved corner of Crittall glazing wrapped around a sculptural internal spiral ramp. Built for the age of the chauffeured Daimler, now offices, still unmistakably itself.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/ExDaimlerHireGarageBloomsbury.jpg/960px-ExDaimlerHireGarageBloomsbury.jpg"},{id:"hooverbuilding",n:"Hoover Building",a:"Perivale",pc:"UB6",lat:51.5258,lng:-0.3217,c:"artdeco",s:"the drive-by palace",q:"Hoover Building Perivale",w:"Wallis, Gilbert & Partners’ dazzling ‘Modern Palace of Industry’ of 1933 on the A40 — sunburst tiles, Egyptian and Aztec colour, a façade built to be admired at speed from a passing car. Betjeman called it ‘a sort of Art Deco Wentworth Woodhouse’; it’s flats now, with a supermarket behind, but the front still stops the traffic.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"senatehouse",n:"Senate House",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"WC1E",lat:51.5216,lng:-0.13,c:"artdeco",s:"Orwell’s Ministry of Truth",q:"Senate House University of London",w:"Charles Holden’s monumental stone tower of 1937 for the University of London — stripped-classical, faintly sinister, and the acknowledged model for the Ministry of Truth in Orwell’s ‘Nineteen Eighty-Four’ (the wartime Ministry of Information really was based here). London’s first true skyscraper in spirit if not in height.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Senate_House%2C_University_of_London.jpg/960px-Senate_House%2C_University_of_London.jpg"},{id:"troxy",n:"Troxy",a:"Stepney",pc:"E1",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.0432,c:"artdeco",s:"the East End super-cinema",q:"Troxy Commercial Road Stepney",w:"A vast Art Deco super-cinema of 1933 by George Coles on the Commercial Road — 3,500 seats, a Wurlitzer and a ceiling like a sunset. Later a Mecca bingo hall and, improbably, the Royal Opera House’s rehearsal studio; now a restored events palace, the grandest surviving picture-house of the East End.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Troxy_80_2013_%281_of_19%29.jpg/960px-Troxy_80_2013_%281_of_19%29.jpg"},{id:"chislehurstcaves",n:"Chislehurst Caves",a:"Chislehurst",pc:"BR7 6LB",lat:51.4115,lng:0.0555,c:"oddity",s:"Bowie, Hendrix, Pink Floyd underground",q:"Chislehurst Caves",w:"Twenty-two miles of man-made chalk and flint tunnels beneath Kent's edge — a WWI munitions store, a 1920s mushroom farm, then London's largest deep air-raid shelter, sheltering 15,000 with a chapel and a hospital during the Blitz. The only child born down here was christened Rose Cavena Wakeman. In the 50s and 60s it turned music venue: Bowie played four times, plus Hendrix, Pink Floyd and the Yardbirds. Guided tours only.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"whitgiftalmshouses",n:"Whitgift Almshouses",a:"Croydon",pc:"CR0 1NX",lat:51.3735,lng:-0.0985,c:"almshouses",s:"Tudor bricks in the shopping street",q:"Whitgift Almshouses Croydon North End",w:"Built in 1596 by Archbishop John Whitgift as a hospital for the elderly, and still a working almshouse for around a dozen residents — a knot of Tudor brick hiding in plain sight amid the glass shopfronts of Croydon's commercial heart. The courtyard opens to the public during Open House; the rest of the year you simply walk past four centuries without noticing.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"honeywoodmuseum",n:"Honeywood Museum",a:"Carshalton",pc:"SM5 3NX",lat:51.366,lng:-0.1665,c:"museum",s:"the house over the springs",q:"Honeywood Museum Carshalton Ponds",w:"A 17th-century chalk-and-flint chequer house beside Carshalton Ponds, possibly built as a commercial cold-bath house over the spring outflows. Now Sutton's local-history museum, with an Edwardian billiard room and a room devoted to vanished Nonsuch Palace. The water still rises where it always did, under the floor.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"littlehollandhouse",n:"Little Holland House",a:"Carshalton Beeches",pc:"SM5 3LW",lat:51.3565,lng:-0.1655,c:"history",s:"the ultimate DIY house",q:"Little Holland House Frank Dickinson Carshalton",w:"Self-built between 1902 and 1904 by Frank Dickinson, a working-class disciple of Ruskin and Morris, who made the house and almost everything in it by hand — the furniture, the paintings, the metalwork, the carvings. An entire Arts and Crafts philosophy executed by one man on a suburban plot. Grade II; open first Sundays.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bowiebandstand",n:"The Bowie Bandstand",a:"Beckenham",pc:"BR3",lat:51.4135,lng:-0.0285,c:"music",s:"Memory of a Free Festival",q:"Bowie Bandstand Croydon Road Recreation Ground Beckenham",w:"A Grade II-listed cast-iron bandstand of 1905, believed the only surviving example by McCallum & Hope of Glasgow. On 16 August 1969 David Bowie compered and played the Beckenham Arts Lab free festival here, five days after burying his father; it became 'Memory of a Free Festival'. The Arts Lab had started in the back room of the Three Tuns up the High Street — now a Zizzi.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cahchi",n:"Cah Chi",a:"New Malden",pc:"KT3",lat:51.4055,lng:-0.254,c:"food",s:"Europe's Little Korea",q:"Cah Chi New Malden Korean barbecue",w:"New Malden holds an estimated 10,000 ethnic Koreans — and, per CNN, some hundreds of North Korean defectors, the largest such community in Europe, seeded in the 1970s when the South Korean ambassador and Samsung's HQ settled nearby. Cah Chi is the long-running family barbecue institution at its heart: charcoal-grilled meat, soju, and the kind of cooking the diaspora actually eats.",city:"london"},{id:"koreanartscentre",n:"Korean Culture & Arts Centre",a:"New Malden",pc:"KT3",lat:51.406,lng:-0.2545,c:"diaspora",s:"the community hub behind the café",q:"Korean Culture and Arts Centre New Malden High Street",w:"A modest community hub and function room behind a white-walled café on the High Street — the cultural engine room of New Malden's Korean diaspora, where the events, classes and gatherings happen that the restaurants only hint at. The unglamorous heart of Europe's Little Korea.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stpeterspetersham",n:"St Peter's Church, Petersham",a:"Petersham",pc:"TW10 7AB",lat:51.4445,lng:-0.3015,c:"history",s:"the explorer's pre-Victorian box pews",q:"St Peter's Church Petersham Vancouver grave",w:"A church of Saxon origin, in Domesday, that Pevsner loved for an interior the Victorians never touched: Georgian box pews and a two-decker pulpit, frozen before the great refit. Captain George Vancouver — who charted the Pacific Northwest and gave his name to the city — lies in the churchyard, a long way from the coast he mapped.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Former_church_of_All_Saints%2C_Bute_Avenue%2C_Petersham_%28geograph_1787040%29.jpg"},{id:"hamhouse",n:"Ham House",a:"Ham",pc:"TW10 7RS",lat:51.4465,lng:-0.3115,c:"history",s:"the dog that wasn't there",q:"Ham House ghost spaniel Duchess of Lauderdale",w:"A 1610 Stuart mansion famous for its cantilevered Great Staircase and the Duchess of Lauderdale's private 'bathing roome'. Her ghostly King Charles spaniel is still said to pad the halls — a visitor once complained about a dog she'd seen indoors, and was told no real dog is ever allowed in. One of the least-altered seventeenth-century houses in the country.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"southallgurdwara",n:"Gurdwara Sri Guru Singh Sabha",a:"Southall",pc:"UB2 5AA",lat:51.5055,lng:-0.379,c:"faith",s:"the largest gurdwara outside India",q:"Gurdwara Sri Guru Singh Sabha Southall",w:"London's largest Sikh temple, opened in March 2003 at a cost of £17.5m raised entirely from the local Sikh community, and visited by the then-Prince Charles that June. Marble prayer halls, gold and white, and free langar served all day to anyone who walks in, regardless of faith. The civic heart of Southall.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ambalasweet",n:"Ambala Sweet Centre",a:"Southall",pc:"UB1 1LN",lat:51.5095,lng:-0.3775,c:"food",s:"Britain's first mithai counter",q:"Ambala Sweet Centre Southall Broadway",w:"Founded in 1965 by Mohammed Ali Khan and named for his Haryana birthplace — the first shop opened in Drummond Street, among the very first South Asian confectioners in Britain. Trays of barfi, jalebi and laddoo glow under the lights. Khan died in 2023; in 2025 the chain was bought out, 22 stores strong, but the original counters still pull the Diwali queues.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"watersteammuseum",n:"London Museum of Water & Steam",a:"Brentford",pc:"TW8 0EN",lat:51.488,lng:-0.289,c:"museum",s:"the monster beam engine",q:"London Museum of Water and Steam Kew Bridge",w:"The former Kew Bridge Pumping Station, home to the world's largest collection of Cornish beam engines. The Grand Junction 90-inch was once the largest working beam engine on earth — Dickens called it 'a monster' — and the 100-inch is the biggest single-cylinder beam engine left anywhere. On steaming days the great beams still rock overhead, slow as breathing.",city:"london",oh:"Th-Su 10:00-16:00; Jul 22-Aug 28 We 10:00-16:00; SH 10:00-16:00"},{id:"musicalmuseum",n:"The Musical Museum",a:"Brentford",pc:"TW8 0DU",lat:51.4875,lng:-0.29,c:"museum",s:"the self-playing Wurlitzer",q:"Musical Museum Brentford High Street",w:"A few doors from the steam museum, a building full of machines that play themselves — a theremin, self-playing pianos, orchestrions, and a mighty Wurlitzer that once rose on its lift to soundtrack a west London cinema and still does, for visitors. Music made by ghosts and gears.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"pitzhanger",n:"Pitzhanger Manor",a:"Ealing",pc:"W5 5EQ",lat:51.512,lng:-0.307,c:"history",s:"Soane's failed dynasty house",q:"Pitzhanger Manor Walpole Park Ealing",w:"Sir John Soane designed and built this Regency villa between 1800 and 1804 as a country retreat and a school for the architectural dynasty his sons flatly refused to become. He added mock-Roman ruins to the grounds and fished the ponds with a young J.M.W. Turner. The dream died; the house, beautifully restored, did not.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Pitz_eastface.JPG/960px-Pitz_eastface.JPG"},{id:"bobbunker",n:"Battle of Britain Bunker",a:"Uxbridge",pc:"UB10 0GG",lat:51.5475,lng:-0.4205,c:"history",s:"76 steps down to 1940",q:"Battle of Britain Bunker Uxbridge No 11 Group",w:"The Grade I-listed underground operations room of No. 11 Group Fighter Command, sixty feet down, from which the Battle of Britain and later the D-Day air cover were directed. Churchill visited in September 1940 and, on the way out, reputedly first said the line about so much being owed by so many to so few. Tours only; the map table is still set.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bobhouseruins",n:"Battle of Britain House Ruins",a:"Ruislip",pc:"HA4",lat:51.5825,lng:-0.431,c:"history",s:"the spy-school in the woods",q:"Battle of Britain House ruins Ruislip Woods",w:"Overgrown garden steps and concrete foundations in Ruislip Woods are all that survive of a mansion, burned in 1984, reputedly used to train clandestine agents for occupied France. Nature is quietly reclaiming the formal terraces. You can walk straight past the place where people were taught to disappear.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"grimsdyke",n:"Grim's Dyke",a:"Harrow Weald",pc:"HA3",lat:51.6155,lng:-0.349,c:"history",s:"Boudica's rampart (probably not)",q:"Grim's Dyke earthwork Harrow Weald",w:"An ancient linear earthwork running some six miles from Cuckoo Hill to Pear Wood, still up to two metres high, wrapped in unproven legends of Boudica's last stand and a 'Boadicea's Grave' on Stanmore Common. The Grim's Dyke estate beside it housed the eccentric W.S. Gilbert and his menagerie of lemurs, monkeys and a lynx.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"caesarspond",n:"Caesar's Pond",a:"Stanmore",pc:"HA7",lat:51.6195,lng:-0.312,c:"history",s:"where Caesar supposedly drank",q:"Caesar's Pond Stanmore Little Common",w:"A pond on Stanmore's Little Common, near the site of the Roman station Sulloniacae, where local myth insists Julius Caesar tasted the water during his conquest. He almost certainly didn't — but the name has stuck for centuries, which is its own kind of truth.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"pinnerchalkmines",n:"Pinner Wood Chalk Mines",a:"Pinner",pc:"HA5",lat:51.6035,lng:-0.3915,c:"history",s:"the tunnels under the school",q:"Pinner Wood chalk mines sinkhole 2017",w:"A forgotten network of Victorian chalk-mining tunnels, up to twenty metres deep, that announced itself in 2017 when a sinkhole opened at Pinner Wood School, shutting it for nine months while engineers grouted the voids. Generations of children had been taught directly over the dark.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"southgatetube",n:"Southgate Tube Station",a:"Southgate",pc:"N14 6BP",lat:51.6325,lng:-0.128,c:"artdeco",s:"the flying saucer that landed in 1933",q:"Southgate Underground station Charles Holden",w:"Charles Holden's streamline-moderne masterpiece — Googie before Googie existed — a circular building whose flat roof seems to float on a band of windows, crowned by a 'Tesla coil' finial that local lore (apocryphally) says inspired the Daleks. Grade II*; the original Art Deco clock opposite was restored in 2023.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Southgate_Station_%281%29.jpg/960px-Southgate_Station_%281%29.jpg"},{id:"fortyhall",n:"Forty Hall & Elsyng Palace",a:"Enfield",pc:"EN2 9HA",lat:51.6745,lng:-0.0735,c:"history",s:"the lost Tudor palace next door",q:"Forty Hall Elsyng Palace Enfield",w:"A Grade I Jacobean manor built around 1630 for Lord Mayor Sir Nicholas Rainton, who was jailed in the Tower in 1640 for defying Charles I. In the grounds lie the buried, scheduled remains of Elsyng Palace, where the future Edward VI and Elizabeth I were reportedly told of Henry VIII's death in 1547 — excavated most years, and shown on Digging for Britain.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"myddeltonhouse",n:"Myddelton House Gardens",a:"Enfield",pc:"EN2 9HG",lat:51.681,lng:-0.068,c:"green",s:"the Crocus King's Lunatic Asylum",q:"Myddelton House Gardens E A Bowles Enfield",w:"The lifelong garden of plantsman E.A. 'Gussie' Bowles, who coined the word galanthophile. His 'Lunatic Asylum' is a bed of deliberately deformed plants — corkscrew hazel, contorted forms, botanical misfits. The New River once cut through here, filled in the late 1960s with spoil from the Victoria line dig, its 1832 footbridge left stranded under a wisteria Bowles grew from seed in 1903.",city:"london"},{id:"trentpark",n:"Trent Park",a:"Cockfosters",pc:"EN4",lat:51.651,lng:-0.149,c:"history",s:"the bugged generals' country club",q:"Trent Park M Room German generals MI6",w:"A WWII secret-listening site where 59 captured German generals were held in luxury — Harrods trips, fine dining, a fake aristocrat to flatter them — while microphones hidden in the walls, fireplaces and garden benches fed three basement 'M Rooms', staffed by German-speaking Jewish refugees under MI6's Colonel Kendrick. The transcripts captured some of the earliest evidence of the Holocaust. A museum has been announced — check before you go.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wansteadgrotto",n:"Wanstead Park Grotto",a:"Wanstead",pc:"E11 2LT",lat:51.568,lng:0.0455,c:"follies",s:"the £2,000 shell grotto",q:"Wanstead Park Grotto ruin",w:"The fossil-, crystal- and shell-lined ruin of an eighteenth-century grotto-boathouse, built in the 1760s for two thousand pounds — the last grand survival of vanished Wanstead House, once one of England's grandest Palladian mansions. Gutted by fire in 1884 when the lake had been drained for cleaning, it now stands fenced, on the Heritage at Risk register.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"championsstatue",n:"The Champions Statue",a:"Upton Park",pc:"E13 9AB",lat:51.5345,lng:0.026,c:"pop",s:"1966, in bronze",q:"The Champions statue Bobby Moore Green Street West Ham",w:"Bobby Moore, Geoff Hurst and Martin Peters hoisting the Jules Rimet trophy in bronze at 'Morley's Corner' — the West Ham trio at the heart of England's 1966 World Cup win, frozen mid-celebration. Beside the Boleyn Tavern, where Gandhi reputedly drank cream soda and talked football in 1931.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenstreetmarket",n:"Green Street & Queens Market",a:"Upton Park",pc:"E13",lat:51.535,lng:0.025,c:"diaspora",s:"Little India of the East End",q:"Green Street Queens Market Upton Park saris",w:"A South Asian retail corridor of saris, gold and fabric, anchored by the covered Queens Market — and the historic boundary between East and West Ham. The lost Green Street House nearby, 'Boleyn Castle', wrapped in Anne Boleyn legend, gave West Ham the castle on their crest.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cityoflondoncem",n:"City of London Cemetery",a:"Manor Park",pc:"E12 5DQ",lat:51.5535,lng:0.042,c:"death",s:"Haywood's sunken catacombs",q:"City of London Cemetery Manor Park catacomb valley",w:"A vast Grade I-registered Victorian cemetery, laid out in the 1850s by William Haywood on the former Aldersbrook estate — serpentine drives, a formally planted sunken Catacomb Valley, and the quiet grandeur of a place built to bury a whole city. One of the largest municipal cemeteries in Europe, and still working.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dialarch",n:"The Dial Arch & Royal Brass Foundry",a:"Woolwich",pc:"SE18 6GH",lat:51.492,lng:0.0695,c:"history",s:"where Arsenal FC was born",q:"Dial Arch Royal Arsenal Woolwich Arsenal FC",w:"The Royal Arsenal — once a 1,300-acre 'Secret City' employing some 80,000 in the First World War — survives as twenty-odd listed buildings. Engineers at the Dial Arch, now a pub, founded Dial Square FC in 1886; it became Arsenal. The 1717 Royal Brass Foundry, where the empire's cannon were cast, still stands nearby.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"woolwichferry",n:"Woolwich Free Ferry",a:"Woolwich",pc:"SE18",lat:51.497,lng:0.0625,c:"maritime",s:"free since 1889",q:"Woolwich Free Ferry Thames",w:"A roll-on, roll-off ferry across the Thames, free of all tolls by an Act of 1885 and running since 1889 — one of the last places you cross the river for nothing, cars and foot passengers together. A 2018 vessel is named for Ben Woollacott, a nineteen-year-old crewman who died on the job in 2011.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Woolwich_Ferry_from_the_Southeast_%2801%29.jpg/960px-Woolwich_Ferry_from_the_Southeast_%2801%29.jpg"},{id:"lesnesabbey",n:"Lesnes Abbey & Fossil Pit",a:"Abbey Wood",pc:"SE2 0LR",lat:51.487,lng:0.131,c:"history",s:"dig for shark teeth by the ruins",q:"Lesnes Abbey fossil pit Abbey Wood",w:"The ruins of a twelfth-century Augustinian abbey, founded in 1178 by Richard de Lucy — possibly in penance for the murder of Becket — beside a Site of Special Scientific Interest where the public may dig for fifty-five-million-year-old shark teeth and Eocene fossils. A mulberry planted around 1620 still fruits among the stones.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thediver",n:"The Diver: Regeneration",a:"Rainham",pc:"RM13",lat:51.4974,lng:0.1824,c:"oddity",s:"the man standing in the Thames",q:"The Diver Regeneration John Kaufman Rainham riverside",w:"John Kaufman's 'The Diver: Regeneration' stands fifteen feet tall in the river itself — built from some 300 metres of galvanised steel banding and three thousand bolts, weighing three tons, and believed the only sculpture standing in the Thames. It honours his grandfather, a clearance diver in the London Docks around 1900, and is submerged at every high tide. Kaufman died in 2002, soon after finishing it.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/The_Diver.jpg/960px-The_Diver.jpg"},{id:"concretebarges",n:"The Concrete Barges",a:"Rainham",pc:"RM13",lat:51.4955,lng:0.2055,c:"oddity",s:"not the D-Day barges everyone thinks",q:"Concrete barges Coldharbour Point Rainham",w:"Around sixteen WWII ferro-concrete barges lie half-sunk in the Thames mud as 1953 flood defences. The on-site plaque's story — Mulberry harbour pieces towed back from Normandy — is a myth: these were 1940 petrol barges that never crossed to France. Wintering pipits roost on them now. Cross the deep silt only in sturdy boots.",city:"london"},{id:"rainhammarshes",n:"RSPB Rainham Marshes",a:"Rainham",pc:"RM19 1SZ",lat:51.4895,lng:0.221,c:"green",s:"the rifle range turned wetland",q:"RSPB Rainham Marshes Purfleet",w:"Medieval grazing marsh used as a military firing range for nearly a century before the RSPB reopened it in 2006. Brick stop-butts still pocked with .303 bullet holes survive among the reeds, with wide skies and a clean view to Canary Wharf across the water. The visitor centre's future has been reported under review.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"erithpier",n:"Erith Pier",a:"Erith",pc:"DA8 1QA",lat:51.4825,lng:0.1775,c:"oddity",s:"London's longest Thames pier",q:"Erith Pier",w:"A boomerang-shaped concrete pier, around 360 metres of it, built in the 1950s as a cargo wharf, abandoned, then reopened to walkers in 1999 — London's longest pier on the Thames. Erith's brief 1840s career as a riverside resort was killed by the railway, then finished off by Crossness sewage flowing upstream from 1865.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"crossnessnr",n:"Crossness Nature Reserve",a:"Belvedere",pc:"DA17",lat:51.5085,lng:0.1335,c:"green",s:"marsh wilderness beside the Great Stink's cure",q:"Crossness Nature Reserve Erith Marshes",w:"A 25-hectare reserve of grazing marsh and Bexley's largest reedbeds — water voles, the rare shrill carder bee, big estuary skies — sitting right beside the eastern terminus of Bazalgette's Southern Outfall Sewer, opened in 1865 to cure the Great Stink. Wilderness engineered out of a city's effluent.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"apollobananaleaf",n:"Apollo Banana Leaf",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW17 0SF",lat:51.4258,lng:-0.1689,c:"food",s:"Tooting, we have a problem",q:"Apollo Banana Leaf Tooting Sri Lankan",w:"A no-frills, BYOB family Sri Lankan and South Indian curry house beloved of London's Tamil community — squid curry, mutton kothu, lurid-green Sri Lankan cream soda, mains for a few pounds, cash only. The name riffs on the NASA missions; the pilgrimage is real, with food bloggers and r/london regulars in the same queue.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lahorekarahi",n:"Lahore Karahi",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW17 0SN",lat:51.4276,lng:-0.168,c:"food",s:"Sadiq Khan's local",q:"Lahore Karahi Tooting High Street",w:"A local-legend Pakistani BYOB grill running since the mid-1990s — the first venue at the south end of Tooting's curry corridor — turning out charcoal skewers and meat karahi. Publicly a favourite of Tooting's most famous son, the Mayor of London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dosanchutny",n:"Dosa n Chutny",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW17 0RN",lat:51.4272,lng:-0.1681,c:"food",s:"best dosa in London",q:"Dosa n Chutny Tooting",w:"A canteen-bright Chennai-style café, widely rated for the best dosas in London, with Tamil films running on the flatscreen and idly going two-for-not-very-much. Time Out called it close to a perfect dosa; the regulars never doubted it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"jaffnahouse",n:"Jaffna House",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW17 0RN",lat:51.4268,lng:-0.1683,c:"food",s:"short eats and watalappam",q:"Jaffna House Tooting Sri Lankan Tamil",w:"A Jaffna Tamil institution on the curry corridor for cheap mutton rolls, fish cutlets, chicken kotthu and palm-sugar watalappam custard — the snacks and sweets a Sri Lankan diaspora actually grew up on, served without ceremony.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tootingmarket",n:"Tooting Market",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW17 0SN",lat:51.4274,lng:-0.1682,c:"food",s:"we are Tooting",q:"Tooting Market covered market",w:"South London's original covered indoor market, founded in 1930 — a multicultural warren of Jamaican, Mauritian and Guyanese traders, with blue plaques over the entrances reading 'A community of all, for all. We are Tooting.' Lonely Planet once named the area among the coolest places on earth; the market is why.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ocantinho",n:"O Cantinho de Portugal",a:"Stockwell",pc:"SW9 9TN",lat:51.4647,lng:-0.1135,c:"food",s:"Madeira in Lambeth",q:"O Cantinho de Portugal Stockwell Little Portugal",w:"A family-run Portuguese restaurant in Lambeth's Little Portugal — London's largest, mostly Madeiran, Portuguese-speaking community — split Lisbon-style with the football on Portuguese TV. Bacalhau com natas, moelas, grilled sardines, and a room that feels imported wholesale from the Atlantic.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"madeirapatisserie",n:"Madeira Patisserie",a:"Vauxhall",pc:"SW8 1RL",lat:51.4838,lng:-0.124,c:"bakery",s:"the nation's custard tart",q:"Madeira Patisserie South Lambeth Road pastel de nata",w:"Opened in 1988, the wholesale powerhouse behind a startling share of the UK's pastéis de nata — blasted at 300 degrees until the custard scorches and the pastry shatters. Time Out has called these among London's best pastries; half the city has eaten one without knowing it came from a South Lambeth Road counter.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cranewandsworth",n:"The Crane",a:"Wandsworth",pc:"SW18",lat:51.4607,lng:-0.1934,c:"pub",s:"boy in knickerbockers",q:"The Crane Wandsworth oldest pub haunted",w:"Wandsworth's oldest pub, once called the most haunted building in the borough — reputedly walked by a boy in short trousers in the bar and a woman who passes straight through the attic wall. Reported closed of late; verify the trading status before you cross town for a pint with a ghost.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tootingghostsigns",n:"Tooting Ghost Signs",a:"Tooting",pc:"SW17",lat:51.427,lng:-0.1685,c:"ghostsign",s:"gowns and grooves",q:"Tooting ghost signs Mitcham Road 20th Century Gowns",w:"A pair of ghost signs revealed when a billboard came down on Mitcham Road: '20th Century Gowns' painted over 'Dallas Star Tested Band Instruments', a 1920s HMV dealership. Two vanished trades, one wall, surfacing together after decades in the dark.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rvpb",n:"Royal Victoria Patriotic Building",a:"Wandsworth Common",pc:"SW18 3SX",lat:51.4546,lng:-0.1696,c:"history",s:"Crimean asylum, spy school",q:"Royal Victoria Patriotic Building Le Gothique Wandsworth",w:"A vast 1859 Gothic pile, built as an asylum for the orphans of the Crimean War, later a WWII MI5 and MI6 interrogation centre where arrivals to Britain were screened for spies. It now houses the Le Gothique restaurant, complete with a resident ghost named Charlotte and turrets that don't quite belong to any century you can name.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"crossnesspumping",n:"Crossness Pumping Station",a:"Abbey Wood",pc:"SE2 9AQ",lat:51.506,lng:0.136,c:"oddity",s:"the Cathedral of Sewage",q:"Crossness Pumping Station Bazalgette beam engines",w:"Bazalgette and Charles Driver's 1865 Romanesque temple of polychrome cast iron — home to the largest remaining rotative beam engines in the world, with 52-ton flywheels named Victoria, Prince Consort, Albert Edward and Alexandra. Grade I-listed, opened to the public in 2016, with volunteer steaming days and a narrow-gauge 'Excrement Express'. A cathedral built to move a city's filth.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"charltonhouse",n:"Charlton House",a:"Charlton",pc:"SE7 8RE",lat:51.4856,lng:0.0345,c:"history",s:"uglier faces going up",q:"Charlton House Jacobean mansion",w:"One of London's finest surviving Jacobean mansions, built 1607–12 for Prince Henry's tutor Sir Adam Newton. The carved faces on the bannister get uglier as you climb — the evil spirits, it was held, worsened the higher you went. A 1607 mulberry from James I's doomed silk scheme survives, and a summerhouse that ended its days as a 1930s public toilet.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"persepolis",n:"Persepolis",a:"Peckham",pc:"SE15 5DT",lat:51.4738,lng:-0.068,c:"food",s:"Persia in Peckham",q:"Persepolis Snackistan Peckham Sally Butcher",w:"A bright-yellow Persian emporium-cum-café opened around 1995 by Sally Butcher and her Iranian husband Jamshid — once 'the only Iranian shop south of the river'. Cult sweets, BYOB vegetarian food, shelves of rosewater and dried limes, and Sally's best-selling cookbooks written from behind the till.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"phohanoi",n:"Pho Hanoi",a:"Deptford",pc:"SE8 3NT",lat:51.477,lng:-0.0262,c:"food",s:"boat-people pho",q:"Pho Hanoi Deptford Vietnamese High Street",w:"A modest, authentic Vietnamese spot on Deptford High Street, part of a community descended from the late-1970s refugees who settled around Evelyn Street. Pho, bún bò Huế, chả lá lốt — the cooking of a boat-people diaspora that quietly made this stretch of south-east London its own.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mmanzedeptford",n:"M. Manze, Deptford",a:"Deptford",pc:"SE8 3PR",lat:51.4775,lng:-0.0265,c:"pieandmash",s:"the secret recipe",q:"M Manze Deptford High Street pie and mash",w:"A Grade II-listed eel, pie and mash shop opened in 1914 by Pantaleone Manze of the Anglo-Italian Manze dynasty — original terrazzo, marble tables and a black-glass shopfront, all unchanged. Distinct from the Manze shops at Tower Bridge: same Ravello-immigrant root, separate enterprise. Reported closing on its owner's retirement, but the listed interior endures.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Deptford_High_Street%2C_SE8_%282%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1498383.jpg"},{id:"mangal1",n:"Mangal 1 (Ocakbaşı)",a:"Dalston",pc:"E8 2DJ",lat:51.5478,lng:-0.076,c:"food",s:"London's first grill",q:"Mangal 1 Ocakbasi Arcola Street Dalston",w:"The first ocakbaşı in London, opened in the late 1980s — a poky BYOB whose entrance grill smokes halfway down Arcola Street and whose chefs wear 'simply the best' t-shirts. The original that an entire Turkish grill scene grew out of; Mangal 2 around the corner is the offspring, not the source.",city:"london"},{id:"nunheadmunicipal",n:"Nunhead & District Municipal Museum",a:"Telegraph Hill",pc:"SE14",lat:51.473,lng:-0.056,c:"oddity",s:"the museum that never was",q:"Nunhead District Municipal Museum Telegraph Hill Festival",w:"A house transformed each Telegraph Hill Festival into a fake municipal museum — complete with an invented Victorian industrialist, George Gellatly, a lost post-punk band called The Nunhead Cemeteries, and fictional blue plaques scattered across the neighbourhood. A whole local history, entirely made up, and lovingly believed. Festival-only.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sprigganparkland",n:"The Spriggan of Parkland Walk",a:"Crouch End",pc:"N8",lat:51.579,lng:-0.123,c:"oddity",s:"the goat-man's guardian",q:"Spriggan Parkland Walk Crouch End sculpture",w:"Marilyn Collins's 1993 Cornish spriggan crawls out of a railway arch on the disused Finsbury Park–Alexandra Palace line, inspired by a local legend of a ghostly 'goat-man'. Often linked to Stephen King's story 'Crouch End', though the tale predates the sculpture by a decade — so the spriggan can't have inspired King, only the walk might have. Either way, it watches you pass.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"markfieldbeam",n:"Markfield Beam Engine & Museum",a:"South Tottenham",pc:"N15 4RB",lat:51.579,lng:-0.068,c:"museum",s:"1886 steam, still turning",q:"Markfield Beam Engine Museum Tottenham",w:"A Grade II-listed 1886 Wood Bros. beam engine in a former sewage pumping station in Markfield Park, where the buried River Moselle meets the Lea. On steam days the great beam still turns over, exactly as it did when it was lifting north London's waste — Victorian engineering kept alive by volunteers.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brucecastle",n:"Bruce Castle",a:"Tottenham",pc:"N17 8NU",lat:51.602,lng:-0.072,c:"history",s:"the mystery round tower",q:"Bruce Castle Tottenham museum",w:"A Grade I sixteenth-century manor with an enigmatic Tudor round tower whose purpose is still argued over — dovecote, hawk mews, nobody is sure. Now Haringey's local museum, it holds the Rowland Hill collection charting the postal reform that gave the world the penny stamp. Tudor brick marooned in modern Tottenham.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"newingtongreenchurch",n:"Newington Green Unitarian Church",a:"Newington Green",pc:"N16 9PR",lat:51.553,lng:-0.087,c:"history",s:"the birthplace of feminism",q:"Newington Green Unitarian Church Mary Wollstonecraft",w:"London's oldest Nonconformist place of worship still in use, from 1708. Richard Price preached radical sermons here; Mary Wollstonecraft had her political awakening in the congregation. The terrace opposite, of 1658, is London's oldest surviving brick row, and Maggi Hambling's contested Wollstonecraft sculpture stands on the green outside.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"churchstudios",n:"Church Studios",a:"Crouch End",pc:"N8",lat:51.58,lng:-0.121,c:"music",s:"Dave Stewart's church",q:"Church Studios Crouch Hill Dave Stewart",w:"A nineteenth-century church converted into a recording studio by Eurythmics' Dave Stewart in the 1980s, and since used by Adele, Radiohead, Bowie and Massive Attack. Local legend has Bob Dylan knocking on the wrong door and ending up taking tea with a startled Crouch End resident. A working studio — admire it from the street.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gokyuzu",n:"Gökyüzü",a:"Harringay",pc:"N4 1LG",lat:51.58,lng:-0.096,c:"food",s:"the full platter",q:"Gokyuzu Green Lanes Harringay Turkish grill",w:"Opened in 1999 by the Yavuz family, the slickest of Green Lanes' big-four charcoal-grill houses — famous for mixed-grill sharing platters the size of small countries, and queues that build after half past eight. The flagship of Harringay's 'Little Turkey'.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"antepliler",n:"Antepliler",a:"Harringay",pc:"N4 1AG",lat:51.581,lng:-0.0958,c:"food",s:"first baklava in London",q:"Antepliler Green Lanes Harringay baklava",w:"Begun in 1993 as a Green Lanes baklava producer — reputedly London's first authentic baklava maker, from Gaziantep, a UNESCO city of gastronomy. Now a mini-empire of grill, döner and künefe shops, but the trays of pistachio baklava in the window are still the point.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"notedeelpie",n:"Noted Eel & Pie House",a:"Leytonstone",pc:"E11 4JU",lat:51.564,lng:0.008,c:"pieandmash",s:"the last live eels",q:"Noted Eel and Pie House Leytonstone Hak family",w:"Run by the Hak family, descendants of Dutch eel-fishing schuyts — started in Bow in 1926, moved to Leytonstone in the 1970s, and one of the only shops still keeping live eels out the back. It was renamed 'Noted' in the war so the German-looking old sign wouldn't draw suspicion. Now, improbably, a TikTok pilgrimage for the eel-curious.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lmanze",n:"L. Manze",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"E17 7LD",lat:51.5835,lng:-0.021,c:"pieandmash",s:"the listed Art Deco pie shop",q:"L Manze Walthamstow High Street pie mash Art Deco",w:"East London's oldest surviving pie-and-mash building, from 1929, Grade II-listed in 2013 for its complete Art Deco interior — tilework, mirrors, marble. The pie counter itself was reported closed in 2022, but the listing protects one of the most beautiful eel-and-liquor rooms ever built.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"walthamstowmarket",n:"Walthamstow Market",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"E17 7LD",lat:51.584,lng:-0.0205,c:"food",s:"Europe's longest street market",q:"Walthamstow Market High Street",w:"Established in 1885 and running the length of the High Street — reputed to be a mile, though it actually measures about a kilometre — with some five hundred stalls. Officially the longest outdoor market in Europe, fully alive six days a week, with L. Manze's Art Deco frontage watching over one end.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Waltham_Forest_Town_Hall_%281%29_-_2022-10-01.jpg/960px-Waltham_Forest_Town_Hall_%281%29_-_2022-10-01.jpg"},{id:"bryantmay",n:"Bryant & May / Bow Quarter",a:"Bow",pc:"E3 2SE",lat:51.5295,lng:-0.0235,c:"history",s:"the matchgirls struck here",q:"Bryant and May matchgirls strike Bow Quarter",w:"The 1888 matchgirls' strike against phossy-jaw conditions and brutal fines began at this Bryant & May factory — now the gated, gentrified Bow Quarter. Led by women and girls and backed by Annie Besant, it was a hinge moment for the British labour movement. Strike leader Sarah Chapman is commemorated nearby.",city:"london"},{id:"minnielansbury",n:"Minnie Lansbury Memorial Clock",a:"Bow",pc:"E3 4LL",lat:51.5275,lng:-0.0185,c:"history",s:"the rebel councillor's clock",q:"Minnie Lansbury memorial clock Bow Road",w:"A green memorial clock on Bow Road to the suffragette and Poplar councillor Minnie Lansbury, jailed in 1921 for refusing to over-charge her poorest constituents during the Poplar Rates Rebellion. She died soon after her release. The clock keeps her time on the high street she fought for.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"eastburymanor",n:"Eastbury Manor House",a:"Barking",pc:"IG11 9SN",lat:51.532,lng:0.089,c:"history",s:"the Hampton Court of the East",q:"Eastbury Manor House Barking Gunpowder Plot",w:"A Grade I Elizabethan brick manor of the 1570s, marooned amid a council estate on former Barking Abbey land. Defoe spread the debunked legend that the Gunpowder Plot was hatched here, and resident ghosts are duly reported. A complete Tudor merchant's house, improbably surviving in the far East End.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"valencehouse",n:"Valence House Museum",a:"Dagenham",pc:"RM8 3HT",lat:51.556,lng:0.137,c:"museum",s:"the moated survivor",q:"Valence House Museum Dagenham",w:"Dagenham's only surviving manor house, medieval-moated, named for Agnes de Valence. Now a museum with a gloriously eccentric collection — Egyptian ushabti figures, a fragment of Queen Victoria's wedding cake, and the Tsarina Alexandra's walking stick — sitting in green calm where Ford's vast plant once roared nearby.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"barkingabbey",n:"Barking Abbey",a:"Barking",pc:"IG11 8AS",lat:51.536,lng:0.081,c:"history",s:"founded 666 AD",q:"Barking Abbey Curfew Tower St Margaret's Captain Cook",w:"The ruins of one of England's most important medieval royal nunneries, founded around 666 AD. The surviving Curfew Tower and St Margaret's Church remain — where Captain Cook married in 1762, before he sailed off the edge of the known map. Lawns and low walls now, but for a thousand years this was real power.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"eelpieisland",n:"Eel Pie Island",a:"Twickenham",pc:"TW1 3DY",lat:51.447,lng:-0.327,c:"music",s:"Stones on the sprung floor",q:"Eel Pie Island Twickenham Rolling Stones hotel",w:"A tiny Thames island whose vanished hotel hosted the Rolling Stones' five-month 1963 residency — nearly every Wednesday, just before fame — plus the Who, Bowie and the Bluesbreakers. Later it became the UK's largest hippie commune, until a 1971 fire. Now artists' studios, reachable on foot only on twice-yearly open weekends.",city:"london",oh:"Th-Su 12:00-18:00",wq:"a"},{id:"eelpiemuseum",n:"Eel Pie Island Museum",a:"Twickenham",pc:"TW1 3AB",lat:51.4465,lng:-0.329,c:"museum",s:"Charlie Watts's drumsticks",q:"Eel Pie Island Museum Twickenham Trevor Baylis",w:"A tiny museum to the island's rock-and-roll decade and to its inventor-islander Trevor Baylis, who gave the world the wind-up radio. You can sit in his thinking chair. A whole scene's worth of memory packed into a couple of rooms on the Twickenham bank.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rcp",n:"Royal College of Physicians",a:"Regent's Park",pc:"NW1 4LE",lat:51.5258,lng:-0.1494,c:"history",s:"a modernist box full of skulls",q:"Royal College of Physicians Regent's Park Lasdun museum",w:"Denys Lasdun's 1964 white modernist building on the edge of Regent's Park — Grade I-listed, all floating planes and a sweeping staircase — hides a museum of medical curiosities, a 'spit-and-polish' collection of anatomical relics, and a medicinal garden of plants that can cure or kill. Free, and far stranger inside than the cool exterior admits.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"svevo",n:"Italo Svevo's Charlton Home",a:"Charlton",pc:"SE7 7AB",lat:51.488,lng:0.033,c:"literary",s:"the Trieste novelist in SE7",q:"Italo Svevo Charlton home plaque",w:"For years the Italian modernist Italo Svevo — author of 'Confessions of Zeno', friend and English pupil of James Joyce — spent long stretches in Charlton, running the family marine-paint business. A quiet south-east London footnote to one of the strangest careers in European literature: a Trieste novelist commuting to the Thames.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"buddhapadipa",n:"Buddhapadipa Temple",a:"Wimbledon",pc:"SW19 5HJ",lat:51.4316,lng:-0.2137,c:"faith",s:"a Thai temple behind the tennis",q:"Buddhapadipa Temple Wimbledon",w:"The first Thai Buddhist temple built in the UK — a golden-roofed wat set in four acres of Wimbledon garden, with a lake, ornamental trees and an interior of vivid murals painted by Thai artists. A few streets from the tennis, and a complete transplant of another country's sacred architecture into suburban SW19.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"peacepagoda",n:"London Peace Pagoda",a:"Battersea Park",pc:"SW11 4NJ",lat:51.4818,lng:-0.1665,c:"faith",s:"Buddhist monks by the river",q:"London Peace Pagoda Battersea Park",w:"A double-roofed Buddhist peace pagoda raised in Battersea Park in 1985 by the monks and nuns of Nipponzan-Myōhōji, its gilded Buddhas facing out across the Thames. A resident monk tended it for decades. A startling note of stillness between the power station and the river.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"grimaldi",n:"Joseph Grimaldi Park",a:"Pentonville",pc:"N1 9RP",lat:51.532,lng:-0.1175,c:"death",s:"dance on the clown's grave",q:"Joseph Grimaldi Park Pentonville musical tomb",w:"A former churchyard turned park around the grave of Joseph Grimaldi, the father of modern clowning, who died in 1837. An artist's installation set bronze coffin-shaped tiles into the ground that chime musical notes when you dance across them — so you can, quite literally, play a tune on the clown's tomb.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"henrysmound",n:"King Henry's Mound",a:"Richmond Park",pc:"TW10 5HX",lat:51.4445,lng:-0.273,c:"view",s:"the protected ten-mile sightline",q:"King Henry's Mound Richmond Park St Paul's view",w:"A prehistoric mound in Richmond Park where, through a gap in the trees and a fixed telescope, you can see St Paul's Cathedral ten miles away — a sightline protected by law from any building that would block it. Legend says Henry VIII stood here to watch for the rocket signalling Anne Boleyn's execution; the dates don't quite work, but the view does.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"onetreehill",n:"One Tree Hill",a:"Honor Oak",pc:"SE23 3QA",lat:51.454,lng:-0.056,c:"view",s:"ringed by the dead",q:"One Tree Hill Honor Oak Oak of Honor view",w:"A wooded south London ridge crowned by the Oak of Honor, where Elizabeth I supposedly picnicked, with a sudden view to the City from the top. The whole area is uniquely encircled by cemeteries — Nunhead, Camberwell Old and New — which has fed a persistent, treat-it-as-folklore local rumour of satanic worship on the hill after dark.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thepoint",n:"The Point, Greenwich",a:"Greenwich",pc:"SE10 8QY",lat:51.472,lng:-0.0075,c:"view",s:"the locals' Greenwich panorama",q:"The Point Greenwich Hyde Vale viewpoint",w:"A small, half-hidden promontory above Greenwich, off the beaten path the tourists take to the Observatory — a sudden grandstand view over the Thames, the towers and the spread of the city. The viewpoint Greenwich locals send you to instead of the queue.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"richmonddeer",n:"Richmond Park Deer",a:"Richmond",pc:"TW10 5HS",lat:51.4425,lng:-0.273,c:"green",s:"700 wild deer, ten minutes from the Tube",q:"Richmond Park red fallow deer",w:"Around 600 red and fallow deer roam Richmond Park freely, as they have since Charles I enclosed it for hunting in 1637 — the largest of London's Royal Parks and a National Nature Reserve. In the autumn rut the stags bellow and clash; year-round, keep your distance, especially in birthing season. Genuinely wild, barely zone 4.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bushydeer",n:"Bushy Park Deer",a:"Hampton",pc:"TW11 0EQ",lat:51.4115,lng:-0.345,c:"green",s:"Hampton Court's deer-cropped wilderness",q:"Bushy Park deer Hampton",w:"The second-largest Royal Park, beside Hampton Court — herds of red and fallow deer grazing among the grasslands and the Chestnut Avenue that Wren laid out as a grand approach. Quieter and wilder than its famous neighbour, with the deer wandering close enough to remind you the place was made for the chase.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wwtbarnes",n:"WWT London Wetland Centre",a:"Barnes",pc:"SW13 9WT",lat:51.476,lng:-0.235,c:"green",s:"reservoirs turned bird city",q:"WWT London Wetland Centre Barnes",w:"Forty-odd hectares of lakes, reedbed and marsh created on four redundant Victorian reservoirs in Barnes — opened in 2000 and now one of the best urban birdwatching sites in Europe, with bitterns, sand martins and wintering wildfowl a short bus ride from Hammersmith. A working argument that cities can manufacture wilderness.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"walthamstowwetlands",n:"Walthamstow Wetlands",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"N17 9NH",lat:51.587,lng:-0.06,c:"green",s:"Europe's largest urban wetland reserve",q:"Walthamstow Wetlands Thames Water reservoirs",w:"Ten working Thames Water reservoirs, opened to the public in 2017 as Europe's largest urban wetland nature reserve — 211 hectares of water and reedbed on the Lea, with herons, peregrines on the old engine-house chimney, and the city skyline floating beyond. Still supplying London's taps while the birds move in.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"eppingforest",n:"Epping Forest",a:"Loughton",pc:"IG10 4AF",lat:51.658,lng:0.033,c:"green",s:"London's ancient hunting wood",q:"Epping Forest Loughton ancient woodland",w:"Six thousand acres of ancient woodland and heath straddling the city's north-east edge — a former royal hunting forest saved for the public by the Corporation of London in 1878, after locals fought the enclosures. Iron Age earthworks, veteran pollarded hornbeams, and the highwayman Dick Turpin's old country, all within a Central line ride.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"unionchapel",n:"Union Chapel",a:"Islington",pc:"N1 2UN",lat:51.5478,lng:-0.1027,c:"music",s:"gigs in a working church",q:"Union Chapel Islington venue",w:"A soaring Gothic Congregational chapel of 1877 on Upper Street — still a working church and a homeless drop-in, and one of the most atmospheric music venues in Britain, where the audience sits in the pews under the great rose window. Everyone from Björk to Amy Winehouse has played the room; the acoustics do half the work.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cartandhorses",n:"The Cart & Horses",a:"Stratford",pc:"E15 1EL",lat:51.5462,lng:0.0019,c:"music",s:"the birthplace of Iron Maiden",q:"Cart and Horses Stratford Iron Maiden",w:"A Stratford pub that bills itself the birthplace of Iron Maiden — Steve Harris's band cut their teeth on its tiny stage in the mid-1970s, and the heavy-metal pilgrimage has never really stopped. Loud, unpretentious, and proud of a noise that went on to fill stadiums.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 12:00-00:30, Fr 12:00-03:00, Sa 12:00-03:30, Su 12:00-00:30",wq:"a"},{id:"sadlerswells",n:"Sadler's Wells",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1R 4TN",lat:51.5295,lng:-0.1063,c:"music",s:"dance on a holy well",q:"Sadler's Wells Theatre Clerkenwell",w:"Britain's leading dance house, on a Clerkenwell site that has held a theatre since 1683 — named for Richard Sadler and the medieval holy well found in his grounds. The present building is the sixth on the spot; the well itself still survives under the stalls, beneath the world's contemporary choreography.",city:"london"},{id:"maltbystreet",n:"Maltby Street Market",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"SE1 3PA",lat:51.4985,lng:-0.0735,c:"food",s:"the railway-arch food run",q:"Maltby Street Market Bermondsey Ropewalk",w:"A weekend food market strung along the Ropewalk under the Bermondsey railway arches — born when traders spilled out of Borough, scrappier and better for it. St John doughnuts, gin from a working distillery, cheese toasties and natural wine, eaten standing as the trains rumble overhead.",city:"london",oh:"Sa 10:00-17:00; Su 11:00-16:00",wq:"a"},{id:"leatherlane",n:"Leather Lane Market",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1N 7TJ",lat:51.5205,lng:-0.109,c:"food",s:"the lunchtime street market",q:"Leather Lane Market Clerkenwell",w:"A street market running since at least the seventeenth century, now the weekday lunch engine of Clerkenwell — a long line of stalls slinging everything from Ethiopian wraps to grilled cheese, with the white-collar queue stretching down the lane by half past twelve. Trade, not heritage, but five hundred years of it.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 10:00-16:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Leather_Lane_-_geograph.org.uk_-_680292.jpg"},{id:"mercatomayfair",n:"Mercato Mayfair",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1K 6ZA",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.1525,c:"food",s:"a food hall in a Pugin church",q:"Mercato Mayfair St Mark's North Audley Street",w:"A sustainable food hall built inside St Mark's, a deconsecrated Grade I Mayfair church partly by Pugin's circle — traders and a wine bar arranged beneath the nave, the organ pipes and stained glass still overhead. Communion replaced by carbonara, in one of the more surreal reuses of sacred space in London.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 09:00-23:00; Fr-Sa 09:00-24:00; Su 09:00-22:30",wq:"a"},{id:"rochellecanteen",n:"Rochelle Canteen",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"E2 7ES",lat:51.5258,lng:-0.0758,c:"food",s:"lunch in the old bike shed",q:"Rochelle Canteen Arnold Circus Shoreditch",w:"Hidden behind a blue door in the wall of a former Victorian school off Arnold Circus, in what was the bicycle shed — Margot Henderson's spare, brilliant British canteen, with a short daily-changing menu and tables in the old playground. You have to know it's there, which is rather the point.",city:"london"},{id:"churchillarms",n:"The Churchill Arms",a:"Kensington",pc:"W8 7LN",lat:51.5072,lng:-0.1944,c:"pub",s:"the pub that vanishes under flowers",q:"Churchill Arms Kensington flowers Thai",w:"A Kensington corner pub buried under a cascade of flowers — hundreds of baskets and a few hundred pounds a month in blooms — with a Christmas display of nearly a hundred trees. Inside, Churchilliana, butterflies and bric-a-brac, and one of London's first pub Thai kitchens, going since the late 1980s in the conservatory out back.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 11:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/119_Kensington_Church_Street.jpg/960px-119_Kensington_Church_Street.jpg"},{id:"wcclapham",n:"WC Clapham",a:"Clapham",pc:"SW4 0AA",lat:51.4628,lng:-0.1378,c:"wine",s:"wine and cheese in the gents",q:"WC Clapham wine bar Victorian toilets",w:"A wine-and-charcuterie bar built into a disused Victorian underground public lavatory on Clapham Common — hence the name, 'Wine & Charcuterie', and the joke. Original tiling, cisterns repurposed, and a list of low-intervention bottles served below pavement level, where Londoners once did something altogether less convivial.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Clapham_Common_bldg_front.jpg/960px-Clapham_Common_bldg_front.jpg"},{id:"sketch",n:"Sketch",a:"Mayfair",pc:"W1S 2XG",lat:51.5125,lng:-0.141,c:"food",s:"the pink room and the egg pods",q:"Sketch Mayfair Gallery David Shrigley",w:"A Mayfair townhouse turned surrealist playground — the blush-pink Gallery hung with David Shrigley's drawings, and a set of pod-shaped, egg-like toilet capsules under a stained-glass dome that have launched a million photos. Tea, art and theatre, deliberately too much of everything. Go for the rooms as much as the food.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/The_Faceless_Dog_Statue_at_Sketch_Restaurant_%286249317594%29.jpg/960px-The_Faceless_Dog_Statue_at_Sketch_Restaurant_%286249317594%29.jpg"},{id:"feministlibrary",n:"The Feminist Library",a:"Peckham",pc:"SE15 6JL",lat:51.473,lng:-0.064,c:"archive",s:"fifty years of the movement, shelved",q:"Feminist Library Peckham",w:"A community-run archive founded in 1975, holding tens of thousands of books, periodicals and the paper records of the Women's Liberation Movement — pamphlets, badges, banners, the ephemera that institutions threw away. After years of precarious moves it found a Peckham home; a living memory of a revolution, kept by volunteers.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"conwayhall",n:"Conway Hall",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC1R 4RL",lat:51.518,lng:-0.118,c:"archive",s:"the home of free thought",q:"Conway Hall Red Lion Square humanism library",w:"Opened in 1929 and run by the Conway Hall Ethical Society, the oldest free-thought organisation in the world — a Holborn hall that has hosted radicals, humanists and protest since before the war, with a Humanist Library and a Sunday concert series stretching back over a century. Reason and dissent, with good acoustics.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mollondocklands",n:"Museum of London Docklands",a:"West India Quay",pc:"E14 4AL",lat:51.5075,lng:-0.0235,c:"museum",s:"the sugar warehouse of empire",q:"Museum of London Docklands West India Quay",w:"Inside a Grade I Georgian sugar warehouse on West India Quay — a free museum of the river, the port and the trade that built London, including 'London, Sugar & Slavery', a permanent gallery confronting the city's role in the transatlantic slave economy. The building itself is the exhibit: brick, beams, and a very uncomfortable history.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 10:00-17:00"},{id:"bgwmc",n:"Bethnal Green Working Men's Club",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"E2 6HG",lat:51.5285,lng:-0.0625,c:"music",s:"cabaret in a 1950s social club",q:"Bethnal Green Working Men's Club cabaret",w:"A genuine 1953 working men's club that became one of East London's great cabaret and club nights — flock wallpaper, a sprung dance floor, glitter and drag and swing under the old members' photos. The committee-room formality and the chaos of the night sit on top of each other, and somehow it works.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"raggedschool",n:"Ragged School Museum",a:"Mile End",pc:"E3 4RR",lat:51.5216,lng:-0.033,c:"museum",s:"Barnardo's biggest classroom",q:"Ragged School Museum Mile End Copperfield Road",w:"Canalside warehouses that once held Dr Barnardo's largest 'ragged school', educating the poorest East End children for free between 1877 and 1908. The museum recreates a Victorian classroom — slates, cane, hard benches — and a 1900 East End kitchen, in a building that was the closest thing thousands of children got to a chance.",city:"london"},{id:"drjohnson",n:"Dr Johnson's House",a:"Gough Square",pc:"EC4A 3DE",lat:51.5151,lng:-0.1086,c:"literary",s:"where the Dictionary was written",q:"Dr Johnson's House Gough Square",w:"A rare surviving Georgian townhouse in a hidden City square, where Samuel Johnson compiled his great Dictionary of the English Language in the attic between 1748 and 1755, with six clerks at long tables. The garret survives, the books survive, and outside sits Hodge, his cat, in bronze — 'a very fine cat indeed'.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Dr._Johnson%27s_House.jpg/960px-Dr._Johnson%27s_House.jpg"},{id:"twotempleplace",n:"Two Temple Place",a:"Embankment",pc:"WC2R 3BD",lat:51.5113,lng:-0.113,c:"history",s:"Astor's neo-Gothic jewel box",q:"Two Temple Place Embankment Astor",w:"Built in the 1890s as the estate office of William Waldorf Astor, then the richest man in the world — a small neo-Gothic mansion stuffed with carved oak, stained glass, silver-gilt and characters from Dumas and Arthurian legend, money turned into fantasy. Free to enter during its winter exhibitions, and almost unknown.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Former_Astor_Estate_Office_2_%286086928430%29.jpg/960px-Former_Astor_Estate_Office_2_%286086928430%29.jpg"},{id:"middletemple",n:"Middle Temple Hall",a:"Temple",pc:"EC4Y 9AT",lat:51.5119,lng:-0.1107,c:"history",s:"Twelfth Night's first night",q:"Middle Temple Hall Elizabethan Twelfth Night",w:"A magnificent Elizabethan hall of 1573 in the lawyers' warren of the Inns of Court, under a double hammer-beam roof, with a serving table said to be made from the timbers of Drake's Golden Hinde. Shakespeare's 'Twelfth Night' had what may be its premiere here in 1602 — performed for the barristers in the very room that still stands.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Middle_Temple_Hall_Exterior%2C_London%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/960px-Middle_Temple_Hall_Exterior%2C_London%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg"},{id:"georgetavern",n:"The George Tavern",a:"Stepney",pc:"E1 0LA",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.0445,c:"pub",s:"the gig pub that fought the wreckers",q:"George Tavern Stepney Commercial Road",w:"A battered, beautiful old coaching inn on Commercial Road that became a fierce little music and arts venue — and then spent years fighting off developers whose flats next door would have killed its noise. Shot by photographers and bands for its peeling grandeur; saved, for now, by the people who love it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"novocemetery",n:"Novo Cemetery",a:"Mile End",pc:"E1 4NS",lat:51.5235,lng:-0.04,c:"death",s:"a Sephardic field inside a university",q:"Novo Cemetery Queen Mary Mile End Sephardic",w:"A Sephardic Jewish burial ground from 1733, the resting place of London's Spanish and Portuguese community — now improbably surrounded by the campus of Queen Mary University, students crossing between worn flat ledger-stones laid in the old Iberian style. A piece of the seventeenth-century diaspora preserved among the lecture halls.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Mile_End_%E2%80%93_Nuevo_%28New%29_Jewish_Cemetery.jpg/960px-Mile_End_%E2%80%93_Nuevo_%28New%29_Jewish_Cemetery.jpg"},{id:"fleetgrating",n:"The Fleet Beneath Ray Street",a:"Clerkenwell",pc:"EC1R 3DJ",lat:51.5238,lng:-0.108,c:"oddity",s:"press your ear to a lost river",q:"Fleet River Ray Street grating Clerkenwell",w:"London's largest buried river, the Fleet, runs in a sewer beneath the streets — and on Ray Street in Clerkenwell there is a grating in the road where, if you stop and listen over the traffic, you can actually hear it rushing below. A whole river, demoted to a drain, still audible if you know where to stand.",city:"london"},{id:"floralears",n:"The Floral Street Ears",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"WC2E 9DJ",lat:51.5132,lng:-0.122,c:"oddity",s:"two ears on a Covent Garden wall",q:"Floral Street ears sculpture Tim Fishlock",w:"High on a wall in a Covent Garden alley, two small sculpted ears — cast by the artist Tim Fishlock and left to eavesdrop on the passing street. Most people walk under them for years without ever looking up. The building has been listening the whole time.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"parakeets",n:"Kensington Gardens Parakeets",a:"Bayswater",pc:"W2",lat:51.506,lng:-0.179,c:"green",s:"feral parrots in the royal park",q:"Kensington Gardens ring-necked parakeets",w:"Flocks of bright green ring-necked parakeets — escaped or released, and now thoroughly Londoner — swirl through Kensington Gardens and Hyde Park, where they'll land on an outstretched hand for seed near the Italian Gardens. A tropical absurdity gone fully native, screeching through the planes above the joggers.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"oasispool",n:"Oasis Sports Centre Pool",a:"Holborn",pc:"WC2H 9AG",lat:51.5165,lng:-0.125,c:"lido",s:"an open-air pool in the West End",q:"Oasis Sports Centre heated outdoor pool Holborn",w:"A heated outdoor swimming pool in the middle of Holborn, hiding behind Covent Garden — you swim lengths under the open sky with office towers leaning in on every side. Improbable, slightly surreal, and beloved by Londoners who like to do their morning crawl with the city watching.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Outdoor_pool_at_Oasis_Sports_Centre%2C_London.jpg/960px-Outdoor_pool_at_Oasis_Sports_Centre%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"fabric",n:"Fabric",a:"Farringdon",pc:"EC1M 3HN",lat:51.5198,lng:-0.102,c:"music",s:"the bodysonic dancefloor",q:"Fabric nightclub Farringdon Smithfield",w:"London's most storied nightclub, open since 1999 in a former Smithfield cold store — home of Room One's 'bodysonic' floor, wired so the bass comes up through your feet. It was briefly shut down in 2016 and saved by a global outcry; the all-night crowd files in past the meat market still.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Fabric_Club_London_2020.jpg/960px-Fabric_Club_London_2020.jpg"},{id:"mothclub",n:"Moth Club",a:"Hackney",pc:"E9 6DA",lat:51.5448,lng:-0.0535,c:"music",s:"gigs under a gold-glitter ceiling",q:"Moth Club Hackney Valette Street",w:"An old Memorial Working Men's Club in Hackney, its ceiling drowned in gold tinsel, that doubles as one of East London's best small gig and club rooms — sweaty, sticky-floored and gloriously incongruous, with veterans' photos on the walls and a guitar band roaring under the glitter.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Moth_Club%2C_Hackney%2C_E9.jpg/960px-Moth_Club%2C_Hackney%2C_E9.jpg"},{id:"broadwaymarket",n:"Broadway Market",a:"Hackney",pc:"E8 4QJ",lat:51.538,lng:-0.0617,c:"food",s:"the Saturday market by the canal",q:"Broadway Market Hackney London Fields",w:"A market street between London Fields and the Regent's Canal, trading since the nineteenth century and reborn as Hackney's Saturday ritual — cheese, oysters, flat whites and flowers, the towpath busy with bikes. Gentrified, certainly, but built on a genuinely old line of trade from the fields to the city.",city:"london",oh:"Sa 09:00-17:00; Su 10:00-16:00; PH off",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Hackney%2C_Broadway_Market_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1728061.jpg/960px-Hackney%2C_Broadway_Market_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1728061.jpg"},{id:"bca",n:"Black Cultural Archives",a:"Brixton",pc:"SW9 8EN",lat:51.4628,lng:-0.113,c:"archive",s:"the home of Black British history",q:"Black Cultural Archives Windrush Square Brixton",w:"The only national archive dedicated to the history of African and Caribbean people in Britain — founded in 1981 out of the Brixton uprisings, and since 2014 housed in a restored Georgian building on Windrush Square. Records, photographs and papers that no mainstream institution was keeping; a community insisting on its own memory.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bishopsgate",n:"Bishopsgate Institute",a:"Liverpool Street",pc:"EC2M 4QH",lat:51.517,lng:-0.0795,c:"archive",s:"London's radical memory bank",q:"Bishopsgate Institute library archives",w:"A free cultural institute and library opened in 1895 beside Liverpool Street — home to extraordinary collections the establishment overlooked: protest, the co-operative movement, LGBTQ+ history, London's own ephemera. An ornate Arts and Crafts reading room, an open door, and a quiet insistence that ordinary people's records matter.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kelvedonhatchbunker",n:"Kelvedon Hatch Secret Nuclear Bunker",a:"Brentwood, Essex",pc:"CM15 0LA",lat:51.6718,lng:0.2566,c:"oddity",s:"Behind an ordinary bungalow, a staircase drops 125ft into a three-storey cold-war bunker built to run Britain after a nuclear strike",q:"Kelvedon Hatch Secret Nuclear Bunker",w:"Behind an innocuous Essex bungalow, a hundred-yard tunnel and a staircase drop 125 feet into a three-storey concrete fortress sheathed in ten-foot walls — built in 1952-53 as a ROTOR radar centre, later a Regional Government HQ meant to run the south-east after a nuclear strike. It could shelter hundreds for months on its own water and power. Decommissioned in 1992, it is now a museum signposted, with magnificent cheek, \"Secret Nuclear Bunker.\"",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Entrance_to_Kelvedon_Hatch_Nuclear_Bunker.jpg/960px-Entrance_to_Kelvedon_Hatch_Nuclear_Bunker.jpg"},{id:"pinnervillage",n:"Pinner Village & Pinner Fair",a:"Pinner, Harrow",pc:"HA5 5PJ",lat:51.593,lng:-0.381,c:"history",s:"A medieval hamlet swallowed by Metro-land that still holds a royally chartered street fair, granted in 1336",q:"Pinner village Pinner Fair charter",w:"A medieval hamlet that Metro-land swallowed but never quite digested — its High Street still tilts up to the 14th-century church of St John the Baptist, consecrated in 1321. Edward III granted Pinner a fair by royal charter in 1336, and it is held to this day on the Wednesday after the spring bank holiday, dodgems clattering past the old houses. Betjeman filmed it for Metro-Land in 1973. A charter older than the Tube that brought the crowds.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dianafountain",n:"Diana Fountain",a:"",pc:"KT8",lat:51.40993,lng:-0.33621,c:"streetart",s:"public statue",q:"Diana Fountain London",w:"The Diana Fountain in Bushy Park, in the London Borough of Richmond upon Thames, England, is a seventeenth-century statue ensemble and water feature in an eighteenth-century setting with a surrounding pool and mile long tree lined vistas which honors the Roman Goddess Diana.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Diana_Fountain%2C_Bushy_Park.jpeg/960px-Diana_Fountain%2C_Bushy_Park.jpeg"},{id:"royaltankregimentmemoria",n:"Royal Tank Regiment Memorial",a:"",pc:"SW1A",lat:51.50613,lng:-0.12445,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Royal Tank Regiment Memorial London",w:"Five bronze tankmen, one and a half times life size, at Whitehall Court: hull gunner, turret gunner, commander, loader and driver of a WWII Comet. Vivien Mallock cast them from a 1953 maquette; the Queen unveiled it in 2000. A memorial to the men, the fundraisers insisted, not the machines.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Royal_Tank_Regiment_Memorial_%2835287681110_cropped%29.jpg/960px-Royal_Tank_Regiment_Memorial_%2835287681110_cropped%29.jpg"},{id:"thegurkhasoldier",n:"The Gurkha Soldier",a:"",pc:"SW1A",lat:51.50509,lng:-0.125,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"The Gurkha Soldier London",w:"The Memorial to the Brigade of Gurkhas on Horse Guards Avenue, Whitehall, London, was unveiled by Queen Elizabeth II on 3 December 1997. This was the first memorial to Gurkha soldiers in the United Kingdom, and was occasioned by transfer of their headquarters and training centre from Hong Kong to London in 1997.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"royalairforcememorial",n:"Royal Air Force Memorial",a:"",pc:"SW1A",lat:51.50399,lng:-0.12308,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Royal Air Force Memorial London",w:"A golden eagle on a globe atop a Portland stone pylon, wings raised over the Thames at Whitehall Steps. Sir William Reid Dick's bird, gilded by the Parlanti foundry, has watched the river since the Prince of Wales unveiled it in 1923. The inscription is Exodus: \"I carried you on eagle's wings.\" The RAF's own.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Royal_Air_Force_Memorial%2C_London.png/960px-Royal_Air_Force_Memorial%2C_London.png"},{id:"douglashaig",n:"Douglas Haig",a:"",pc:"SW1A",lat:51.50428,lng:-0.12624,c:"streetart",s:"public statue",q:"Douglas Haig London",w:"The statue that started a row. Alfred Hardiman's bronze of the WWI commander took eight years and drew fire throughout: critics savaged Haig's seat, his uniform, and above all the horse, a classical Renaissance charger nothing like his real mount Poperinghe. Lady Haig objected; the sculptor held firm. Unveiled 1937.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ruinedarch",n:"Ruined Arch",a:"",pc:"TW9",lat:51.47389,lng:-0.29297,c:"follies",s:"folly",q:"Ruined Arch London",w:"Folly in Kew Gardens, London. Built in 1759. Designed by William Chambers. Grade II* listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"walterdelamare",n:"Walter de la Mare",a:"",pc:"TW1",lat:51.44975,lng:-0.31643,c:"plaque",s:"commemorative plaque",q:"Walter de la Mare London",w:"Commemorative plaque in London. Inscribed: “Walter de la Mare (1873–1956) Poet lived here 1940–1956”.",city:"london"},{id:"thesassoongallery",n:"The Sassoon Gallery",a:"",pc:"SE15",lat:51.46975,lng:-0.07008,c:"museum",s:"art gallery",q:"The Sassoon Gallery London",w:"Art gallery in London. The Sassoon Gallery is behind Bar Story, and shows are open from 12-6pm.",city:"london"},{id:"stpaulscentre",n:"St Paul's Centre",a:"",pc:"EN2",lat:51.65311,lng:-0.08812,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"St Paul's Centre London",w:"Church in London.",city:"london"},{id:"standrewsunitedreformedc",n:"St Andrew's United Reformed Church",a:"",pc:"SM2",lat:51.3469,lng:-0.20982,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"St Andrew's United Reformed Church London",w:"Surprisingly modern for 1933, and that's the point. The first minister saw the British Empire Exhibition at Wembley, was struck by the pavilions, and talked their architect Maxwell Ayrton out of a traditional church and into this. Grade II listed, the interest mostly inside. A flash of ambition in suburban Sutton.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"whittonmethodistchurch",n:"Whitton Methodist Church",a:"",pc:"TW2",lat:51.44488,lng:-0.36189,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"Whitton Methodist Church London",w:"A suburban Methodist church that ran out of road. Founded 1938 on Percy Road, it lost a hall to fire in September 2015 and never quite recovered, holding its last service on 18 July 2021. No grand story, just a local congregation that gathered for eight decades and then didn't. Quietly gone.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"williambooth",n:"William Booth",a:"",pc:"E1",lat:51.52035,lng:-0.05424,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"William Booth London",w:"Here it began. The plaque is blunt: \"Here William Booth commenced the work of the Salvation Army, July 1865.\" On the rough common of Mile End Waste the preacher pitched a tent and worked the East End poor, founding a worldwide movement. The statue went up in 1979 for his 150th birthday. Ground zero of the Sally Army.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"leonardmontefioredrinkin",n:"Leonard Montefiore Drinking Fountain",a:"",pc:"E1",lat:51.51853,lng:-0.04594,c:"history",s:"memorial fountain",q:"Leonard Montefiore Drinking Fountain London",w:"A Victorian fountain for a man who barely got started. Leonard Montefiore died at 25 in 1879: friend of Oscar Wilde and Arnold Toynbee, member of the Jewish Board of Guardians, early backer of women's suffrage. Erected 1884 at a local school, moved to Stepney Green in 1939, recently restored to run again. Grade II.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stpaulsunitedreformedchu",n:"St. Paul's United Reformed Church",a:"",pc:"CR2",lat:51.36038,lng:-0.08362,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"St. Paul's United Reformed Church London",w:"A Gothic Revival church on the Croham Road corner, built around 1904 and still gathering a congregation in South Croydon. Its hall earned Grade II listing in 1983. No great drama in the record, just a solid Edwardian chapel doing the same job it always has. Honest and intact.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mertonpriorychapterhouse",n:"Merton Priory Chapter House",a:"",pc:"SW19",lat:51.41409,lng:-0.18169,c:"history",s:"archaeological site",q:"Merton Priory Chapter House London",w:"Merton Priory was an English Augustinian priory founded in 1114 by Gilbert Norman, Sheriff of Surrey under King Henry I (1100–1135). It was situated within the manor of Merton in the county of Surrey, in what is today the Colliers Wood area in the London Borough of Merton.",city:"london"},{id:"spekememorial",n:"Speke Memorial",a:"",pc:"W2",lat:51.50901,lng:-0.17917,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Speke Memorial London",w:"A red granite obelisk to the man who found the source of the Nile and didn't live to be believed. John Hanning Speke reached Lake Victoria in 1858; he died in 1864 from his own gun, an accident the day before he was to debate rival Burton. Designed by Euston's architect Philip Hardwick, raised by subscription in 1866.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stjohnschurch",n:"St John's Church",a:"",pc:"EN5",lat:51.64809,lng:-0.17819,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"St John's Church London",w:"A church shaped like a Bedouin tent. When two New Barnet congregations merged in 1963, member Jon Finlayson, barely thirty, took his first big commission: this Scandinavian-influenced building with almost no vertical walls, on the old St Augustine's foundations. Built 1969, it won a Civic Trust Award the next year.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/St_John%27s_United_Reformed_Church_04.JPG/960px-St_John%27s_United_Reformed_Church_04.JPG"},{id:"sttheodore",n:"St Theodore",a:"",pc:"TW12",lat:51.41575,lng:-0.36885,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"St Theodore London",w:"Sixty years of Mass in a wood-and-asbestos shed, then this. Hampton's Catholics worshipped in a private chapel from 1897 and a 1927 hut on Station Road before Austin Winkley's new church opened in 1987, dedicated to Theodore of Canterbury. Inside: stained glass by Patrick Reyntiens, carvings by David John.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/St_Theodore%27s_Roman_Catholic_Church%2C_Hampton_-_geograph.org.uk_-_53869.jpg"},{id:"cavalrymemorial",n:"Cavalry Memorial",a:"",pc:"SW1X",lat:51.50312,lng:-0.15591,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Cavalry Memorial London",w:"The Cavalry of the Empire Memorial: a bronze St George on horseback trampling a dragon, by the sculptor Adrian Jones (1924). It was cast from guns captured in the First World War, the horse adapted from a Dürer engraving, and moved to Hyde Park in 1961.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"kodakchimney",n:"Kodak Chimney",a:"",pc:"HA1",lat:51.59451,lng:-0.34588,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Kodak Chimney London",w:"The 65-metre (213 ft) chimney of the old Kodak power station in Wealdstone — the last landmark of Kodak's first factory outside the United States, opened here in 1891. The plant closed in 2016 but the chimney was kept.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"threebridges",n:"Three Bridges",a:"",pc:"UB2",lat:51.50438,lng:-0.35529,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Three Bridges London",w:"Brunel's last great trick. Despite the name there are two bridges stacked at Southall: Windmill Lane carries road over the Grand Union Canal, which rides a cast-iron aqueduct over the railway in its cutting. Opened 1859, the year he died; now a scheduled monument. A windmill once stood here, painted by Turner.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/The_Three_Bridges_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1183859.jpg"},{id:"stavehill",n:"Stave Hill",a:"",pc:"SE16",lat:51.50109,lng:-0.04046,c:"view",s:"viewpoint",q:"Stave Hill London",w:"A man-made hill built from the rubble of drowned docks. When the Surrey Commercial Docks were filled in the 1980s, the spoil became this 9-metre cone in Rotherhithe. At the top, Michael Rizzello's 1989 bronze relief map of the docklands fills with rainwater. Climb it for Canary Wharf, the City, Wembley on a clear day.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"broadgatevenus",n:"Broadgate Venus",a:"",pc:"EC2A",lat:51.51971,lng:-0.08046,c:"streetart",s:"public artwork",q:"Broadgate Venus London",w:"A five-tonne bronze nude by the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, reclining in Exchange Square beside Liverpool Street. Commissioned in 1989, it is one of his largest outdoor works and a study in his exaggerated 'Boterismo' style.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Broadgate_Venus_2020.jpg/960px-Broadgate_Venus_2020.jpg"},{id:"harlingtonbaptistchurch",n:"Harlington Baptist Church",a:"",pc:"UB3",lat:51.48692,lng:-0.43529,c:"faith",s:"church",q:"Harlington Baptist Church London",w:"Protestant church building in Hillingdon, London, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"peterpanstatue",n:"Peter Pan Statue",a:"",pc:"W2",lat:51.50862,lng:-0.17592,c:"streetart",s:"public sculpture",q:"Peter Pan Statue London",w:"Public sculpture in London. Dates from 1912. Work by George Frampton. Grade II* listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Peter_Pan_monument.jpg/960px-Peter_Pan_monument.jpg"},{id:"lullingstoneromanvilla",n:"Lullingstone Roman Villa",a:"",pc:"DA4",lat:51.36402,lng:0.19642,c:"roman",s:"Roman remains",q:"Lullingstone Roman Villa London",w:"Lullingstone Roman Villa is a villa built during the Roman occupation of Britain, situated in Lullingstone near the village of Eynsford in Kent, south-eastern England. The villa is located in the Darent Valley, along with six others, including those at Crofton, Crayford and Dartford.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/LullingstonVilla-Kent_Interior_May2001.jpg/960px-LullingstonVilla-Kent_Interior_May2001.jpg"},{id:"spitfirepilotmemorial",n:"Spitfire Pilot Memorial",a:"",pc:"DA4",lat:51.37812,lng:0.21605,c:"history",s:"historic landmark",q:"Spitfire Pilot Memorial London",w:"Historic landmark in London.",city:"london"},{id:"georgegreysobelisk",n:"George Grey's Obelisk",a:"",pc:"EN4",lat:51.66653,lng:-0.14253,c:"history",s:"monument",q:"George Grey's Obelisk London",w:"Monument in London. Inscribed: “To the memory of the birth of George Grey, Earl of Harold, son of Henry and Sophia, Duke and Duchess of Kent.”. Built in 1733. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"romanvilla",n:"Roman Villa",a:"",pc:"AL2",lat:51.71428,lng:-0.34101,c:"roman",s:"Roman remains",q:"Roman Villa London",w:"Roman remains in London.",city:"london"},{id:"londonbuddhistcentre",n:"London Buddhist Centre",a:"",pc:"E2",lat:51.52803,lng:-0.05125,c:"faith",s:"temple",q:"London Buddhist Centre London",w:"A Victorian fire station turned meditation hall. Built 1888 and manned until 1969, the red-brick, Grade II listed block on Roman Road was gutted by fire in the 1970s, then rebuilt by volunteers. Home to the Triratna community since the late 1970s. Bells now, not sirens.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/LondonBuddhistCentre.jpg/960px-LondonBuddhistCentre.jpg"},{id:"thetypemuseum",n:"The Type Museum",a:"",pc:"SW9",lat:51.47361,lng:-0.11558,c:"museum",s:"museum",q:"The Type Museum London",w:"The place that saved how the world set words. Susan Shaw founded it in 1992 after rescuing Monotype's hot-metal plant from the scrapyard. This Stockwell archive hoarded punches, matrices and machines that once cut type for some 300 languages. Shaw died in 2020; the collection dispersed in 2022.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"saintanneswell",n:"Saint Anne's Well",a:"",pc:"TW19",lat:51.45544,lng:-0.48199,c:"history",s:"old well",q:"Saint Anne's Well London",w:"A medieval holy well at Stanwell dedicated to St Anne — a shallow square basin with steps leading down into it, now a scheduled ancient monument.",city:"london"},{id:"egham",n:"Egham",a:"",pc:"TW20",lat:51.4289,lng:-0.5479,c:"oddity",s:"town in the borough of Runnymede in Surrey, England",q:"Egham London",w:"Egham is a town in the Borough of Runnymede in Surrey, England, approximately 19 miles (31 km) west of central London. First settled in the Bronze Age, the town was under the control of Chertsey Abbey for much of the Middle Ages.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/High_Street%2C_Egham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1499881.jpg"},{id:"shahjahanmosque",n:"Shah Jahan Mosque",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.3218,lng:-0.5475,c:"faith",s:"mosque in Woking, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Shah Jahan Mosque London",w:"The Shah Jahan Mosque (also known as Woking Mosque) on Oriental Road, Woking, England, is the first purpose-built mosque in the United Kingdom. Built in 1889, it is located 30 miles (50 km) southwest of London. It is a Grade I listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"horsellcommon",n:"Horsell Common",a:"",pc:"GU21",lat:51.3358,lng:-0.54833,c:"oddity",s:"site of Special Scientific Interest in Horsell, Surrey, Engl",q:"Horsell Common London",w:"Horsell Common is a 355-hectare (880-acre) open space in Horsell, near Woking in Surrey. It is owned and managed by the Horsell Common Preservation Society. An area of 152 hectares (380 acres) is a biological Site of Special Scientific Interest and part of the Thames Basin Heaths Special Protection Area.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Horsell_Common_-_geograph.org.uk_-_166262.jpg"},{id:"mclarentechnologycentre",n:"McLaren Technology Centre",a:"",pc:"GU21",lat:51.34583,lng:-0.54833,c:"oddity",s:"headquarters of the McLaren Group",q:"McLaren Technology Centre London",w:"The McLaren Technology Centre (MTC) is the headquarters of the McLaren Group and its subsidiaries, located on a 500,000 m2 (50 ha) site in Woking, Surrey, England.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/McLaren_Technology_Centre_geograph-4478964-by-Alan-Hunt.jpg/960px-McLaren_Technology_Centre_geograph-4478964-by-Alan-Hunt.jpg"},{id:"muslimburialgroundhorsel",n:"Muslim Burial Ground, Horsell Common",a:"",pc:"GU21",lat:51.3291,lng:-0.54203,c:"death",s:"former cemetery and current peace garden in Woking, Surrey, ",q:"Muslim Burial Ground, Horsell Common London",w:"The Muslim Burial Ground, in the town of Woking in the English county of Surrey, was the original resting place of two dozen Muslim soldiers who died during World War I and World War II. It is now a peace garden dedicated to all the Muslim soldiers of the British Indian Army who died during both wars.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Muslim_Burial_Ground_Peace_Garden_%28geograph_5683871%29.jpg/960px-Muslim_Burial_Ground_Peace_Garden_%28geograph_5683871%29.jpg"},{id:"wokinghundred",n:"Woking Hundred",a:"",pc:"GU4",lat:51.27,lng:-0.55,c:"oddity",s:"historical division of Surrey, England",q:"Woking Hundred London",w:"See Woking for the town or Borough of Woking for the district. Woking was a hundred in what is now Surrey, England. It includes the town of Woking and the Borough of Woking.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Wanborough_barn2_20060909.jpg/960px-Wanborough_barn2_20060909.jpg"},{id:"greatfosters",n:"Great Fosters",a:"",pc:"TW20",lat:51.416,lng:-0.545,c:"history",s:"grade I listed country house in Egham, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Great Fosters London",w:"Great Fosters is a 16th-century mansion which originally lay within Windsor Great Park and is still adjacent to the town of Egham, Surrey, England. It is a Grade I listed building, close to Heathrow and the M25 London orbital motorway.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Great_Fosters_-_geograph.org.uk_-_143091.jpg"},{id:"stdunstanschurchwoking",n:"St Dunstan's Church, Woking",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.3192,lng:-0.5453,c:"faith",s:"church in Surrey, United Kingdom",q:"St Dunstan's Church, Woking London",w:"St Dunstan's Church is a Roman Catholic Parish church in Woking, Surrey. At first it was built in 1899, replaced by a larger church in 1923 and its final form was built in 2008. The church was dedicated that year by the Bishop of Arundel and Brighton and Cardinal Cormac Murphy-O'Connor.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/St_Dunstan%27s_Church%2C_Shaftesbury_Road%2C_Maybury%2C_Woking_%28June_2015%29_%283%29.jpg/960px-St_Dunstan%27s_Church%2C_Shaftesbury_Road%2C_Maybury%2C_Woking_%28June_2015%29_%283%29.jpg"},{id:"hoeplace",n:"Hoe Place",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.3081,lng:-0.53695,c:"oddity",s:"building in Woking, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Hoe Place London",w:"Building in Woking, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II* listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"bellbarrowanddiscbarrowo",n:"Bell barrow and disc barrow on Horsell Common",a:"",pc:"GU21",lat:51.3283,lng:-0.54551,c:"history",s:"disc barrow in Woking, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Bell barrow and disc barrow on Horsell Common London",w:"Disc barrow in Woking, Surrey, England, UK. Scheduled monument.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"bellbarrowonhorsellcommo",n:"Bell barrow on Horsell Common immediately east of Monument Road",a:"",pc:"GU21",lat:51.328,lng:-0.54291,c:"history",s:"bell barrow in Woking, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Bell barrow on Horsell Common immediately east of Monument Road London",w:"Bell barrow in Woking, Surrey, England, UK. Scheduled monument.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Muslim_Burial_Ground_Peace_Garden_%28geograph_5683871%29.jpg/960px-Muslim_Burial_Ground_Peace_Garden_%28geograph_5683871%29.jpg"},{id:"royaldramaticcollege",n:"Royal Dramatic College",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.32333,lng:-0.54306,c:"oddity",s:"former retirement home for actors in England",q:"Royal Dramatic College London",w:"The Royal Dramatic College was a home for retired actors in Woking, England; it was opened by the Prince of Wales (later Edward VII) in 1865, and closed because of financial difficulty in 1877.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"hoestream",n:"Hoe Stream",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.31007,lng:-0.54498,c:"oddity",s:"watercourse in the United Kingdom",q:"Hoe Stream London",w:"Watercourse in the United Kingdom.",city:"london"},{id:"memorialstoneforsiteofro",n:"Memorial Stone For Site Of Roman Road",a:"",pc:"TW20",lat:51.41959,lng:-0.54365,c:"history",s:"building in Runnymede, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Memorial Stone For Site Of Roman Road London",w:"Building in Runnymede, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"westendfarm",n:"West End Farm",a:"",pc:"TW20",lat:51.40955,lng:-0.53969,c:"oddity",s:"building in Runnymede, Surrey, England, UK",q:"West End Farm London",w:"Building in Runnymede, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"sendholme",n:"Sendholme",a:"",pc:"GU23",lat:51.28353,lng:-0.54232,c:"oddity",s:"building in Send, Guildford, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Sendholme London",w:"Building in Send, Guildford, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"hazlewood",n:"Hazlewood",a:"",pc:"TW20",lat:51.40681,lng:-0.53691,c:"oddity",s:"building in Runnymede, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Hazlewood London",w:"Building in Runnymede, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"ashwood",n:"Ashwood",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.31266,lng:-0.54914,c:"history",s:"listed Building in Woking, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Ashwood London",w:"Listed Building in Woking, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"greenways",n:"Greenways",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.31423,lng:-0.54579,c:"oddity",s:"building in Woking, Surrey, England, UK",q:"Greenways London",w:"Building in Woking, Surrey, England, UK. Grade II listed building.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"thorpeinterchange",n:"Thorpe Interchange",a:"",pc:"KT16",lat:51.40076,lng:-0.53746,c:"oddity",s:"road junction",q:"Thorpe Interchange London",w:"The Thorpe Interchange is a large motorway free-flow intersection in Surrey, United Kingdom, connecting the M3 with the M25.",city:"london",wq:"r"},{id:"gaumontwoking",n:"Gaumont Woking",a:"",pc:"GU21",lat:51.33216,lng:-0.54653,c:"cinema",s:"former cinema in Woking, England",q:"Gaumont Woking London",w:"A former Woking cinema that traded as the Gaumont from 1950 until it closed in 1959 — earlier the Central Cinema and then the Plaza — built in the Art Deco style. It was later demolished.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"whiteroselane",n:"White Rose Lane",a:"",pc:"GU22",lat:51.309,lng:-0.544,c:"green",s:"park",q:"White Rose Lane London",w:"White Rose Lane is a 3.4-hectare (8.4-acre) Local Nature Reserve in Woking in Surrey. It is owned and managed by Woking Borough Council. This site is damp alder woodland in two areas on the north bank of the Hoe Stream. There are several species of rare fungi and fauna include owls, bats, deer and frogs.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/White_Rose_Lane_Local_Nature_Reserve_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1185018.jpg"},{id:"thetallyho",n:"The Tally Ho",a:"",pc:"N12",lat:51.6141,lng:-0.17656,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Tally Ho London",w:"The landmark on the corner that named the corner. This big 1927 Charrington pub sits on a wedge where Ballards Lane meets the High Road, the apex of North Finchley. Tally Ho Corner was a tram, then trolleybus, terminus; the coaching-company name behind it may never have existed at all.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/The_Tally_Ho_pub_north_Finchley_Oct_2019.jpg/960px-The_Tally_Ho_pub_north_Finchley_Oct_2019.jpg"},{id:"thecheshirecheese2",n:"The Cheshire Cheese",a:"",pc:"WC2R",lat:51.51239,lng:-0.11318,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Cheshire Cheese London",w:"Mock-Tudor done by a master. There's been a tavern off Essex Street since at least the 1790s, but this building was rebuilt in 1928 by T.H. Nowell Parr, the noted pub architect, for the Style & Winch brewery, all brewers' Tudor inside and out. That theatrical reworking is why it's Grade II listed.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Cheshire_Cheese%2C_Strand%2C_WC2_%284497852235%29.jpg/960px-Cheshire_Cheese%2C_Strand%2C_WC2_%284497852235%29.jpg"},{id:"thecrown",n:"The Crown",a:"",pc:"UB8",lat:51.53102,lng:-0.48205,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Crown London",w:"The Crown is a Grade II listed public house at High Street, Cowley, London. It dates from the 16th century.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Su 12:00-22:00",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/London_Borough_of_Hillingdon_-_The_Crown_-_20240923042208.jpg/960px-London_Borough_of_Hillingdon_-_The_Crown_-_20240923042208.jpg"},{id:"queenshead",n:"Queen's Head",a:"",pc:"UB8",lat:51.54594,lng:-0.47998,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"Queen's Head London",w:"The Queen's Head is a public house at 54 Windsor Street, Uxbridge, London. The Grade II listed building is constructed of brick and dates from the early-mid 19th century.",city:"london",wq:"r",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/The_Queen%27s_Head_pub_Uxbridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_860679.jpg"},{id:"thewonder",n:"The Wonder",a:"",pc:"EN2",lat:51.66308,lng:-0.08541,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Wonder London",w:"Named after a stagecoach that beat the clock. The Wonder ran London to Shrewsbury in under sixteen hours; the Enfield pub took its name and has been a McMullen's house since 1877. Fittings from a 1929 refit, plus a dead off-sales hatch, put its interior on the historic-pub inventory.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 11:00-23:00; Su 12:00-23:00; Fr,Sa 11:00-24:00",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/The_Wonder_pub%2C_Enfield_01.jpg/960px-The_Wonder_pub%2C_Enfield_01.jpg"},{id:"thepiedbull",n:"The Pied Bull",a:"",pc:"EN2",lat:51.67817,lng:-0.05936,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Pied Bull London",w:"Where James I's hunting dogs are said to have slept. This low-beamed, weatherboarded Grade II house at Bull's Cross looks C17 or older, and the story goes it began as the king's seventeenth-century kennels rather than a tavern. A long way north of town, out where Enfield Chase once ran.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thekingandtinker",n:"The King and Tinker",a:"",pc:"EN2",lat:51.68136,lng:-0.0771,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The King and Tinker London",w:"A pub built on a ballad. The story goes James I, hunting and lost from his court, drank here with a tinker who never knew he shared ale with the king, until the courtiers arrived and the king knighted him. Little proves it, but the old Whitewebbs alehouse took the legend's name around 1814.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"roseandcrown2",n:"Rose and Crown",a:"",pc:"EN2",lat:51.67121,lng:-0.08378,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"Rose and Crown London",w:"The highwayman's bolt-hole, so the story goes. This Grade II house on Clay Hill is said to date to around 1706 and to have belonged to Dick Turpin's grandparents, with Turpin holing up here between robberies. Legend points to an iron ring where he tied Black Bess. Hard to prove, fun to tell.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Sa 12:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Rose_and_Crown_Public_House%2C_Clay_Hill%2C_Enfield_-_geograph.org.uk_-_371058.jpg"},{id:"no8sevensisters",n:"No.8 Seven Sisters",a:"",pc:"N15",lat:51.57836,lng:-0.0819,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"No.8 Seven Sisters London",w:"A big local pub on Seven Sisters Road in South Tottenham, formerly the Woodberry Tavern; since 2014 it has doubled as a hostel.",city:"london"},{id:"theshanakee",n:"The Shanakee",a:"",pc:"W5",lat:51.51484,lng:-0.30266,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Shanakee London",w:"An Irish pub opposite Ealing Broadway station — formerly Doherty’s — long a hub of Irish Ealing, showing the GAA on the big screen with live music at weekends.",city:"london"},{id:"thestartavern",n:"The Star Tavern",a:"",pc:"SW1X",lat:51.49844,lng:-0.15585,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Star Tavern London",w:"The mews pub where the Great Train Robbery was hatched. In 1963 Bruce Reynolds and his gang plotted Britain's most audacious heist upstairs here, timetables passed around, never more than four men at once. The job took 2.6 million pounds off a mail train. A genteel local down Belgrave Mews West.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Fr 11:00-23:00; Sa 12:00-23:00; Su 12:00-22:30",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Star_Tavern%2C_Belgravia%2C_SW1_%284588158213%29.jpg/960px-Star_Tavern%2C_Belgravia%2C_SW1_%284588158213%29.jpg"},{id:"thebayswaterarms",n:"The Bayswater Arms",a:"",pc:"W2",lat:51.51264,lng:-0.18779,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Bayswater Arms London",w:"A corner pub where Queensway meets Moscow Road in Bayswater, its faux-Victorian rooms hung with old photographs of the Edwardian neighbourhood.",city:"london"},{id:"thelordpalmerston",n:"The Lord Palmerston",a:"",pc:"SM5",lat:51.3689,lng:-0.16198,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Lord Palmerston London",w:"A Carshalton pub on Mill Lane, sold to the brewers Mann, Crossman & Paulin in 1911 and later run by Courage. It has long been known simply as 'The Palmerston'.",city:"london"},{id:"yeoldeswan",n:"Ye Olde Swan",a:"",pc:"KT7",lat:51.39292,lng:-0.33175,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"Ye Olde Swan London",w:"A riverside inn old enough to claim Henry VIII. This Grade II Swan, two early-18th-century houses linked into one pub, backs straight onto the Thames at Thames Ditton, and the story goes its roots reach to the 13th century. Theodore Hook rhymed it as a snug inn in sweet Ditton. Pints by the water.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thekingharold",n:"The King Harold",a:"",pc:"RM3",lat:51.59409,lng:0.23513,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The King Harold London",w:"A pub on Nazeing Common named for the legend that King Harold himself stopped here for an ale; today a refurbished village gastropub.",city:"london"},{id:"thegrasshopperinn",n:"The Grasshopper Inn",a:"",pc:"TN16",lat:51.2619,lng:0.04671,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Grasshopper Inn London",w:"A Westerham coaching inn said to date back to 1271, built to house the masons working on the parish church and named for the grasshopper in the Gresham family crest. It was once the boarding point for a daily coach to Fleet Street, and its timber-framed core is 16th-century or older.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thesirjulianhuxley",n:"The Sir Julian Huxley",a:"",pc:"CR2",lat:51.34344,lng:-0.06246,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Sir Julian Huxley London",w:"A Wetherspoon named for the man who saved the woods next door. Sir Julian Huxley, biologist and first Director-General of UNESCO, was a key backer of Selsdon Wood Nature Reserve, opened 1936 and now National Trust land. The Addington Road pub borrows his name and his green credentials.",city:"london",oh:"Mo-Th 08:00-24:00; Fr-Sa 08:00-01:00; Su 08:00-24:00",wq:"a"},{id:"yegeorgeinn",n:"Ye George Inn",a:"",pc:"BR3",lat:51.40724,lng:-0.02765,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"Ye George Inn London",w:"The oldest building on Beckenham High Street — a weatherboarded 17th-century coaching inn, the last stop on the Dover road before London. Charles Darwin sat here as a visiting magistrate, and in the 1860s it became the first building in Beckenham lit by gas. Grade II listed, barely changed in 350 years.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"theswanandmitre",n:"The Swan and Mitre",a:"",pc:"BR1",lat:51.40709,lng:0.01164,c:"pub",s:"historic pub",q:"The Swan and Mitre London",w:"A coaching inn with a loft full of abandoned crutches. One of the three inns Bromley's prosperity rested on, it served carters hauling produce to London; an 1855 landlord reputedly found a hoard of crutches left by visitors cured at Dr Scott's. Stables from the early 1700s; skittles added 1852.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thepanyerboy",n:"The Panyer Boy",a:"St Paul's, City of London",pc:"",lat:51.5148,lng:-0.0978,c:"history",s:"A 1688 stone of a naked boy on a bread basket, marking what was once 'the highest ground' in the City.",q:"The Panyer Boy London",w:"A naked baker's boy carved in stone in 1688, perched on an upturned bread basket in a wall by St Paul's station — once the heart of the City's bread trade on Panyer Alley. His verse brags 'still this is the highest Ground,' though Cornhill quietly beats it. Bank mascot, Blitz survivor, reset here in 1964.",city:"london"},{id:"strandlaneromanbath",n:"Strand Lane 'Roman' Bath",a:"Strand / Aldwych",pc:"",lat:51.51159,lng:-0.1156,c:"roman",s:"A 'Roman' plunge bath that is really a Jacobean cistern, glimpsed through a window down a hidden lane off the Strand.",q:"Strand Lane 'Roman' Bath London",w:"A 'Roman' plunge bath down a hidden lane off the Strand — except it isn't Roman at all, but a 1612 cistern that once fed a fountain in old Somerset House's gardens. The Roman tale took hold in 1838 and stuck, helped along by David Copperfield taking his cold dips here. Peer through the window; the National Trust holds the key.",city:"london"},{id:"theoldcuriosityshop",n:"The Old Curiosity Shop",a:"Holborn / Clare Market",pc:"",lat:51.5145,lng:-0.1178,c:"literary",s:"A crooked 16th-century timber shop, said to be London's oldest, that borrowed its name from a Dickens novel.",q:"The Old Curiosity Shop London",w:"A crooked timber shop of around 1567 behind the LSE, built from old ship's timbers and through both the Great Fire and the Blitz — often called the oldest shop in central London. 'Immortalised by Charles Dickens,' says the sign, but a Victorian owner painted that on; his real shop stood elsewhere. A lovely lie, beautifully kept.",city:"london"},{id:"stdunstaninthewestclock",n:"St Dunstan-in-the-West Clock",a:"Fleet Street",pc:"",lat:51.51425,lng:-0.11022,c:"oddity",s:"Two club-wielding giants strike the hour on London's first minute-hand clock, watched over by the statues of King Lud.",q:"St Dunstan-in-the-West Clock London",w:"Two club-wielding giants — Gog and Magog, by legend the City's guardians — strike the hours on this 1671 Fleet Street clock, said to be London's first with a minute hand. Above stands Elizabeth I, carved 1586; below, three figures of King Lud and his sons from the old Ludgate. The church dodged the Great Fire by a few doors.",city:"london"},{id:"hoxtonstreetmonstersuppl",n:"Hoxton Street Monster Supplies",a:"Hoxton",pc:"",lat:51.5314,lng:-0.0803,c:"oddity",s:"A deadpan shop 'purveying since 1818' tinned fear and brain jam to the living, dead and undead.",q:"Hoxton Street Monster Supplies London",w:"Purveyors to London's vampires, werewolves and ghosts since 1818 — or so the straight-faced fiction goes. The shelves hold Tinned Fear, Cubed Earwax and Salt Made From Tears of Anger, and every penny funds the Ministry of Stories, a children's writing charity hidden behind the shop. It opened to humans, at least, in 2010.",city:"london"},{id:"caledonianparkclocktower",n:"Caledonian Park Clock Tower",a:"Barnsbury / Holloway",pc:"",lat:51.5471,lng:-0.1236,c:"view",s:"A 46m Italianate tower stranded in a park, the last relic of London's vast Victorian cattle market.",q:"Caledonian Park Clock Tower London",w:"A 46-metre Italianate tower marooned in a north-London park — the last real relic of the Metropolitan Cattle Market that Prince Albert opened in 1855 to take the livestock trade off Smithfield. The drovers and beasts are long gone; the tower, restored, now lets you climb it for the view. A campanile for cattle.",city:"london"},{id:"girothedogsgrave",n:"Giro the Dog's Grave",a:"St James's / Carlton House Terrace",pc:"",lat:51.5063,lng:-0.1305,c:"death",s:"A tiny tombstone behind the railings off the Mall marks the dog of a Weimar ambassador — wrongly dubbed 'London's Nazi memorial'.",q:"Giro the Dog's Grave London",w:"Behind the railings off the Mall, by the old German Embassy, a tiny stone marks Giro — ambassador von Hoesch's dog, electrocuted in 1934 after biting through a garden cable. 'Ein treuer Begleiter,' it reads: a faithful companion. Guidebooks love to call it London's Nazi memorial; it isn't — von Hoesch served Weimar, not Hitler.",city:"london"},{id:"lcornelissenandson",n:"L. Cornelissen & Son",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.5175,lng:-0.129,c:"history",s:"A jewel-box Victorian artists' colourman, all dark wood and jars of raw pigment, opposite the British Museum.",q:"L. Cornelissen & Son London",w:"A jewel-box of an artists' colourman opposite the British Museum, all dark wood and apothecary jars of raw pigment, founded in 1855. It still grinds and sells the old materials — pigments, gold leaf, sable brushes — behind a period shopfront. The family line ended in 1977, the shop nearly with it, until a 1979 rescue. Pigment by the jar.",city:"london"},{id:"syonhouse",n:"Syon House",a:"Syon Park, Brentford / Isleworth",pc:"",lat:51.4768,lng:-0.3115,c:"history",s:"The last great ducal house in Greater London still in family hands, hiding Robert Adam's first masterpiece of interiors behind a plain battlemented box.",q:"Syon House London",w:"The last great ducal house in London still in family hands, hiding Robert Adam's first masterpiece behind a plain battlemented box. Built on a dissolved 1431 abbey, Percy seat since 1594. Catherine Howard was held here; the story goes Henry VIII's coffin burst overnight at Syon, dogs found licking the remains by morning.",city:"london"},{id:"thegreatconservatorysyon",n:"The Great Conservatory, Syon Park",a:"Syon Park, Brentford",pc:"",lat:51.4787,lng:-0.309,c:"follies",s:"A sweeping glasshouse with a soaring central dome, claimed as the first large-scale building of metal and glass, predating the Crystal Palace by a generation.",q:"The Great Conservatory, Syon Park London",w:"A sweeping dome of gunmetal and glass, finished 1827 -- claimed as the first large-scale metal-and-glass building, a generation before the Crystal Palace it's said to have inspired. Charles Fowler curved its wings out to two pavilions around the Dukes' exotics. Sixty feet of Bath stone and glazing, still standing among the lake and lawns.",city:"london"},{id:"lotsait",n:"Lot's Ait",a:"Brentford, River Thames",pc:"",lat:51.4847,lng:-0.2983,c:"maritime",s:"A willow-grown river island midstream off Brentford, once a barge yard, then a feral wilderness of rotting boats, now a working boatyard again.",q:"Lot's Ait London",w:"A willow island midstream off Brentford -- osier beds and barbel fishing, then a barge yard working 150 men, then three decades of feral wilderness and rotting hulls. In 2012 the Dahlia footbridge reached across and boat-building restarted. Its neighbour Brentford Ait was planted up to screen the gasworks from Kew.",city:"london"},{id:"watermanspark",n:"Watermans Park",a:"High Street, Brentford",pc:"",lat:51.4856,lng:-0.2986,c:"green",s:"A green riverside terrace facing Kew Gardens across the Thames, laid out on the cleared ground of the old Brentford gasworks.",q:"Watermans Park London",w:"A green riverside terrace facing Kew across the Thames, laid on the cleared ground of the old Brentford gasworks, where coal barges once unloaded under the gasholders. The 1984 arts centre opened with Ravi Shankar on sitar and ran cinema and theatre until 2024. The moorings and the long view to the Gardens remain.",city:"london"},{id:"thebrentfordmonument",n:"The Brentford Monument",a:"High Street, Brentford",pc:"",lat:51.487,lng:-0.3082,c:"history",s:"A squat granite pillar that boldly claims four of the most pivotal moments in English history all happened at this one Thames ford.",q:"The Brentford Monument London",w:"A squat granite pillar that boldly pins four turning points of English history to one Thames ford: Caesar's crossing in 54 BC, Offa's church council of 781, Ironside routing Cnut in 1016, and the 1642 battle. Cut partly from the old Brentford Bridge. The Caesar claim, palisades and all, stays firmly in the realm of legend.",city:"london"},{id:"stlawrenceschurchformer",n:"St Lawrence's Church (former)",a:"High Street, New Brentford",pc:"",lat:51.4866,lng:-0.309,c:"faith",s:"A medieval Kentish-rag tower marooned beside a Georgian brick body, derelict for over half a century and the oldest standing structure in Brentford.",q:"St Lawrence's Church (former) London",w:"A medieval Kentish-rag tower marooned beside a Georgian brick body -- Brentford's oldest standing structure, derelict for over half a century. The 15th-century Great West Tower went up during the Wars of the Roses; the church was rebuilt in brown brick in 1764, closed in 1961, and now sits on the Heritage at Risk register.",city:"london"},{id:"thameslockbrentford",n:"Thames Lock, Brentford",a:"Brentford Dock, Grand Union Canal",pc:"",lat:51.4831,lng:-0.3098,c:"canals",s:"West London's tidal gateway where the Grand Union Canal finally meets the Thames, the bottleneck through which 150 years of cargo squeezed in and out.",q:"Thames Lock, Brentford London",w:"West London's tidal gateway, where the Grand Union Canal finally drops into the Thames -- the bottleneck 150 years of cargo squeezed through. Lock 101, first cut in 1805, mechanised in 1962, still tidal enough that passages must be booked. Just upstream the double Gauging Lock kept a 1911 toll house where boats were measured.",city:"london"},{id:"theviewfromrichmondhill",n:"The View from Richmond Hill",a:"Richmond Hill / Terrace Walk",pc:"",lat:51.453,lng:-0.2978,c:"view",s:"The only view in England protected by its own Act of Parliament — the meadow loop of the Thames that Turner and Reynolds painted, deliberately frozen in time.",q:"The View from Richmond Hill London",w:"The only view in England guarded by its own Act of Parliament -- the meadow loop of the Thames that Turner and Reynolds painted, frozen by the 1902 Act after a housing estate threatened the foreground. The Terrace Walk was laid out around 1700; on a clear day Windsor Castle shows on the horizon. A landscape kept deliberately still.",city:"london"},{id:"marblehillhouse",n:"Marble Hill House",a:"Marble Hill, Twickenham",pc:"",lat:51.4494,lng:-0.3133,c:"history",s:"A gleaming white Palladian villa built in the 1720s for a king's mistress — and a rare Georgian house designed for, and largely by, a woman.",q:"Marble Hill House London",w:"A gleaming white Palladian villa of the 1720s, built as a Thames retreat for Henrietta Howard, mistress of George II -- and rare for a grand Georgian house designed for and partly by a woman. Pope and Bridgeman shaped the grounds; the same 1902 Act that saved the Richmond view rescued its setting. Free to walk into, still.",city:"london"},{id:"richmondpalacegatehouseo",n:"Richmond Palace Gate House (Old Palace)",a:"Old Palace Yard, Richmond Green",pc:"",lat:51.4612,lng:-0.3089,c:"history",s:"A Tudor gatehouse on Richmond Green still bearing Henry VII's restored coat of arms — the surviving doorway to the vanished palace where Elizabeth I died.",q:"Richmond Palace Gate House (Old Palace) London",w:"A Tudor gatehouse on Richmond Green, still carrying Henry VII's restored arms above the arch -- one of the few survivors of the vanished palace where Elizabeth I died. Henry rebuilt the manor after a 1497 fire and renamed it for his earldom around 1501. Parliament sold the rest in 1649 for building stone; the street names map what's gone.",city:"london"},{id:"richmondtheatre",n:"Richmond Theatre",a:"Little Green, Richmond",pc:"",lat:51.462,lng:-0.3038,c:"history",s:"Frank Matcham's red-brick-and-terracotta jewel of 1899, reputed to be one of England's most haunted theatres.",q:"Richmond Theatre London",w:"Frank Matcham's red-brick-and-terracotta jewel of 1899, among the most completely preserved of his interiors left in London -- and, by long report, one of England's most haunted theatres. Staff tell of shifting boxes, scratch marks, lights flicking on alone. It opened with 'As You Like It' beside Richmond Green.",city:"london"},{id:"thewhitecross",n:"The White Cross",a:"Riverside, off Water Lane, Richmond",pc:"",lat:51.459,lng:-0.3083,c:"pub",s:"The Thames-side pub that floods on a high spring tide — and keeps a working fireplace beneath a window, one of only a handful left in the country.",q:"The White Cross London",w:"The Richmond riverside pub that floods on a high spring tide -- it keeps wellington boots for drinkers when the forecourt goes under. Its listing singles out a working fireplace set, oddly, beneath a window, one of a handful left in use in Britain. Local lore puts a Franciscan friary's remains in the cellars; treat that as pub legend.",city:"london"},{id:"royalstarandgarterhome",n:"Royal Star and Garter Home",a:"Richmond Hill",pc:"",lat:51.4502,lng:-0.2974,c:"history",s:"A vast neoclassical pile at the top of Richmond Hill, built to nurse the most severely disabled soldiers of the First World War.",q:"Royal Star and Garter Home London",w:"A vast neoclassical pile crowning Richmond Hill, raised 1921-24 to nurse the most severely disabled soldiers of the Great War. The Red Cross bought the old Star and Garter hotel for the purpose in 1915; George V opened the present building in 1924 as the women of the Empire's war memorial. Now carved into private flats, the name kept.",city:"london"},{id:"petershamnurseries",n:"Petersham Nurseries",a:"Church Lane, Petersham",pc:"",lat:51.4471,lng:-0.3023,c:"green",s:"A ramshackle Eden of glasshouses and trailing plants down a Petersham lane, where the café once held a Michelin star among the terracotta pots.",q:"Petersham Nurseries London",w:"A ramshackle Eden of glasshouses and trailing plants down a Petersham lane, where the greenhouse cafe won a Michelin star in 2011 among the terracotta pots. The Bogliones carved it from the grounds of Petersham House and reopened it in 2004, swapping plastic for clay and coir. Reached down the same lane as St Peter's church.",city:"london"},{id:"thegreatpagodakew",n:"The Great Pagoda, Kew",a:"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew",pc:"",lat:51.4713,lng:-0.2957,c:"follies",s:"A 163-foot Chinese pagoda rising from a Surrey botanic garden, its eaves once guarded by eighty gilded dragons that vanished for two centuries.",q:"The Great Pagoda, Kew London",w:"A 163-foot Chinese pagoda rising over a botanic garden, its eaves once guarded by eighty gilded dragons that vanished for 230 years. Chambers built it in 1762 as a surprise for Princess Augusta; the rotting dragons came down in 1784, and only 72 returned in 2018. The story that George IV pawned the gold ones stays a story.",city:"london"},{id:"stanneschurchkewgreen",n:"St Anne's Church, Kew Green",a:"Kew Green, Kew",pc:"",lat:51.4838,lng:-0.2879,c:"faith",s:"The neat Georgian church presiding over Kew Green, in whose raised churchyard two founding Royal Academicians lie a few yards apart.",q:"St Anne's Church, Kew Green London",w:"The neat Georgian church presiding over Kew Green, in whose raised churchyard two founding Royal Academicians lie yards apart: Gainsborough, who asked to rest near his friend Kirby, and Zoffany. Founded on Queen Anne's grant in 1714 and enlarged at the expense of George III, who worshipped here from Kew Palace nearby.",city:"london"},{id:"theoriginalmaidsofhonour",n:"The Original Maids of Honour",a:"288 Kew Road, Kew",pc:"",lat:51.4807,lng:-0.2883,c:"bakery",s:"A Kew tea room baking a curd tart whose recipe is supposedly so prized that Henry VIII locked it away at Richmond Palace.",q:"The Original Maids of Honour London",w:"A Kew tea room baking a curd tart so prized, the story runs, that Henry VIII locked the recipe away at Richmond Palace after catching his maids of honour eating it. The Newens family have kept their puff-pastry version secret since at least 1957, reputedly in a locked box, on the same Kew Road site for over 150 years.",city:"london"},{id:"thebullsheadstrandontheg",n:"The Bull's Head, Strand-on-the-Green",a:"Strand-on-the-Green, Chiswick",pc:"",lat:51.4844,lng:-0.2782,c:"pub",s:"A riverside pub whose frontage notice insists Oliver Cromwell escaped through a tunnel to the island now bearing his name.",q:"The Bull's Head, Strand-on-the-Green London",w:"A white-painted riverside warren at Strand-on-the-Green whose frontage notice swears Oliver Cromwell slipped out through a tunnel to the island now bearing his name. Licensed by 1722, a maze of little rooms. Donald Pleasence lived at the end of the terrace, blue-plaqued. The Cromwell tunnel, historians shrug, is folklore.",city:"london"},{id:"thecitybarge",n:"The City Barge",a:"Strand-on-the-Green, Chiswick",pc:"",lat:51.485,lng:-0.2791,c:"pub",s:"A medieval Thames-side tavern with a watertight bulkhead door against the tide, named for the Lord Mayor's barge once moored at its steps and a fleeting star of a Beatles film.",q:"The City Barge London",w:"A Thames-side tavern traced to 1484, named for the Lord Mayor's ceremonial barge once moored at its steps -- and fitted with a ship's steel bulkhead door against a tide that rises seven metres at the wall. The old riverside bar floods without it. The Beatles filmed part of 'Help!' here at Strand-on-the-Green in 1965.",city:"london"},{id:"thelondonapprentice",n:"The London Apprentice",a:"Church Street, Old Isleworth",pc:"",lat:51.4711,lng:-0.3209,c:"pub",s:"A grand riverside inn at Isleworth Stairs, named for City apprentices who rowed upstream on their day off, with a smugglers' tunnel said to run to the church next door.",q:"The London Apprentice London",w:"A grand riverside inn at Isleworth Stairs, named for the City apprentices who rowed up the Thames on their day off. Licensed by 1731, it overlooks the ferry steps that once linked Richmond Palace to the north bank. The long-told legend runs a smugglers' tunnel from the cellars to All Saints next door -- or just a good story.",city:"london"},{id:"gunnersburyparkandmuseum",n:"Gunnersbury Park & Museum",a:"Popes Lane, Gunnersbury / Acton",pc:"",lat:51.4934,lng:-0.2867,c:"museum",s:"A Regency mansion that was Princess Amelia's pleasure ground and then the Rothschilds' suburban seat, its grounds still scattered with temple, orangery and boating pond.",q:"Gunnersbury Park & Museum London",w:"A Regency mansion that was Princess Amelia's pleasure ground, then the Rothschilds' suburban seat, its grounds still scattered with a Chambers temple, an orangery and a boating pond. Amelia's parties and intrigues ran until 1786; the Rothschilds reunited the estate by 1889. Ealing and Acton bought the 200 acres for the public in 1925.",city:"london"},{id:"thehivekew",n:"The Hive, Kew",a:"Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew",pc:"",lat:51.481,lng:-0.2901,c:"oddity",s:"A 17-metre aluminium lattice in a wildflower meadow that glows and hums in real time to the activity of a living Kew beehive.",q:"The Hive, Kew London",w:"A 17-metre aluminium lattice standing in a Kew wildflower meadow, glowing and humming in real time to a living beehive -- a thousand LEDs and a soundtrack pitched in C, driven by vibration data from the bees. Wolfgang Buttress built it from 170,000 parts as the UK's gold-winning pavilion at Milan's 2015 Expo, re-raised here in 2016.",city:"london"},{id:"goldsmithsalmshouses",n:"Goldsmiths' Almshouses",a:"East Churchfield Road, Acton, W3",pc:"",lat:51.5093,lng:-0.2597,c:"almshouses",s:"A serene stuccoed quadrangle of charity homes built by the Goldsmiths' Company in 1811 on land an Acton goldsmith left them in 1657.",q:"Goldsmiths' Almshouses London",w:"A serene stuccoed quadrangle of charity homes, raised by the Goldsmiths' Company in 1811 on land an Acton goldsmith willed them back in 1657. Three brown-brick ranges close round a court behind an iron screen; over the porch sit a clock and two staffs of Mercury, the company's emblem. Twelve homes at first, eight more by 1838.",city:"london"},{id:"soapsudsislandsouthacton",n:"Soapsuds Island (South Acton)",a:"South Acton, W3",pc:"",lat:51.4996,lng:-0.2725,c:"history",s:"Victorian South Acton was nicknamed 'Soapsuds Island' — the washhouse of London, where thousands of hand-launderers scrubbed the linen of West End hotels and the rich.",q:"Soapsuds Island (South Acton) London",w:"Victorian South Acton went by a nickname: Soapsuds Island, the washhouse of London. Soft local water drew over 170 hand-laundries by 1890, and the 1901 census counted nearly 2,500 women scrubbing the linen of West End hotels. The last closed in the 1970s; the crowded streets were swept away for west London's largest estate.",city:"london"},{id:"twyfordabbey",n:"Twyford Abbey",a:"Park Royal, NW10",pc:"",lat:51.5346,lng:-0.2854,c:"follies",s:"A romantic mock-Gothic 'abbey' that never was — a stagecoach magnate's 1806 fantasy mansion, later a Catholic nursing home, now a brooding derelict by the North Circular.",q:"Twyford Abbey London",w:"A romantic mock-Gothic abbey that never was -- a stagecoach magnate's 1806 fantasy, dressed up by William Atkinson with all the faux-monastic trimmings on a site that only ever held a manor house. The Alexian Brothers ran it as a nursing home until 1988; it has brooded derelict by the North Circular ever since. Pure invented antiquity.",city:"london"},{id:"londontransportmuseumdep",n:"London Transport Museum Depot",a:"Gunnersbury Lane, Acton Town, W3",pc:"",lat:51.5036,lng:-0.2791,c:"museum",s:"A working store opposite Acton Town station holding the iceberg beneath the Covent Garden museum: hundreds of thousands of objects, from vintage Tube stock to enamel signs.",q:"London Transport Museum Depot London",w:"The iceberg beneath the Covent Garden museum, parked opposite Acton Town: a working store and restoration shed holding over 300,000 objects, from vintage Tube stock to enamel signage. Opened in 1999, the first museum store in Britain built for public access -- though only on guided tours and Open Days. Aladdin's cave on rails.",city:"london"},{id:"actontownstation",n:"Acton Town station",a:"Gunnersbury Lane, Acton Town, W3",pc:"",lat:51.5028,lng:-0.28,c:"artdeco",s:"Charles Holden's brick, concrete and glass interchange of 1932 — a textbook of his 'modern European' Underground style, with clerestory-lit ticket hall and concrete platform canopies.",q:"Acton Town station London",w:"Charles Holden's brick, concrete and glass interchange of 1932, a textbook of his cool modern-European Underground manner. A tall clerestory-lit ticket hall meets a low range of shops; paired piers carry the platform canopies in broad and narrow bays. Built when the Piccadilly line reached Acton Town. Listed, and quietly influential.",city:"london"},{id:"dnapierandsonworkssite",n:"D. Napier & Son works site",a:"Acton Vale / The Vale, W3",pc:"",lat:51.5066,lng:-0.251,c:"history",s:"On Acton Vale stood the works of D. Napier & Son — first luxury motor cars, then the legendary Napier Lion aero engine — anchoring Acton and Park Royal's car-and-aviation boom.",q:"D. Napier & Son works site London",w:"On Acton Vale stood the works of D. Napier and Son -- luxury motor cars first, then the mighty 12-cylinder Napier Lion aero engine from 1916, with 600 aircraft built here through the Great War. The plant seeded the whole Park Royal cluster: CAV, Lucas, Wilkinson Sword. The engine works closed around 1963; the boom it lit outlived it.",city:"london"},{id:"actoncountygrammarschool",n:"Acton County Grammar School site (The Who)",a:"Gunnersbury Lane, Acton, W3",pc:"",lat:51.5043,lng:-0.2806,c:"music",s:"On Gunnersbury Lane stood the grammar school where three Acton boys — Roger Daltrey, John Entwistle and Pete Townshend — met and set The Who in motion.",q:"Acton County Grammar School site (The Who) London",w:"On Gunnersbury Lane stood the grammar school where three Acton boys collided: Roger Daltrey, a year above, pulled in John Entwistle, who proposed a guitarist named Pete Townshend. From that came the band that turned into The Who. The old buildings fell in 2005, rebuilt on the same ground. Maximum R and B started in a school corridor.",city:"london"},{id:"emerywalkershouse",n:"Emery Walker's House",a:"7 Hammersmith Terrace, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.48982,lng:-0.24251,c:"museum",s:"A Georgian riverside terrace house that the Emery Walker Trust calls the last authentic Arts & Crafts interior in Britain — Morris wallpapers, Philip Webb furniture, frozen since 1933.",q:"Emery Walker's House London",w:"A Georgian riverside terrace the Emery Walker Trust calls the last authentic Arts and Crafts interior in Britain -- Morris wallpapers, Philip Webb furniture, Persian rugs, frozen since 1933. The engraver and Morris confidant moved into number 7 in 1903; his daughter Dorothy kept every room as he left it. Time-capsule by the Thames.",city:"london"},{id:"riversidestudios",n:"Riverside Studios",a:"Crisp Road, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.48831,lng:-0.22808,c:"film",s:"A former Victorian iron foundry turned film and TV studio where William Hartnell's Doctor Who regenerated and Tony Hancock recorded his half hour — rebuilt and reopened on its riverside site in 2019.",q:"Riverside Studios London",w:"A Victorian iron foundry turned film and television studio, where Tony Hancock recorded his half hour and William Hartnell's Doctor Who first regenerated. Triumph Films converted it in 1933; the BBC filmed here from 1954 to 1974. After decades as an arts centre it was demolished and rebuilt on the same riverside site, reopening in 2019.",city:"london"},{id:"lyrichammersmith",n:"Lyric Hammersmith",a:"Lyric Square, King Street, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.49306,lng:-0.22639,c:"history",s:"Walk in through a plain 1970s shopping precinct and climb two floors into Frank Matcham's 1895 Victorian rococo auditorium — dismantled, saved, and rebuilt piece by piece inside the modern shell.",q:"Lyric Hammersmith London",w:"Walk in off a plain 1970s precinct, climb two floors, and step into Frank Matcham's 1895 rococo auditorium -- dismantled piece by piece when it faced the wreckers in 1966, then rebuilt inside the modern shell on King Street. Lillie Langtry opened the original; it reopened on Lyric Square in 1979. A Victorian jewel smuggled upstairs.",city:"london"},{id:"eventimapollohammersmith",n:"Eventim Apollo (Hammersmith Odeon)",a:"Queen Caroline Street, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.491,lng:-0.224,c:"artdeco",s:"The art-deco picture palace where David Bowie killed off Ziggy Stardust on stage in July 1973 — still hiding a working 1930s Compton organ behind the stalls.",q:"Eventim Apollo (Hammersmith Odeon) London",w:"The art-deco picture palace where David Bowie killed off Ziggy Stardust on stage in July 1973, with Pennebaker's cameras rolling. Robert Cromie built it as the Gaumont Palace in 1932; the Beatles played 38 nights here in 1964-65. Behind the stalls a fully restored 1930s Compton organ still waits. Grade II glamour on the Broadway.",city:"london"},{id:"stpaulschurchhammersmith",n:"St Paul's Church, Hammersmith",a:"Queen Caroline Street, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.49151,lng:-0.22538,c:"faith",s:"Marooned beside the Hammersmith flyover, this church preserves a bronze bust of the 'martyr' Charles I beneath which a Royalist merchant had his own heart buried in an urn.",q:"St Paul's Church, Hammersmith London",w:"Marooned beside the Hammersmith flyover, this church keeps a bronze bust of the 'martyr' Charles I, the gift of Royalist brickmaker Nicholas Crispe -- who asked that his own heart be sealed in an urn beneath it. The story goes the heart was refreshed yearly with a glass of wine on the anniversary of the king's death. Devotion, bottled.",city:"london"},{id:"theblueanchor",n:"The Blue Anchor",a:"13 Lower Mall, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.4895,lng:-0.2308,c:"pub",s:"A white-washed riverside pub licensed in 1722, draped in rowing memorabilia, where Gustav Holst is said to have composed his Hammersmith Suite.",q:"The Blue Anchor London",w:"A white-washed riverside pub licensed back in 1722 as the Blew Anchor and Washhouses, now draped in oars and rowing colours beside Kensington Rowing Club. Gwyneth Paltrow danced here in 'Sliding Doors'. Local lore has Gustav Holst, who taught nearby, composing his Hammersmith Suite at a table -- pleasant to believe, hard to prove.",city:"london"},{id:"furnivalgardens",n:"Furnival Gardens",a:"Lower Mall, Hammersmith",pc:"",lat:51.49,lng:-0.233,c:"green",s:"A quiet riverside lawn that hides a buried tidal inlet — Hammersmith Creek once ran right through here, crossed by the High Bridge, until it was filled in for this 1951 park.",q:"Furnival Gardens London",w:"A quiet riverside lawn laid out for the 1951 Festival of Britain -- and hiding a buried tidal creek. Hammersmith Creek once ran right through here, navigable by barges and crossed by the High Bridge, until it was culverted under the grass. Named for the philologist Frederick Furnivall, who founded a sculling club for local girls and men.",city:"london"},{id:"bushhall",n:"Bush Hall",a:"Uxbridge Road, Shepherd's Bush, W12",pc:"",lat:51.50652,lng:-0.23161,c:"music",s:"An Edwardian dance hall behind a plain shopfront, its six Austrian crystal chandeliers surviving a century as soup kitchen, bingo hall and snooker club before becoming one of London's prettiest gig rooms.",q:"Bush Hall London",w:"Behind a plain Uxbridge Road shopfront hangs an Edwardian dance hall of 1904, lit by six tiers of Austrian crystal. The chandeliers outlasted a century of hard use -- wartime soup kitchen, snooker club, bingo -- before a 2001 restoration made it one of London's prettiest gig rooms.",city:"london"},{id:"bushtheatreoldpassmoreed",n:"Bush Theatre (old Passmore Edwards Library)",a:"Uxbridge Road, Shepherd's Bush, W12",pc:"",lat:51.50528,lng:-0.22583,c:"literary",s:"A celebrated new-writing theatre housed since 2011 in the ornate Passmore Edwards free library, a Victorian gift to Shepherd's Bush that fell empty when Westfield's library opened.",q:"Bush Theatre (old Passmore Edwards Library) London",w:"A celebrated new-writing theatre tucked since 2011 into an ornate Victorian free library -- Maurice Adams's 1896 Passmore Edwards gift to Shepherd's Bush, opened by Lord Rosebery. When Westfield's library replaced it, the old reading rooms fell empty; the Bush, born above a pub in 1972, moved in. Drama in the stacks.",city:"london"},{id:"thedimcobuildings",n:"The Dimco Buildings",a:"Wood Lane, White City, W12",pc:"",lat:51.50872,lng:-0.22312,c:"history",s:"A pair of red-brick Edwardian engine houses, the oldest surviving power station built for the London Underground, later disguised on screen as a cartoon factory and the British Museum.",q:"The Dimco Buildings London",w:"A pair of red-brick Edwardian engine houses, the oldest power station ever built for the London Underground -- raised in 1898 to electrify the Central line, idle since 1928. On screen they have played the Acme Factory in Roger Rabbit and the British Museum in The Mummy Returns. Often mislabelled Ghostbusters; the credits say otherwise.",city:"london"},{id:"stpaulsgirlsschool",n:"St Paul's Girls' School",a:"Brook Green, Hammersmith, W6",pc:"",lat:51.4951,lng:-0.2188,c:"music",s:"The Brook Green school where Gustav Holst taught for nearly thirty years and wrote 'The Planets' in a purpose-built sound-proofed room that is still used for music lessons.",q:"St Paul's Girls' School London",w:"The Brook Green school where Gustav Holst taught for thirty years and wrote 'The Planets'. When a music wing went up, the school gave him a sound-proofed room of his own; he composed most of his work there, and dedicated two of his suites to the pupils. The room still hosts music lessons. The cosmos, scored on Brook Green.",city:"london"},{id:"hmprisonwormwoodscrubs",n:"HM Prison Wormwood Scrubs",a:"Du Cane Road, East Acton, W12",pc:"",lat:51.51686,lng:-0.2403,c:"history",s:"A twin-towered Victorian prison whose gatehouse carries plaster portraits of reformers Elizabeth Fry and John Howard, and over whose wall the spy George Blake vanished on a rope ladder in 1966.",q:"HM Prison Wormwood Scrubs London",w:"A twin-towered Victorian prison whose gatehouse carries plaster portraits of the reformers Elizabeth Fry and John Howard, watching over Du Cane's 1891 convict-built blocks. Over this wall the Soviet double agent George Blake vanished in 1966, five years into a 42-year term, up a ladder said to be stiffened with knitting needles.",city:"london"},{id:"olympialondon",n:"Olympia London",a:"Hammersmith Road, West Kensington, W14",pc:"",lat:51.49639,lng:-0.20972,c:"history",s:"A vast Victorian glass barrel-vaulted exhibition hall, briefly England's largest enclosed space, that has held everything from the first World Scout Jamboree to Jimi Hendrix.",q:"Olympia London London",w:"A vast glass barrel-vault thrown up in 1886 as the National Agricultural Hall, briefly the largest enclosed space in England -- 440 feet of floor under a single glazed roof. It has held the first World Scout Jamboree in 1920 and Jimi Hendrix in 1967, circuses to motor shows in between. Grade II* engineering, still drawing crowds.",city:"london"},{id:"brookgreen",n:"Brook Green",a:"Brook Green, Hammersmith, W6",pc:"",lat:51.4945,lng:-0.2185,c:"green",s:"A long elm- and chestnut-lined strip of common land that once carried a polluted brook turned sewer, hosted a rowdy fair banned in 1823, and whose Queen's Head pub claims Dick Turpin as a regular.",q:"Brook Green London",w:"A long elm-lined strip of common where a brook once ran -- Black Bull Ditch, polluted into a covered sewer by 1876. An annual fair grew so rowdy it was banned after 1823. The Queen's Head pub, a pair of 1796 villas at the green's edge, still claims the highwayman Dick Turpin as a regular, on no evidence anyone can produce.",city:"london"},{id:"bentleypriorymuseum",n:"Bentley Priory Museum",a:"Stanmore, Harrow",pc:"",lat:51.6267,lng:-0.3342,c:"history",s:"The Grade II* country house where Air Chief Marshal Dowding ran RAF Fighter Command and won the Battle of Britain, now a museum with a recreated Filter Room.",q:"Bentley Priory Museum London",w:"From this elegant Stanmore mansion, Air Chief Marshal Dowding ran Fighter Command and won the Battle of Britain in 1940, directing 'the Few' from the Filter Room below. Once a stately home and a girls' school, it reopened in 2013 as a museum of that desperate summer. Where the battle was directed, not fought.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Bentley_Priory_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1095794.jpg"},{id:"croydonairportvisitorcen",n:"Croydon Airport Visitor Centre",a:"Croydon",pc:"",lat:51.3565,lng:-0.119,c:"history",s:"Britain's first international airport, birthplace of air traffic control and the 'Mayday' call, with a museum in the world's first purpose-built airport terminal and control tower.",q:"Croydon Airport Visitor Centre London",w:"Long before Heathrow, Croydon was Britain's gateway to the world — the first purpose-built international airport, where Amy Johnson took off for Australia and air-traffic control was invented. The airfield closed in 1959, but the 1928 control tower survives as Airport House, with a museum open monthly. Aviation's forgotten cradle.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Croydon_Airport_former_terminal_building_-_geograph_3044446.jpg/960px-Croydon_Airport_former_terminal_building_-_geograph_3044446.jpg"},{id:"whalebonesarch",n:"Whalebones Arch",a:"Arkley, Barnet",pc:"",lat:51.6512,lng:-0.2155,c:"oddity",s:"A roadside archway made of two genuine blue-whale jawbones, marking the drive to a hidden Barnet estate.",q:"Whalebones Arch London",w:"On a quiet lane in Arkley, two giant blue-whale jawbones arch over the drive to an old estate, a piece of Victorian eccentricity in the Barnet hedgerows. The present bones went up in 1939, replacing an even older pair. Locals say a kiss beneath the arch brings luck. Whale bones in the green belt.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thefanmuseum",n:"The Fan Museum",a:"Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.47861,lng:-0.00778,c:"museum",s:"The world's first museum devoted entirely to hand fans, set in a pair of restored Georgian townhouses on Crooms Hill.",q:"The Fan Museum London",w:"The world's only museum devoted to the hand fan fills a pair of Georgian townhouses on Greenwich's Crooms Hill, its thousands of folding fans ranging from jewelled court pieces to propaganda in pleated silk. Opened in 1991, it keeps an orangery and a fan-shaped garden. A vanished art of flirtation. Small, exquisite and gloriously niche.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/The_Fan_Museum_entrance3.png/960px-The_Fan_Museum_entrance3.png"},{id:"camletmoat",n:"Camlet Moat",a:"Trent Country Park, Enfield",pc:"",lat:51.6576,lng:-0.1492,c:"oddity",s:"A water-filled medieval moat lost in the woods at the edge of Trent Park, whose name folklore stretches into 'Camelot'.",q:"Camlet Moat London",w:"Deep in the woods of Trent Park lies a square moated island, all that remains of a medieval manor — but the name, a corruption of 'Camelot,' has snagged it on Arthurian legend and tales of treasure guarded by the ghost of Geoffrey de Mandeville. Witches and dowsers still visit. A scrap of Camelot in suburban Enfield.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lumleychapel",n:"Lumley Chapel",a:"Cheam, Sutton",pc:"",lat:51.3606,lng:-0.2161,c:"faith",s:"The lone surviving medieval chancel of Cheam's vanished old church, kept standing to shelter its Tudor tombs.",q:"Lumley Chapel London",w:"When old St Dunstan's church in Cheam was demolished in the 1860s, its medieval chancel was spared — and survives as Lumley Chapel, a rare fragment of the original village church. Inside wait grand Elizabethan tombs and a fine plaster ceiling, kept by the Churches Conservation Trust. A medieval room that outlived its church.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/The_Lumley_Chapel%2C_Cheam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_915125.jpg"},{id:"nonsuchpark",n:"Nonsuch Park",a:"Cheam, Sutton",pc:"",lat:51.358,lng:-0.222,c:"green",s:"Parkland on the Cheam fringe of London where Henry VIII's most extravagant palace once stood, now utterly vanished.",q:"Nonsuch Park London",w:"This handsome parkland on the Sutton edge hides one of England's great vanished buildings: Nonsuch Palace, Henry VIII's fantastical 'none such' hunting lodge of gilded plaster and towers, built to outshine the French and gone within 150 years. Nothing of it stands; faint earthworks mark the spot. A palace remembered only in its name.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"follywallbarkingtownsqua",n:"Folly Wall, Barking Town Square",a:"Barking, Barking & Dagenham",pc:"",lat:51.5392,lng:0.0807,c:"oddity",s:"A deliberately fake brick 'ruin' built in the 2000s to give a new civic square an instant fragment of the past.",q:"Folly Wall, Barking Town Square London",w:"A crumbling, ivy-hung 'ancient ruin' rises beside Barking Town Hall — except it was built in 2008, conjured by the architects muf from reclaimed bricks as a deliberate fake folly for the new town square. Part garden wall, part stage set, part sly joke about heritage. Children clamber over instant history. A ruin with no past at all.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"coronationstone",n:"Coronation Stone",a:"Kingston upon Thames",pc:"",lat:51.4085,lng:-0.3062,c:"history",s:"A weathered sarsen block by the Guildhall said to be where seven Anglo-Saxon kings were crowned.",q:"Coronation Stone London",w:"A weathered lump of sarsen by Kingston's old guildhall claims an outsized history: seven Anglo-Saxon kings, Athelstan and Ethelred the Unready among them, are said to have been crowned upon it in the 900s. Railed and a little forlorn now, it anchors the town's ancient name. England's coronation seat, before the Abbey.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Coronation_Stone%2C_Kingston-upon-Thames_%282%29.jpg/960px-Coronation_Stone%2C_Kingston-upon-Thames_%282%29.jpg"},{id:"upminsterwindmill",n:"Upminster Windmill",a:"Upminster, Havering",pc:"",lat:51.558,lng:0.2451,c:"history",s:"An 1803 smock mill on St Mary's Lane, restored to working order in 2023 after a Dutch millwright rebuilt its cap and sails.",q:"Upminster Windmill London",w:"A handsome white-capped smock mill from 1803 stands improbably amid the Havering streets, its sails turning again after a restoration finished in 2023. For over a century it ground the corn of rural Essex before the suburbs swallowed the fields. Volunteers run open days when the sails sweep the sky. The countryside, refusing to leave.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"welshharpbrentreservoir",n:"Welsh Harp (Brent Reservoir)",a:"Neasden / Hendon, Brent & Barnet",pc:"",lat:51.571,lng:-0.245,c:"green",s:"A reservoir built in the 1830s to feed the canals, now a 170-acre nature reserve and SSSI famed for its breeding great crested grebes.",q:"Welsh Harp (Brent Reservoir) London",w:"Built in the 1830s to feed the canals, this reservoir on the Brent became a Victorian playground of regattas, fairs and a pub that lent it the name 'Welsh Harp.' The crowds are long gone; it's now a tranquil 170-acre nature reserve, loud with dinghies and birdsong. A Victorian leisure lake, gone gently wild. Open water in the suburbs.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Brent_Res_view.JPG/960px-Brent_Res_view.JPG"},{id:"yasarhalim",n:"Yasar Halim",a:"Harringay",pc:"",lat:51.57966,lng:-0.09757,c:"bakery",s:"Turkish-Cypriot bakery, butcher and market anchoring the Green Lanes ladder since 1981",q:"Yasar Halim London",w:"Yasar Halim came through Victoria station from Cyprus at 18 and learned his trade over a Kentish Town oven before opening here in 1981, anchoring the Green Lanes ladder for the Turkish-Cypriot diaspora. Bread, baklava and borek baked at the back, butcher and greengrocer up front. He died in 2020; the family bakes on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lilyvanilli",n:"Lily Vanilli",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"",lat:51.52924,lng:-0.06857,c:"bakery",s:"Cobbled-courtyard bakery off Columbia Road from London's self-taught 'rock 'n' roll baker'",q:"Lily Vanilli London",w:"Lily Jones, a philosophy graduate turned graphic designer, sold her first cakes off Brick Lane when the recession bit, earning the tag 'London's rock 'n' roll baker' for things like a notorious anatomical brain cake. Since 2010 she bakes in a tucked-away brick courtyard off Ezra Street. Sundays, the flower-market queue snakes out.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"oldpostofficebakery",n:"Old Post Office Bakery",a:"Clapham North",pc:"",lat:51.46447,lng:-0.11588,c:"bakery",s:"London's self-styled oldest organic bakery, born from a squat and a bathtub mixing bowl",q:"Old Post Office Bakery London",w:"Karl Heinz Rossbach started baking sourdough here in 1982 while squatting a disused post office, mixing his dough, they say, in an old cast-iron bathtub. The counterculture-meets-old-Europe ethos stuck: London's self-styled oldest organic bakery still turns out eccles cakes, bloomers and pain au levain on Landor Road.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"paulrhodesbakery",n:"Paul Rhodes Bakery",a:"Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.48168,lng:-0.0089,c:"bakery",s:"Michelin-trained chef's artisan bakery feeding Greenwich Market off stone-based ovens",q:"Paul Rhodes Bakery London",w:"Paul Rhodes earned a Michelin star and a stint under Pierre Koffmann before swapping the pass for the proving room in 2003, baking on stone-based ovens by Greenwich Market. Croissants laminated properly, sourdough with a crust that argues back. The chef who decided bread made the harder craft.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"araratbread",n:"Ararat Bread",a:"Dalston",pc:"",lat:51.54795,lng:-0.07155,c:"bakery",s:"Hole-in-the-wall Armenian flatbread bakery on Ridley Road, topped to order",q:"Ararat Bread London",w:"A hole-in-the-wall on the Ridley Road market strip, baking Armenian-style flatbread to order: dough slapped out, blistered, handed over hot and topped with cheese, egg or spiced meat. Named for Mount Ararat, the snow-capped peak the Armenians lost to a 1932 border. Diaspora baking at its most direct.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"manddgrodzinski",n:"M & D Grodzinski",a:"Golders Green",pc:"",lat:51.5736,lng:-0.2064,c:"bakery",s:"Survivor of London's oldest kosher bakery dynasty, founded on an East End barrow in 1888",q:"M & D Grodzinski London",w:"Harris and Judith Grodzinski stepped off the boat from a Belarusian shtetl in 1888 and hired kosher ovens in the East End, starting from a barrow. By the 1960s the largest kosher bakery in Europe, supplying Selfridges and Harrods. The name survives on Golders Green Road: challah, bilkele, doughnuts at Chanukah.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"carmellibakery",n:"Carmelli Bakery",a:"Golders Green",pc:"",lat:51.572,lng:-0.1962,c:"bakery",s:"Near-round-the-clock kosher bakery whose rugelach and bagels feed northwest London",q:"Carmelli Bakery London",w:"Golders Green's late-night engine room since 1987, baking through the small hours so the Sabbath challah and a wall of rugelach, chocolate, cinnamon, vanilla, hit the shelves warm. The salt-beef bagel has its loyalists; the Israeli Embassy and the House of Lords have both put in orders. Sleep is for amateurs.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dunnsbakery",n:"Dunn's Bakery",a:"Crouch End",pc:"",lat:51.5792,lng:-0.1227,c:"bakery",s:"Run by London's oldest baking family, on a hay-wagon line traced to 1820",q:"Dunn's Bakery London",w:"Robert Dunn rode south from Northamptonshire on a hay wagon in 1820 to bake on Highgate Hill, founding what the family reckons London's oldest baking line. Six generations on, Lewis Freeman holds the Crouch End ovens. Two centuries of bread, unbroken, under a clock-tower sky.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"clarkes",n:"Clarke's",a:"Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.5064,lng:-0.1936,c:"bakery",s:"Sally Clarke's pioneering Kensington kitchen and bakery, Chez Panisse cooking brought to London",q:"Clarke's London",w:"Sally Clarke trained in Paris, fell under Alice Waters at Chez Panisse, then opened on Kensington Church Street days before Christmas 1984, doing seasonal Californian cooking when London barely knew the idea. The bakery next door followed, its loaves quietly feeding the city's best kitchens. Quietly influential, decades deep.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"littlebreadpedlar",n:"Little Bread Pedlar",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"",lat:51.4937,lng:-0.0685,c:"bakery",s:"Railway-arch sourdough bakery at Spa Terminus, public only on Saturday mornings",q:"Little Bread Pedlar London",w:"One of the sourdough arches that turned Bermondsey's Spa Terminus into a Saturday pilgrimage, baking since 2010 under the brick vaults where the trains rumble overhead. Wholesale all week; doors open to the public only on Saturday mornings, when the queue forms for warm loaves and pastries. Blink and you miss the window.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"breadahead",n:"Bread Ahead",a:"Borough",pc:"",lat:51.5055,lng:-0.0906,c:"bakery",s:"Borough Market doughnut shrine and baking school from St John's former head baker",q:"Bread Ahead London",w:"Justin Gellatly spent twelve years building St John's legendary bread before opening here in 2013, and the Borough Market crowds still scrum for his custard-filled doughnuts, the 'pillows of joy'. He baked rolls for a royal wedding; now he teaches the rest of us. Sourdough on one side, a queue for sugar on the other.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thehummingbirdbakery",n:"The Hummingbird Bakery",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"",lat:51.5158,lng:-0.2033,c:"bakery",s:"Portobello Road shop that introduced Britain to the red velvet cupcake",q:"The Hummingbird Bakery London",w:"Tarek Malouf spent a Thanksgiving in North Carolina, ate his weight in cupcakes and banana cream pie, and wondered why London had none. The answer opened on Portobello Road in 2004, more or less inventing the British cupcake craze and the red velvet obsession. Whatever you think of the frosting, blame starts here.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cocomaya",n:"Cocomaya",a:"Bayswater",pc:"",lat:51.5142,lng:-0.1638,c:"bakery",s:"Fashion-world chocolatier and artisan bakery hidden on a Connaught Street corner",q:"Cocomaya London",w:"Two fashion men, Joel and Walid, bonded over chocolate and proper baking, then in 2009 turned a Connaught Street corner into a small Willy Wonka of a shop near Marble Arch. Seventy-odd breads and pastries, hand-rolled truffles, cakes built like couture. A bakery that wandered in from a different industry and stayed.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"forno",n:"Forno",a:"London Fields",pc:"",lat:51.5376,lng:-0.0587,c:"bakery",s:"Vaulted railway-arch Italian bakery that popularised Rome's cream-filled maritozzi",q:"Forno London",w:"Mitshel Ibrahim, of the restaurant Ombra, opened Forno in 2022 beneath a cathedral-like railway arch near the canal, and did as much as anyone to make London queue for the maritozzi, Rome's split bun stuffed with whipped cream. Focaccia by the slab, fresh pasta at the deli counter. Roman conviction under Hackney brick.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gkellyromanroad",n:"G. Kelly (Roman Road)",a:"Bow",pc:"",lat:51.53005,lng:-0.03345,c:"pieandmash",s:"Roman Road's marble-counter eel and pie shop, in the Kelly family since the 1930s",q:"G. Kelly (Roman Road) London",w:"Pie, mash and a slick of parsley liquor, served at marble counters on Roman Road since the 1920s; George Kelly took the lease in 1939 and four generations have minced the beef and baked out the back ever since. Melanie McGrath wrote a whole book about it. Eels jellied, tea stewed, tradition unbudged.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gkellybethnalgreenroad",n:"G. Kelly (Bethnal Green Road)",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"",lat:51.52535,lng:-0.0572,c:"pieandmash",s:"Vintage eel-and-pie house on Bethnal Green Road, once the Krays' regular",q:"G. Kelly (Bethnal Green Road) London",w:"Hot pies, mash and liquor doled out across tiled benches at 414 Bethnal Green Road for the best part of a century, long known to regulars as S&R Kelly. The Krays grew up round the corner on Vallance Road; legend has it they took their pie and mash here as boys. Jellied eels, stewed tea, no airs.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"maureenspieandmash",n:"Maureen's Pie & Mash",a:"Poplar",pc:"",lat:51.51215,lng:-0.0139,c:"pieandmash",s:"Chrisp Street Market's tiled pie-and-mash bar, more than half a century deep",q:"Maureen's Pie & Mash London",w:"Looks like a tiled greasy spoon, cooks like an East End institution. Maureen's has held its pitch on pedestrianised Chrisp Street Market for over fifty years, the beef minced on site, the dark liquor almost minty. Son Jason runs it now with wife Karen. Pie, mash, jellied eels and a salt-beef beigel on the side.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"castlespieandmash",n:"Castle's Pie & Mash",a:"Camden Town",pc:"",lat:51.53865,lng:-0.1368,c:"pieandmash",s:"Camden's 1934 pie shop, decor unchanged for the best part of a century",q:"Castle's Pie & Mash London",w:"A time capsule on Royal College Street, open since 1934 and barely touched since: original tiling, foxed mirrors, hard benches. Eels, meat pies and a scrape of mash, finished with proper old puds, jam sponge and spotted dick. One of north London's last survivors of the trade. Liquor green, decor frozen.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cockneyspieandmash",n:"Cockneys Pie & Mash",a:"North Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.52315,lng:-0.2064,c:"pieandmash",s:"Portobello Road's pie-and-mash holdout under the shadow of Trellick Tower",q:"Cockneys Pie & Mash London",w:"West London's pie and mash all but vanished; Cockneys, at the rough top end of Portobello Road under Trellick Tower, keeps the tradition alive. Suet-bottomed pies, buttery crusts, mash and liquor heaped into heavy white china. A relative youngster, opened 1995, but proper to the bone. Eels jellied, market roaring outside.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"harringtonspieandmash",n:"Harringtons Pie & Mash",a:"Tooting",pc:"",lat:51.4279,lng:-0.1671,c:"pieandmash",s:"Tooting's eel-and-pie shop, the menu unchanged since 1908",q:"Harringtons Pie & Mash London",w:"Since 1908 the menu at Harringtons on Selkirk Road has barely moved: pie, mash, 'licker' green with parsley, eels jellied or stewed. A south London survivor where the tiles gleam and the regulars are lifers. The family announced in 2024 they would sell up; catch it while the ovens are still lit. Time, ticking.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"andrewsrestaurant",n:"Andrew's Restaurant",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.5222,lng:-0.11355,c:"caff",s:"Gray's Inn Road fry-up institution, Italian-run since the war",q:"Andrew's Restaurant London",w:"An Italian family fry-up on Gray's Inn Road since the war years, when it stood across the street feeding cabbies, clerks and MoD types. Andrew's still does a stupendous full English with proper fried slice, then home-cooked lunches, shepherd's pie, liver and bacon, stewed steak. Formica, banter and gravy. No reinvention required.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"starcafe2",n:"Star Cafe",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51548,lng:-0.13458,c:"caff",s:"Soho's 1933 Italian caff, fry-ups by day and a gin den by night",q:"Star Cafe London",w:"Opened in 1933 by 'Pop' Ambrose, fresh off the boat from southern Italy, the Star has fed Soho fry-ups and milky tea from a Great Chapel Street side-room ever since. After dark the same tables turn into The London Gin Club, pouring rare bottles where bacon sandwiches sat at noon. Two lives, one room. Old Soho, hanging on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"laporchettapollobar",n:"La Porchetta Pollo Bar",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.5133,lng:-0.1303,c:"caff",s:"Old Compton Street's 1960s Italian bar where the prices nearly stood still",q:"La Porchetta Pollo Bar London",w:"Generations of skint students and Soho stragglers have squeezed into the ox-blood booths of Pollo on Old Compton Street for vast plates of cheap pasta. The 1960s fittings, murals and lamps survive intact; for years the prices barely moved either. Trading now as La Porchetta Pollo Bar. Carbs, banter, bargain. Soho's stubborn heart.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mariasmarketcafe",n:"Maria's Market Café",a:"Borough",pc:"",lat:51.5056,lng:-0.0909,c:"caff",s:"Borough Market's family greasy spoon, frying since the early 1960s",q:"Maria's Market Café London",w:"While Borough Market gentrified into a foodie cathedral, Maria's kept frying eggs and bacon at caff prices. The Moruzzi family opened up on Park Street in 1961 and Maria's daughter carries it on by Southwark Street, tiled and steamy amid the artisan stalls. A bacon roll and a mug of tea, held firm against the tasting menus.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"rivercafeputneybridge",n:"River Cafe (Putney Bridge)",a:"Putney",pc:"",lat:51.4682,lng:-0.2088,c:"caff",s:"Three-generation greasy spoon facing Putney Bridge station, seascape murals and all",q:"River Cafe (Putney Bridge) London",w:"Not the Rogers-designed Italian one upriver; this River Cafe sits squat opposite Putney Bridge station, family-run across three generations. Inside, decades unchanged: blue-and-white tiling, plywood panelling, Formica, hand-painted seascape murals over the fry-ups. A greasy spoon hiding in plain sight. Tea, two sugars.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Putney_Bridge_stn_look_north_2019.jpg/960px-Putney_Bridge_stn_look_north_2019.jpg"},{id:"thefryersdelight",n:"The Fryer's Delight",a:"Holborn",pc:"",lat:51.51915,lng:-0.11685,c:"caff",s:"Holborn's beef-dripping chippy, Formica unchanged since the early sixties",q:"The Fryer's Delight London",w:"Cod and chips fried the old way in beef dripping, eaten in at Formica tables that have not budged since around 1962. The Fryer's Delight feeds Holborn cabbies, lawyers and shift-workers from a room where the fixtures are, mercifully, fixed. No batter trends, no small plates. Just golden fish, mushy peas and a steaming pot of tea.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/83/The_Fryer%27s_Delight_2013.jpg/960px-The_Fryer%27s_Delight_2013.jpg"},{id:"randolfis",n:"Randolfi's",a:"Bow",pc:"",lat:51.53015,lng:-0.0338,c:"caff",s:"Old Roman Road sandwich-bar caff with marble tables and bentwood chairs",q:"Randolfi's London",w:"A Roman Road sandwich bar from the old East End, run by the same family for generations. When a lorry ploughed into the original premises in 2008 they salvaged what mattered, the marble tables and bentwood Thonet chairs, and carried them up the street. Filled rolls, frothy coffee, a vanished London preserved in furniture. Quietly defiant.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thecoffeecup",n:"The Coffee Cup",a:"Hampstead",pc:"",lat:51.55605,lng:-0.1779,c:"caff",s:"Hampstead's coffee lounge since 1954, wood-panelled and stubbornly intact",q:"The Coffee Cup London",w:"Pouring coffee on Hampstead High Street since 1954, the Coffee Cup claims a spot among London's oldest coffee lounges. Dark wood panelling, pavement seats and a menu running from frothy cappuccino to a full plate; it has watched the village shift for seventy years. Locals, writers and loiterers. Wonderfully unbothered.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"petriemuseumofegyptianar",n:"Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.52361,lng:-0.13306,c:"museum",s:"UCL's overstuffed Egyptology study collection, all dust and labelled drawers",q:"Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology London",w:"Flinders Petrie dug pottery shards and trinkets nobody else wanted, then built a science of dating from them. His 80,000 objects fill cramped cases down a back lane at UCL, lit by hand torches some evenings. Among them, a linen dress older than the pyramids. Egyptology without the crowds.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Petrie_Museum_of_Egyptian_Archaeology.jpg/960px-Petrie_Museum_of_Egyptian_Archaeology.jpg"},{id:"thecartoonmuseum",n:"The Cartoon Museum",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.5176,lng:-0.1386,c:"museum",s:"British satire and comic art, Gillray to the Beano, on a quiet Fitzrovia street",q:"The Cartoon Museum London",w:"Three centuries of British rudeness under one roof: Gillray's bloated royals, Bell's lizard politicians, the inked originals of the Beano and Dennis the Menace. A small charity keeps the lights on north of Oxford Street while the crowds shuffle past below. Proof the nation has always preferred mockery to monuments.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thefoundlingmuseum",n:"The Foundling Museum",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.52528,lng:-0.12167,c:"museum",s:"Britain's first children's charity, and the heartbreak of the leaving tokens",q:"The Foundling Museum London",w:"Desperate mothers handed over babies and left a token, a bent coin, a button, a snipped heart of cloth, so they might one day prove which child was theirs. Most never came back. The cases of tokens here gut you quietly. Coram's hospital also gave Britain its first art gallery, courtesy of Hogarth and Handel.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Foundling_Museum_-Brunswick_Square_-London_-15July2009.jpg/960px-Foundling_Museum_-Brunswick_Square_-London_-15July2009.jpg"},{id:"benjaminfranklinhouse",n:"Benjamin Franklin House",a:"Charing Cross",pc:"",lat:51.5075,lng:-0.125,c:"history",s:"Franklin's only surviving home, with 1,200 human bones under the floor",q:"Benjamin Franklin House London",w:"Franklin lodged here for eighteen years, flying kites and plotting revolution a stroll from Trafalgar Square. In 1998 builders found 1,200 human bones beneath the garden room. Not murder, but a quiet anatomy school run by the landlady's son-in-law, dissecting bodies snatched for science. Trepanned skulls still on show.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Benjamin_Franklin_House%2C_36_Craven_Street%2C_London.jpg/960px-Benjamin_Franklin_House%2C_36_Craven_Street%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"stpaulstheactorschurch",n:"St Paul's, the Actors' Church",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.51139,lng:-0.12417,c:"faith",s:"The thespians' parish, where Punch and Judy and Pygmalion both began",q:"St Paul's, the Actors' Church London",w:"Inigo Jones reputedly built England's plainest church facing the piazza, then locals demanded the altar end face it, so the grand portico became a back door. Pepys watched the first English Punch and Judy beneath it in 1662. Inside, plaques to Karloff, Chaplin, Vivien Leigh. Pygmalion opens on this very step.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"imperialcamelcorpsmemori",n:"Imperial Camel Corps Memorial",a:"Victoria Embankment",pc:"",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.12167,c:"oddity",s:"A lone bronze camel and rider, easy to walk straight past",q:"Imperial Camel Corps Memorial London",w:"Half life-size and tucked among the Embankment shrubbery, a bronze trooper rides his camel through a London drizzle that would have baffled him. Cut by a major who served alongside the men he carved, it names 346 dead of a forgotten desert war. The unlikeliest cavalry, stranded by the Thames.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Camel_Corps_Memorial%2C_Victoria_Embankment_Gardens_-_front_view.jpg/960px-Camel_Corps_Memorial%2C_Victoria_Embankment_Gardens_-_front_view.jpg"},{id:"thelambandflag",n:"The Lamb and Flag",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.5116,lng:-0.1256,c:"pub",s:"A creaking back-alley boozer once nicknamed the Bucket of Blood",q:"The Lamb and Flag London",w:"Down a passage barely wide enough for shoulders sits a low timber pub they once called the Bucket of Blood for the bare-knuckle bouts upstairs. In 1679 the poet Dryden was set upon in the alley outside, legend says by thugs hired by a furious earl. Order a pint, mind the beams, and watch the door.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Lamb_and_Flag%2C_Covent_Garden%2C_WC2_%283578392482%29.jpg/960px-Lamb_and_Flag%2C_Covent_Garden%2C_WC2_%283578392482%29.jpg"},{id:"stmaryleboneparishchurch",n:"St Marylebone Parish Church",a:"Marylebone",pc:"",lat:51.523,lng:-0.1518,c:"faith",s:"Where the Brownings married in secret, above bricked-up catacombs",q:"St Marylebone Parish Church London",w:"In 1846 Elizabeth Barrett slipped from her father's house, married Robert Browning here without a word to anyone, and fled to Florence. Their certificate survives in the archive. Beneath the floor, catacombs once packed with 850 coffins, since emptied to Brookwood and turned over to a counselling centre. Romance over a crypt.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/St_Marylebone_Church%2C_Marylebone_Road%2C_London_W1_-_geograph.org.uk_-_297548.jpg"},{id:"thephotographersgallery",n:"The Photographers' Gallery",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.5148,lng:-0.1389,c:"museum",s:"Britain's first public gallery devoted wholly to photography, in a Soho warehouse",q:"The Photographers' Gallery London",w:"When it opened in 1971 no public gallery in Britain took photography seriously as art; this one staked the claim and won. Now stacked inside a black-clad former rag-trade warehouse on a Soho backstreet, five floors of prints, a camera obscura window, and the prize that crowns the year. The medium's London home.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_Photographers%27_Gallery%2C_London.jpg/960px-The_Photographers%27_Gallery%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"fitzroviachapel",n:"Fitzrovia Chapel",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.519,lng:-0.1383,c:"faith",s:"A jewel-box Byzantine chapel, all that survives of a vanished hospital",q:"Fitzrovia Chapel London",w:"Generations of patients prayed here before surgery in the old Middlesex Hospital. When the wrecking ball came in 2008, everything fell but this: a tiny Byzantine box glittering with gold mosaic and seventeen kinds of marble, never quite finished. It survives, marooned in a glass-and-steel square, a lit ghost of the ward.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Fitzrovia_Chapel_2017-09-17-1.jpg/960px-Fitzrovia_Chapel_2017-09-17-1.jpg"},{id:"brownhartgardens",n:"Brown Hart Gardens",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.5131,lng:-0.1516,c:"green",s:"An Italianate terrace garden sitting on the roof of an electricity substation",q:"Brown Hart Gardens London",w:"Lose the Mayfair garden to a power station and the Duke of Westminster's answer was simple: put the garden on the roof. So a balustraded Italian terrace with stone pavilions sits atop a humming substation, transformers below, planters above. Climb the steps off Duke Street for a quiet bench over the grid.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Brown-hart-gardens.jpg"},{id:"stjamesschurchpiccadilly",n:"St James's Church, Piccadilly",a:"St James's",pc:"",lat:51.50861,lng:-0.13667,c:"faith",s:"Wren's favourite church, where Blake was christened and a courtyard market hums",q:"St James's Church, Piccadilly London",w:"Wren said this one pleased him most, the rare church he built from scratch on open ground. At the Grinling Gibbons font, carved with birds and fruit, the infant William Blake was christened in 1757. Outside, a scruffy courtyard market trades antiques and crafts to keep the roof on. Visionaries and bric-a-brac.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Church_of_St_Jamess_Piccadilly_2_%285123798865%29.jpg/960px-Church_of_St_Jamess_Piccadilly_2_%285123798865%29.jpg"},{id:"thewallacecollectionarmo",n:"The Wallace Collection Armouries",a:"Marylebone",pc:"",lat:51.5175,lng:-0.153,c:"museum",s:"A Manchester Square mansion crammed with one of Europe's great armouries",q:"The Wallace Collection Armouries London",w:"Most come for the Fragonards and the Laughing Cavalier, then stumble into the basement and the gleaming racks of steel. Three galleries of European armour and an Eastern hoard, etched breastplates and royal wheel-lock guns, gathered by a French-raised heir and left to the nation. Free, and almost always empty downstairs.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/Front_entrance_to_the_Wallace_Collection%2C_Manchester_Square_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1600012.jpg"},{id:"newmanpassage",n:"Newman Passage",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.5179,lng:-0.1362,c:"oddity",s:"A cobbled Georgian cut-through with a sinister cinematic afterlife",q:"Newman Passage London",w:"Duck through the low arch beside the Newman Arms and the city drops away into damp cobbles and brick. Powell opened Peeping Tom here in 1960, a killer stalking the dark, and the film was so reviled it ended his career. Orwell supposedly drank at the pub. After nightfall the passage still earns its shivers.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stalphagelondonwall",n:"St Alphage London Wall",a:"London Wall",pc:"",lat:51.51766,lng:-0.09223,c:"medieval",s:"A church tower ruin growing straight out of the Roman city wall",q:"St Alphage London Wall London",w:"Layered like an archaeological cake: Roman defensive wall at the base, medieval brickwork above, and a flint church tower sprouting from the top, arches gaping on three sides. Closed by Act of Parliament around 1600 and turned to garden in 1837. Two thousand years of wall, holding up a ruin.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Alphage_london_wall_godwin.jpg"},{id:"stmaryaldermanburygarden",n:"St Mary Aldermanbury Garden",a:"Aldermanbury",pc:"",lat:51.51663,lng:-0.09248,c:"history",s:"A Wren church shipped stone by stone to Missouri, leaving only its footprint",q:"St Mary Aldermanbury Garden London",w:"Wren built it, the Blitz gutted it, and in 1965 someone numbered 7,000 stones, shipped them to Fulton Missouri, and rebuilt the whole church there as a Churchill memorial. What stayed behind: the bare footprint, foundations and column stumps, planted as a quiet garden by Guildhall. A church that emigrated.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/StMaryAldermanbury.jpg"},{id:"stvedastaliasfosterfount",n:"St Vedast-alias-Foster Fountain Court",a:"Foster Lane",pc:"",lat:51.51445,lng:-0.0974,c:"green",s:"A hidden paved courtyard behind a Wren church, set with salvaged Roman pavement",q:"St Vedast-alias-Foster Fountain Court London",w:"Slip down the passage beside this Wren church, named for an obscure Flemish saint whose name the English mangled into 'Foster,' and you reach a paved court with a wall fountain, set on old graveyard ground. Underfoot, salvaged Roman pavement from a long-demolished church nearby. A courtyard built from leftovers.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Church_of_St_Vedast_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7049159.jpg"},{id:"collegeofarms",n:"College of Arms",a:"Queen Victoria Street",pc:"",lat:51.5121,lng:-0.0981,c:"history",s:"England's heraldry HQ, still granting coats of arms from a 1670s brick courtyard",q:"College of Arms London",w:"Behind iron gates on Queen Victoria Street, heralds with titles like Rouge Dragon and Bluemantle still research pedigrees and grant coats of arms, exactly as Richard III chartered them to in 1484. The Great Fire took the first building; this brick courtyard replaced it by 1677. Medieval bureaucracy, fully operational.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Coat_of_Arms_of_the_College_of_Arms.svg/960px-Coat_of_Arms_of_the_College_of_Arms.svg.png"},{id:"standrewundershaft",n:"St Andrew Undershaft",a:"St Mary Axe",pc:"",lat:51.514,lng:-0.0821,c:"faith",s:"A church named for a maypole, holding the tomb of London's first historian",q:"St Andrew Undershaft London",w:"Its odd name comes from a maypole, the 'shaft', once raised here each spring taller than the tower, until a 1547 mob sawed it up as a pagan idol. Inside lies John Stow, who walked and catalogued Tudor London street by street. Every few years the Lord Mayor renews the quill pen in his stone hand.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Church_of_St_Andrew_Undershaft_20130324_012.JPG"},{id:"stgileswithoutcripplegat",n:"St Giles-without-Cripplegate",a:"Barbican",pc:"",lat:51.5184,lng:-0.0938,c:"faith",s:"Milton's burial church, marooned among Barbican towers beside the Roman wall",q:"St Giles-without-Cripplegate London",w:"Milton lies buried here; Cromwell married here in 1620. The medieval church burned in the Blitz, then found itself stranded inside the concrete of the Barbican, a stone survivor ringed by brutalist towers. Outside, a Roman bastion and an ornamental lake trace the line of the old city ditch. Old bones, new walls.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/St_Giles%27_Church-without-Cripplegate_Barbican_London.jpg/960px-St_Giles%27_Church-without-Cripplegate_Barbican_London.jpg"},{id:"stalbanstowerwoodstreet",n:"St Alban's Tower, Wood Street",a:"Wood Street",pc:"",lat:51.5153,lng:-0.0938,c:"oddity",s:"A lone Gothic Wren tower stranded on a traffic island, now a private home",q:"St Alban's Tower, Wood Street London",w:"Traffic streams around it on both sides: a 92ft Gothic tower, pinnacles and all, marooned alone in the middle of Wood Street. The Blitz took the rest of Wren's church; only this fairy-tale spike remained. Someone now lives inside it. A church tower turned roundabout, turned house.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/ONL_%281887%29_1.366_-_Wood_Street_Compter%2C_1793.jpg/960px-ONL_%281887%29_1.366_-_Wood_Street_Compter%2C_1793.jpg"},{id:"stmagnusthemartyr",n:"St Magnus the Martyr",a:"Lower Thames Street",pc:"",lat:51.5089,lng:-0.0874,c:"faith",s:"Gateway church to medieval London Bridge, with stones from the old crossing in its yard",q:"St Magnus the Martyr London",w:"For centuries every soul crossing Old London Bridge into the City walked past this door; the bridge roadway ran right by its tower clock. Wren's costliest parish church, it now keeps two weathered stones from the vanished bridge in its yard, and a model of the houses-on-a-bridge that Eliot called 'inexplicable splendour'.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/St_Magnus_the_Martyr_and_Adelaide_House_from_the_top_of_The_Monument.JPG/960px-St_Magnus_the_Martyr_and_Adelaide_House_from_the_top_of_The_Monument.JPG"},{id:"stmichaelpaternosterroya",n:"St Michael Paternoster Royal",a:"College Hill",pc:"",lat:51.5102,lng:-0.0896,c:"faith",s:"Dick Whittington's burial church, with the famous cat in a stained-glass window",q:"St Michael Paternoster Royal London",w:"Dick Whittington, four times Lord Mayor, poured money into rebuilding this church and was buried here around 1423. The pantomime says he climbed from rags with a magic cat; the truth says he was born rich and probably catless. Either way, a stained-glass moggy sits by the door, honouring a legend the facts never quite killed.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stethelburgasbishopsgate",n:"St Ethelburga's, Bishopsgate",a:"Bishopsgate",pc:"",lat:51.51553,lng:-0.08166,c:"faith",s:"A tiny medieval church flattened by an IRA bomb, rebuilt as a peace centre",q:"St Ethelburga's, Bishopsgate London",w:"This pocket-sized medieval church dodged the Great Fire and the Blitz, then in 1993 an IRA truck bomb seven metres away brought it down. Rather than clear the rubble, they rebuilt it, stone by stone, and reopened it as a centre for reconciliation. Wreckage turned, deliberately, into a workshop for peace.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/St_ethelburga_bishopsgate.jpg"},{id:"ststephenwalbrook",n:"St Stephen Walbrook",a:"Walbrook",pc:"",lat:51.51251,lng:-0.09022,c:"faith",s:"Wren's dome rehearsal for St Paul's, centred on a Henry Moore stone altar",q:"St Stephen Walbrook London",w:"Wren practised here: this airy dome on twelve columns rehearsed the one he'd later raise over St Paul's. At its heart squats a round altar Henry Moore carved from travertine quarried where Michelangelo cut his marble, divisive then, beloved now. And in the crypt in 1953, a rector founded the Samaritans by a single telephone.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Ward_Walbrook_plaque_London.jpg"},{id:"thegreshamgrasshopper",n:"The Gresham Grasshopper",a:"Lombard Street",pc:"",lat:51.5128,lng:-0.0868,c:"history",s:"A gilded grasshopper hanging over Lombard Street since the days before house numbers",q:"The Gresham Grasshopper London",w:"Before street numbers, buildings wore signs, and Tudor financier Thomas Gresham hung his family grasshopper over his Lombard Street house. It still dangles, gilded, above number 68. Legend claims a foundling Gresham ancestor was discovered in a field by a grasshopper's chirp. A bug that founded a banking dynasty, still chirping in gold.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thecornhillwaterpump",n:"The Cornhill Water Pump",a:"Cornhill",pc:"",lat:51.5134,lng:-0.0864,c:"oddity",s:"A 1799 street pump marking a medieval well and a vanished house of correction",q:"The Cornhill Water Pump London",w:"Easy to walk past: a Georgian iron pump on Cornhill, paid for in 1799 by the Bank of England, the East India Company and the fire offices, whose insurance marks it still wears like sponsor logos. Its inscription remembers a well here in 1282 and a medieval house of correction. Centuries of City thirst, frozen mid-handle.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"attendantfitzrovia",n:"Attendant Fitzrovia",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.51922,lng:-0.1407,c:"coffee",s:"Specialty coffee bar inside a restored Victorian gents' urinal",q:"Attendant Fitzrovia London",w:"Descend the steps off Foley Street and you order a flat white where Victorian gents once stood: an 1890s underground urinal, shut in the 1960s, boarded up for half a century. They slung a marble counter through the porcelain stalls and kept the green tiles. Coffee, served below ground, between the cisterns.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thefieldsbeneath",n:"The Fields Beneath",a:"Kentish Town",pc:"",lat:51.54699,lng:-0.14676,c:"coffee",s:"Tiny vegan coffee bar wedged into a live railway arch",q:"The Fields Beneath London",w:"Trains rumble overhead as you sip, because the whole room sits inside a live railway arch under Kentish Town West. A dozen seats, brick vault, named for Tom Bolton's book on the lost villages buried under the city. Went fully vegan in 2017. Coffee in the fields beneath the rails.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thewrencoffee",n:"The Wren Coffee",a:"City of London",pc:"",lat:51.5121,lng:-0.09685,c:"coffee",s:"Specialty coffee inside a working Christopher Wren church",q:"The Wren Coffee London",w:"Order at the nave: this coffee bar trades inside St Nicholas Cole Abbey, the church Christopher Wren rebuilt after the Great Fire flattened the medieval one. Named for its architect, it opened in 2014 and took best new London coffee shop that year. Caravan beans, poured under a Wren ceiling.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"theespressoroom",n:"The Espresso Room",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.52191,lng:-0.12007,c:"coffee",s:"Closet-sized coffee bar that fuelled London's specialty wave",q:"The Espresso Room London",w:"Barely wider than a phone box, this 2009 espresso bar helped drag London out of its burnt-flat-white years. It sits opposite Great Ormond Street hospital, so the queue mixes worn-out parents, surgeons on a ten-minute break and coffee obsessives. No room to sit. The flat white earns the squeeze.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"departmentofcoffeeandsoc",n:"Department of Coffee and Social Affairs",a:"Holborn",pc:"",lat:51.51988,lng:-0.10931,c:"coffee",s:"The founding shop of a homegrown London coffee mini-empire",q:"Department of Coffee and Social Affairs London",w:"Everything started here, in a former ironmonger's on Leather Lane in 2010, the deadpan civil-service name a joke about London's coffee bureaucracy. From this one shop sprang a whole estate of cafes. Step out into the lunchtime market crush of frying onions and fake watches. The original department.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tapcoffee",n:"TAP Coffee",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51561,lng:-0.13563,c:"coffee",s:"Pioneering Wardour Street roaster from London's early specialty days",q:"TAP Coffee London",w:"Back when Soho still ran on instant and froth, this Wardour Street room opened as Tapped and Packed and started taking beans seriously. One of the first proper specialty bars in the West End, fond of numbering its shops instead of naming them. Roasts its own. An old hand from the early days.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vagabondn4",n:"Vagabond N4",a:"Stroud Green",pc:"",lat:51.56649,lng:-0.10759,c:"coffee",s:"Scratch-built specialty cafe raised from reclaimed scaffolding",q:"Vagabond N4 London",w:"The counter you lean on used to be scaffolding. Two builders raised this Stroud Green cafe in 2011 from reclaimed wood and skip finds, then started roasting their own. Tables, chairs, the whole fit-out had a previous life holding up someone's wall. Coffee that began as a pile of salvage.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"caravankingscross",n:"Caravan King's Cross",a:"King's Cross",pc:"",lat:51.53524,lng:-0.1254,c:"brunch",s:"All-day brunch and on-site roastery in a vast Victorian granary",q:"Caravan King's Cross London",w:"First through the door when King's Cross reinvented itself, this 2012 canteen fills an old Victorian grain store on Granary Square. A roaster chugs away in the dining room, scenting the brunch of cornbread, kimchi eggs and well-travelled plates. Industrial bones, ducting overhead. Coffee roasted where you eat.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/King%27s_Cross_Central_2019.jpg/960px-King%27s_Cross_Central_2019.jpg"},{id:"thebreakfastclubsoho",n:"The Breakfast Club Soho",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51452,lng:-0.13634,c:"brunch",s:"The original branch of London's everyman all-day brunch institution",q:"The Breakfast Club Soho London",w:"The mothership of London's brunch boom started here on D'Arblay Street in 2005, all John Hughes nostalgia and pancake stacks the size of hubcaps. The queue down the pavement became part of the furniture. Later branches hid speakeasies behind fridge doors. Where the city learned to brunch.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"andinashoreditch",n:"Andina Shoreditch",a:"Shoreditch",pc:"",lat:51.52438,lng:-0.07697,c:"brunch",s:"Andean picanteria serving quinoa-fed Peruvian weekend brunch",q:"Andina Shoreditch London",w:"Martin Morales, who dragged ceviche into the London mainstream, runs this Redchurch Street picanteria on Andean highland cooking. Weekend brunch means quinoa, smashed avocado, chicharron rolls and a pisco sour before noon. Relaunched bigger in 2024. The Andes, transplanted to a Shoreditch corner.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"pavilioncafe",n:"Pavilion Cafe",a:"Victoria Park",pc:"",lat:51.5334,lng:-0.04289,c:"brunch",s:"Lakeside park cafe doing Sri Lankan-tinged East End brunch",q:"Pavilion Cafe London",w:"Out by the boating lake in Victoria Park, this little pavilion drew the Hackney brunch crowd long before brunch went citywide. The kitchen runs a tight seasonal menu with a Sri Lankan tilt beside the full Englishes. Opening hours bend to the park gates. Eggs by the water, dogs underfoot.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lantanafitzrovia",n:"Lantana Fitzrovia",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.51893,lng:-0.13605,c:"brunch",s:"Pioneering Australian cafe that helped import brunch culture",q:"Lantana Fitzrovia London",w:"Tucked down the pedestrian sliver of Charlotte Place, this 2008 Aussie cafe is one of the rooms that taught London to take brunch seriously: flat whites, smashed avo, the famous corn fritters with streaky bacon. Founder Shelagh Ryan brought the Antipodean playbook north. A quiet pioneer.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"milkbeachsoho",n:"Milk Beach Soho",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51545,lng:-0.13055,c:"brunch",s:"Modern Australian all-day brunch down a Greek Street passage",q:"Milk Beach Soho London",w:"Slip down the passage off Greek Street into Ilona Rose House and you reach this modern Australian dining room named for a Sydney swimming cove. Weekend breakfasts of crumpets and confit before the all-day menu kicks in. Grew out of a Queen's Park original. Sunshine cooking under Soho brick.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thegentlemenbaristas",n:"The Gentlemen Baristas",a:"Borough",pc:"",lat:51.5045,lng:-0.0985,c:"coffee",s:"Flat-cap-themed specialty roaster minutes from Borough Market",q:"The Gentlemen Baristas London",w:"A wry conceit runs through the whole place: politeness costs nothing, so the staff wear flat caps and the menu plays Victorian gentlemen's club. The first shop opened on Union Street in 2014, a short walk from Borough Market, and now roasts for the rest. Good manners, properly extracted.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wongkei",n:"Wong Kei",a:"Chinatown",pc:"",lat:51.51146,lng:-0.13246,c:"dumpling",s:"Cantonese canteen long famed as the rudest dining room in London",q:"Wong Kei London",w:"Four storeys, 500 seats, and a reputation as the rudest dining room in town. They say waiters once barked 'upstairs!', snatched your plate and chased short tippers down Wardour Street; ask for a fork and get scolded. Tamer since 2014, the swagger lingers. Order the roast duck and behave.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/WILLY_CLARKSON_-_Wong_Kei_-_41-43_Wardour_Street_Soho_London_W1D_6PY.jpg/960px-WILLY_CLARKSON_-_Wong_Kei_-_41-43_Wardour_Street_Soho_London_W1D_6PY.jpg"},{id:"goldendragon",n:"Golden Dragon",a:"Chinatown",pc:"",lat:51.51165,lng:-0.13112,c:"dumpling",s:"Gerrard Street trolley dim sum, the old-school Chinatown ritual",q:"Golden Dragon London",w:"Carts rattle between the tables, lids lift on steaming bamboo, and the whole rowdy ritual of trolley dim sum survives here when half of London has gone a la carte. Char siu bao, har gau, cheung fun, ticked off a paper slip. Cantonese to the core, gloriously loud. Get there before the carts stop at five.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"fourseasons",n:"Four Seasons",a:"Chinatown",pc:"",lat:51.51195,lng:-0.13097,c:"dumpling",s:"Roast duck the FT once called the best in the world",q:"Four Seasons London",w:"Lacquered ducks hang in the window, skin like burnished glass, the meat a Financial Times pick for best on earth. They say the birds get soft music to keep them calm before the oven. Plain room, charmless service, perpetual queue, nobody cares. Crisp skin, tender flesh, secret spice. Order the duck.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"baoziinn",n:"Baozi Inn",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51289,lng:-0.13125,c:"dumpling",s:"Sichuan street snacks and steamed baozi, retro propaganda decor",q:"Baozi Inn London",w:"Pillowy steamed baozi and tongue-numbing dan dan noodles in a snug room papered with Mao-era posters and old enamel adverts. Started on Newport Court in 2008, an early voice for proper Sichuan street snacking before the West End caught on, now tucked on Romilly Street. Order the pork buns and the dan dan.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mandarinkitchen",n:"Mandarin Kitchen",a:"Bayswater",pc:"",lat:51.5108,lng:-0.1869,c:"dumpling",s:"The original ginger-and-scallion lobster noodle, since 1978",q:"Mandarin Kitchen London",w:"Behind the dated carpet and brusque waiters, a kitchen that claims to have invented London's lobster noodle back in 1978: sweet shellfish over ginger-and-scallion egg noodles that soak up every drop of sauce. Soft-shell crab and salted-egg prawns keep the regulars loyal. Bayswater's seafood institution. Bring a wallet.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"silkroad",n:"Silk Road",a:"Camberwell",pc:"",lat:51.47391,lng:-0.08926,c:"dumpling",s:"Xinjiang dumplings and big-plate chicken at shared wooden benches",q:"Silk Road London",w:"Xinjiang cooking from the far western edge of China, served at long shared benches in a no-frills Camberwell room: hand-pinched dumplings, cumin-soaked lamb, and da pan ji, the big-plate chicken hauled out for the whole table. A south London cult, shuttered then triumphantly reopened in 2024. Go hungry, share everything.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"murgerhan",n:"Murger Han",a:"Euston",pc:"",lat:51.52948,lng:-0.13288,c:"dumpling",s:"Xi'an murgers and dumplings, the flagship by Euston",q:"Murger Han London",w:"The name nods to the murger, Xi'an's answer to a burger: spiced slow-cooked meat crammed into a crisp griddled flatbread. Opened by Euston in 2014, it spread the gospel of biang biang noodles and proper northern dumplings across town. Glass-noodle soups, wontons, chilli oil everywhere. Come for the flatbread, stay for the noodles.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"littlegeorgia",n:"Little Georgia",a:"Haggerston",pc:"",lat:51.53326,lng:-0.06526,c:"dumpling",s:"Georgian khinkali soup dumplings, twisted by hand and eaten by the knot",q:"Little Georgia London",w:"Georgian cooking off a Hackney back street: khinkali, fat soup dumplings pinched into a doughy topknot you grip but never eat. Tradition says you pile the knots to tally who ate most. Cheese-boat khachapuri, walnut everything, bring your own wine. Pierce, slurp the broth, repeat.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dumplingshack",n:"Dumpling Shack",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"",lat:51.5196,lng:-0.07536,c:"dumpling",s:"Market-stall sheng jian bao, pan-fried and bursting with broth",q:"Dumpling Shack London",w:"From a roving market stall to a permanent counter under the Spitalfields canopy, built on the sheng jian bao: pork buns pan-fried crisp on the bottom, soft on top, a scalding slurp of broth sealed inside. Sichuan chicken and chilli wontons alongside. Bite the corner, sip the soup, do not wear white.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tibetkitchen",n:"Tibet Kitchen",a:"Brick Lane",pc:"",lat:51.52096,lng:-0.07241,c:"dumpling",s:"Handmade Tibetan momos off the old Truman Brewery yard",q:"Tibet Kitchen London",w:"Tucked on Dray Walk by the old Truman Brewery, a family kitchen pleating Tibetan momos by hand since 2008: half-moon dumplings of pork or beef or veg, steamed or fried, slicked in a chilli-tomato momo sauce that earns its place. Thukpa noodle soup to follow. Plateau cooking on the Brick Lane fringe.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"eatmomo",n:"Eat Momo",a:"Borough",pc:"",lat:51.50721,lng:-0.09252,c:"dumpling",s:"Nepalese steamed momos under a Borough railway arch",q:"Eat Momo London",w:"Under a Borough railway arch, sisters Trishna and Dipa Chamling pleat Nepalese momos to order: juicy half-moons of chicken, pork or veg, steamed or crisped, served with the nutty-spicy achar that Kathmandu swears by. A bright little canteen squeezed between the Borough Yards stones. Steamed, dunked, gone in seconds.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bubbleology2",n:"Bubbleology",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51196,lng:-0.13358,c:"boba",s:"London's first bubble-tea lab, shakers and lab coats since 2011",q:"Bubbleology London",w:"London's bubble-tea pioneer dressed as a chemistry set: flasks bubbling in the window, chalkboard formulae on the walls, staff in lab coats shaking milk tea over ice like cocktails. Opened on Rupert Street in 2011, before boba lined every high street. Tapioca pearls optional, theatre included. Order it shaken.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mooboo",n:"Mooboo",a:"Camden",pc:"",lat:51.54265,lng:-0.14766,c:"boba",s:"The homegrown UK boba chain born in Camden's Stables Market",q:"Mooboo London",w:"Britain's own boba story started here in Camden in 2012, a single counter that bred a hundred-shop chain. The original still pours among the cobbles and railway arches of the Stables Market, milk tea shaken fresh, tapioca sunk to the bottom of the cup. Homegrown bubble tea, brewed where it began. Get the brown sugar.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"yifangtaiwanfruittea",n:"Yifang Taiwan Fruit Tea",a:"Chinatown",pc:"",lat:51.51525,lng:-0.12679,c:"boba",s:"Taiwanese fruit tea, the brand's first UK shop on Chinatown's edge",q:"Yifang Taiwan Fruit Tea London",w:"Taiwan's fruit-tea specialist chose the Chinatown edge of Shaftesbury Avenue for its first British shop, trading on hand-squeezed sugarcane and a winter-melon brew steeped the slow way. Less neon than its boba neighbours, more about the tea than the pearls. Order the pineapple green and watch them pour.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thewisdomofsolomonpyrami",n:"The Wisdom of Solomon Pyramid, St Anne's Limehouse",a:"Limehouse",pc:"",lat:51.50703,lng:-0.02483,c:"faith",s:"A stranded Hawksmoor pyramid sulking in a Limehouse churchyard",q:"The Wisdom of Solomon Pyramid, St Anne's Limehouse London",w:"Hawksmoor meant two stone pyramids to crown St Anne's east corners. One vanished; the other got marooned in the churchyard, squat and inscrutable, carved 'The Wisdom of Solomon'. Nobody quite knows why. Psychogeographers read ley-lines into it. Most likely just a leftover. A baroque shrug in Portland stone.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stgeorgeintheeast",n:"St George in the East",a:"Shadwell",pc:"",lat:51.51,lng:-0.06,c:"faith",s:"A modern church built inside the bombed shell of a Hawksmoor giant",q:"St George in the East London",w:"A 1941 bomb gutted Hawksmoor's giant on Cannon Street Road, leaving only walls and the squat pepper-pot towers. For seventeen years a prefab chapel huddled in the ruin, 'St George in the Ruins'. In 1964 they slotted a modern church inside the old shell. A building wearing its own corpse like a coat.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/StGeorgeInTheEast.JPG/960px-StGeorgeInTheEast.JPG"},{id:"stmatthiasoldchurchpopla",n:"St Matthias Old Church, Poplar",a:"Poplar",pc:"",lat:51.50904,lng:-0.01537,c:"faith",s:"A Commonwealth-era chapel the East India Company built for its sailors",q:"St Matthias Old Church, Poplar London",w:"The East India Company built this chapel for its Blackwall shipwrights and sailors in the 1650s, one of the rare churches raised under Cromwell's godly republic. Later centuries wrapped it in Gothic stone, but inside lurk ship motifs and Company arms. Now a community hall. A merchant empire's parish church, hidden in plain sight.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Former_Poplar_Board_of_Works%2C_Poplar_High_Street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1516142.jpg"},{id:"stdunstanandallsaintsste",n:"St Dunstan and All Saints, Stepney",a:"Stepney",pc:"",lat:51.51688,lng:-0.04169,c:"faith",s:"The Saxon-rooted 'mother church of the East End', with an 11th-century carved rood",q:"St Dunstan and All Saints, Stepney London",w:"Stepney's mother church, traceable to around 952 and once parish to everything east of the City wall. Behind the altar hangs a carved Saxon rood from the early 1000s, one of only two such survivals in London. Sailors born at sea were registered here, hence 'the Church of the High Seas'. Older than the East End itself.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Northwest_View_of_the_Church_of_St_Dunstan%2C_Stepney.jpg/960px-Northwest_View_of_the_Church_of_St_Dunstan%2C_Stepney.jpg"},{id:"staugustinestowerhackney",n:"St Augustine's Tower, Hackney",a:"Hackney Central",pc:"",lat:51.5478,lng:-0.05451,c:"medieval",s:"Hackney's oldest building, a lone medieval tower with a Tudor turret clock",q:"St Augustine's Tower, Hackney London",w:"When Hackney's medieval church came down in 1798, they kept the tower. It still stands in a corner of the churchyard, Kentish ragstone over rougher early stonework, with a turret clock ticking since around the 1570s, most of its gears original. Climb it on open days. The oldest thing in the borough, outliving its own church.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Side_view_of_St_Augustine%27s%2C_Hackney.jpg/960px-Side_view_of_St_Augustine%27s%2C_Hackney.jpg"},{id:"soupkitchenforthejewishp",n:"Soup Kitchen for the Jewish Poor, Brune Street",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"",lat:51.5172,lng:-0.0758,c:"diaspora",s:"A 1902 terracotta facade still reading 'Soup Kitchen for the Jewish Poor'",q:"Soup Kitchen for the Jewish Poor, Brune Street London",w:"Buff terracotta lettering still spells it out on a Spitalfields side street: 'Soup Kitchen For the Jewish Poor', dated 5662 in the Hebrew calendar, 1902 in ours. Doorways marked Way In and Way Out. In the Depression it dispensed bread, meat and soup to over 5,000 souls a week. Closed in 1992. Flats now, but the words remain.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stairwaytoheavenmemorial",n:"Stairway to Heaven Memorial",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"",lat:51.52723,lng:-0.0551,c:"history",s:"Memorial to 173 crushed to death on a tube shelter stairway in 1943",q:"Stairway to Heaven Memorial London",w:"On a wet March night in 1943, a woman stumbled on the dark stairs down to Bethnal Green's tube shelter. The crowd behind kept pushing. In minutes 173 people lay dead, crushed, no bomb involved, the worst civilian disaster of the British war. Hushed up for years. A bronze inverted staircase now hangs above the spot.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"mudchuteparkandfarm",n:"Mudchute Park and Farm",a:"Isle of Dogs",pc:"",lat:51.489,lng:-0.015,c:"green",s:"A working city farm on Victorian dock-mud heaped over WWII gun emplacements",q:"Mudchute Park and Farm London",w:"The name says it: spoil and silt pumped from Millwall Dock in the 1860s, heaped over East Ferry Road into a man-made hill. Ack-ack guns crowned it in the Blitz. Locals fought off the tower blocks in 1977 and put sheep, llamas and pigs on it instead. Now a farm grazing in Canary Wharf's shadow.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"wappinghydraulicpowersta",n:"Wapping Hydraulic Power Station",a:"Wapping",pc:"",lat:51.50748,lng:-0.05186,c:"history",s:"The last hydraulic power station on earth, once driving London's lifts by water",q:"Wapping Hydraulic Power Station London",w:"London once ran on water pressure. From 1890, this Wapping Wall station pushed 33 million gallons a week through 186 miles of buried mains, raising lifts, cranes and West End stage curtains. The pumps stopped at 8am on 1 July 1977, the last public hydraulic station on the planet. Industrial sorcery, switched off.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Wapping_Hydraulic_Power_Station_1.jpg/960px-Wapping_Hydraulic_Power_Station_1.jpg"},{id:"theguncoldharbour",n:"The Gun, Coldharbour",a:"Isle of Dogs",pc:"",lat:51.50182,lng:-0.00774,c:"pub",s:"A riverside pub where Nelson kept his trysts with Lady Hamilton, they say",q:"The Gun, Coldharbour London",w:"Tucked on the riverbank at Coldharbour, this old dockers' tavern took its name from the cannon fired when West India Docks opened in 1802. Smugglers landed contraband below, watching for the Revenue through a spy-hole on a secret stair. Legend has Nelson slipping upstairs to meet Lady Hamilton. Thames lapping at the windows.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/The_Gun%2C_Coldharbour.jpg/960px-The_Gun%2C_Coldharbour.jpg"},{id:"limehousechinatownstreet",n:"Limehouse Chinatown Street Names",a:"Poplar",pc:"",lat:51.5085,lng:-0.0145,c:"diaspora",s:"Ming, Pekin and Canton Streets, all that survives of London's first Chinatown",q:"Limehouse Chinatown Street Names London",w:"London's first Chinatown grew in Limehouse from sailors off the East India ships, a few hundred strong by the 1930s, with laundries and chophouses around Pennyfields. Pulp novels turned it into an opium fantasy. Bombs and slum clearance did the rest. All that survives now: street signs reading Ming, Pekin, Canton, Nankin. Ghost names.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thegreenbridgemileendpar",n:"The Green Bridge, Mile End Park",a:"Mile End",pc:"",lat:51.5235,lng:-0.0335,c:"oddity",s:"A grass-and-tree bridge carrying a park clean over the Mile End Road",q:"The Green Bridge, Mile End Park London",w:"Mile End Park got sliced in two by the roaring A11, so they lifted the grass over it. The Green Bridge carries trees and turf clean across the traffic, designed by local architect Piers Gough off a 1995 community weekend. Locals call it the banana bridge for its yellow belly. Walk through a park and never notice the road below.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"captaincookshousewallmil",n:"Captain Cook's House Wall, Mile End Road",a:"Mile End",pc:"",lat:51.5215,lng:-0.0463,c:"plaque",s:"A plaque on a bare wall where the explorer's London home once stood",q:"Captain Cook's House Wall, Mile End Road London",w:"James Cook kept house at 88 Mile End Road from 1764 to his death in Hawaii in 1779, returning between voyages that redrew the Pacific. In 1959 the borough knocked it down to widen a car-park entrance. All that marks the great navigator's London home now: a slate plaque, screwed to a blank brick wall. Charted the world, lost his house.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"islandgardens",n:"Island Gardens",a:"Isle of Dogs",pc:"",lat:51.4878,lng:-0.0098,c:"view",s:"The exact riverbank spot of Canaletto's famous Greenwich view",q:"Island Gardens London",w:"Stand at the Isle of Dogs' southern tip and the river frames Greenwich exactly as Canaletto painted it in the 1750s: the Queen's House threaded between Wren's twin domes, the Observatory on its hill. Wren reputedly studied his hospital from this very bank. A glazed rotunda beside you drops to the foot tunnel under the Thames.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/IslandGardensDLR.jpg"},{id:"yorkhallturkishbaths",n:"York Hall Turkish Baths",a:"Bethnal Green",pc:"",lat:51.5295,lng:-0.0555,c:"oddity",s:"Edwardian-style hammam beneath an East End boxing temple",q:"York Hall Turkish Baths London",w:"Above, the canvas where East End boys boxed for world titles since the 1940s. Below, a steam-wreathed hammam that has scrubbed Bethnal Green since 1929, opened by the future George VI on the bones of an old starch factory. One of London's last public Turkish baths. Heavyweight glory upstairs, hot marble and quiet down.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/York_Hall%2C_Old_Ford_Road%2C_E2_-_geograph.org.uk_-_394883.jpg"},{id:"stsophiasgreekcathedral",n:"St Sophia's Greek Cathedral",a:"Bayswater",pc:"",lat:51.5126,lng:-0.1894,c:"faith",s:"A Byzantine cathedral of gold mosaic behind a plain Bayswater street.",q:"St Sophia's Greek Cathedral London",w:"Behind a plain Bayswater street front opens a blaze of gold: a Byzantine-revival cathedral raised by London's Greek merchant colony in 1879, its dome and apse smothered in glittering mosaic, some by Boris Anrep. The poet Seferis worshipped here; the bells still ring for the diaspora. Constantinople, off the Moscow Road.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Saint_Sophia_Cathedral%2C_Moscow_Road_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Saint_Sophia_Cathedral%2C_Moscow_Road_-_panoramio.jpg"},{id:"stcuthbertsearlscourt",n:"St Cuthbert's, Earls Court",a:"Earls Court",pc:"",lat:51.4915,lng:-0.1985,c:"faith",s:"London's wildest Arts and Crafts church, hidden behind a plain brick front.",q:"St Cuthbert's, Earls Court London",w:"From outside, a sober brick barn; inside, the wildest Arts and Crafts church in London. Anglo-Catholic zealots filled St Cuthbert's from 1887 with a towering gilded reredos, beaten-metal Art Nouveau fittings and a sgraffito frieze. Anti-ritualists once stormed in and smashed the place up. Riotous faith behind a plain face.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/St_Cuthberts_from_the_NW_listed_building_number_1266119.jpg/960px-St_Cuthberts_from_the_NW_listed_building_number_1266119.jpg"},{id:"tombo",n:"Tombo",a:"South Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.49405,lng:-0.1731,c:"matcha",s:"Family-run Japanese cafe and matcha bar across from the museums",q:"Tombo London",w:"Across from the museum crowds, a family have whisked ceremonial-grade matcha here since 2009. Rice bowls and katsu fill the lunch trade, but regulars come for the green stuff: lattes, soft serve, parfaits stacked with red bean and white chocolate. South Ken's antidote to the chain coffee march.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"postcardteas",n:"Postcard Teas",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.5141,lng:-0.1462,c:"matcha",s:"Provenance-obsessed rare-tea shop with a postbox in the wall",q:"Postcard Teas London",w:"Tim d'Offay's tiny Dering Street shop turned tea provenance into a creed: every leaf carries its maker's name and farm. He coined 'small tea' for growers working under fifteen acres, then built a little red postbox into the wall so you can mail a tea card from the counter. Matcha you can trace to the field.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tsujiri",n:"Tsujiri",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51115,lng:-0.1331,c:"matcha",s:"Kyoto matcha house pouring soft serve down a Soho side street",q:"Tsujiri London",w:"The London arm of a Uji tea house founded in 1860, when matcha meant Kyoto and little else. On a narrow stretch of Rupert Street it grinds its own, then ranks it by strength so you can climb from gentle latte to a pour that turns properly bitter. Soft serve and warabimochi for the unconverted.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"japanhousethestand",n:"Japan House, The Stand",a:"Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.5009,lng:-0.1908,c:"matcha",s:"Takeaway tea counter inside Japan's cultural embassy on Ken High St",q:"Japan House, The Stand London",w:"Just inside Japan House, the Japanese government's polished cultural outpost on Kensington High Street, a small stand pours hot and iced matcha, roasted hojicha and sencha to take away. No queue theatre, no branding circus, just specialist leaf done plainly. Upstairs: a gallery and a shop of things you didn't know you wanted.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Attus_Amip_displayed_at_Japan_House%2C_West_London.jpg/960px-Attus_Amip_displayed_at_Japan_House%2C_West_London.jpg"},{id:"theteahouse",n:"The Tea House",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.51405,lng:-0.1264,c:"matcha",s:"Loose-leaf specialist on Neal Street since the early Eighties",q:"The Tea House London",w:"A loose-leaf survivor on Neal Street, selling proper tea since 1982 when the street still smelled of fruit and veg. Walls of caddies, matcha and sencha among the blends, novelty teapots shaped like cats. It nearly vanished after a founder died, then quietly reopened. Covent Garden's last true tea shop.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"keikouchida",n:"Keiko Uchida",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"",lat:51.5176,lng:-0.2024,c:"matcha",s:"Tea and ceramics gallery running chanoyu workshops in Notting Hill",q:"Keiko Uchida London",w:"Keiko Uchida has whisked matcha the formal way for over twenty-five years, and her Talbot Road gallery makes a quiet shrine of it: shelves of single-estate tea beside studio ceramics chosen by hand. Book a chanoyu workshop and you learn the choreography, kettle to bowl. Ritual, not a takeaway cup.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"newcomerwines",n:"Newcomer Wines",a:"Dalston",pc:"",lat:51.5461,lng:-0.0753,c:"wine",s:"Natural wine bar that grew out of a Shoreditch shipping container",q:"Newcomer Wines London",w:"It started in a shipping container at Boxpark in 2014, an Austrian leading with the wines his home country never gets credit for. The Dalston Lane bar that followed keeps the bias: Gruner and skin-contact oddities, a back garden, shelves you drink from at shop price. Central European natural wine, championed before it was cool.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"gnarlyvines",n:"Gnarly Vines",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"",lat:51.5728,lng:-0.0188,c:"wine",s:"Neighbourhood natural wine shop and bar at the Bakers Arms",q:"Gnarly Vines London",w:"A shop that became a bar by popular demand, down by the Bakers Arms. Hundreds of biodynamic bottles line the walls; pay the corkage and drink one where you stand, or carry it out to the walled garden under the trees. Georgia, the Jura, Kent. Walthamstow's cellar door, run by people who actually taste the stock.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"terroirseastdulwich",n:"Terroirs East Dulwich",a:"East Dulwich",pc:"",lat:51.4619,lng:-0.0714,c:"wine",s:"South London survivor of the bar that lit London's natural wine fuse",q:"Terroirs East Dulwich London",w:"When the original Terroirs opened off Charing Cross in 2008, London barely knew what natural wine was; it became the bar that taught the city. The Covent Garden room has since closed, but this Lordship Lane offshoot carries the torch: bare wood, brick, a shop wall of bottles, and proper French plates to soak them up.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"theremedy",n:"The Remedy",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.5227,lng:-0.141,c:"wine",s:"Cleveland Street wine bar built by two old Terroirs hands",q:"The Remedy London",w:"Two Terroirs alumni broke away in 2013 to open this narrow room at the top of Cleveland Street, near Fitzroy Square. The brief stayed simple: mostly natural, mostly European, mostly small growers, with Italian small plates that bend toward whatever's open. A grown-up bolthole where the list rewards trusting the staff.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"highburyvintners",n:"Highbury Vintners",a:"Highbury",pc:"",lat:51.5587,lng:-0.0993,c:"wine",s:"Old off-licence reborn as one of London's sharpest wine merchants",q:"Highbury Vintners London",w:"Once a corner offie of the lottery-and-lager school, now one of the capital's most decorated merchants. Around 1,400 bottles crowd 71 Highbury Park, weighted to small growers you won't meet in a supermarket. Tastings, a tucked-in tasting room, an Islington crowd that knows its skin-contact. Quietly excellent.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"antidote",n:"Antidote",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.5136,lng:-0.1383,c:"wine",s:"Two-floor organic and biodynamic wine bar off Carnaby Street",q:"Antidote London",w:"A two-floor hold-out on Newburgh Street, behind the Carnaby foot traffic, pouring only organic and biodynamic bottles for over fifteen years. Downstairs a bar for a quick glass, upstairs a kitchen of French plates that shift weekly. The terrace seats a lucky few. Soho natural wine before the hashtag found it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"highburylibrary",n:"Highbury Library",a:"Highbury",pc:"",lat:51.5553,lng:-0.1059,c:"wine",s:"Laid-back natural wine bar with craft beer and a vinyl habit",q:"Highbury Library London",w:"Not a library, despite the name, but a snug Drayton Park bar that runs on natural wine, craft beer and a turntable. Through lockdown it survived as a bottle shop; the by-the-glass counter is back upstairs. Records spin, dogs get water and treats, the quiz fills the room. A proper neighbourhood local that happens to drink well.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"provisionswineandcheese",n:"Provisions Wine & Cheese",a:"Holloway",pc:"",lat:51.5519,lng:-0.1143,c:"wine",s:"Deli of natural wine and raw-milk cheese that flips into a tapas bar",q:"Provisions Wine & Cheese London",w:"By day a Holloway Road deli stacked with raw-milk cheese and natural wine, over a hundred of each; by night the lights drop and it turns into TOPA, a pintxos bar elbow-to-elbow with regulars. Europe's small producers under one roof, sold to take home or drink on the spot. The neighbourhood gem, relaunched.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vagabondurbanwinery",n:"Vagabond Urban Winery",a:"Canada Water",pc:"",lat:51.4983,lng:-0.0488,c:"wine",s:"Britain's largest urban winery, a working cellar you drink inside",q:"Vagabond Urban Winery London",w:"The biggest urban winery in Britain, opened late 2025 in Canada Water's Dock Shed: a hundred-tonne working cellar you can stand inside, tasting straight from tank or barrel. Grapes trucked in from within two hours of London, then more than a hundred wines on self-pour taps. Wine-making as spectator sport, by the glass.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"estorickcollection",n:"Estorick Collection",a:"Canonbury, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.54389,lng:-0.09915,c:"museum",s:"Britain's only museum of modern Italian art, hidden in a Georgian villa off a leafy square",q:"Estorick Collection London",w:"Honeymooning in 1947, an American sociologist and his wife found Boccioni's Futurist manifesto and went straight to buy out a Milanese painter's studio. The drawings and canvases they amassed now hang in a Georgian villa off Canonbury Square: Britain's only home for modern Italian art. Marinetti's speed, framed and quiet.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/07/Ardengo_Soffici%2C_1912-13%2C_Deconstruction_of_the_Planes_of_a_Lamp%2C_oil_on_panel%2C_45_x_35_cm%2C_Estorick_Collection%2C_London.jpg/960px-Ardengo_Soffici%2C_1912-13%2C_Deconstruction_of_the_Planes_of_a_Lamp%2C_oil_on_panel%2C_45_x_35_cm%2C_Estorick_Collection%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"isokonbuilding",n:"Isokon Building",a:"Belsize Park",pc:"",lat:51.55236,lng:-0.16662,c:"brutalism",s:"A 1934 concrete experiment in minimal living that sheltered Bauhaus masters and Soviet spies",q:"Isokon Building London",w:"Concrete liner of a ship, run aground in Hampstead in 1934: 36 tiny flats built for people with few possessions and no time to dust. Gropius and Breuer fled here from the Nazis, Agatha Christie wrote upstairs, and at least four Soviet agents shared the foyer. A Bauhaus dream that doubled as a spy nest.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"burghhouse",n:"Burgh House",a:"Hampstead",pc:"",lat:51.55698,lng:-0.17795,c:"museum",s:"Queen Anne house from Hampstead's spa boom, now the village's own little museum",q:"Burgh House London",w:"When Hampstead sold its chalybeate spring water as a cure, the spa doctor moved into this 1704 house and enlarged it. The wells dried up; the bricks stayed. Now it keeps the village's memory: 5,000 objects, a hoard of Helen Allingham watercolours, a panelled music room. Queen Anne brick with a spa-town heart.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"londoncanalmuseumandthei",n:"London Canal Museum & the Ice Well",a:"King's Cross",pc:"",lat:51.53435,lng:-0.12326,c:"museum",s:"A Victorian ice warehouse over two vast wells that once held Norwegian ice for ice cream",q:"London Canal Museum & the Ice Well London",w:"Carlo Gatti taught working London the taste of ice cream, then dug two thirty-metre wells under his canal-side warehouse to hold the Norwegian ice that made it. Filled with rubble in the 1950s and forgotten, one well now gapes open below the floor. The ground around it never thawed. Permafrost under King's Cross.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"canonburytower",n:"Canonbury Tower",a:"Canonbury, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5445,lng:-0.0979,c:"medieval",s:"Islington's oldest building, a Tudor brick tower with a Francis Bacon mystery on the wall",q:"Canonbury Tower London",w:"Sixty feet of Tudor brick, the oldest thing in Islington, raised for the canons before Cromwell, Bacon and Goldsmith took rooms. Legend says an imprisoned heiress was lowered from a window in a baker's basket to elope. On a wall, a name beside Elizabeth I scrubbed to a lone 'F': Baconians swear it names who really wrote Shakespeare.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Canonbury_Tower_20200803_071402.jpg/960px-Canonbury_Tower_20200803_071402.jpg"},{id:"newriverhead",n:"New River Head",a:"Finsbury, Clerkenwell",pc:"",lat:51.52797,lng:-0.11037,c:"history",s:"The terminus where a 1613 artificial river delivered clean water to thirsty London",q:"New River Head London",w:"From 1613 a hand-cut river ran forty miles out of Hertfordshire to end here in a round brick pond, then trickled into hollow elm pipes feeding the City. Windmills and horse gins pumped it uphill before Smeaton's steam took over. The water industry's birthplace, soon to reopen as a gallery for Quentin Blake's scribbles.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lauderdalehouse",n:"Lauderdale House",a:"Highgate",pc:"",lat:51.56917,lng:-0.14333,c:"history",s:"Tudor merchant's house in Waterlow Park, tangled in a Nell Gwynne legend",q:"Lauderdale House London",w:"A Lord Mayor raised this timber house in 1582; an earl gave it his name; Pepys came to dine. Legend says Nell Gwynne lodged here and dangled her infant son from an upper window, threatening to drop him until Charles II shouted 'God save the Earl of Burford!' No proof she ever stayed. Good story all the same.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Lauderdale_House.jpg/960px-Lauderdale_House.jpg"},{id:"pondsquare",n:"Pond Square",a:"Highgate",pc:"",lat:51.57165,lng:-0.1465,c:"oddity",s:"Highgate's old village square, haunted by a half-plucked ghost chicken",q:"Pond Square London",w:"On a snowy day in 1626 Francis Bacon, struck by a thought about refrigeration, stuffed a chicken with snow somewhere near here, caught a chill, and died of it. The bird, they say, never left. A half-plucked phantom fowl still flaps and shrieks around the square, witnessed by a wartime warden and a startled courting couple.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"whittingtonstone",n:"Whittington Stone",a:"Archway, Holloway",pc:"",lat:51.56619,lng:-0.13767,c:"oddity",s:"A roadside stone and a black cat marking where Dick Whittington turned again",q:"Whittington Stone London",w:"A weary kitchen boy quit London and trudged up this hill, only to hear Bow Bells peal 'turn again, thrice Lord Mayor.' He turned. He was made. So runs the legend, marked by an 1821 stone at the foot of Highgate Hill, topped since 1964 by a small black cat in polished limestone. The real Whittington never owned a cat.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Monument_and_Pub_-_%22The_Whittington_Stone%22_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1180836.jpg"},{id:"hornseytownhall",n:"Hornsey Town Hall",a:"Crouch End",pc:"",lat:51.57863,lng:-0.12275,c:"artdeco",s:"Britain's first big Modernist public building, a brick civic landmark in Crouch End",q:"Hornsey Town Hall London",w:"In 1935 a young New Zealander gave suburban Crouch End the first serious Modernist building in Britain: slim, pinkish-grey brick, a clean clock tower, Scandinavian calm. RIBA gave it a medal; film crews adopted its Deco interiors. After years dark, it reopened as serviced flats. Civic ambition, faced in beautiful brick.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Crouch_end_town_hall_ed.jpg"},{id:"crouchendclocktower",n:"Crouch End Clock Tower",a:"Crouch End",pc:"",lat:51.5795,lng:-0.1229,c:"history",s:"An 1895 Gothic tower thanking the man who saved Highgate Wood for the public",q:"Crouch End Clock Tower London",w:"Crouch End's Broadway pivots round a spiky 1895 Gothic tower raised by grateful neighbours for Henry Reader Williams, the council chairman who fought developers to keep Highgate Wood public. His bronze head sits in a niche, carved by Alfred Gilbert, who also made the Eros in Piccadilly. Gratitude, set in stone and clockwork.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Crouch_End_20_Sept_2011.jpg/960px-Crouch_End_20_Sept_2011.jpg"},{id:"thecastleclimbingcentre",n:"The Castle Climbing Centre",a:"Stoke Newington",pc:"",lat:51.56878,lng:-0.09863,c:"history",s:"A Victorian waterworks dressed as a Scottish castle, now full of climbers",q:"The Castle Climbing Centre London",w:"Cholera frightened Victorian London into cleaning its water, so the New River Company hid a pumping station inside a turreted Scottish castle on Green Lanes, all towers and battlements over steam machinery. The pumps fell silent in 1971; a listing saved the fakery. Climbers now haul up its walls. Industry in fancy dress.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tottenhamhighcross",n:"Tottenham High Cross",a:"Tottenham",pc:"",lat:51.58889,lng:-0.07028,c:"history",s:"A 17th-century brick cross marking old Tottenham, near the legendary Seven Sisters elms",q:"Tottenham High Cross London",w:"An octagonal brick cross of about 1605, Gothicised in stucco and forever mistaken for an Eleanor cross, marks the heart of old Tottenham village. A short walk south stood seven elms ringing a walnut on Page Green. Legend says seven sisters planted them before scattering. Replanted again and again, the ring still holds.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Tottenham_High_Cross_%28View_from_South_-_01%29.jpg/960px-Tottenham_High_Cross_%28View_from_South_-_01%29.jpg"},{id:"finsburyhealthcentre",n:"Finsbury Health Centre",a:"Finsbury",pc:"",lat:51.52487,lng:-0.10882,c:"history",s:"Lubetkin's 1938 health centre that promised free care a decade before the NHS",q:"Finsbury Health Centre London",w:"Moscow-trained Lubetkin built this in 1938 for a Finsbury choked with soot and tuberculosis, and gave the poor free, modern medicine a full decade before the NHS existed. Where older clinics loomed Gothic and charged at the door, his glided in glass and light, open and free. 'Nothing is too good for ordinary people.'",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Finsbury_Health_Centre_%28February_2019%29.jpg/960px-Finsbury_Health_Centre_%28February_2019%29.jpg"},{id:"veeraswamy",n:"Veeraswamy",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.5099,lng:-0.13792,c:"food",s:"Britain's oldest surviving Indian restaurant, serving curry above Regent Street since 1926",q:"Veeraswamy London",w:"Britain's oldest surviving Indian restaurant opened in 1926, the venture of a retired Anglo-Indian army officer who brought the Raj club dining room to Regent Street. Nehru, Indira Gandhi and Chaplin supposedly ate here; the spices still arrive by the sackful. A century up the stairs.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Veeraswamy_2008_07_01.jpg/960px-Veeraswamy_2008_07_01.jpg"},{id:"tayyabs",n:"Tayyabs",a:"Whitechapel",pc:"",lat:51.51724,lng:-0.0636,c:"food",s:"Sizzling Punjabi grill house in Whitechapel, family-run since 1972 and famous for the queue",q:"Tayyabs London",w:"Mohammed Tayyab opened a tiny cafe off Whitechapel Road in 1972; his sons turned it into three sizzling floors of Punjabi grill. Lamb chops arrive spitting on hot iron, you bring your own beer, and the queue out the door became the most famous in the East End. Chaos, marinated.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"lahorekebabhouse",n:"Lahore Kebab House",a:"Whitechapel",pc:"",lat:51.51447,lng:-0.06314,c:"food",s:"Whitechapel Pakistani institution grilling tandoori meat and ghee-rich curry since 1972",q:"Lahore Kebab House London",w:"From a small Umberston Street diner in 1972 to a cavernous canteen seating hundreds, this place built its name on ghee-laden karahi and lamb chops charred over fierce heat. Diners travel across the city and queue at weekends for Punjabi cooking with no airs. Bring an appetite and a bottle.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"jsheekey",n:"J Sheekey",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.51092,lng:-0.1277,c:"food",s:"Theatreland's red-fronted seafood warren, serving oysters off St Martin's Court since 1896",q:"J Sheekey London",w:"In 1896 Lord Salisbury let an oyster man named Josef Sheekey serve fish in St Martin's Court, on condition he cater the PM's post-theatre suppers. The red frontage and warren of little rooms still pull in actors after curtain-down, walls thick with their photographs. Theatreland's fish counter.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"bleedingheart",n:"Bleeding Heart",a:"Farringdon",pc:"",lat:51.51918,lng:-0.10697,c:"food",s:"French bistro hidden in a cobbled yard tangled in a gruesome murder legend",q:"Bleeding Heart London",w:"French cooking served around a cobbled courtyard with a grisly name. Legend says Lady Elizabeth Hatton was found here torn limb from limb, her heart still pumping blood onto the stones; the dull truth says she died in bed. Dickens put the Plornishes in this yard. Steak frites, with folklore.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Bleeding_Heart_Yard_%28by_Orphan_Wiki%29.JPG/960px-Bleeding_Heart_Yard_%28by_Orphan_Wiki%29.JPG"},{id:"lescargot2",n:"L'Escargot",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51397,lng:-0.13096,c:"food",s:"London's oldest French restaurant, named for the snails farmed in its Soho basement",q:"L'Escargot London",w:"Georges Gaudin became the first man to serve snails in England, then in 1927 moved to Greek Street and let customers rename the place after his signature dish. He ran a snail farm in the basement, a talking point for Soho. Coco Chanel and de Gaulle ate here. The garlic butter endures.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Frontage_of_L%27Escargot_Restaurant_Soho_London_-_2024.jpg/960px-Frontage_of_L%27Escargot_Restaurant_Soho_London_-_2024.jpg"},{id:"theivyweststreet",n:"The Ivy (West Street)",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.51287,lng:-0.12811,c:"food",s:"The original theatreland canteen, an unlicensed cafe in 1917 that became London's starriest table",q:"The Ivy (West Street) London",w:"It began in 1917 as a lino-floored cafe with no drinks licence, the waitresses nipping to the pub for a customer's wine. Sending hot meals to actors across the road built its theatrical fame. Legend says the name came from a star's line: we will cling together like the ivy. So it did.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/26_West_Street%2C_London_%283%29.jpg/960px-26_West_Street%2C_London_%283%29.jpg"},{id:"bentleysoysterbarandgril",n:"Bentley's Oyster Bar & Grill",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.50946,lng:-0.13774,c:"food",s:"Marble-topped oyster bar off Piccadilly, shucking on the same spot since 1916",q:"Bentley's Oyster Bar & Grill London",w:"Bill Bentley sold oysters from a barrow by Clacton pier, bought his own beds at West Mersea, then in 1916 opened this oyster bar down a Mayfair side street. The marble counter where shuckers have worked for over a century stands unchanged. Order a dozen native, watch the knife. Briny ritual.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Bentleys_sign_2.jpg/960px-Bentleys_sign_2.jpg"},{id:"quaglinos",n:"Quaglino's",a:"St James's",pc:"",lat:51.50745,lng:-0.13884,c:"food",s:"Cavernous art-deco supper room that fed Mrs Simpson, the Prince of Wales and bright young things",q:"Quaglino's London",w:"Giovanni Quaglino opened this glittering basement in 1929; the Mountbattens, Evelyn Waugh and the Prince of Wales with Mrs Simpson swept down its staircase. Shuttered in 1977, Conran resurrected it in 1993 as the loudest table in town. Make an entrance, then order the seafood platter. Pure deco.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"scotts2",n:"Scott's",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.50982,lng:-0.15093,c:"food",s:"Mount Street oyster house, founded 1851, where Ian Fleming took his martini shaken not stirred",q:"Scott's London",w:"An 1851 oyster warehouse turned Mayfair grandee, draped in green and crowded with art. Ian Fleming dined here through the 1950s, and the place claims his James Bond martini, shaken not stirred, was settled at its bar. The crushed-ice counter still gleams. Old money, on the half shell.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Scott%27s_Restaurant%2C_Mount_Street%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_948875.jpg"},{id:"bombaybrasserie",n:"Bombay Brasserie",a:"South Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.49394,lng:-0.18288,c:"food",s:"Grand 1982 Indian dining room that dragged the curry house upmarket into the Raj revival",q:"Bombay Brasserie London",w:"When it opened in 1982, the Raj-revival palm court and white-linen ambition rewrote what an Indian restaurant could be in London, marrying regional home cooking to brasserie polish. The conservatory dining room still feels like a colonial fever dream. Where the curry house grew up. Order the dhansak.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Bombay_Brasserie_%283518874566%29.jpg/960px-Bombay_Brasserie_%283518874566%29.jpg"},{id:"stmoritz",n:"St Moritz",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51452,lng:-0.13497,c:"food",s:"London's oldest Swiss restaurant, melting fondue on Wardour Street since 1974",q:"St Moritz London",w:"Cowbells, pine panelling and bubbling cheese on Wardour Street since 1974, the capital's oldest Swiss restaurant and a fondue stronghold five decades deep. The owner reckons he shifts a hundred pots a day. Spear bread, twirl, lose your fork to a forfeit. Alpine kitsch, defiantly Soho.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thegoldenhind",n:"The Golden Hind",a:"Marylebone",pc:"",lat:51.51799,lng:-0.15096,c:"food",s:"Marylebone Lane chippy frying cod in the same room since 1914",q:"The Golden Hind London",w:"Cod and chips from the same Marylebone Lane room since 1914, with an ornate old cast-iron frying range still standing in the corner like a museum piece. No licence, so bring your own bottle. Battered haddock, mushy peas, formica calm in a postcode that forgot to gentrify it. A proper chippy.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"cafediana",n:"Cafe Diana",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"",lat:51.5099,lng:-0.19265,c:"food",s:"A greasy-spoon shrine to Princess Diana, wallpapered with her photographs near Kensington Palace",q:"Cafe Diana London",w:"Every inch of wall by Kensington Palace papered with photographs of Princess Diana, who lived round the corner and reportedly dropped in. The owner treasures a signed picture she gave him in 1989. Part cafe, part roadside shrine, serving fry-ups and mezze under her gaze. Devotion with chips.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"piedaterre",n:"Pied a Terre",a:"Fitzrovia",pc:"",lat:51.5191,lng:-0.13509,c:"food",s:"Slender Charlotte Street fine-dining room that has held a Michelin star since the 1990s",q:"Pied a Terre London",w:"A narrow Charlotte Street townhouse that quietly became one of London's longest-running fine-dining survivors, open since 1991 and Michelin-starred since 1996. It launched young chefs who went on to fame while the room itself stayed small, serious and discreet. Fitzrovia's stubborn temple of the tasting menu.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Pied_%C3%A0_Terre%2C_Fitzrovia%2C_W1.jpg/960px-Pied_%C3%A0_Terre%2C_Fitzrovia%2C_W1.jpg"},{id:"monplaisir",n:"Mon Plaisir",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.51436,lng:-0.12675,c:"food",s:"London's oldest family-run French restaurant, with a zinc bar salvaged from a Lyon brothel",q:"Mon Plaisir London",w:"London's oldest family-run French restaurant, founded in 1943 and grown from one room to four, the head waiter buying it from his boss in 1972. The zinc bar reputedly came from a Lyon brothel; the original dining room hardly changed. Snails, steak and Gallic clutter off Seven Dials. Vieux Paris.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thepumphouseandlavenderp",n:"The Pumphouse & Lavender Pond",a:"Rotherhithe",pc:"",lat:51.50311,lng:-0.04472,c:"history",s:"A 1929 dock pumphouse beside a reborn timber pond",q:"The Pumphouse & Lavender Pond London",w:"They built this brick pumphouse in 1929 to keep the vast Surrey timber docks brimming with water. The pond beside it once floated imported logs so they wouldn't crack in the air. Docks gone, pumps stilled, the lot reborn in 1981 as reedbed and marsh. Industry, rewilded.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"woolwichfoottunnel",n:"Woolwich Foot Tunnel",a:"Woolwich",pc:"",lat:51.4971,lng:0.066,c:"maritime",s:"A 1912 tiled tube under the Thames for Arsenal workers",q:"Woolwich Foot Tunnel London",w:"Down a domed Edwardian rotunda, then a white-tiled tube boring 504 metres under the Thames, ten feet below the riverbed. Opened 1912 so munitions workers could reach the Royal Arsenal in any weather, dry-shod. Eighty thousand once toiled across the water. Walk it; the river hums overhead.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"statueofpeterthegreat",n:"Statue of Peter the Great",a:"Deptford",pc:"",lat:51.4872,lng:-0.0268,c:"history",s:"A wild bronze tsar where he wrecked Evelyn's house",q:"Statue of Peter the Great London",w:"A lurching bronze tsar, a throne, a cannon and a tiny dwarf, all by Chemiakin. In 1698 Peter the Great lodged nearby at Sayes Court to learn shipbuilding, then he and his crew wrecked John Evelyn's house and wheelbarrowed through his prize hedges. London's strangest statue marks the rampage.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vanbrughcastle",n:"Vanbrugh Castle",a:"Maze Hill, Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.4814,lng:0.0028,c:"follies",s:"An architect's mock-Bastille on the hill above Greenwich",q:"Vanbrugh Castle London",w:"The man who built Blenheim raised himself a toy fortress here around 1719, all turrets and arrow-slit windows. Locals dubbed it Bastille House, the legend being Vanbrugh modelled it on the Paris cell where he'd been locked up for years as a suspected spy. Baroque genius, gone gleefully gothic.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"jacobsisland",n:"Jacob's Island",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"",lat:51.5013,lng:-0.0634,c:"literary",s:"Bill Sikes met his end in Dickens's foulest slum",q:"Jacob's Island London",w:"Tidal ditches once ringed this block of rotting tenements, the water thick with carcasses and filth. Mayhew called it a pest island that smelled of the grave. Dickens sent Bill Sikes to his death here, swinging from a rooftop above Folly Ditch. Cleared in the 1860s; the dread name lingers.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stalfegechurch",n:"St Alfege Church",a:"Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.4815,lng:-0.009,c:"faith",s:"Hawksmoor's church on the spot a saint was clubbed to death",q:"St Alfege Church London",w:"Hawksmoor's great Portland-stone box, rebuilt 1714 after the old roof caved. It stands where Danish raiders murdered Archbishop Alfege in 1012, legend telling that a drunken mob pelted him with ox bones before an axe ended it. Henry VIII was christened here; Tallis lies beneath. Blood under the floor.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Southeast_View_of_Saint_Alfege%27s_Church%2C_Greenwich_%2803%29.jpg/960px-Southeast_View_of_Saint_Alfege%27s_Church%2C_Greenwich_%2803%29.jpg"},{id:"thedeptfordanchor",n:"The Deptford Anchor",a:"Deptford",pc:"",lat:51.4777,lng:-0.0261,c:"maritime",s:"A giant anchor locals fought the council to keep",q:"The Deptford Anchor London",w:"A vast iron anchor planted on the high street in 1988, a nod to Henry VIII's Royal Dockyard that launched the navy from here. The council hauled it off in 2013, blaming drinkers. Deptford revolted: four thousand signed 'Give Us Back Our Bloomin' Anchor', and back it came in 2018. Local pride, cast in iron.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Deptford_High_Street%2C_SE8_%282%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1498383.jpg"},{id:"brockleyandladywellcemet",n:"Brockley & Ladywell Cemeteries",a:"Brockley",pc:"",lat:51.457,lng:-0.036,c:"death",s:"Twin Victorian burial grounds split by an invisible line",q:"Brockley & Ladywell Cemeteries London",w:"Two cemeteries opened in 1858, side by side but divided by faith: Anglican Brockley, nonconformist Ladywell, an invisible line down the middle. Pauper 'public graves' went unmarked, shared with strangers. Now a wild green sweep of leaning stones, foxes and over six hundred war dead. Quietly enormous.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thedogandbell",n:"The Dog & Bell",a:"Deptford",pc:"",lat:51.487,lng:-0.0232,c:"pub",s:"A back-street ale house with a rare bar billiards table",q:"The Dog & Bell London",w:"Tucked down a Deptford back lane, a name in the Old Bailey records by 1814. Fifteen handpumps, a wall of Belgian bottles, and out the back a bar billiards table, almost extinct elsewhere. Once a year the regulars throw a Pickle Festival. Defiantly itself while everything around it changes.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"telegraphhillpark",n:"Telegraph Hill Park",a:"New Cross",pc:"",lat:51.4735,lng:-0.0406,c:"view",s:"Where Waterloo's victory was flashed toward London",q:"Telegraph Hill Park London",w:"Around 1795 a semaphore tower stood on this summit, one shutter-station in the Admiralty chain flashing signals between London and the coast. Word of Waterloo passed this way. The tower's long gone, the name stayed, and the steep upper park still hands you the City skyline over the rooftops. Old signals, fresh view.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenwichpowerstation",n:"Greenwich Power Station",a:"East Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.4828,lng:-0.0044,c:"history",s:"Four chimneys cut short to spare the Observatory's sky",q:"Greenwich Power Station London",w:"Four brick chimneys loom over riverside Greenwich, built from 1902 to run London's trams. The astronomers up the hill complained the smoke and height fouled their sightlines, so the stacks were lopped shorter. Coal once barged to a jetty on sixteen Doric iron columns. Still wired as the Tube's last-ditch backup.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hillyfieldsstonecircle",n:"Hilly Fields Stone Circle",a:"Brockley",pc:"",lat:51.4643,lng:-0.0238,c:"oddity",s:"A working millennium sundial of Highland granite",q:"Hilly Fields Stone Circle London",w:"Twelve granite boulders dragged down from a Highland hillside and set in a ring on the spring equinox of 2000, the stones aligned so the circle tells the time by shadow. Not ancient at all, a millennium folly by sculptor Kindersley, dreamed up by locals. Hilltop neo-megalith with a fine view thrown in.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenlanddock",n:"Greenland Dock",a:"Rotherhithe",pc:"",lat:51.4918,lng:-0.0339,c:"maritime",s:"London's oldest wet dock, named for the whalers",q:"Greenland Dock London",w:"Dug at the close of the 1690s, the oldest wet dock left on the Thames. Whaling ships limped back from the Arctic to boil blubber into lamp oil in quayside houses, the bones sold off for corsets and umbrella ribs. Now still water and yachts, but the street names, Norway, Finland, Onega, keep the cold trade alive.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"eastgreenwichpleasaunce",n:"East Greenwich Pleasaunce",a:"East Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.4846,lng:0.014,c:"death",s:"A pocket park holding three thousand reburied sailors",q:"East Greenwich Pleasaunce London",w:"A leafy local park with a secret weight: when the navy needed land in 1875, some three thousand Greenwich Pensioners were dug up and carted here, men who'd stood at Trafalgar and in the Crimea. Seamen to the west plot, officers east, even in death by rank. Children play above an old man-of-war's crew.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Greenwich_Union_Workhouse_-_map_c._1882.jpg/960px-Greenwich_Union_Workhouse_-_map_c._1882.jpg"},{id:"thecinemamuseum",n:"The Cinema Museum",a:"Kennington",pc:"",lat:51.49223,lng:-0.10517,c:"cinema",s:"Cinema history hoarded inside the Lambeth workhouse where Chaplin was admitted as a boy",q:"The Cinema Museum London",w:"Eight-year-old Charlie Chaplin landed in this Lambeth workhouse when his mother went under. Decades later two collectors filled the old master house with ushers uniforms, art deco seats and seventeen million feet of film. A shrine to the picture palace, in the building that nearly swallowed its most famous child.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Exterior_of_Cinema_Museum_lecture%2C_Kennington%2C_Lambeth.jpg/960px-Exterior_of_Cinema_Museum_lecture%2C_Kennington%2C_Lambeth.jpg"},{id:"pullmancourt",n:"Pullman Court",a:"Streatham Hill",pc:"",lat:51.4395,lng:-0.1264,c:"brutalism",s:"International-style flats designed by a 23-year-old, Bauhaus by way of Streatham",q:"Pullman Court London",w:"A 23-year-old Frederick Gibberd dropped a slice of the Bauhaus onto Streatham Hill in 1936: 218 flats in white concrete, continuous balconies, roof terraces for sunbathing and a pool, all aimed at young moderns sick of digs. Constructivism for the commuter belt. The pool's covered over now, but the rhythm holds.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"nucleardawnmural",n:"Nuclear Dawn Mural",a:"Brixton",pc:"",lat:51.4628,lng:-0.1144,c:"streetart",s:"A skeletal reaper strides over London hurling bombs, painted in 1981 against the Bomb",q:"Nuclear Dawn Mural London",w:"A colossal skeleton in the flags of the nuclear powers strides over the Thames, scattering bombs as a mushroom cloud blooms. Brian Barnes and the Carlton Mansions co-op painted it in 1981, deep in the second Cold War, when the four-minute warning felt real. A rare survivor of Brixton's mural decade. Death, draped in superpower colours.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stockwellwarmemorial",n:"Stockwell War Memorial",a:"Stockwell",pc:"",lat:51.47227,lng:-0.12281,c:"history",s:"A green-domed clock tower on a traffic island, Stockwell's own cenotaph",q:"Stockwell War Memorial London",w:"Marooned on a traffic island where Clapham Road forks, this green-domed clock tower of 1922 carries the names of Stockwell's war dead, the locals' own cenotaph. Round the corner a deep-shelter vent shaft wears a painted roll of honour to the neighbourhood, Violette Szabo and Roger Moore among them. Grief, planted at a junction.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"stockwellbusgarage",n:"Stockwell Bus Garage",a:"Stockwell",pc:"",lat:51.47466,lng:-0.12628,c:"brutalism",s:"A concrete cathedral for buses, once Europe's largest unsupported roof span",q:"Stockwell Bus Garage London",w:"Postwar steel ran short, so the engineers cast the roof in concrete instead: ten shallow arched ribs sweeping over an unbroken floor that swallows 200 buses. When it opened in 1952 the span beat anything in Europe. Grade II* now, a cathedral nave with route numbers. Light pours through the skylight ridges onto the diesel.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"tibetanpeacegarden",n:"Tibetan Peace Garden",a:"Lambeth",pc:"",lat:51.4958,lng:-0.1088,c:"faith",s:"A bronze Kalachakra mandala laid out behind the Imperial War Museum's guns",q:"Tibetan Peace Garden London",w:"Behind the Imperial War Museum's naval guns, the Dalai Lama consecrated this circle in 1999. At its heart, the first bronze cast of the Kalachakra mandala, ringed by eight meditation seats for the eightfold path, with a pillar echoing a 9th-century Lhasa treaty stone. Contemplation, planted in the shadow of artillery. Quiet defiance.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"ruskinpark2",n:"Ruskin Park",a:"Denmark Hill",pc:"",lat:51.4668,lng:-0.0919,c:"green",s:"A salvaged portico and a Mendelssohn sundial, all that survives of Denmark Hill's lost mansions",q:"Ruskin Park London",w:"Grand villas once lined Denmark Hill; when they fell, the park kept their bones. A lone stone portico, swung around to face the lawns, marks where number 170 stood, and a terracotta sundial recalls Mendelssohn lodging at long-gone Dane House in 1842. Ruskin grew up here and wrote of its wildness. Faded gentility, mown into a public green.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"theoldenglishgardenbatte",n:"The Old English Garden, Battersea Park",a:"Battersea",pc:"",lat:51.4793,lng:-0.1587,c:"green",s:"A walled secret garden inside the park, replanted by a Chelsea gold medallist",q:"The Old English Garden, Battersea Park London",w:"Slip through the high brick walls and the city drops away: a lily pond, a fountain urn, climbing roses on iron arches, a sundial. Laid out around 1900, it fell into neglect until the charity Thrive coaxed it back, gardened largely by disabled hands. A room of green walled off from the park noise.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Southwest_View_of_the_Battersea_Park_Pagoda_%2802%29.jpg/960px-Southwest_View_of_the_Battersea_Park_Pagoda_%2802%29.jpg"},{id:"streathampumpingstation",n:"Streatham Pumping Station",a:"Streatham",pc:"",lat:51.4287,lng:-0.1326,c:"oddity",s:"A neo-Byzantine water palace dressed up to placate the man who sold the land",q:"Streatham Pumping Station London",w:"The landowner would only sell on one condition: whatever they built must look handsome. So the water company gave Streatham a striped neo-Byzantine palace in 1894, banded brick and arched windows, to hide the pumps inside. Pure municipal vanity, like Abbey Mills downriver. A waterworks pretending to be a basilica.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"therookerystreathamcommo",n:"The Rookery, Streatham Common",a:"Streatham",pc:"",lat:51.4146,lng:-0.1242,c:"green",s:"Terraced spa gardens on the site of the mineral wells that made Streatham fashionable",q:"The Rookery, Streatham Common London",w:"In 1659 someone found medicinal springs up here and Streatham briefly became a spa, gentry trooping out to take the waters. The wells faded and the big house went, but the terraced gardens stayed, an original well head still among the ponds. Saved by public subscription in 1913. A vanished resort gone to azaleas.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"dulwichtollgate",n:"Dulwich Tollgate",a:"Dulwich",pc:"",lat:51.4356,lng:-0.0847,c:"oddity",s:"London's last working toll gate, still charging cars to cross a private road",q:"Dulwich Tollgate London",w:"Parliament abolished London's toll gates in 1864, but this one on College Road never got the memo. Standing since 1789, it still levies a charge to drive the private road to Sydenham Hill, the last working tollgate in the capital. The old board survives, pricing oxen, donkeys and hogs by the score. A turnpike that simply refused to die.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"louisehouse",n:"Louise House",a:"Forest Hill",pc:"",lat:51.43611,lng:-0.05556,c:"history",s:"A Victorian girls' industrial home built to train the poor in domestic service",q:"Louise House London",w:"Queen Victoria daughter laid the stone in 1890 for a home that took poor girls off the streets and trained them, scrubbing and sewing, for a life in service downstairs. An offshoot of the Ragged Schools, dressed in red-brick respectability beside the baths. Later it sheltered artists instead of orphans.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"holytrinityclapham",n:"Holy Trinity Clapham",a:"Clapham Common",pc:"",lat:51.4618,lng:-0.1386,c:"faith",s:"The church of the abolitionists, where Wilberforce's Clapham Sect plotted the end of slavery",q:"Holy Trinity Clapham London",w:"Round this plain Georgian church in the 1790s gathered the Clapham Sect, rich evangelicals who lived nearby and decided slavery had to end. Wilberforce and Thornton worshipped here, drafting campaigns between sermons. Decades of petitions and parliamentary grind launched from a common-side pew. Quiet faith with a long fuse.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"batterseadogsandcatshome",n:"Battersea Dogs and Cats Home",a:"Battersea",pc:"",lat:51.4772,lng:-0.143,c:"history",s:"A refuge for strays founded by a woman the Times mocked, defended by Dickens",q:"Battersea Dogs and Cats Home London",w:"Mary Tealby started it in her Holloway scullery in 1860, taking in the strays Victorian London left to starve. The Times sneered that fussing over dogs made an absurd waste while the poor went hungry; Dickens leapt to her defence. The shelter outlived the mockery, crossing to Battersea in 1871. Compassion, ridiculed into respectability.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sevendialsmarket",n:"Seven Dials Market",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.51442,lng:-0.12693,c:"food",s:"KERB's food hall inside a Victorian cucumber-and-banana warehouse",q:"Seven Dials Market London",w:"Bananas and cucumbers once ripened in this brick warehouse, freight for the old Covent Garden veg market a few streets over. KERB took it on in 2019 and packed twenty traders across two floors, plus a conveyor belt that ferries cheese past your stool. Fruit store turned feed trough.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Seven_Dials_%28Geograph-5808655-by-John-Sutton%29.jpg/960px-Seven_Dials_%28Geograph-5808655-by-John-Sutton%29.jpg"},{id:"mercatometropolitano",n:"Mercato Metropolitano",a:"Elephant and Castle",pc:"",lat:51.4962,lng:-0.09926,c:"food",s:"Sprawling Italian-led market in a former paper warehouse, final summer at Newington Causeway",q:"Mercato Metropolitano London",w:"Born at Milan's 2015 Expo, this rambling Italian market landed in a derelict Elephant paper warehouse in 2016 and never tidied up the edges. Now living its last summer before 38-storey towers take the plot: World Cup screenings, kilo sales, beer in the yard. Going out loud, not quietly.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"popbrixton",n:"Pop Brixton",a:"Brixton",pc:"",lat:51.46406,lng:-0.1124,c:"food",s:"Shipping-container village of micro-restaurants on a former car park",q:"Pop Brixton London",w:"A council car park rose into a stack of painted shipping containers in 2015, meant to last three years. It refused. Ten kitchens, three bars, a greenhouse and a pirate-spirited radio station crammed into the steel, smoke drifting over the balcony. Temporary, on paper only.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brixtonvillageandmarketr",n:"Brixton Village and Market Row",a:"Brixton",pc:"",lat:51.46247,lng:-0.115,c:"food",s:"Grade II listed 1920s arcades, heart of Afro-Caribbean London turned food destination",q:"Brixton Village and Market Row London",w:"Covered arcades from 1928 and 1937, where Windrush-era Brixton found its yams, salt fish and barbers. Threatened with demolition, then listed in 2010 for exactly that social history. Now a hundred traders trade curries and natural wine under the same concrete trusses. Heritage you can eat.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/BrixtonMarket.jpg"},{id:"netilmarket",n:"Netil Market",a:"London Fields",pc:"",lat:51.53889,lng:-0.05847,c:"food",s:"Compact weekend yard of street-food kitchens and makers behind London Fields",q:"Netil Market London",w:"Tucked behind Broadway Market on an old cab-and-bus yard, this scruffy weekend pocket trades in tacos, dumplings and flat whites from a huddle of sheds and containers. Makers sell alongside the cooks; a rooftop bar perches above. Small, sharp-elbowed, gloriously East London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"whitecrossstreetmarket",n:"Whitecross Street Market",a:"Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5226,lng:-0.0923,c:"food",s:"Lunchtime street-food market on one of London's oldest market streets",q:"Whitecross Street Market London",w:"Costermongers worked this street under Victoria, and possibly back in the 1600s; the Victorians called it 'Squalors' Market' for its poverty. The grime has gone, the trade hasn't. Fifty weekday stalls now fling Kurdish wraps and bibimbap at the lunchtime office crowd. Old bones, new queues.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Whitecross_Debtors_Prison_1813_-_1870.jpg/960px-Whitecross_Debtors_Prison_1813_-_1870.jpg"},{id:"modelmarket",n:"Model Market",a:"Lewisham",pc:"",lat:51.45447,lng:-0.0117,c:"food",s:"A derelict 1950s indoor market reborn as a neon night-time street-food haunt",q:"Model Market London",w:"A 1950s covered market that sat shuttered for years, its old shopfronts strung with bare bulbs and reborn after dark as a street-food warren. Street Feast ran it, then folded; brewer German Kraft revives the whole gritty-glamorous thing in summer 2026. Lewisham's dressed-up ruin, back open.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"flatironsquare",n:"Flat Iron Square",a:"Southwark",pc:"",lat:51.50488,lng:-0.0939,c:"food",s:"Street-food courtyard threaded through seven Victorian railway arches",q:"Flat Iron Square London",w:"Seven brick railway arches near Borough, stitched into one rowdy courtyard of souvlaki, fried chicken and Neapolitan pizza, trains rumbling overhead. A taproom brews on site and the Omeara stage hides round the back. Bankside's old goods yard, now fed and watered.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"chrispstreetmarket",n:"Chrisp Street Market",a:"Poplar",pc:"",lat:51.5124,lng:-0.0141,c:"food",s:"Britain's first purpose-built pedestrian shopping precinct, under a Festival of Britain clock tower",q:"Chrisp Street Market London",w:"Visitors paid to tour this as the future in 1951: Frederick Gibberd's Lansbury Estate, the first purpose-built pedestrian precinct in Britain, shown off at the Festival of Britain. Stalls still cluster under his Grade II clock tower in Poplar. Tomorrow's world, gone to lunch.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Chrisp_Street_Market_Clock_Tower%2C_Poplar_%28Southeast_View_-_01%29.jpg/960px-Chrisp_Street_Market_Clock_Tower%2C_Poplar_%28Southeast_View_-_01%29.jpg"},{id:"struttongroundmarket",n:"Strutton Ground Market",a:"Westminster",pc:"",lat:51.49736,lng:-0.1352,c:"food",s:"Victorian Westminster street market that once supplied Parliament's flowers",q:"Strutton Ground Market London",w:"Traders set up here around 1862, and between the wars the stalls cut flowers for grand do's at the Houses of Parliament up the road. The bouquets gave way to bibimbap and Korean rice boxes, sold to civil servants between noon and two. Peak chaos at one o'clock sharp.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Strutton_Ground_market_-_geograph.org.uk_-_679945.jpg"},{id:"romanroadmarket",n:"Roman Road Market",a:"Bow",pc:"",lat:51.5294,lng:-0.0335,c:"food",s:"150-year-old East End market that once defied attempts to shut it down",q:"Roman Road Market London",w:"Costermongers started trading here in 1843 when the market broke the law, and the stalls outlasted every effort to close them, legal at last by 1888. The Roman road to Colchester runs buried beneath. Sylvia Pankhurst rallied here; grime later found its feet. East End grit, three days a week.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"watneymarket",n:"Watney Market",a:"Shadwell",pc:"",lat:51.5119,lng:-0.0567,c:"food",s:"Bombed, rebuilt East End market where Sainsbury opened his first shop",q:"Watney Market London",w:"At number 68, in 1881, a young grocer named John James Sainsbury opened the first of his shops. By 1902 over a hundred stalls jammed Watney Street; the Blitz then flattened much of it. Rebuilt and pedestrianised, it still feeds Shadwell six days a week. Where a supermarket empire was born.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"greenwichmarket",n:"Greenwich Market",a:"Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.4815,lng:-0.0093,c:"food",s:"Covered market trading under a 1700 royal charter, now packed with food stalls",q:"Greenwich Market London",w:"A royal charter regulated this market back in 1700; by 1831 it traded meat and fish under a roof, slaughterhouses ringing the edges. The blood and bone are long gone. Now Thai curries, dumplings and craft stalls fill the glass-roofed hall in the heart of maritime Greenwich. Chartered, covered, ravenous.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"vinegaryard",n:"Vinegar Yard",a:"London Bridge",pc:"",lat:51.5033,lng:-0.0849,c:"food",s:"Street-food and flea-market yard with a graffiti tube carriage on the roof",q:"Vinegar Yard London",w:"An old yard by London Bridge, named for the vinegar trade that once soured the air, dressed up with junk-art: a graffiti-bombed tube carriage perched overhead, a stray dinosaur. Burgers and croquettes by night, a vintage flea market by weekend day. Scruffy, theatrical, very Bermondsey-adjacent.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"brockleymarket",n:"Brockley Market",a:"Brockley",pc:"",lat:51.4657,lng:-0.0236,c:"food",s:"Saturday farmers-and-street-food market in a Lewisham college car park",q:"Brockley Market London",w:"Every Saturday a college car park on Lewisham Way fills with farmers, fishmongers and a cult burger stall called Mother Flipper, the queue snaking past wheels of Alpine cheese. No frills, no roof, just seasonal produce and griddle smoke since 2011. South London does its weekly shop, then eats it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"sambournehouse18stafford",n:"Sambourne House (18 Stafford Terrace)",a:"Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.50143,lng:-0.1942,c:"museum",s:"Punch cartoonist's untouched Aesthetic-era townhouse, frozen since 1875",q:"Sambourne House (18 Stafford Terrace) London",w:"Edward Linley Sambourne drew for Punch and never threw anything out. His Kensington terrace, redecorated in 1875 in full Aesthetic clutter of Morris wallpaper, stained glass and blue-and-white china, stayed untouched after his death; his son kept it as a shrine. The best-preserved Victorian home anywhere. A house that forgot to move on.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"hydeparkpetcemetery",n:"Hyde Park Pet Cemetery",a:"Bayswater",pc:"",lat:51.5122,lng:-0.1817,c:"death",s:"Tiny Victorian graveyard of beloved dogs behind a park lodge",q:"Hyde Park Pet Cemetery London",w:"Behind Victoria Lodge, off Bayswater Road, sit hundreds of headstones no bigger than house-bricks. A park keeper began burying pets here around 1881, starting with a Maltese named Cherry; some thousand dogs, cats, monkeys and birds followed before it closed. Peer through the railings at the saddest little necropolis in London.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"michelinhouse",n:"Michelin House",a:"South Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.4926,lng:-0.1692,c:"artdeco",s:"Art Nouveau tyre depot studded with Bibendum stained glass",q:"Michelin House London",w:"Michelin's UK depot, opened 1911 and drawn by one of their own engineers, turned tyre-fitting into pageantry: jolly Bibendum glaring out of stained glass, racing cars rendered in coloured tiles, a former fitting bay now a smart restaurant. The wartime windows vanished and were remade from old posters. Art Nouveau, in rubber.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Michelin_House%2C_London_-_2022-09-04_%281%29.jpg/960px-Michelin_House%2C_London_-_2022-09-04_%281%29.jpg"},{id:"thetroubadour",n:"The Troubadour",a:"Earl's Court",pc:"",lat:51.49,lng:-0.1909,c:"music",s:"1950s coffee house where Dylan played a London cellar",q:"The Troubadour London",w:"Pete Seeger told a young Bob Dylan to find Anthea at the Troubadour; in 1962 he turned up and played the cellar as Blind Boy Grunt. Open since 1954, this Old Brompton Road coffee house kept the British folk revival alive downstairs while bohemians argued upstairs. Coffee, candlelight and a sticky little stage.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/2d/Troubadour%2C_London%2C_18_Apr_2009.jpg"},{id:"crosbymoranhall",n:"Crosby Moran Hall",a:"Chelsea",pc:"",lat:51.4829,lng:-0.1732,c:"medieval",s:"Medieval merchant's hall moved stone by stone from the City",q:"Crosby Moran Hall London",w:"London's only surviving medieval merchant's hall sits in the wrong place on purpose. Raised in Bishopsgate in 1466 and once held by Thomas More, the Great Hall faced demolition in 1910, so it was dismantled into 1,500 numbered pieces and rebuilt by the Chelsea river. Now wrapped inside a billionaire's private house.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Crosby_Hall_2021.jpg/960px-Crosby_Hall_2021.jpg"},{id:"stmaryabbots",n:"St Mary Abbots",a:"Kensington",pc:"",lat:51.5013,lng:-0.1935,c:"faith",s:"London's tallest church spire, looming over Kensington",q:"St Mary Abbots London",w:"The tallest church spire in London hides in plain sight above Kensington High Street: 278 feet of Gilbert Scott neo-Gothic, finished only after he died. Reach it through a long, hushed Victorian cloister that swallows the traffic noise. Modelled on Bristol's St Mary Redcliffe, and quietly outsoaring everything around it.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thetabernacle",n:"The Tabernacle",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"",lat:51.51691,lng:-0.20176,c:"music",s:"Squatted Victorian church turned Carnival-born arts centre",q:"The Tabernacle London",w:"A curved red-brick Romanesque church went up on Powis Square in 1887; by 1973 squatters had taken it and locals were demanding it as a community hall. Carnival steel bands rehearsed inside, and it became the cultural engine of Notting Hill. Now theatre, gallery and garden bar, held on a peppercorn rent. The people won this one.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Tabernacle_1.jpg/960px-Tabernacle_1.jpg"},{id:"hollandhouseruins",n:"Holland House Ruins",a:"Holland Park",pc:"",lat:51.5013,lng:-0.2044,c:"history",s:"Bombed Jacobean mansion, half ruin half garden theatre",q:"Holland House Ruins London",w:"Cope Castle went up in 1605 and grew into the grandest salon in Whig London, until incendiaries gutted it one night in September 1940. Only the east wing and a skeletal arcade survived. Each summer opera is staged against that roofless wall while peacocks stalk the lawns. Faded grandeur with a stage set built from ruin.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"thecoronet",n:"The Coronet",a:"Notting Hill Gate",pc:"",lat:51.50865,lng:-0.19798,c:"cinema",s:"Faded 1898 playhouse turned cinema turned wild little theatre",q:"The Coronet London",w:"Sprague built this Notting Hill Gate playhouse in 1898; Bernhardt and Ellen Terry played it before the talkies turned it into a cinema for seventy years. The faded gilt and worn velvet never got tidied up, which is exactly the appeal. Now a fearless little theatre that leaves the peeling grandeur on show. Beautifully unrestored.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Coronet_Theatre%2C_London.jpg/960px-Coronet_Theatre%2C_London.jpg"},{id:"fulhampotterykiln",n:"Fulham Pottery Kiln",a:"Fulham",pc:"",lat:51.469,lng:-0.2081,c:"history",s:"Lone bottle kiln, last relic of England's oldest stoneware works",q:"Fulham Pottery Kiln London",w:"John Dwight set up his stoneware works by Putney Bridge in 1672, the first documented English stoneware maker. The pottery ran for three centuries, then fell to redevelopment in 1975, with a single brick bottle kiln spared. It stands marooned among newer blocks on New King's Road. One stubborn chimney where an industry began.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Fulham_Pottery%2C_September_2016_01.jpg/960px-Fulham_Pottery%2C_September_2016_01.jpg"},{id:"stlukeschurchchelsea",n:"St Luke's Church, Chelsea",a:"Chelsea",pc:"",lat:51.4878,lng:-0.1688,c:"faith",s:"Early Gothic Revival church where Dickens married",q:"St Luke's Church, Chelsea London",w:"Two days after the first Pickwick Papers appeared, Charles Dickens married Catherine Hogarth here, in 1836. James Savage's 1819 church gambled on real stone vaulting and flying buttresses decades before Gothic Revival went mainstream. Tall, pale and earnest off the King's Road. A wedding venue with a stone ceiling that actually holds.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"chelseaoldchurch",n:"Chelsea Old Church",a:"Chelsea",pc:"",lat:51.4819,lng:-0.1697,c:"faith",s:"River church with Thomas More's surviving Tudor chapel",q:"Chelsea Old Church London",w:"Thomas More rebuilt the south chapel here in 1528, with capitals supposedly drawn by Holbein, and worshipped a short walk from his Chelsea house. A parachute mine flattened most of the church in 1941, yet More's chapel rode out the blast. Rebuilt stone by stone on its old footprint by the river. Tudor survival, rescued from rubble.",city:"london",wq:"a",ph:"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Chelsea_Old_Church.jpg/960px-Chelsea_Old_Church.jpg"},{id:"voyseyhouse",n:"Voysey House",a:"Chiswick",pc:"",lat:51.4921,lng:-0.2567,c:"artdeco",s:"White-tiled wallpaper factory, a proto-Modernist landmark",q:"Voysey House London",w:"Voysey designed wallpapers for Sanderson before he designed their factory, and in 1902 gave Chiswick a building decades ahead of itself: crisp white glazed brick banded in blue, all clean verticals. Architectural historians call it a hinge toward Modernism. A wallpaper shed that changed buildings.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"worldsend430kingsroad",n:"World's End (430 King's Road)",a:"Chelsea",pc:"",lat:51.4833,lng:-0.1845,c:"history",s:"Westwood's punk shop with a 13-hour clock spinning backwards",q:"World's End (430 King's Road) London",w:"At the unfashionable end of the King's Road, Vivienne Westwood and Malcolm McLaren ran the shop that birthed punk, trading as Let It Rock, then SEX, then Seditionaries. Reborn as Worlds End in 1979 with a tilting galleon floor and a 13-hour clock whirling backwards over the door. Still Westwood's, time still running the wrong way.",city:"london",wq:"a"},{id:"londonsilvervaults",n:"London Silver Vaults",a:"Chancery Lane, City of London",pc:"",lat:51.5172,lng:-0.1126,c:"history",s:"The bomb-proof bunker under Chancery Lane that survived the Blitz",q:"London Silver Vaults London",w:"Forty-plus shops of silver in a bunker under Chancery Lane, many family businesses for three generations. During the Blitz a direct hit destroyed the building above; the vaults — walls 1.2 metres of steel-lined concrete — survived intact. World's largest collection of silver for public sale.",city:"london"},{id:"bradystreetcemetery",n:"Brady Street Cemetery",a:"Whitechapel, Tower Hamlets",pc:"",lat:51.5219,lng:-0.0607,c:"death",s:"Whitechapel's forgotten Ashkenazi burial ground — opened 1761, closed by 1857",q:"Brady Street Cemetery London",w:"One of London's oldest Ashkenazi Jewish burial grounds, laid out in Whitechapel in 1761 and closed to burials less than a century later. The community had moved east. Headstones lean in the long grass, Hebrew letters worn smooth. A quiet acre that time and the city forgot together.",city:"london"},{id:"goldenlaneestate",n:"Golden Lane Estate",a:"Barbican, City of London",pc:"",lat:51.5226,lng:-0.0961,c:"brutalism",s:"The Barbican's dry run — built on City bombsite rubble by the same architects",q:"Golden Lane Estate London",w:"Chamberlin, Powell and Bon built this estate on City of London bombsite rubble in the 1950s before turning to the Barbican next door. Great Arthur House was Britain's tallest residential block when completed. Grade II listed since 1997 — a proof of concept for everything that followed.",city:"london"},{id:"crossrailplaceroofgarden",n:"Crossrail Place Roof Garden",a:"Canary Wharf, Tower Hamlets",pc:"",lat:51.5061,lng:-0.0176,c:"green",s:"Free botanical garden on a roof, under the UK's largest timber structure",q:"Crossrail Place Roof Garden London",w:"Free public garden on the roof of the Elizabeth line's Canary Wharf station, sheltered by the largest timber structure in the UK — a lattice canopy by Foster + Partners. Plants from east and west hemispheres face each other across a meridian line. Winner of the 2017 European Garden Award.",city:"london"},{id:"willesdenjewishcemetery",n:"Willesden Jewish Cemetery",a:"Willesden Green, Brent",pc:"",lat:51.5454,lng:-0.2403,c:"death",s:"Rosalind Franklin is buried here. So is Nathan Rothschild.",q:"Willesden Jewish Cemetery London",w:"Rosalind Franklin is buried here, alongside Nathan Rothschild, 1st Baron. Opened 1873, the cemetery holds 29,800 graves and a Grade II listing on Historic England's parks register — once nicknamed the 'Rolls-Royce' of London's Jewish cemeteries. Known to few outside the community.",city:"london"},{id:"highgatewood",n:"Highgate Wood",a:"Muswell Hill, Haringey",pc:"",lat:51.5824,lng:-0.1519,c:"green",s:"Ancient woodland recorded in the Domesday Book, managed by the City of London",q:"Highgate Wood London",w:"Ancient woodland between Highgate, Muswell Hill and East Finchley — part of the old Forest of Middlesex, recorded in the Domesday Book. Twenty-eight hectares managed by the City of London Corporation, miles from the Square Mile. A fragment older than the city that surrounds it.",city:"london"},{id:"fitzroytavern",n:"Fitzroy Tavern",a:"Fitzrovia, Camden",pc:"",lat:51.5186,lng:-0.1346,c:"pub",s:"The pub that named Fitzrovia — Dylan Thomas, Orwell, Augustus John, all regulars",q:"Fitzroy Tavern London",w:"The pub that named a neighbourhood. Judah Kleinfeld hung out a sign reading 'Fitzroy Tavern' in 1919 and the streets around it became Fitzrovia. Through the 1920s to 50s the bar packed in Dylan Thomas, George Orwell, Augustus John and Nina Hamnett. Samuel Smith's now. The spirit remains.",city:"london"},{id:"spagreenestate",n:"Spa Green Estate",a:"Clerkenwell, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5288,lng:-0.1055,c:"brutalism",s:"Lubetkin's Clerkenwell block — restored in 2008 to its Constructivist colours",q:"Spa Green Estate London",w:"Lubetkin's most complete post-war realisation of his 1930s radical vision, restored in 2008 to its original palette — Russian Constructivist reds and blues recalled from his Moscow contacts. Grade II* listed, squeezed between Rosebery Avenue and St John Street in Clerkenwell. Living proof.",city:"london"},{id:"bevincourt",n:"Bevin Court",a:"Finsbury, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5304,lng:-0.1131,c:"brutalism",s:"Lenin lived here. Lubetkin buried a bronze memorial to him under the staircase.",q:"Bevin Court London",w:"Lenin edited Iskra here in 1902, exiled in a Finsbury flat. Lubetkin planned to call the new block 'Lenin Court' after the war; the Cold War renamed it Bevin Court instead. His tribute — a bronze memorial — now lies buried under the constructivist staircase at the heart of it. Grade II*.",city:"london"},{id:"graysinngardens",n:"Gray's Inn Gardens",a:"Holborn, Camden",pc:"",lat:51.5255,lng:-0.1159,c:"history",s:"The Walks open since 1597 — and Shakespeare's Comedy of Errors first played here",q:"Gray's Inn Gardens London",w:"The Walks have been open since at least 1597. Shakespeare's Comedy of Errors is believed to have had its first performance in the hall just behind. Francis Bacon lived and worked at Gray's Inn, shaping the garden. Still closed on weekday lunchtimes, briefly open otherwise. Blink and miss it.",city:"london"},{id:"beckenhamplaceparklake",n:"Beckenham Place Park Lake",a:"Beckenham, Lewisham",pc:"",lat:51.4188,lng:-0.0135,c:"lido",s:"London's only natural swimming lake open to the public — and once a golf course",q:"Beckenham Place Park Lake London",w:"The only natural open-water swimming lake open to the public in London — a Victorian boating lake restored as a wild swimming spot in 2019, after decades as a golf course. A Palladian mansion watches from the hill above. Ninety-six hectares of Lewisham that feel nothing like it.",city:"london"},{id:"fentonhouse",n:"Fenton House",a:"Hampstead, Camden",pc:"",lat:51.559,lng:-0.18,c:"museum",s:"A 1693 merchant's house where harpsichords are sometimes played for visitors",q:"Fenton House London",w:"Built by a merchant in 1693 and sitting in its walled Hampstead garden ever since, Fenton House holds the Benton Fletcher collection of early keyboard instruments — harpsichords and spinets sometimes played for visitors during opening hours. Grade I listed. One acre. National Trust.",city:"london"},{id:"londoncharterhouse",n:"London Charterhouse",a:"Clerkenwell, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5215,lng:-0.1001,c:"history",s:"Monks died here for refusing Henry VIII. Brothers still live here today.",q:"London Charterhouse London",w:"Founded on a 1348 Black Death burial ground, it became a Carthusian priory in 1371. At dissolution in 1537, Prior Houghton was hanged, drawn and quartered at Tyburn. Elizabeth I used the buildings for coronation preparations. An almshouse since 1611 — Brothers still live here today. Tours most days.",city:"london"},{id:"arnosgrovestation",n:"Arnos Grove Station",a:"Arnos Grove, Enfield",pc:"",lat:51.6126,lng:-0.1251,c:"history",s:"Holden's 1932 brick drum — one of twelve Great Modern Buildings per The Guardian",q:"Arnos Grove Station London",w:"Charles Holden designed this 1932 Piccadilly line station after a Scandinavian study tour — the circular brick drum of the ticket hall borrowed from Swedish modernism. Grade II* listed since 2011. The Guardian named it one of twelve Great Modern Buildings. A masterpiece in the ordinary commuter belt.",city:"london"},{id:"dollisbrookviaduct",n:"Dollis Brook Viaduct",a:"West Finchley, Barnet",pc:"",lat:51.6051,lng:-0.2021,c:"history",s:"The highest point on the London Underground above ground — 60 feet over a stream",q:"Dollis Brook Viaduct London",w:"The highest above-ground point on the whole London Underground — a Northern line viaduct nearly 60 feet above Dollis Brook in West Finchley, carrying trains of the old Edgware, Highgate and London Railway. Walk beneath it on the loop path; the Tube carriages overhead seem remarkably small.",city:"london"},{id:"skinnershall",n:"Skinners' Hall",a:"Dowgate Hill, City",pc:"",lat:51.5111,lng:-0.0916,c:"livery",s:"At sixes and sevens since 1484",q:"Skinners' Hall London",w:"'At sixes and sevens' comes directly from here — Skinners and Merchant Taylors have disputed sixth place in the livery order since the Lord Mayor's arbitration of 1484, alternating annually ever since. William Jupp's hall of 1770 replaced three predecessors. Oldest unsettled argument in the City.",city:"london"},{id:"tallowchandlershall",n:"Tallow Chandlers' Hall",a:"Dowgate Hill, City",pc:"",lat:51.5114,lng:-0.0913,c:"livery",s:"Animal-fat candle guild, Dowgate Hill since 1462",q:"Tallow Chandlers' Hall London",w:"Before gas and electricity, London ran on tallow — rendered animal fat, shaped into candles by this guild. Their hall on Dowgate Hill has stood since a royal charter of 1462, one of the oldest continuously occupied livery sites in the City. The company now tests lubricants. Candlemakers turned oil inspectors.",city:"london"},{id:"vintnershall",n:"Vintners' Hall",a:"Upper Thames Street, City",pc:"",lat:51.5107,lng:-0.0936,c:"livery",s:"Two nicks mark a royal swan",q:"Vintners' Hall London",w:"One of only three bodies allowed to own mute swans on the Thames, the Vintners mark theirs with two nicks on the beak at the annual Swan Upping. Their hall dates to 1446 and the wine cellar holds around 16,000 bottles. Royal privilege, carved into every swan's bill. Still.",city:"london"},{id:"charteredaccountantshall",n:"Chartered Accountants' Hall",a:"Moorgate Place, City",pc:"",lat:51.5164,lng:-0.0882,c:"finance",s:"The City's only indoor bridge",q:"Chartered Accountants' Hall London",w:"Built in 1893 for the Institute of Chartered Accountants, this Neo-Baroque pile contains the only indoor bridge in the City — a glass-roofed loggia between two wings. Hamo Thornycroft carved the friezes of Commerce and Industry outside. Extended in 1930 and 1970. Accountancy's most theatrical address.",city:"london"},{id:"royalexchange",n:"Royal Exchange",a:"Cornhill, City",pc:"",lat:51.5136,lng:-0.0872,c:"finance",s:"Elizabeth I named it; proclamations still read from the steps",q:"Royal Exchange London",w:"Thomas Gresham founded it in 1571; Elizabeth I visited and granted it the name herself. The current William Tite colonnade dates to 1844 after two earlier buildings burned. Leighton murals inside; royal proclamations still read from the steps. Boutiques where merchants once haggled.",city:"london"},{id:"londonmetropolitanarchiv",n:"London Metropolitan Archives",a:"Northampton Road, Clerkenwell",pc:"",lat:51.5249,lng:-0.1079,c:"archive",s:"72 miles of London records, back to 1067",q:"London Metropolitan Archives London",w:"The largest local government record office in the UK, holding London's administrative memory from 1067 to last week — 72 miles of shelving in a former print works on Northampton Road. Parish registers, maps, photographs, court records: all freely searchable. London's memory in a Clerkenwell printshop.",city:"london"},{id:"ironmongerrowbaths",n:"Ironmonger Row Baths",a:"Norman Street, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5266,lng:-0.0947,c:"lido",s:"Art Deco pool where Eastern Promises was filmed",q:"Ironmonger Row Baths London",w:"Built 1931, extended to Art Deco glory in 1938, this Islington baths survived the Blitz and near-closure before restoration in 2012. The Turkish baths still hold original marble slabs. Cronenberg shot the fight scene from Eastern Promises in the pool. Working-class institution.",city:"london"},{id:"charltonlido",n:"Charlton Lido",a:"Shooters Hill Road, Charlton",pc:"",lat:51.4754,lng:0.0389,c:"lido",s:"Opened 1939, closed 4 months later for a war",q:"Charlton Lido London",w:"The last municipal lido built in London opened in June 1939 and closed that October, requisitioned before most Londoners had swum a length. It sat derelict for decades — storage, army training ground — before a community campaign forced its restoration. Reopened 2013. Seventy years between dips.",city:"london"},{id:"tobaccodock",n:"Tobacco Dock",a:"Wapping Lane, Wapping",pc:"",lat:51.5083,lng:-0.0595,c:"maritime",s:"Rennie's granite vaults held 8 million gallons of wine",q:"Tobacco Dock London",w:"John Rennie built this in 1812 as the most secure warehouse in the world: underground granite vaults holding 8 million gallons of wine, prison-like security to deter endemic dockland theft. The tobacco came later. Grade I listed. The 1980s shopping mall failed inside two years. Rennie's vaults endure.",city:"london"},{id:"islingtontunnel",n:"Islington Tunnel",a:"Regent's Canal, Islington",pc:"",lat:51.5349,lng:-0.1155,c:"canals",s:"960 yards, no towpath — bargees walked the ceiling",q:"Islington Tunnel London",w:"London's longest canal tunnel runs 960 yards under Islington. No towpath: bargees 'legged' their boats through by lying on the roof and walking the tunnel ceiling with their feet, while the horse was led over the hill above. Waymarker stones on the surface still trace that overland route.",city:"london"},{id:"phonicarecords",n:"Phonica Records",a:"Poland Street, Soho",pc:"",lat:51.5143,lng:-0.137,c:"vinyl",s:"Soho's electronic underground, since 2003",q:"Phonica Records London",w:"Specialist in electronic, dance and experimental music since 2003, Phonica has become one of Europe's most respected independent shops — stocking releases most shops never carry, hosting in-stores for The Vinyl Factory and Ninja Tune. The listening posts are the point. You discover music by being here.",city:"london"},{id:"shaperorarebooks",n:"Shapero Rare Books",a:"New Bond Street, Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.5141,lng:-0.1467,c:"bookshops",s:"The only antiquarian bookseller on Bond Street",q:"Shapero Rare Books London",w:"Since 1979, Bernard Shapero has run the only antiquarian bookshop on Bond Street — a street otherwise given entirely to watches and handbags. Stock runs from medieval manuscripts to first-edition travel and antique maps. One of the world's leading dealers in early cartography. A library tucked into a jewellery box.",city:"london"},{id:"skoobbooks",n:"Skoob Books",a:"Brunswick Centre, Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.5243,lng:-0.1238,c:"bookshops",s:"70,000 titles below street level",q:"Skoob Books London",w:"'Books' backwards, underground in the Brunswick Centre's basement — Skoob is Bloomsbury's secondhand institution, with over 70,000 titles across every subject. Moved below ground in 2008 after 25 years above. The philosophy section fills a wall. Time Out's best London bookshop, 2010. You'll lose an afternoon.",city:"london"},{id:"clothfair",n:"Cloth Fair",a:"West Smithfield, City",pc:"",lat:51.5192,lng:-0.0994,c:"alley",s:"London's oldest surviving street of houses",q:"Cloth Fair London",w:"Numbers 41 and 42 are the oldest domestic houses in the City — around 1614, pre-dating the Great Fire by 52 years, timber-framed upper storeys intact. Betjeman lived at no.43 and called it his favourite London street. The cloth fair ran here from 1133 to 1855. Gates shut nightly until 1910.",city:"london"},{id:"stapleinn",n:"Staple Inn",a:"High Holborn",pc:"",lat:51.5174,lng:-0.1113,c:"medieval",s:"Last intact Elizabethan street facade in London",q:"Staple Inn London",w:"London's only surviving Inn of Chancery: the timber-framed black-and-white facade on High Holborn dates to 1585, one of the last intact Elizabethan frontages in the city. Survived the Great Fire and the Blitz. Johnson wrote Rasselas in one week in a room here to pay for his mother's funeral.",city:"london"},{id:"stgeorgesbloomsbury",n:"St George's Bloomsbury",a:"Bloomsbury Way",pc:"",lat:51.5177,lng:-0.1247,c:"follies",s:"Hawksmoor pyramid topped with George I in a toga",q:"St George's Bloomsbury London",w:"Hawksmoor's last London church (1716-31) is also his strangest: the steeple is a stepped pyramid after the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, and on top — instead of a cross — stands King George I dressed as a Roman emperor. Hogarth mocked it in 'Gin Lane'. He had a point.",city:"london"},{id:"55broadway",n:"55 Broadway",a:"St James's Park, Westminster",pc:"",lat:51.4994,lng:-0.1337,c:"artdeco",s:"Underground HQ whose sculptures caused a national scandal",q:"55 Broadway London",w:"Charles Holden's 1929 London Underground headquarters was the tallest steel-framed office in London at completion. Jacob Epstein's 'Day' and 'Night' on the exterior so shocked the press that the Underground chairman resigned. The sculptures stayed. Grade I listed. Still there.",city:"london"},{id:"dailyexpressbuilding2",n:"Daily Express Building",a:"Fleet Street, City",pc:"",lat:51.5144,lng:-0.1059,c:"artdeco",s:"Black vitrolite and chrome — the 'Black Lubyanka'",q:"Daily Express Building London",w:"The 1932 Daily Express building clad in black vitrolite glass and chrome — London's first fully curtain-walled building — was nicknamed the 'Black Lubyanka' by rival papers. Its streamlined facade conceals a gilded Art Deco foyer with Eric Aumonier relief sculptures. Now Goldman Sachs. The lobby is occasionally open.",city:"london"},{id:"thehorsehospital",n:"The Horse Hospital",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.52279,lng:-0.12436,c:"oddity",s:"London's only surviving double-decker stable",q:"The Horse Hospital London",w:"Built in 1797 by James Burton to rest ailing horses en route to the city — the concrete ramps inside, grooved for grip, still slope between floors. Became an underground arts venue in 1993 and never really recovered from that sentence. Grade II listed. Still weird.",city:"london"},{id:"oldewineshades",n:"Olde Wine Shades",a:"City",pc:"",lat:51.5105,lng:-0.08806,c:"wine",s:"The City's only pre-Fire tavern still trading",q:"Olde Wine Shades London",w:"A Martin Lane cellar that opened before Charles II's navy had a single ship ready for war — and survived both the Great Fire of 1666 and the Blitz. Charles Dickens drank here. The ceilings are low, the wine list is serious, and history is the house speciality.",city:"london"},{id:"cutlershall",n:"Cutlers' Hall",a:"City",pc:"",lat:51.51557,lng:-0.10094,c:"livery",s:"Blitz survivor granted by Henry V post-Agincourt",q:"Cutlers' Hall London",w:"Henry V granted the Cutlers their charter after Agincourt — the French couldn't have fought without English blades. The Warwick Lane hall is the only livery building in the City to survive the 1940 Blitz alongside St Paul's. The frieze was cut by a sculptor who started as a Sheffield knife-grinder's boy.",city:"london"},{id:"stantholinsspire",n:"St Antholin's Spire",a:"Sydenham",pc:"",lat:51.43566,lng:-0.05795,c:"follies",s:"Wren spire exiled to a suburban garden",q:"St Antholin's Spire London",w:"When the Corporation of London demolished St Antholin's Watling Street in 1874, a local vicar rescued Christopher Wren's elegant stone spire from the rubble and re-erected it in a South London garden. It stands in a residential street in Sydenham still — a 17th-century City church tower marooned among Victorian villas.",city:"london"},{id:"gasholderpark",n:"Gasholder Park",a:"King's Cross",pc:"",lat:51.53702,lng:-0.12815,c:"canals",s:"A Victorian gasometer ring, now a park",q:"Gasholder Park London",w:"Three cast-iron gasholders stood here from the 1850s. The guide frames — 240 tonnes of Victorian ironwork — were dismantled, shot-blasted, and rebuilt as a circular park. The Regent's Canal laps one edge. Inside the ring: lawn, benches, a fountain. Outside: the most photographed backdrop in King's Cross.",city:"london"},{id:"bleedingheartyard",n:"Bleeding Heart Yard",a:"Holborn",pc:"",lat:51.51939,lng:-0.10681,c:"alley",s:"Named for a lady found torn apart at a ball",q:"Bleeding Heart Yard London",w:"Legend says Lady Elizabeth Hatton was discovered here in January 1626, her heart still bleeding in the cobbles — torn apart by the Devil at a Hatton Garden ball. More likely a disputed estate. The courtyard is real, the atmosphere is vintage London. The story is better than the facts.",city:"london"},{id:"56artillerylane",n:"56 Artillery Lane",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"",lat:51.51818,lng:-0.07683,c:"history",s:"London's finest Georgian shopfront, barely noticed",q:"56 Artillery Lane London",w:"Built 1750-56 for Huguenot silk merchants Jourdain and Rybot — possibly by Sir Robert Taylor — this is the most complete mid-Georgian shopfront in London. Five Doric columns, a carved fascia, Venetian window above. The interior period rooms were shipped to the Art Institute of Chicago in 1927. The facade stayed.",city:"london"},{id:"elderstreet",n:"Elder Street",a:"Spitalfields",pc:"",lat:51.52127,lng:-0.07681,c:"history",s:"The most complete Georgian street in London",q:"Elder Street London",w:"Two terraces of 1720s-30s houses between Norton Folgate and Fleur de Lis Street — built when Spitalfields was Huguenot silk-weaving country. The area faced slum clearance in the 1970s; residents fought and won. Grade II listed streetscape. A ghost of Georgian London, walkable and mostly unchanged.",city:"london"},{id:"stmaryathill",n:"St Mary-at-Hill",a:"City",pc:"",lat:51.51012,lng:-0.08371,c:"medieval",s:"The City church Betjeman called least-spoiled",q:"St Mary-at-Hill London",w:"Thomas Tallis was organist here in 1538. Wren rebuilt the interior after the Great Fire but left the 12th-century walls standing — archaeologists have since found Roman and Anglo-Saxon layers beneath. Betjeman called it 'the least spoiled interior in the City'. Skulls carved above the passageway. Hidden off Lovat Lane.",city:"london"},{id:"croftonromanvilla",n:"Crofton Roman Villa",a:"Orpington",pc:"",lat:51.37296,lng:0.08786,c:"roman",s:"Largest intact Roman villa in Greater London",q:"Crofton Roman Villa London",w:"A working Roman farm from the 2nd century AD, with underfloor hypocaust heating still readable in its footprint. The largest accessible Roman villa in Greater London — and the least visited. Open weekends, hidden inside a housing estate in Orpington. The floors that warmed a Roman family are where they left them.",city:"london"},{id:"redhouse",n:"Red House",a:"Bexleyheath",pc:"",lat:51.45541,lng:0.13033,c:"history",s:"William Morris built his dream house and left it",q:"Red House London",w:"Philip Webb designed this red-brick house for William Morris in 1859 — the two of them inventing what became the Arts and Crafts movement in the process. Morris lived here 1860-65, then abandoned it when the commute to London became too much. National Trust now. The garden is resolutely medieval.",city:"london"},{id:"gillespieparknaturereser",n:"Gillespie Park Nature Reserve",a:"Highbury",pc:"",lat:51.56059,lng:-0.10645,c:"green",s:"Chalk grassland hidden in Islington's back streets",q:"Gillespie Park Nature Reserve London",w:"A decommissioned railway siding in Islington that nature quietly colonised. Five hectares of chalk grassland, secondary woodland, and scrub — habitats you'd normally travel to the Chilterns to find. SSSI designated. Ranger-led events in season. The path emerges on Drayton Park Road and you wonder where you've been.",city:"london"},{id:"marshallstreetleisurecen",n:"Marshall Street Leisure Centre",a:"Soho",pc:"",lat:51.51375,lng:-0.13769,c:"lido",s:"Soho's marble-lined pool with LGBTQ+ history",q:"Marshall Street Leisure Centre London",w:"Opened 1931, lined with Sicilian and Swedish green marble, a bronze fountain at the shallow end. The Marshall Street baths became an important LGBTQ+ meeting place in the decades when that carried real risk. Closed 1997, the council nearly sold it off; a campaign saved it and it reopened 2010 with the marble intact.",city:"london"},{id:"fishmongershall",n:"Fishmongers' Hall",a:"City",pc:"",lat:51.50937,lng:-0.08764,c:"livery",s:"Oldest livery hall on its original site",q:"Fishmongers' Hall London",w:"The Fishmongers have stood on this bank of the Thames since the 13th century — the only livery company on its original site. A Fishmonger Lord Mayor killed Wat Tyler with a dagger at Smithfield in 1381; the dagger is still here. The hall overlooks the bridge and has Roman wharf timbers beneath its foundations.",city:"london"},{id:"supertonerecords",n:"Supertone Records",a:"Brixton",pc:"",lat:51.45972,lng:-0.12355,c:"vinyl",s:"Brixton's last great Caribbean record shop",q:"Supertone Records London",w:"Wally Bryant opened Supertone on Acre Lane in 1984 — the shop grew from a soundsystem founded in 1969 that rocked Brixton long before the riots. Reggae, roots, dub, dancehall, lovers rock: every pressing, every sleeve. One of the last specialist Caribbean music shops in south London. Still going.",city:"london"},{id:"whitechapelgallery",n:"Whitechapel Gallery",a:"Whitechapel",pc:"",lat:51.51617,lng:-0.07031,c:"museum",s:"Where Guernica came to London for a pair of boots",q:"Whitechapel Gallery London",w:"Founded 1901 to bring great art to the East End; when Picasso's Guernica came for its only UK showing in 1939, the entrance fee was a pair of boots donated to the Spanish Republic. The Art Nouveau building launched Hockney, Freud, Kapoor. Still free. The boots story alone earns it a spot.",city:"london"},{id:"stbridelibrary",n:"St Bride Library",a:"Fleet Street",pc:"",lat:51.51351,lng:-0.10502,c:"archive",s:"65,000 books on type, ink, and the press",q:"St Bride Library London",w:"When the newspapers left Fleet Street, their typefaces stayed — here, in the world's largest specialist collection of printing and typography. Opened 1895. Original punches from the Caslon and Figgins foundries, 65,000 books, type specimens no one else preserved. Down a passage off Bride Lane.",city:"london"},{id:"wienerlibrary",n:"Wiener Library",a:"Bloomsbury",pc:"",lat:51.52161,lng:-0.128,c:"archive",s:"World's oldest Holocaust archive, open to all",q:"Wiener Library London",w:"Alfred Wiener began collecting documents on Nazi persecution in Berlin in the late 1920s, moved them to Amsterdam, then London. The library arrived in 1939 and never left. Now at Russell Square: over a million items — testimony, documents, photographs — open to any researcher, no appointment needed.",city:"london"},{id:"macclesfieldbridge",n:"Macclesfield Bridge",a:"Regent's Park",pc:"",lat:51.5346,lng:-0.16326,c:"canals",s:"Blow Up Bridge — the 1874 gunpowder explosion",q:"Macclesfield Bridge London",w:"On 2 October 1874 a gunpowder barge exploded beneath this canal bridge — four killed, blast heard 12 miles away, the Zoo animals panicked for hours. Bridge rebuilt from the salvage; the columns reportedly went back in upside-down. Parliament passed the Explosives Act 1875 as a direct result.",city:"london"},{id:"williammorrisgallery",n:"William Morris Gallery",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"",lat:51.59129,lng:-0.0203,c:"museum",s:"Morris's own boyhood home, now a world collection",q:"William Morris Gallery London",w:"William Morris's family moved to Water House in 1848 when he was 14; he left at 19 and kept designing for six more decades. The Georgian villa now holds the world's largest public collection of his work — textiles, tiles, furniture, drawings. Clement Attlee opened it in 1950. Won Museum of the Year in 2013. Free.",city:"london"},{id:"oxotowerwharf",n:"OXO Tower Wharf",a:"South Bank",pc:"",lat:51.50838,lng:-0.1083,c:"ghostsign",s:"Ad ban dodged by building the letters into the tower",q:"OXO Tower Wharf London",w:"Liebig (Oxo) bought a Royal Mail power station in the 1920s and needed a sign. The LCC refused. The architect designed tower windows in O-X-O pattern instead — architecture, not advertising, technically. The LCC allowed it. Tower built 1929. Nobody was fooled and nobody cared.",city:"london"},{id:"anchortap",n:"Anchor Tap",a:"Bermondsey",pc:"",lat:51.50355,lng:-0.07592,c:"pub",s:"CAMRA-listed Courage tap, hidden off Tower Bridge",q:"Anchor Tap London",w:"The tap room to the Courage Anchor Brewery that once dominated Horsleydown — tucked into a back street behind Tower Bridge. Samuel Smith's bought it in the early 1990s and kept the Victorian bar counter and tiled fireplaces untouched. CAMRA National Inventory. You have to know it's there.",city:"london"},{id:"dutchchurchaustinfriars",n:"Dutch Church Austin Friars",a:"City",pc:"",lat:51.5155,lng:-0.08563,c:"faith",s:"World's oldest Dutch church, granted by Edward VI in 1550",q:"Dutch Church Austin Friars London",w:"Edward VI granted the nave of a medieval Augustinian friary to Protestant refugees from the Low Countries in 1550 — making this the first officially sanctioned nonconformist chapel in England. The original building was destroyed in the Blitz on 15 October 1940; the rebuilt church holds services in Dutch to this day.",city:"london"},{id:"goldsmithscentreforconte",n:"Goldsmiths Centre for Contemporary Art",a:"New Cross",pc:"",lat:51.47435,lng:-0.03702,c:"museum",s:"Turner Prize winners converted Victorian baths to gallery",q:"Goldsmiths Centre for Contemporary Art London",w:"An 1898 public baths closed in 1991, left empty, then handed to Assemble — the architecture collective who won the Turner Prize in 2015. They built a gallery inside it: cast-iron tank panels as walls, turquoise concrete echoing the original tiles. Opened 2018. The pool is gone but the room still holds its shape.",city:"london"},{id:"limehousecut",n:"Limehouse Cut",a:"Limehouse",pc:"",lat:51.51657,lng:-0.02161,c:"canals",s:"London's oldest canal, dug in 16 months",q:"Limehouse Cut London",w:"Authorised 1767, opened 17 September 1770 — 1.4 miles from Bow Locks to the Thames at Limehouse Basin, dug in 16 months to bypass the tidal Lea. London's oldest canal, still navigable. Factories once lined every inch of bank; now the towpath runs between converted warehouses and the A12.",city:"london"},{id:"highburyfields",n:"Highbury Fields",a:"Highbury",pc:"",lat:51.55129,lng:-0.1001,c:"history",s:"Site of Britain's first gay rights demo, 1970",q:"Highbury Fields London",w:"On 27 November 1970, 150 Gay Liberation Front members gathered here with torches to protest the arrest of Louis Eaks for 'importuning' — Britain's first organised gay rights demo. The Peasants' Revolt mustered on the same fields in 1381. Islington parkland. More history than it looks.",city:"london"},{id:"hollyvillage",n:"Holly Village",a:"Highgate",pc:"",lat:51.56286,lng:-0.14612,c:"oddity",s:"England's first gated community, in Gothic Revival",q:"Holly Village London",w:"Baroness Angela Burdett-Coutts — one of the wealthiest women in Victorian Britain — commissioned eight Gothic fantasy cottages around a central green from Henry Astley Darbishire in 1865. Still a private estate behind an ornate archway. Claimed to be England's first gated community. You peer through the gates.",city:"london"},{id:"middlesexfilterbeds",n:"Middlesex Filter Beds",a:"Clapton",pc:"",lat:51.56073,lng:-0.04073,c:"green",s:"Victorian water filter beds, returned to nature",q:"Middlesex Filter Beds London",w:"Six filter beds built in 1852 by the East London Waterworks Company after cholera outbreaks — purifying River Lea water for east London. Decommissioned 1969; nature moved in. Now a Lee Valley wildlife reserve: 200 plant species, 60 bird species, the brick filter walls still standing.",city:"london"},{id:"wellhallpleasaunce",n:"Well Hall Pleasaunce",a:"Eltham",pc:"",lat:51.45703,lng:0.04923,c:"history",s:"Edith Nesbit's moated manor, now a public park",q:"Well Hall Pleasaunce London",w:"Well Hall estate has a moat recorded in the 13th century; Margaret Roper, daughter of Thomas More, lived here after 1521. Edith Nesbit — who wrote The Railway Children — made it her home from 1899 to 1921, hosting literary parties in the Tudor Barn that still stands. The park opened 1933. The barn is now a restaurant.",city:"london"},{id:"harmondsworthgreatbarn",n:"Harmondsworth Great Barn",a:"Harmondsworth, Hillingdon",pc:"",lat:51.4898,lng:-0.4799,c:"medieval",s:"Cathedral of Middlesex, shadow of Heathrow",q:"Harmondsworth Great Barn London",w:"Betjeman called it the 'Cathedral of Middlesex'. Built c.1426 by Winchester College, this 190 ft aisled barn is the largest timber-framed building in England — oak cruck frames still standing after 600 years, within earshot of Heathrow's runways. Extraordinary survivor.",city:"london"},{id:"downhouse",n:"Down House",a:"Downe, Bromley",pc:"",lat:51.3314,lng:0.0534,c:"history",s:"Where Darwin overthrew the world, one worm at a time",q:"Down House London",w:"Darwin lived here for 40 years — it was at this desk that he wrote 'On the Origin of Species', pacing the Sandwalk garden path to think. He spent his last years quietly obsessing over earthworms. English Heritage; the garden is unchanged.",city:"london"},{id:"bromleyhall",n:"Bromley Hall",a:"Bromley-by-Bow, Tower Hamlets",pc:"",lat:51.5192,lng:-0.0099,c:"medieval",s:"London's oldest brick house, hidden in Bow",q:"Bromley Hall London",w:"Tree-ring dating puts this at c.1485 — year of Bosworth — the oldest surviving brick house in London. Built by Holy Trinity Priory, seized by Henry VIII in 1531. Its tenant John Blount was father of the King's mistress. Tudor wall paintings survived inside until 2005. Now offices.",city:"london"},{id:"oldpalaceofcroydon",n:"Old Palace of Croydon",a:"Old Town, Croydon",pc:"",lat:51.3723,lng:-0.1043,c:"medieval",s:"Archbishops' summer retreat since before the Conquest",q:"Old Palace of Croydon London",w:"Archbishops' summer palace for 500+ years — records go back before 960. Henry VIII called it 'rheumatick'; Elizabeth I danced with Hatton in its Long Gallery. The 15th-century Great Hall still stands inside what is now a school.",city:"london"},{id:"carshaltonwatertower",n:"Carshalton Water Tower",a:"Carshalton, Sutton",pc:"",lat:51.3651,lng:-0.1668,c:"follies",s:"Sutton's 1720 garden folly with England's oldest bathroom",q:"Carshalton Water Tower London",w:"Built 1717-20 as a garden pavilion and water pump, it holds what may be the earliest surviving country-house bathroom in England — the Bagnio, a plunge bath with original 18th-century tiles. A Hermitage and Folly Bridge complete the lakeside trio. Volunteers run the whole thing.",city:"london"},{id:"surbitonstation",n:"Surbiton Station",a:"Surbiton, Kingston upon Thames",pc:"",lat:51.3922,lng:-0.3043,c:"artdeco",s:"The purest Art Deco station on the network",q:"Surbiton Station London",w:"Widely regarded as the finest Art Deco station in Britain — white reinforced concrete, curved canopies, marbled booking hall, opened 1938. The architect, James Robb Scott, also gave Waterloo its Victory Arch. Filmed for Poirot and Harry Potter. Grade II listed, quietly iconic.",city:"london"},{id:"hallplace",n:"Hall Place",a:"Bexley",pc:"",lat:51.448,lng:0.1593,c:"history",s:"Tudor-Stuart manor in flint and rubble, deep in Bexley",q:"Hall Place London",w:"Two houses fused into one: the 1537 flint-and-rubble Tudor wing built by Sir John Champneys, and the 1649 red-brick Stuart extension added after the Civil War. Grade I listed, it sits beside the River Cray. During WWII the house relayed signals to Bletchley Park; topiary animals still stalk the gardens.",city:"london"},{id:"rainhamhall",n:"Rainham Hall",a:"Rainham, Havering",pc:"",lat:51.5179,lng:0.1908,c:"history",s:"A Georgian captain's house, shuttered for 66 years",q:"Rainham Hall London",w:"Built 1729 for a Baltic merchant captain, it went to the National Trust in 1949 then sat behind locked doors for 66 years — private tenants, no public access until 2015. Grade II* listed; the ironwork gate may be Jean Tijou's work. Appeared in the BBC's 'A Christmas Carol' (2019).",city:"london"},{id:"stationershall",n:"Stationers' Hall",a:"Blackfriars, City of London",pc:"",lat:51.5143,lng:-0.1015,c:"livery",s:"Where copyright was invented, in a hall since 1606",q:"Stationers' Hall London",w:"On this site since 1606, the Stationers' Company invented copyright: their 1557 charter required printers to register every title here. The King James Bible was finalised in this building. One of the most consequential rooms in publishing history, hidden behind a City courtyard.",city:"london"},{id:"poeplaquestokenewington",n:"Poe Plaque, Stoke Newington",a:"Stoke Newington, Hackney",pc:"",lat:51.5618,lng:-0.0823,c:"literary",s:"Edgar Allan Poe went to school here, aged eight",q:"Poe Plaque, Stoke Newington London",w:"A plaque at 172 Stoke Newington Church Street marks the Manor House School, where Poe studied from 1817-1820, aged eight. The headmaster, Reverend Bransby, appears by name in 'William Wilson' — the story set in 'an old, irregular house' in Stoke Newington. The horror was seeded here.",city:"london"},{id:"walthamforesttownhall",n:"Waltham Forest Town Hall",a:"Walthamstow",pc:"",lat:51.5912,lng:-0.0135,c:"artdeco",s:"Built during the Blitz, V2 metal in its walls",q:"Waltham Forest Town Hall London",w:"Completed 1942 in the Blitz, this stripped Art Deco civic palace has a plaque cast from metal of a V2 rocket that landed nearby on 14 September 1944. Its square is named for William Morris's motto 'Fellowship is Life'. Keith Richards' grandmother was Mayor of Walthamstow in 1941-42.",city:"london"},{id:"bethlemmuseumofthemind",n:"Bethlem Museum of the Mind",a:"Monks Orchard, Bromley",pc:"",lat:51.3826,lng:-0.0312,c:"museum",s:"Bedlam's own museum, inside Bedlam",q:"Bethlem Museum of the Mind London",w:"'Bedlam' has treated the mentally ill since 1247 — the oldest psychiatric institution in the world. The museum, opened 2015, covers that full history including the era when Londoners paid to gawp at patients. The gallery beside it shows work by current and former patients. Unexpectedly moving.",city:"london"},{id:"manorfarmruislip",n:"Manor Farm, Ruislip",a:"Ruislip, Hillingdon",pc:"",lat:51.5785,lng:-0.4277,c:"medieval",s:"Norman motte, 13th-century barn, Ruislip's hidden core",q:"Manor Farm, Ruislip London",w:"Hidden in suburban Ruislip: a 13th-century Great Barn (the oldest timber-framed barn in Greater London), a Norman motte-and-bailey mound, and a site used since the 9th century. Bec Abbey in Normandy owned it from 1096 to 1404. Now a park, lido, and arts centre.",city:"london"},{id:"haveringattebower",n:"Havering-atte-Bower",a:"Havering-atte-Bower, Havering",pc:"",lat:51.6192,lng:0.1824,c:"history",s:"Royal palace village, stocks and ducking pond still here",q:"Havering-atte-Bower London",w:"A royal manor from before the Conquest until 1686 — queens consort held it for centuries, which gave it the 'Bower'. The original village stocks still stand on the green opposite a pond said, by legend, to have been used for ducking witches. One of the highest points in east London.",city:"london"},{id:"theoldvic",n:"The Old Vic",a:"Waterloo",pc:"",lat:51.502,lng:-0.10931,c:"history",s:"Renamed four times before 'Old Vic' finally stuck",q:"The Old Vic London",w:"Renamed four times before anyone settled on 'the Old Vic'. Manager Lilian Baylis made it a Shakespeare house from 1914, it took a bombing in the Blitz and kept going, and its own repertory company quietly became the National Theatre before that outfit grew up and moved to the South Bank in 1976.",city:"london"},{id:"marlboroughhouse",n:"Marlborough House",a:"St James's",pc:"",lat:51.505,lng:-0.13583,c:"history",s:"A duchess's mansion, later two widowed queens' retreat",q:"Marlborough House London",w:"Built in 1711 for Sarah Churchill, Duchess of Marlborough - Queen Anne's fiercest confidante. It stayed a Marlborough town house for a century, then a royal one: two widowed queens, Alexandra and Mary, each retreated here in old age. Since 1965 it has quietly run the Commonwealth instead.",city:"london"},{id:"earlscourtexhibitioncent",n:"Earls Court Exhibition Centre",a:"Earl's Court",pc:"",lat:51.48889,lng:-0.19778,c:"history",s:"Vanished Art Deco hall where Buffalo Bill met the Queen",q:"Earls Court Exhibition Centre London",w:"Buffalo Bill's Wild West show pitched camp here in the 1880s, with Queen Victoria among the spectators - the start of a site that grew into an Art Moderne exhibition hall in the 1930s. Developers got a Certificate of Immunity from Listing before English Heritage could step in; the whole thing came down in 2017.",city:"london"},{id:"baitulfutuhmosque",n:"Baitul Futuh Mosque",a:"Morden",pc:"",lat:51.39611,lng:-0.19889,c:"faith",s:"Europe's biggest mosque, built purely on donations",q:"Baitul Futuh Mosque London",w:"One of Europe's largest mosque complexes sits in unassuming Morden, paid for entirely by Ahmadi Muslim donations - built partly underground just to fit 13,000 worshippers in. Its Friday sermons go out live across a private global satellite network, and most weeks it's out feeding London's homeless too.",city:"london"},{id:"hrhiggins",n:"H.R. Higgins",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.5131,lng:-0.15066,c:"coffee",s:"coffee merchant by royal warrant",q:"H.R. Higgins London",w:"coffee merchant by royal warrant; wood-panelled tasting room below the shop",city:"london"},{id:"camdencoffeeshop",n:"Camden Coffee Shop",a:"Delancey St",pc:"",lat:51.53682,lng:-0.14209,c:"coffee",s:"one man, one vintage drum roaster, smoke out the door on roa",q:"Camden Coffee Shop London",w:"one man, one vintage drum roaster, smoke out the door on roasting days",city:"london"},{id:"wmartyn",n:"W. Martyn",a:"Muswell Hill",pc:"",lat:51.59063,lng:-0.14346,c:"coffee",s:"tea-and-coffee grocer with original fittings",q:"W. Martyn London",w:"tea-and-coffee grocer with original fittings; the smell reaches the street",city:"london"},{id:"chinchinlabs",n:"Chin Chin Labs",a:"Camden",pc:"",lat:51.5414,lng:-0.14631,c:"food",s:"liquid-nitrogen ice cream theatre",q:"Chin Chin Labs London",w:"liquid-nitrogen ice cream theatre; Europe's first, made to order in clouds of vapour",city:"london"},{id:"lagelatiera",n:"La Gelatiera",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.5111,lng:-0.12677,c:"food",s:"micro-batch counter behind the piazza",q:"La Gelatiera London",w:"micro-batch counter behind the piazza; basil-and-chilli as a house classic",city:"london"},{id:"marineices",n:"Marine Ices",a:"Chalk Farm",pc:"",lat:51.54329,lng:-0.15009,c:"food",s:"the Mansi family's Italian ice dynasty by the Roundhouse",q:"Marine Ices London",w:"the Mansi family's Italian ice dynasty by the Roundhouse; complicated succession, glorious history",city:"london"},{id:"rinkoffs",n:"Rinkoff's",a:"Whitechapel",pc:"",lat:51.52199,lng:-0.06453,c:"bakery",s:"fourth-generation East End Jewish bakery",q:"Rinkoff's London",w:"fourth-generation East End Jewish bakery; invented the crodough before the cronut crossed the Atlantic (they'd say)",city:"london"},{id:"konditor",n:"Konditor",a:"Waterloo",pc:"",lat:51.50416,lng:-0.11028,c:"bakery",s:"the Curly Whirly cake",q:"Konditor London",w:"the Curly Whirly cake; a neighbourhood bakery that became a London noun",city:"london"},{id:"rockandsoleplaice",n:"Rock & Sole Plaice",a:"Covent Garden",pc:"",lat:51.5148,lng:-0.12518,c:"food",s:"bills itself London's oldest chippy",q:"Rock & Sole Plaice London",w:"bills itself London's oldest chippy",city:"london"},{id:"lemonia",n:"Lemonia",a:"Primrose Hill",pc:"",lat:51.54164,lng:-0.15701,c:"food",s:"the Greek-Cypriot taverna as neighbourhood institution",q:"Lemonia London",w:"the Greek-Cypriot taverna as neighbourhood institution",city:"london"},{id:"stevehatt",n:"Steve Hatt",a:"Essex Rd",pc:"",lat:51.5385,lng:-0.09889,c:"food",s:"fourth-generation fishmonger",q:"Steve Hatt London",w:"fourth-generation fishmonger; ice, marble, and no nonsense",city:"london"},{id:"loonfung",n:"Loon Fung",a:"Chinatown",pc:"",lat:51.51189,lng:-0.13033,c:"food",s:"among the first Chinese supermarkets in Britain",q:"Loon Fung London",w:"among the first Chinese supermarkets in Britain",city:"london"},{id:"brindisa",n:"Brindisa",a:"Borough",pc:"",lat:51.50508,lng:-0.09044,c:"food",s:"the counter that introduced Britain to proper jamon",q:"Brindisa London",w:"the counter that introduced Britain to proper jamon",city:"london"},{id:"japancentre",n:"Japan Centre",a:"Piccadilly",pc:"",lat:51.50971,lng:-0.13152,c:"food",s:"from a bookshop to the Japanese pantry of London",q:"Japan Centre London",w:"from a bookshop to the Japanese pantry of London",city:"london"},{id:"chalcotcrescent",n:"Chalcot Crescent",a:"Primrose Hill",pc:"",lat:51.54001,lng:-0.15637,c:"film",s:"the pastel terrace that played Windsor Gardens in Paddington",q:"Chalcot Crescent London",w:"the pastel terrace that played Windsor Gardens in Paddington",city:"london"},{id:"australiahouse",n:"Australia House",a:"Strand",pc:"",lat:51.51287,lng:-0.11559,c:"film",s:"Gringotts' interior",q:"Australia House London",w:"Gringotts' interior; the real marble hall glimpsed through the doors (exterior only)",city:"london"},{id:"millenniumbridge",n:"Millennium Bridge",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.50989,lng:-0.09851,c:"film",s:"the bridge the Death Eaters folded in Half-Blood Prince",q:"Millennium Bridge London",w:"the bridge the Death Eaters folded in Half-Blood Prince; also the Guardians of the Galaxy bridge",city:"london"},{id:"stkatharinedocks",n:"St Katharine Docks",a:"Tower",pc:"",lat:51.50654,lng:-0.07165,c:"maritime",s:"the yacht world of The Long Good Friday's ending",q:"St Katharine Docks London",w:"the yacht world of The Long Good Friday's ending",city:"london"},{id:"grosvenorchapel",n:"Grosvenor Chapel",a:"Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.50905,lng:-0.15121,c:"faith",s:"Love Actually wedding",q:"Grosvenor Chapel London",w:"Love Actually wedding",city:"london"},{id:"40stansfieldroad",n:"40 Stansfield Road",a:"Brixton",pc:"",lat:51.46594,lng:-0.11812,c:"music",s:"Bowie's birthplace",q:"40 Stansfield Road London",w:"Bowie's birthplace; pair with the Tunstall Road mural shrine",city:"london"},{id:"ministryofsound",n:"Ministry of Sound",a:"Elephant and Castle",pc:"",lat:51.49777,lng:-0.0995,c:"music",s:"1991, the club that institutionalised UK club culture",q:"Ministry of Sound London",w:"1991, the club that institutionalised UK club culture",city:"london"},{id:"pointhill",n:"Point Hill",a:"Greenwich",pc:"",lat:51.47453,lng:-0.00986,c:"view",s:"the local's alternative to the tourist summit",q:"Point Hill London",w:"the local's alternative to the tourist summit",city:"london"},{id:"blythehillfields",n:"Blythe Hill Fields",a:"Catford",pc:"",lat:51.44706,lng:-0.0356,c:"view",s:"borders the SE sleeper panorama",q:"Blythe Hill Fields London",w:"borders the SE sleeper panorama",city:"london"},{id:"805",n:"805",a:"Old Kent Rd",pc:"",lat:51.47988,lng:-0.05683,c:"food",s:"Nigerian institution that made the OKR a destination",q:"805 London",w:"Nigerian institution that made the OKR a destination",city:"london"},{id:"london-stadium-brisbaneroad",n:"Brisbane Road",a:"London",pc:"",lat:51.5601,lng:-0.0125,c:"stadium",s:"Home of Leyton Orient",q:"Brisbane Road London",w:"Home ground of Leyton Orient.",city:"london"},{id:"london-stadium-vicarageroad",n:"Vicarage Road",a:"London",pc:"",lat:51.6499,lng:-0.4015,c:"stadium",s:"Home of Watford",q:"Vicarage Road London",w:"Home ground of Watford.",city:"london"},{id:"london-stadium-cherryredrecordsstadium",n:"Cherry Red Records Stadium",a:"London",pc:"",lat:51.4316,lng:-0.19,c:"stadium",s:"Home of Wimbledon",q:"Cherry Red Records Stadium London",w:"Home ground of Wimbledon.",city:"london"},{id:"london-stadium-twickenhamstoop",n:"Twickenham Stoop",a:"London",pc:"",lat:51.4476,lng:-0.3414,c:"stadium",s:"Home of Harlequins",q:"Twickenham Stoop London",w:"Home ground of Harlequins.",city:"london"},{id:"london-stadium-stonexstadium",n:"StoneX Stadium",a:"London",pc:"",lat:51.6028,lng:-0.2367,c:"stadium",s:"Home of Saracens",q:"StoneX Stadium London",w:"Home ground of Saracens.",city:"london"},{id:"petticoatlane",n:"Petticoat Lane",a:"City / Spitalfields",pc:"",lat:51.51678,lng:-0.0749,c:"market",s:"the Sunday institution that predates its own respectability",q:"Petticoat Lane London",w:"the Sunday institution that predates its own respectability",city:"london"},{id:"portobelloroad",n:"Portobello Road",a:"Notting Hill",pc:"",lat:51.51634,lng:-0.20532,c:"market",s:"antiques mile",q:"Portobello Road London",w:"antiques mile",city:"london"},{id:"newcoventgardenflowermar",n:"New Covent Garden Flower Market",a:"Nine Elms",pc:"",lat:51.47999,lng:-0.13829,c:"market",s:"the 4 a",q:"New Covent Garden Flower Market London",w:"the 4 a.m. wholesale flower world, publicly enterable",city:"london"},{id:"eaststreetmarket",n:"East Street Market",a:"Walworth",pc:"",lat:51.48833,lng:-0.09351,c:"market",s:"Only Fools title-sequence territory and a proper south Londo",q:"East Street Market London",w:"Only Fools title-sequence territory and a proper south London institution",city:"london"},{id:"hostcafe",n:"Host Café",a:"City of London",pc:"",lat:51.5127,lng:-0.0932,c:"coffee",s:"coffee beneath Wren's fan-vaulted ceiling",q:"Host Café London",w:"A non-profit café set inside St Mary Aldermary, one of the City's oldest church sites, rebuilt by Christopher Wren after the Great Fire with an unusually intact Gothic fan-vaulted ceiling. Tables sit among the pews under stained glass while the City rushes past outside -- speciality coffee and home baking in a working church repurposed as a genuine pocket of calm.",city:"london"},{id:"towpathcafe",n:"Towpath Café",a:"De Beauvoir / Haggerston",pc:"",lat:51.5372,lng:-0.0811,c:"food",s:"a seasonal canalside café, tables inches from the Regent's Canal",q:"Towpath Café London",w:"A tiny, seasonal cafe (roughly March to November) strung along the Regent's Canal towpath, with a handful of tables set right at the water's edge. The menu changes with what's good that week, the coffee is taken seriously, and the whole thing runs on an unhurried, waterside rhythm that's made it a long-standing favourite among chefs and writers rather than a tourist stop.",city:"london"},{id:"whitehartlane",n:"White Hart Lane",a:"Tottenham",pc:"",lat:51.60333,lng:-0.06583,c:"stadium",s:"Spurs' home 1899-2017, demolished for new ground",q:"White Hart Lane",w:"Home of Tottenham Hotspur from 1899 to 2017, hosting 2,533 competitive Spurs games across 118 years plus England internationals. Capacity fell from near 80,000 to an all-seated 36,284; record attendance 75,038 for an FA Cup tie v Sunderland, 5 March 1938. Demolished after the 2016-17 season.",city:"london"},{id:"twickenhamstadium",n:"Twickenham Stadium",a:"Twickenham",pc:"",lat:51.45611,lng:-0.34167,c:"stadium",s:"World's largest rugby union stadium, England's home",q:"Twickenham Stadium",w:"Home of the Rugby Football Union and England's national rugby union team, and the world's largest rugby union stadium, capacity around 82,000, second largest in the UK behind Wembley and fourth largest in Europe. Hosted NFL London Games in 2016 and 2017 and concerts by acts including the Rolling Stones and Beyonce.",city:"london"},{id:"thevalley",n:"The Valley",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.48749,lng:0.03563,c:"stadium",s:"Charlton Athletic's home since 1919, near the Thames",q:"The Valley stadium",w:"The Valley has been Charlton Athletic's home ground since 1919, apart from spells of exile in 1923-24 and 1985-92, and holds 27,111 in Charlton, southeast London. Charlton Athletic Women have played home matches there since 2024. Charlton railway station sits a three-minute walk away.",city:"london"},{id:"copperboxarena",n:"Copper Box Arena",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.5444,lng:-0.0201,c:"stadium",s:"2012 Olympics handball arena in Queen Elizabeth Park",q:"Copper Box Arena",w:"Copper Box Arena was built for the 2012 Summer Olympics in Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, London, and was originally known as the Handball Arena. Beyond handball it staged modern pentathlon events - fencing, shooting, swimming, show jumping and running - and served as the goalball venue for the 2012 Paralympics.",city:"london"},{id:"basketballarena",n:"Basketball Arena",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.5486,lng:-0.0139,c:"stadium",s:"2012 Olympics venue, later dismantled",q:"Basketball Arena London",w:"Temporary arena in London's Olympic Park at Stratford, built for basketball at the 2012 Summer Olympics and Paralympics. Designed to be fully recyclable, it was dismantled in January 2013. Its seating was sold to Barnet owner Tony Kleanthous and reused in the construction of The Hive Stadium.",city:"london"},{id:"riverbankarena",n:"Riverbank Arena",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.54944,lng:-0.02056,c:"stadium",s:"Olympic Park's water-based hockey venue",q:"Riverbank Arena",w:"Temporary stadium built in London's Olympic Park, in Hackney Wick, for the 2012 Summer Olympics. Its pitch was a water-based astroturf, the surface used for Olympic field hockey competition, hosting matches during the London 2012 Games before the venue was later dismantled.",city:"london"},{id:"meadowpark",n:"Meadow Park",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.66194,lng:-0.27222,c:"stadium",s:"Boreham Wood's ground and Arsenal academy base",q:"Meadow Park Borehamwood",w:"Home ground of Boreham Wood Football Club in Hertfordshire, sponsored as the Mangata Developments Stadium Meadow Park. Also hosts Arsenal's youth teams and select women's team matches. The wider Meadow Park public park includes play areas, tennis courts, football pitches and a wildflower meadow.",city:"london"},{id:"thehivestadium",n:"The Hive Stadium",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.6026,lng:-0.29179,c:"stadium",s:"Barnet's home, ex-Spurs Women ground",q:"The Hive Stadium",w:"Home of EFL League Two club Barnet, built on the former Prince Edward Playing Fields in Canons Park near Edgware. Also hosted Tottenham Hotspur Women from 2019 to 2022 and is home to London Bees. Official capacity 6500; record attendance 6215, set in a 3-3 draw with Brentford on 28 January 2019.",city:"london"},{id:"ploughlane",n:"Plough Lane",a:"Wimbledon",pc:"",lat:51.43136,lng:-0.18667,c:"stadium",s:"AFC Wimbledon's ground since 2020",q:"Plough Lane",w:"AFC Wimbledon's home ground in Wimbledon, south-west London, since 3 November 2020, currently sponsored as the Cherry Red Records Stadium. Named the 42nd best stadium in world football in a 2024 ranking. Rugby league side London Broncos have shared the ground since 2022.",city:"london"},{id:"kingsmeadow",n:"Kingsmeadow",a:"Norbiton",pc:"",lat:51.40508,lng:-0.28194,c:"stadium",s:"Chelsea Women's ground, ex-home of AFC Wimbledon",q:"Kingsmeadow",w:"Used today for Chelsea Women's and Chelsea Under-21 home matches, Kingsmeadow in Norbiton, Kingston upon Thames, was previously the home ground of both Kingstonian and AFC Wimbledon. It holds 4,850 spectators, including 2,265 seats, making it one of the smaller grounds in London football.",city:"london"},{id:"theo2arena",n:"The O2 Arena",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.50294,lng:0.00319,c:"stadium",s:"Greenwich Peninsula arena inside the old Dome",q:"The O2 Arena London",w:"Indoor arena on the Greenwich Peninsula, opened in its present form in 2007 within the shell of the former Millennium Dome. Ranks third among UK indoor venues by seating capacity, behind Co-op Live and Manchester Arena, and was the world's busiest music arena in 2008. Nearest station is North Greenwich.",city:"london"},{id:"whitecitystadium",n:"White City Stadium",a:"",pc:"",lat:51.51362,lng:-0.2274,c:"stadium",s:"1908 Olympics venue, demolished 1985",q:"White City Stadium London",w:"Built for the 1908 Summer Olympics and demolished in 1985, this west London venue hosted the marathon finish, swimming, boxing and a 1966 World Cup match. From 1927 it staged greyhound racing, running the English Greyhound Derby until closing in 1984. The site is now occupied by White City Place.",city:"london"},{id:"boleynground",n:"Boleyn Ground",a:"Upton Park",pc:"",lat:51.53194,lng:0.03944,c:"stadium",s:"West Ham's Upton Park home, 1904-2016",q:"Boleyn Ground",w:"Also known as Upton Park, this was West Ham United's home from 1904 to 2016, closing capacity 35,016. Charlton Athletic briefly groundshared here in the early 1990s. The final match, 10 May 2016, was a 3-2 win over Manchester United; West Ham then moved to London Stadium and the ground was demolished.",city:"london"},{id:"griffinpark",n:"Griffin Park",a:"Brentford",pc:"",lat:51.48817,lng:-0.30264,c:"stadium",s:"Brentford's old ground, a pub on each corner",q:"Griffin Park",w:"Home of Brentford FC from its opening in September 1904 until August 2020, in a residential part of the London Borough of Hounslow. Famous as the only English league ground with a pub on each of its four corners. Its name refers to the griffin in the logo of Fuller's Brewery, once owner of the orchard it was built on.",city:"london"},{id:"fourboroughs",n:"Four Boroughs",a:"Crystal Palace",pc:"",lat:51.4195,lng:-0.079,c:"coffee",s:"a cycling café named for the point where four London boroughs meet",q:"Four Boroughs London",w:"Opened by three friends, Tom, Ed and Ben, on Church Road in Crystal Palace -- named after the nearby point where the boroughs of Croydon, Bromley, Lambeth and Southwark all meet. Coffee, beer and kombucha for a rider crowd that's since supported a second branch in Loughborough Junction.",city:"london"},{id:"ikoyi",n:"Ikoyi",a:"180 Strand",pc:"",lat:51.51242,lng:-0.11508,c:"food",s:"A tasting-menu room on the ground floor of a Brutalist Strand block, built around West African spice work and smoke.",q:"Ikoyi restaurant London",w:"Chef Jeremy Chan and Iré Hassan-Odukale moved Ikoyi from St James's Market to this raw-concrete Strand address in December 2022, pairing dishes like smoked jollof rice and plantain with a wine-and-sake-heavy list. It holds two Michelin stars and a place among the World's 50 Best Restaurants.",city:"london"},{id:"kol",n:"Kol",a:"Seymour Street, Marylebone",pc:"",lat:51.5148,lng:-0.15719,c:"food",s:"A Marylebone dining room where Mexican technique is rebuilt entirely from British and Nordic-trained instinct, with no lime or avocado in sight.",q:"Kol restaurant London",w:"Santiago Lastra, formerly of Noma, opened Kol in October 2020 on Seymour Street, substituting British ingredients -- woodruff, sea buckthorn, kohlrabi -- for the produce a Mexican kitchen would normally reach for. It won a Michelin star in 2022 and has held it since.",city:"london"},{id:"napoliontheroad",n:"Napoli on the Road",a:"Devonshire Road, Chiswick",pc:"",lat:51.492,lng:-0.25615,c:"food",s:"A small Chiswick pizzeria that has been named Europe's single best pizzeria three years running, without ever leaving the neighbourhood parade it started on.",q:"Napoli on the Road Chiswick",w:"Michele opened Napoli on the Road on Devonshire Road in September 2019 after years selling pizza from a three-wheeled Piaggio Ape at markets. The Chiswick original has topped 50 Top Pizza's Europe ranking for 2024, 2025 and 2026, its dough long-fermented and toppings close to Neapolitan orthodoxy.",city:"london"},{id:"50kalodicirosalvo",n:"50 Kalò di Ciro Salvo",a:"Northumberland Avenue",pc:"",lat:51.50719,lng:-0.12697,c:"food",s:"A third-generation Neapolitan pizzaiolo's London outpost on the traffic-heavy stretch between Trafalgar Square and Embankment.",q:"50 Kalo London pizza",w:"Ciro Salvo, whose family has made pizza in Naples for three generations, opened this Northumberland Avenue branch of 50 Kalò in 2018 as his first venture outside Italy. His dough runs a 70% hydration and a long, slow ferment.",city:"london"},{id:"bonheurbymattabe",n:"Bonheur by Matt Abé",a:"Upper Brook Street, Mayfair",pc:"",lat:51.51127,lng:-0.15512,c:"food",s:"A Mayfair dining room on the old Le Gavroche site, taken over by the chef who ran Restaurant Gordon Ramsay's kitchen for eight years running.",q:"Bonheur by Matt Abe London",w:"Matt Abé, who led Restaurant Gordon Ramsay to eight consecutive years of three Michelin stars, opened his first solo restaurant on Upper Brook Street in November 2025, on the site long occupied by Le Gavroche. Three months later, in the Michelin Guide UK 2026, Bonheur was awarded two stars outright.",city:"london"}];
